    {
      "id": "browser-act/skills",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "BrowserAct",
        "description": "Real-browser automation for agents, plus Skill Forge, which turns a site you scrape often into a reusable skill.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the agent to use real browsers on logged-in or bot-protected sites, or to turn a repeated scrape into a reusable skill"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "browser-act drives your Chrome or BrowserAct's stealth browsers from its CLI; browser-act-skill-forge explores a site once and writes a skill that repeats the extraction"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Pages read, clicked through and screenshotted on demand, and tested skill packages (SKILL.md plus Python scripts) for sites you scrape often"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The browser-act CLI (Python 3.12+, uv). Your own Chrome needs no account; stealth browsers, CAPTCHA solving and browser-act-skill-forge need a free BrowserAct account"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "Before creating or deleting browsers, logging in, submitting forms or uploading files"
            }
          ],
          "text": "BrowserAct is a browser-automation service for AI agents, and these skills use its local CLI rather than its cloud product. browser-act lets the agent open pages in your own Chrome, reusing your logins, or in BrowserAct's stealth browsers, which each present their own device fingerprint so sites see separate, ordinary visitors rather than a bot. The agent reads a numbered list of page elements and clicks or types by number, takes screenshots, captures network responses, and can send you a live link to take over when it gets stuck. browser-act-skill-forge builds on browser-act, which it loads first. You describe the data you want from a site and confirm a plan; it works out how the site loads that data, preferring the site's own data APIs over scraping its HTML, then generates a skill of SKILL.md plus Python scripts, has a subagent test it, fixes what fails and installs it. Later runs call that skill without exploring again, which suits bulk jobs such as hundreds of listings. Beyond the free account, only managed proxies and more than five stealth browsers cost money.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/browser-act/skills/tree/11c057b03f92101642cadc9f840564574120d184"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "browser-act/skills/browser-act",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "browser-act/skills/browser-act-skill-forge",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Loads the browser-act skill in its first step and explores every site through it, so it needs that skill and its CLI installed."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills add https://github.com/browser-act/skills --skill browser-act --skill browser-act-skill-forge"
          }
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills@spec-workflow",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Warp spec workflow",
        "description": "Spec-first feature work: a product spec, a tech spec when warranted, then code built from them and checked against them, with the specs kept current in the same PR.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A feature is big, risky or ambiguous enough that an agent will build it better from written specs"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Decides whether specs are worth it, writes a product spec and, when warranted, a tech spec, builds the feature from them, and finally checks code and specs agree"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "specs/<ticket>/PRODUCT.md on what users see and, when warranted, TECH.md on how it's built, in the same PR as the code when practical, updated to match what ships"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Linear MCP server, for spec-driven-implementation, which files a Linear issue when there's none"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike Matt Pocock's to-spec",
              "text": "That one publishes the spec to an issue tracker; here the specs are files checked into the repo and reviewed with the code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Five skills from Warp's shared repo that take a substantial feature from intent to checked code. spec-driven-implementation is the entry point: it decides whether the feature needs specs at all (small bug fixes, simple refactors and narrow UI tweaks usually don't), makes sure a Linear issue exists to name the specs folder after, and hands its three main steps to the next three skills. write-product-spec writes PRODUCT.md: the feature's behavior from its user's side, as numbered, testable rules with no implementation details, after asking for a Figma mock when there's UI. write-tech-spec reads that spec and the code, then writes TECH.md: the current system, the proposed changes and trade-offs, and a test or check for each important rule. implement-specs builds from the approved specs, updating them as soon as the implementation changes, in the same PR when practical, so they describe what ships. validate-changes-match-specs, which you run yourself near the end or in review, lists each material mismatch between the branch's specs and its code, checks what security specs allow and prevent, and asks whether to fix the code or the spec. They're written for Warp's own features; all but the entry point and implement-specs also work alone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/spec-driven-implementation",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Loads write-product-spec, write-tech-spec and implement-specs for its three main steps; on its own it only decides whether specs are needed and files the Linear issue."
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-product-spec",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-tech-spec",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/implement-specs",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Starts only once PRODUCT.md, and TECH.md when warranted, exist and are approved: the specs that write-product-spec and write-tech-spec write."
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/validate-changes-match-specs",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Warp, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills@latest add warpdotdev/common-skills --skill spec-driven-implementation --skill write-product-spec --skill write-tech-spec --skill implement-specs --skill validate-changes-match-specs --agent warp --global"
          },
          {
            "label": "Warp, with the repo's 24 other skills",
            "command": "npx skills@latest add warpdotdev/common-skills --skill '*' --agent warp --global"
          }
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Addy's Agent Skills",
        "description": "Twenty-five engineering skills for every phase of building software: define, plan, build, verify, review and ship.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want your agent to follow a senior engineer's process at every phase, from a vague idea to a production launch"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives each phase its own skills: interview, spec, plan, build in tested slices, debug, review, simplify, secure, commit, document and ship"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A spec you approve, a plan in tasks/plan.md and tasks/todo.md, small tested commits, a five-axis code review and a launch checklist with a rollback plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "By itself: each skill loads when the work matches it, and using-agent-skills maps a task to its skill when the choice isn't obvious"
            },
            {
              "label": "Commands",
              "text": "In Claude Code, slash commands such as /spec, /plan, /build, /test, /review and /ship start the matching skills; /build auto works through a whole plan after one approval"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, Antigravity, Command Code and any agent the skills CLI supports"
            },
            {
              "label": "Install whole",
              "text": "A single-skill install copies only that skill's folder, so its links to the repo's shared checklists break, though the skill still works"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Addy Osmani's pack maps software work onto six phases. using-agent-skills, the entry point, picks the skill for a task and sets shared rules: state assumptions, push back, stay in scope, verify before claiming done. Define: interview-me finds out what you actually want, idea-refine turns a rough idea into a one-pager, spec-driven-development writes the spec and waits for your approval, and constraint-driven-development records the quality bar in CONSTRAINTS.md. Plan: planning-and-task-breakdown turns the spec into ordered tasks. Build: context-engineering loads the right context and source-driven-development checks framework code against official docs; incremental-implementation and test-driven-development then deliver each task as small tested commits, helped by frontend-ui-engineering and api-and-interface-design, with observability-and-instrumentation adding telemetry alongside and doubt-driven-development sending non-trivial decisions to an adversarial reviewer. Verify: browser-testing-with-devtools and debugging-and-error-recovery. Review: code-review-and-quality, code-simplification, security-and-hardening and performance-optimization. Ship: git-workflow-and-versioning, ci-cd-and-automation, documentation-and-adrs, deprecation-and-migration and shipping-and-launch. Every skill but the router has a table of excuses to reject and a verification checklist, and works alone. The repo also ships reviewer personas and shared checklists that only a whole-repo install brings.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/using-agent-skills",
