      "agentReview": {
        "name": "BMad Method",
        "description": "BMad's agile AI-driven development method: brainstorm and research an idea, plan it with product, UX and architecture skills and their personas, then build, review and test each change.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the agent to follow one delivery process, from a vague idea to reviewed, committed code, with as much planning as the change needs"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives each phase its own skills and named personas: explore and research, product and UX documents, architecture and tickets, then build, review and test, ending with a retrospective per epic"
            },
            {
              "label": "Start with",
              "text": "The bmad skill's setup, which creates the _bmad/ folder the other skills run their scripts from; ask bmad what to run next"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Markdown files under one output folder, such as briefs, PRDs, UX specs, an architecture spine, tickets, plans and research reports, plus local commits"
            },
            {
              "label": "For your own skills",
              "text": "Smithy (bmad-toolsmith) builds, reviews and slims skills, agents and modules in BMad's format; bmad-eval tests them against the bare model, a rubric or trigger queries"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv for BMad's scripts, Node.js for npx, and subagents for builds and reviews"
            },
            {
              "label": "Status",
              "text": "The default branch is v7, a preview alpha that changes often; the stable v6.12 installs separately, with npx bmad-method install"
            }
          ],
          "text": "BMad Method carries every decision forward as files. The bmad skill's setup creates the _bmad/ folder whose scripts the other skills run, and bmad later answers what to run next. Work is grouped into initiatives, each a ticket tree: the initiative, its epics and their stories. A small change goes straight to bmad-build, which plans, implements, has a subagent review the diff and commits locally; bmad-build-auto does the same unattended. Bigger work gets planning first. bmad-brainstorming, bmad-forge-idea, bmad-deep-recon and bmad-prfaq explore and test ideas; John (bmad-agent-pm) coaches PRDs and other product documents; bmad-ux writes DESIGN.md and EXPERIENCE.md; bmad-architecture records the decisions separately built parts must share. Sofia (bmad-agent-po) turns those documents into the initiative's requirements, epics and stories, in the repo or on a tracker. Mary, Sally, Winston and Amelia are personas that stay on and run these skills from a menu. After a build, bmad-code-review, bmad-walkthrough and bmad-qa-generate-e2e-tests check it; bmad-retrospective then judges each finished epic by its own criteria. bmad-party-mode, bmad-advanced-elicitation and bmad-review bring other views to a draft or a change, bmad-project-context keeps AGENTS.md's agent rules verified, and bmad-customize changes a skill through override files. Smithy (bmad-toolsmith) builds your own skills, and bmad-eval measures them.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/a867e1604b365d48ea03cbd109da1066ba0c84bc"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Sets up and advises on the other BMad skills: its setup and help work from the modules and skills installed beside it, so alone it has nothing to set up, answer from or recommend."
