    {
      "id": "github/awesome-copilot@breakdown",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Epic breakdown workflow",
        "description": "Takes a product epic from idea to plan in six Markdown documents: epic PRD, architecture, feature PRD, implementation plan, GitHub project plan and test plan.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a product idea taken through requirements, architecture and planning documents before anyone writes code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs six prompts in order, each written for a role: epic PRD, epic architecture, feature PRD, implementation plan, GitHub project plan, test plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Markdown files under /docs/ways-of-work/plan/{epic-name}/: epic.md and arch.md, then a folder per feature for its PRD, plans and test documents"
            },
            {
              "label": "Gaps",
              "text": "The last two steps look for the feature PRD at {feature-name}.md rather than the prd.md the PRD step writes, and for a technical-breakdown.md no step writes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Assumes",
              "text": "TypeScript, Next.js, Turborepo, tRPC, Stack Auth, shadcn/ui and Docker, in the architecture and implementation steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Output",
              "text": "Documents only: GitHub issues are drafted in the project and test plans, not filed"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Six prompts that hand documents down a chain, each played by a role from product manager to QA engineer, all saved under /docs/ways-of-work/plan/{epic-name}/. breakdown-epic-pm turns an epic idea into epic.md: problem, personas, user journeys, requirements, KPIs, what's out of scope and business value, asking questions when the idea is thin. breakdown-epic-arch writes arch.md beside it: a layered Mermaid system diagram, the features and technical enablers, the stack, technical value and a T-shirt size. breakdown-feature-prd takes one feature or enabler of the epic and writes its PRD, with user stories and acceptance criteria, in the feature's own folder. breakdown-feature-implementation turns that PRD into implementation-plan.md: architecture and entity-relationship diagrams, API design and a frontend component tree. breakdown-plan then drafts the GitHub work, an Epic > Feature > Story/Enabler > Task hierarchy with issue templates, priorities, estimates and dependencies, in project-plan.md and issues-checklist.md, and breakdown-test adds a test strategy, test issue checklist and QA plan built on ISTQB test techniques and the ISO 25010 quality model. Those last two expect files the earlier steps don't write at those paths, so point them at yours. The first four also work alone, from an idea or a PRD you supply.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/breakdown-epic-pm",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/breakdown-epic-arch",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/breakdown-feature-prd",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/breakdown-feature-implementation",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/breakdown-plan",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Reads the implementation-plan.md that breakdown-feature-implementation writes, with the feature PRD and a technical-breakdown.md, from /docs/ways-of-work/plan/. It looks for the PRD at {epic-name}/{feature-name}.md, not the {feature-name}/prd.md breakdown-feature-prd writes, and no step writes technical-breakdown.md."
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/breakdown-test",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Reads the project-plan.md that breakdown-plan writes, plus the same feature PRD, technical breakdown and implementation plan, with the same two path gaps: the PRD at {feature-name}.md and a technical-breakdown.md no step writes."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills add https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot --skill breakdown-epic-pm --skill breakdown-epic-arch --skill breakdown-feature-prd --skill breakdown-feature-implementation --skill breakdown-plan --skill breakdown-test"
          },
          {
            "label": "GitHub CLI 2.90.0 or later, one skill per command, as the repo's skills list gives them",
            "command": "gh skills install github/awesome-copilot breakdown-epic-pm && gh skills install github/awesome-copilot breakdown-epic-arch && gh skills install github/awesome-copilot breakdown-feature-prd && gh skills install github/awesome-copilot breakdown-feature-implementation && gh skills install github/awesome-copilot breakdown-plan && gh skills install github/awesome-copilot breakdown-test"
          },
          {
            "label": "GitHub Copilot CLI plugin: only the first four steps, with 4 more of the repo's planning skills and 7 agents",
            "command": "copilot plugin install project-planning@awesome-copilot"
          }
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "github/awesome-copilot@structured-autonomy",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Structured Autonomy",
        "description": "Plans a feature as the testable commits of one pull request, writes out every step's complete code in advance, then implements it one step at a time, stopping for you to test and commit.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a feature researched and planned, and every step's code written out, before an agent applies any of it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Plans the feature as the commits of one pull request, writes each step's complete code into a document, then applies it one step at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "plans/{feature-name}/plan.md, plans/{feature-name}/implementation.md and the code, with a stop after each step for you to test and commit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Subagents for the generate step's research; the plan step does its own research when it can't start one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Written for",
              "text": "GitHub Copilot: the prompts name its tools, as in #tool:runSubagent, and its CLI plugin installs the three as slash commands"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Structured Autonomy splits a feature into three passes, one skill each. structured-autonomy-plan writes no code: it researches the codebase, docs and libraries, in a subagent when it can, and breaks the request into the commits of one pull request on its own branch, a single commit for a simple feature or several testable ones for a complex one. It saves plans/{feature-name}/plan.md with the branch, the goal and each step's files, change and test, marks open questions [NEEDS CLARIFICATION], asks them and waits for your feedback. structured-autonomy-generate reads that plan, has a subagent research the project's stack, conventions, patterns and library docs once, and writes plans/{feature-name}/implementation.md: each step as checkboxes with complete code to paste into named files, a verification checklist and a STOP & COMMIT point, with no decisions left open. structured-autonomy-implement then follows that file exactly: it picks up the next unchecked step, writes only the code the plan specifies, checks items off, runs the plan's build or test commands and stops at the STOP marker, so you can test and commit before calling it for the next step. The plugin that ships the three sums them up as \"premium planning, thrifty implementation\".",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/structured-autonomy-plan",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/structured-autonomy-generate",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Needs the plans/{feature-name}/plan.md that structured-autonomy-plan saves, and takes the feature name, branch, steps and each step's files from it."
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/structured-autonomy-implement",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Carries out the implementation.md that structured-autonomy-generate writes, from its next unchecked step to its next STOP & COMMIT point; without a plan passed in, it refuses to start."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills add https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot --skill structured-autonomy-plan --skill structured-autonomy-generate --skill structured-autonomy-implement"
          },
          {
            "label": "GitHub Copilot CLI plugin, run as /structured-autonomy:structured-autonomy-plan and so on; an older CLI first needs copilot plugin marketplace add github/awesome-copilot",
            "command": "copilot plugin install structured-autonomy@awesome-copilot"
          }
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "github/awesome-copilot@memory",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Memory instructions",
        "description": "Saves lessons as per-domain memory instruction files, then merges the mature ones into that domain's standing instructions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want lessons from your sessions kept as instructions the agent follows next time, and tidied up as they pile up"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "remember writes each lesson into a per-domain memory file; memory-merger later folds the ones you approve into that domain's instruction file"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "<domain>-memory.instructions.md files and, after a merge, a new or updated <domain>.instructions.md"
            },
            {
              "label": "Built for",
              "text": "GitHub Copilot in VS Code: global files in its user prompts folder, the default, or workspace files in .github/instructions/"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Two commands that share one file layout. /remember takes a lesson, such as a mistake the agent keeps repeating, and writes it as a short, generalized rule into <domain>-memory.instructions.md for a domain like clojure or git-workflow, creating the file if needed. It picks the domain unless you name one, asks you when unsure, and reads the existing memory and instruction files first so it doesn't repeat them; a lesson that fits no domain goes into memory.instructions.md. applyTo patterns in each file's front matter say which files its rules apply to. Lessons accumulate there. When a domain's memories have matured, /memory-merger >domain lists each one with where it would go in <domain>.instructions.md and waits for your go, drafts the merge until no detail is lost and nothing is repeated, then writes the instruction file, combining both files' applyTo patterns, and removes the merged lessons from the memory file. It takes one domain per run. Both work in the global scope by default, or in the workspace.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/remember",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/memory-merger",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Needs a <domain>-memory.instructions.md file, which remember writes, and merges its lessons into <domain>.instructions.md."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills add https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot --skill remember --skill memory-merger"
          },
          {
            "label": "GitHub CLI 2.90 or later, one skill per command, as the repo's skills list gives it",
            "command": "gh skills install github/awesome-copilot remember && gh skills install github/awesome-copilot memory-merger"
          }
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills",
      "skew": 45482,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Gamedev Agent Skills",
        "description": "Cross-engine game-development skills for concepts, genres and process, with a router that detects your engine and task and loads only the skills that fit before the agent writes code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a game with an agent, in any engine, and want it to pull in the right game-dev know-how for each request"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "The router detects your engine from project files, sorts the request into concepts, a genre and process steps, and reads only those skills, engine basics first"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Game code built to cross-engine playbooks, such as crash-safe saves, rebindable input, smooth cameras and juice, plus a genre's core loop, must-have systems and tuning knobs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "By itself: you describe the task and never name a skill"
            },
            {
              "label": "Engines",
              "text": "Godot, Unity, Unreal, Ebitengine, Phaser, PixiJS, three.js, Bevy, pygame, LÖVE, Defold and Roblox, or your own engine, mapped from the project's code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not listed here",
              "text": "The engine skills the router loads, plus steam-publish and itch-publish; both installs bring them, since each installs all 77 skills and the router"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Gemini CLI, GitHub Copilot and other agents that read SKILL.md files"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The router is the entry point. It fingerprints the project to pick one engine (a project.godot means Godot), keeps an existing project's pinned version, then sorts the request into three additive groups and reads only the chosen skills, saying what it loaded. Fifteen discipline skills each own one portable concept, such as save-systems, input-systems, camera-systems, game-feel or performance-optimization, and leave the engine's API to its engine skill. Nine genre playbooks, from platformer to visual-novel, set out a game's core loop, must-have systems and tuning knobs, and name the disciplines to build each part from instead of re-teaching them. Two workflows cover process: prototype-fast tests whether a mechanic is fun, and game-jam scopes a jam entry to the clock and plans it through to submission. Asking for a double jump in a Godot game, say, loads the Godot movement skill and platformer, nothing else. For a new project with no engine preference the router picks Godot; a custom engine is kept, and the concepts are mapped onto its own APIs. The engine skills it routes to, and steam-publish and itch-publish, aren't listed on SkillHub, but both installs bring them. Every skill here but the router also works alone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/router",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Picks and reads the bundle's other skills, plus the repo's engine skills, steam-publish and itch-publish, which SkillHub doesn't list; alone, it has nothing to load."
          },
          {
            "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/create-game-assets",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/game-ai",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
