    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents@conductor",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Conductor",
        "description": "Keeps project context in a conductor/ folder, turns each feature or fix into a spec and phased plan, then builds it test-first with your approval after each phase.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an agent to build features from written project context and an agreed plan, across many sessions, approving each phase before it moves on"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Keeps project context files, turns each feature, bug, chore or refactor into a track, a unit of work with a spec and phased plan, then builds it test-first"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A conductor/ folder of product, tech-stack and workflow files and a tracks.md registry; per track, a spec.md, a plan.md marked with task status and commit SHAs, and metadata"
            },
            {
              "label": "Pauses",
              "text": "At the end of each phase, until you approve its verification checklist"
            },
            {
              "label": "Commands",
              "text": "setup, new-track and revert steps call /conductor commands that come with the plugin, not the skills; the skills alone include templates and manual steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Based on",
              "text": "Google's Conductor extension for Gemini CLI, adapted for Claude Code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Three skills behind Conductor, a wshobson/agents plugin, one per stage of its cycle: context, then spec and plan, then implementation. context-driven-development keeps a conductor/ folder of Markdown files the agent reads before each piece of work: product.md for what's built and why, product-guidelines.md for voice and terminology, tech-stack.md, workflow.md for rules such as TDD, commit conventions and coverage targets, and tracks.md, the registry of tracks. It gives starter templates, when to update each file and a checklist to run before starting a track. track-management turns a feature, bug, chore or refactor into a track: a folder with spec.md (requirements, testable acceptance criteria, scope, dependencies, risks), plan.md (phases of tasks, each ending in verification) and metadata.json. workflow-patterns then works through the plan one task at a time: mark it in progress, write failing tests, make them pass, refactor, reach 80% coverage, commit, attach a git note and record the SHA in plan.md. At the end of each phase it runs the full test suite, lists manual checks and waits for your approval before a checkpoint commit. Setting up, creating tracks and reverting call /conductor commands, which come with the plugin, not with the skills.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/conductor/skills/context-driven-development",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/conductor/skills/track-management",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/conductor/skills/workflow-patterns",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Works task by task through a track's plan.md, in the format track-management defines, updating its status markers and recording each commit's SHA there."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI; the skills only, without the plugin's /conductor: commands",
            "command": "npx skills add wshobson/agents --skill context-driven-development --skill track-management --skill workflow-patterns"
          },
          {
            "label": "Claude Code plugin, after /plugin marketplace add wshobson/agents; the skills plus the /conductor: commands",
            "command": "/plugin install conductor@claude-code-workflows"
          },
          {
            "label": "Codex plugin; the skills only, without the /conductor: commands",
            "command": "codex plugin marketplace add wshobson/agents && codex plugin add conductor@claude-code-workflows"
          }
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins@productivity",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Productivity",
        "description": "A workplace productivity system that keeps a TASKS.md task list, a memory of your people, projects and shorthand, and a local dashboard in sync.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the agent to track your tasks and learn your workplace's people, projects and shorthand across sessions"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "start sets up tasks, memory and a dashboard; task-management and memory-management keep them as you work; update syncs and triages"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "TASKS.md, a CLAUDE.md working memory with a memory/ folder, and dashboard.html, a local board of your tasks and memory, all in your working folder"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "start and update as /productivity:start and /productivity:update; task-management and memory-management on their own when relevant"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Chat, email, calendar, documents and project trackers through connected MCP servers, and GitHub Issues through gh, for syncing and deep scans"
            },
            {
              "label": "Built for",
              "text": "Claude Cowork, installed from claude.com/plugins; also works in Claude Code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The productivity plugin of Anthropic's knowledge-work plugins, kept in files in your working folder. start checks the folder for TASKS.md, CLAUDE.md, memory/ and dashboard.html and creates what's missing. On a first run it asks where you keep your todos, goes through them asking what each nickname, acronym and codename means, can scan your chat, email, documents and calendar for more, then writes CLAUDE.md and the memory/ files. From then on, task-management keeps TASKS.md as you add, finish and ask about tasks, and memory-management decodes each request from CLAUDE.md, then the glossary and memory files, then by asking you, saving what you tell it to remember. dashboard.html, copied from the plugin, is a board on the same TASKS.md that you open yourself. update pulls tasks assigned to you from a project tracker or GitHub Issues and offers to add them, flags overdue tasks, tasks Active for 30 days or more and tasks with no person or project for triage, and asks about people and projects memory lacks. With --comprehensive it also scans chat, email, documents and calendar for todos you haven't captured and new people or projects. update never adds tasks or memories without your confirmation.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/tree/ab4cb1cd383b4eb87383be8cf4e70cb5b2a2348d"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/productivity/skills/start",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Creates TASKS.md from task-management's template, writes the memory files memory-management reads, and copies the plugin's dashboard.html."
          },
          {
            "id": "anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/productivity/skills/task-management",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/productivity/skills/memory-management",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/productivity/skills/update",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Works on the TASKS.md and memory/ the other skills keep; without them it suggests running start first."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Claude Code plugin; also adds the dashboard and MCP connections to Slack, Notion and project trackers such as Asana, Linear and Jira",
            "command": "claude plugin marketplace add anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins && claude plugin install productivity@knowledge-work-plugins"
          }
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ljagiello/ctf-skills",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "ctf-skills",
        "description": "A capture-the-flag solving suite: a dispatcher that triages a challenge and routes to deep category skills for web, pwn, crypto, reverse, forensics, OSINT, malware, AI/ML and misc, plus a writeup generator.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You are solving a CTF challenge and want category-specific exploitation playbooks"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Triages the challenge, then loads the matching web, pwn, crypto, reverse, forensics, OSINT, malware, AI/ML or misc skill"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Recon, the right category technique reference, and a standardized writeup when you finish"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "CTF tools installed; a filesystem agent with bash and Python"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A capture-the-flag solving suite whose skills share one repo and install together. solve-challenge is the user-invocable entry point: it runs first-pass recon (file, strings, nc, HTTP probes), detects the CTF platform (CTFd API token flow, or Playwright MCP for JS-rendered sites), decides the dominant category, then loads the matching ctf-* skill. Each category skill (web, pwn, crypto, reverse, forensics, osint, malware, ai-ml, misc) is a deep, self-contained reference of techniques for that category and also works on its own. ctf-writeup runs after a solve to turn the session into a standardized submission writeup. The install pulls the whole repo, which also ships ctf-similar-search, a Tavily-API writeup-search helper that ctf-web's notes point to when stuck; SkillHub does not list it as a separate skill. The repo also ships scripts/install_ctf_tools.sh to install the many CLI tools these skills call.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/ljagiello/ctf-skills/tree/2ad85d368a86c2bc1ce13143a3768f4e55fd1c61"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "ljagiello/ctf-skills/solve-challenge",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "The suite's user-invocable entry point; runs recon then loads the ctf-* category skills, which it expects installed alongside it."
          },
          {
            "id": "ljagiello/ctf-skills/ctf-web",
            "standalone": true,
            "listedOutsideBundle": false
          },
          {
            "id": "ljagiello/ctf-skills/ctf-pwn",
