    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/writing-plans",
      "name": "writing-plans",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/writing-plans",
        "installs": 301997,
        "weeklyInstalls": [6767, 6813, 6653, 5861, 7485, 6988, 6521, 6981],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes an implementation plan from a spec before touching code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have a spec or approved design for a multi-step change, before any code is written"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Breaks the work into small test-first tasks, each naming its files, function signatures, tests and the commands that prove it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A plan file in the repo, checked against the spec, plus a recommendation for how to run it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Written for",
              "text": "Whoever implements it, a person or a subagent, assumed never to have seen the codebase or the spec"
            },
            {
              "label": "Next",
              "text": "subagent-driven-development, a fresh subagent per task, or executing-plans, the agent working through it alone"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The plan holds the decisions an implementer couldn't make alone: which files to create or change, the names and types each task shares with others, the exact values from the spec, and the tests that prove each task. Each task is one test cycle: write the failing test, run it and see it fail, write the code, run it and see it pass, commit, with the expected output of every command. The code itself is left to the implementer, except for an algorithm the tests don't pin down. The plan also lists the project-wide constraints and the five likeliest ways real use could break the software that no test covers yet, each then given a test. The agent checks the plan against the spec for gaps, mismatched names and bloat, fixes what it finds, and asks you to review it and choose how it runs: a fresh subagent and review per task, or the agent working through it inline with one review at the end.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/writing-plans"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning",
          "agent-workflows"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/improve-codebase-architecture",
      "name": "improve-codebase-architecture",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/improve-codebase-architecture",
        "installs": 1261632,
        "weeklyInstalls": [40761, 37241, 42271, 53008, 42480, 38894, 38379, 33691],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-13"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a496f5513d4a4cf889bc48e189e579f4b0bdf6aa",
        "updated": "2026-10-30",
        "skew": 5
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scans a codebase for deepening opportunities, renders an HTML report, then grills through the one you pick.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A codebase has grown hard to change, test or find your way around, and you want to know where to restructure first"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds places where thin, tangled modules could become one deep module (lots of behavior behind a small interface), then designs the one you pick with you"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An HTML report of rated candidates with before/after diagrams, then a design worked out question by question"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Three skills by the same author: codebase-design for the design terms, grilling for the questioning, domain-modeling for the glossary and decision records"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you invoke it; the agent won't start it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A deep module hides a lot of behavior behind a small interface; a shallow one has an interface nearly as complex as its code. This skill hunts for chances to deepen, for code that's easier to test and for agents to navigate. Unless you name an area, it starts where git history shows the most change. It reads the project's glossary and its architecture decision records (ADRs), then has a subagent explore and note friction: one idea spread over many small files, functions pulled out only so they can be tested, modules leaking into each other, untested code. For each suspect it asks whether deleting it would concentrate complexity or just move it. The candidates go into an HTML page in your temp folder, which it opens: per candidate, the files, the problem, the fix, the gains for tests, a before/after diagram and how strongly it recommends it, plus a top pick. It proposes no interfaces until you choose. Then it questions you through the design round by round, adds new terms to GLOSSARY.md, and offers to record an ADR when you reject a candidate for a reason future reviews should know.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/improve-codebase-architecture"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "refactoring"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/tdd",
      "name": "tdd",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/tdd",
        "installs": 1234718,
        "weeklyInstalls": [41124, 36998, 42084, 54986, 44593, 39153, 35932, 32876],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides test-first feature and bug work with a red-green-refactor loop.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a feature or bug fix built test-first, or ask for red-green-refactor or integration tests"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Has you agree which public interfaces get tested, then loops: one failing test, then just enough code to pass it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code built in small slices, with tests that check behavior and survive refactoring"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike test-driven-development",
              "text": "The TDD skill in obra's Superpowers set refactors inside the loop; this one leaves refactoring for a later code review"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "By itself whenever you ask for test-first work"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Less a procedure than the rulebook for each red-green cycle: write a failing test (red), then make it pass (green). Before any test, the agent proposes which public interfaces to test at, the places where behavior can be observed without reaching inside, each with a note on what it catches and misses. You confirm them, and no test goes anywhere else. Then it works in vertical slices: one test, then the least code that passes it, then the next, each shaped by what the last one taught. Tests check what the code does through its interface, not how: no mocks of your own modules (only of outside systems such as payment APIs or the clock), no private methods, no checking the database behind the interface's back. It also bans tests that can't fail because they compute the expected value the same way the code does, and writing all the tests before any code. Test names follow the project's glossary if there is one.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/tdd"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "tdd",
          "testing"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/engineering/implement",
      "name": "implement",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/implement",
        "installs": 860346,
        "weeklyInstalls": [38981, 34652, 35376, 51104, 41033, 35700, 32822, 30350],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-17"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Implements a piece of work from a spec or tickets.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A spec or ticket is ready and you want the agent to build it now, in this session"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds it test-first, type-checks as it goes, runs the full test suite, then reviews the result"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Commits on your current branch, already through a code review"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Two skills by the same author: tdd, its rules for test-first work, and code-review, which checks the diff against the spec and the repo's standards"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you invoke it; the agent won't start it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short skill that strings two others together. Point it at a spec, a set of tickets or a ticket number; it fetches a ticket from your issue tracker and states its title before starting, and asks if the reference is unclear. It builds with the tdd skill where it can, testing at interfaces agreed with you, runs type checks and single test files as it goes and the full suite once at the end. Then it runs the code-review skill over the work and commits to the current branch. One agent does it all, unlike implement-spec, which hands each ticket of a spec to its own subagent.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/engineering/implement"
        },
