      "skillMd": {
        "version": "7ec868225c6caa040558c321ee0fefa0e73f83a8",
        "updated": "2026-10-23",
        "skew": 11499
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs pages that look made, not generated.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an agent-built landing page, app screen or component that doesn't look AI-generated"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks about audience, purpose and tone, then picks a named page layout and theme that differ from its previous builds"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The page code, a tokens.css file of its colours, fonts and spacing, and a .hallmark/log.json of past choices"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also does",
              "text": "audit (ranks a page's telltale AI-design habits, no edits), redesign (new structure, same content) and study (reads a design's structure and type from a screenshot or URL)"
            },
            {
              "label": "Self-check",
              "text": "A 58-point checklist before handing back: no invented stats or testimonials, no italic headings, no fake browser frames, clean layout down to 320px wide"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Hallmark is a strict design rulebook for agents building web pages. Its signature is structural variety: two pages for two briefs shouldn't share the same hero, three feature cards, call to action and footer. For a new page, the agent first reads your project's fonts, colours, spacing and framework and keeps them unless you say otherwise, then always asks about audience, purpose and tone (say \"go ahead\" and it guesses and tells you what it picked). It chooses a page shape from a catalog of 21, such as Bento Grid or Long Document, plus a nav, a footer and a theme, avoiding what it used last in this project, and shows a short preview before writing code. Every colour and font must come from a named token, every button gets eight states such as hover, loading and error, and motion respects reduced-motion settings. A single component gets its file plus a preview page showing all eight states. In an existing codebase it edits in place and asks before deleting anything. On request it locks the result into a design.md that later pages follow. study never copies pixels and won't study template-marketplace pages such as ThemeForest's.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/nutlope/hallmark/tree/13ac0ec7e148655948100b6396439e481361d690/skills/hallmark"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "anti-slop"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/web-quality-skills/skills/accessibility",
      "name": "accessibility",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/web-quality-skills/accessibility",
        "installs": 69884,
        "weeklyInstalls": [1769, 2156, 1902, 2016, 1773, 1923, 1925, 2367],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "afa8da942115f2961fdbfa80807ea0b232ff6c00",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits and fixes accessibility against WCAG 2.2.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a web page or app checked and fixed against WCAG 2.2, the web accessibility standard"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs a Lighthouse audit, inspects the accessibility tree, tries the flow by keyboard, edits the code to fix what fails, then audits again"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Fixed code, guided by rules and before-and-after HTML, CSS and JavaScript for each WCAG area"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Chrome DevTools MCP if available, otherwise the Lighthouse CLI or axe"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike web-design-guidelines",
              "text": "That one, Vercel's skill, reviews UI code against its design rules; this one tests the rendered page and fixes what fails"
            }
          ],
          "text": "When a rendered page is available, it starts from what actually fails: the failing elements in the Lighthouse report point to the component to fix, a snapshot of the accessibility tree shows names, roles, headings and landmarks, and the agent walks the flow with the keyboard. It warns that automated tools catch only some problems, so a score of 100 doesn't mean the page conforms. The rest is a reference organized by WCAG's four principles (perceivable, operable, understandable, robust), with before-and-after code: alt text, contrast ratios, keyboard access, visible focus, skip links, form labels, error messages, native elements over ARIA, live announcements. It flags what's new in WCAG 2.2: tap targets of at least 24 pixels, focus never hidden behind sticky headers, alternatives to dragging, not asking for the same information twice, logins that allow paste or passkeys. It ends with a manual checklist (screen readers, 200% zoom, reduced motion) and common issues ranked by impact.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/web-quality-skills/tree/afa8da942115f2961fdbfa80807ea0b232ff6c00/skills/accessibility"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "accessibility"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
