      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds the spots in your UI that should animate but don't, rejects the rest, and proposes exact motion for each.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know what in your interface could animate, or want it to feel more alive"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sweeps the code for moments with no motion and puts each through four checks: how often it's seen, its purpose, a time budget, and whether motion helps"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "At most 5–7 suggestions with exact curves and durations, the candidates it rejected and why, and a short verdict; it edits no code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike improve-animations",
              "text": "That one audits and plans fixes for animations you already have; this one looks for places that have none"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Built on Emil Kowalski's view that sometimes the best animation is none, it's as much a filter as a finder and expects to reject most candidates. Anything used a hundred times a day, such as keyboard shortcuts and command palettes, never animates; delight is allowed only on rare moments such as onboarding, empty states and success screens. Each suggestion must name its purpose (feedback, showing where something came from, making a state change visible, bridging content that would otherwise pop in, explaining a feature, or delight) and fit under 300ms for UI. It hunts in known places: buttons with no press feedback, content that appears or vanishes instantly, popovers with no link to their trigger, lists that pop in all at once, drags that stop dead, and flat celebration moments. Suggestions reuse the project's existing tokens, animate only transform and opacity, and include reduced-motion handling. The list of rejected candidates is required: it shows what it chose not to animate, and which check ruled it out. It ends by pointing you to improve-animations to turn a suggestion into a plan.",
