              "text": "Only when you call it; the agent never starts it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A lookup table of design engineer Emil Kowalski's picks: base-ui for accessible unstyled components, cmdk for command menus, Sonner (his own) for toasts, input-otp for verification codes, motion for springs and exit animations, NumberFlow for animated numbers, recharts for charts and Liveline for live streaming ones, dnd kit for drag and drop, Virtuoso for long lists, zustand for state, clsx and cva for class names, and next-themes for dark mode, plus picks for control panels, 3D globes, OG images and syntax highlighting. It reasons from the task rather than the library you named, and gives one answer, not a menu. If the project already uses a competitor, it flags its pick but doesn't swap the dependency unless asked. It also catches common mismatches, such as hand-built toasts, div-based dropdowns with manual focus handling, or a 1,000-row list rendered directly. For a task outside the list, it says so before answering from general knowledge. The animate skill points requests for components here.",
