      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds several genuinely different versions of one UI piece behind a switcher, so you can flip through them live and keep the best.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You know which UI piece you need, such as a toast or pricing card, but not which direction it should take"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds three to five working variants, each differing on a named axis such as layout, density, personality or motion, on a separate prototype page or in one HTML file"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A live page with a picker you flip through from the keyboard, a table of each variant's trade-off, then the winner moved into your code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike Matt Pocock's prototype",
              "text": "That one also tests logic and keeps prototypes on a branch; this one only explores UI and deletes the prototype once you choose"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it; the agent never starts it on its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A design exploration, one UI piece per run; a request like \"the dashboard\" gets narrowed to its most important part. It first reads the project's stack, tokens and personality so every variant looks native to the product, then names each direction (Quiet, Editorial, Playful, never Option A/B/C) and the axis it explores; two directions that would differ only in color or copy count as one, so it swaps one for a real alternative. Each variant fully works, with real interactions and realistic content, and meets Emil Kowalski's motion bar. In a project with a dev server, variants live on an isolated route that production code never imports; without one, in a single HTML file you open directly. The picker's markup, styles and keys are copied verbatim from the skill's own spec and never adapt to the project; it shows one variant at a time, full size in realistic context, and switches instantly. The agent checks that every variant renders with a clean console, then stops for you to choose. keep moves the winner into your codebase following its conventions and deletes the prototype unless you ask to keep it; riff starts a new round around a variant you liked.",
