      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds animations for React Native and Expo apps that stay smooth on real devices, including gestures, sheets and haptics.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're adding motion, gestures, sheets, screen transitions, press feedback or haptics to an Expo app, or fixing motion that stutters on a phone"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works through animate's decision sequence adapted to phones, building with Reanimated, Gesture Handler, Expo Router's native screens and expo-haptics, and keeping motion off the JavaScript thread"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The animation code, plus a few lines on the choices and what to try on a real device"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike animate",
              "text": "That one is for the web; this one designs for no hover, keeps motion on the UI thread and adds haptics"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Reanimated 4, which requires React Native's New Architecture"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Emil Kowalski's animation bar adapted to phones, where there's no hover, gestures are the main input, and motion that touches the JavaScript thread stutters as soon as the app does anything else. The same gate comes first: anything used a hundred times a day, such as tab switches, gets no animation, and tabs never slide. Then it picks the cheapest tool, from Reanimated's CSS-style transitions to shared values driven by gestures, and uses native pieces, such as Expo Router stacks, form sheets, native tabs and menus, instead of rebuilding them in JavaScript. It installs packages with npx expo install so versions match the Expo SDK. Anything a finger moves uses a spring that keeps its velocity; everything else uses fixed curves, under 300ms. It forbids setState or calls back to the JavaScript thread on every frame, touching shared values during render, and animating layout properties or blur. Haptics fire on the same frame as the visual, once per action and never as the only feedback. Reduced motion and large text sizes are handled; recipes cover sheets, swipe-to-delete, collapsing headers and keyboard-synced UI; and feel is judged only in a release build on the slowest device you support.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833/skills/animate-expo"
