      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans a website's page hierarchy, navigation, URL structure and internal linking, delivered as a page tree, a Mermaid sitemap and a URL map.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're planning a new site, restructuring one, or asking what pages you need and how they connect"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks about your business, current site and content, then designs the hierarchy, navigation, URL patterns and internal links"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An ASCII page tree, a Mermaid sitemap, a URL map table, a navigation spec and an internal linking plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules it applies",
              "text": "Important pages within 3 clicks, 4-7 header items, readable lowercase hyphenated URLs, and no orphan pages"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike seo-audit",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, handles XML sitemaps and technical SEO; this one plans the structure people navigate"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Information architecture for marketing sites, blogs, stores, docs and small-business sites. After reading your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), it asks what the company does, who the site is for, its top goals, whether it's new or being restructured, which URLs must be kept for redirects, and how many pages exist. Each site type has a starting depth and URL pattern, from 1-2 levels for a small business to 3-4 for e-commerce and documentation. It goes as flat as clean navigation allows, keeps breadcrumbs matching the URL path, and flags common URL mistakes such as dates in blog URLs, IDs, over-nesting and changed URLs without 301 redirects. Content-heavy sites are organized around hub pages that link to their spoke pages and back. A reference covers choosing a platform, adding a section to an existing site and migrating.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/site-architecture"
        },
