        "weeklyInstalls": [3229, 3384, 4103, 4543, 4245, 4458, 4955, 4628],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8a3bde8083bd121971f61cba4bdbe5fd8c0eee7d",
        "updated": "2026-11-10",
        "skew": 107
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a marketing page or form for what's holding back conversions and recommends quick wins, bigger changes, test ideas and alternative copy.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A homepage, landing, pricing or feature page or a lead form isn't converting, or you want feedback on a URL"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks seven areas in order of impact: value proposition, headline, calls to action, visual hierarchy, trust signals, objections, friction"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Recommendations sorted into quick wins, high-impact changes and A/B test ideas, plus 2-3 alternatives for headlines and buttons"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike its flow siblings",
              "text": "signup, onboarding and popups, from the same repo, cover those flows; this one works on marketing pages and forms"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Conversion rate optimization (CRO) is the work of getting more visitors to take a page's main action: sign up, book a demo, buy. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), then pins down the page type, its one conversion goal and where visitors come from. The review starts with whether a visitor understands what this is and why to care within 5 seconds, then moves to headlines, button copy (\"Start Free Trial\" over \"Submit\"), what stands out to someone scanning, logos and testimonials near the calls to action, objections answered through FAQs and guarantees, and friction such as extra form fields or slow loads. Each page type has its own focus: message match and a single call to action for landing pages, a highlighted recommended plan for pricing pages. For form abandonment or experiment ideas, it first loads the matching reference: fields, validation and mobile forms, or tests by page type. Designing and measuring the tests goes to the repo's ab-testing skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/cro"
