        "installs": 79778,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2383, 2226, 2520, 2724, 2831, 3071, 3762, 3403],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8a3bde8083bd121971f61cba4bdbe5fd8c0eee7d",
        "updated": "2026-11-10",
        "skew": 128
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs in-app paywalls and upgrade prompts that convert free or trial users to paid at the right moment, without dark patterns.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Free or trial users won't upgrade, or you're designing an upgrade screen, feature gate or limit-reached prompt"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Times upgrade prompts for after users have seen real value, and builds each screen from seven parts, from headline to a clear \"not now\""
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Advice and text layouts for feature-lock, usage-limit and trial-expiry paywalls, with timing and frequency rules and tests to run"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike cro",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, handles public pricing pages; this one covers upgrade moments inside the product"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Hide the close button, guilt-trip, or use fake countdowns and \"limited\" offers"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Upgrade screens inside a product, for freemium, trials, tier upgrades and usage limits. Its principles: ask only after the user has had real value, show what the paid feature does rather than just naming it, make upgrading easy, and make saying no easy too, so trust survives for later. It covers four triggers (a paid feature clicked, a limit hit, a trial ending, and gentle reminders after some days of free use) and the parts of a paywall screen: headline, value demonstration, plan comparison, pricing, social proof, a specific button, and an escape hatch. Text layouts are given for a feature-lock, a usage-limit and a trial-expiration paywall. Timing rules say when not to show one (during onboarding, mid-task, again right after a dismissal), with per-session limits and cool-downs measured in days. It lists what to test and which metrics to track, and its references cover more experiments and consumer iOS and Android subscription apps. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/paywalls"
        },
