      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs popups, slide-ins and banners that capture emails or promote offers without annoying visitors, from trigger and targeting to copy.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an email capture, exit-intent, discount or announcement popup, or your current one annoys people"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks the popup type and trigger, such as scroll depth, exit intent or a click, then sets targeting, frequency caps and copy"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A popup spec with type, trigger, audience, frequency, headline, button and decline text, rules so several popups don't collide, and test ideas"
            },
            {
              "label": "Respects",
              "text": "Easy closing, remembered dismissals, GDPR consent, keyboard and screen-reader access, and Google's stance on intrusive mobile popups"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Conversion work on overlays that interrupt: modals, slide-ins, exit-intent popups, sticky bars and announcement banners. Timing comes first: it prefers 30 to 60 seconds over 5, scroll depth for articles, exit intent for a last chance, and click-triggered popups, which annoy nobody, for lead magnets. Each popup type gets its own practices, such as a single email field with a specific benefit for list capture, or an offer on exit that differs from the entry popup. Design rules cover hierarchy, sizes for desktop and mobile, a visible close button and a \"no thanks\" link; copy covers headline formulas, first-person buttons (\"Get My Discount\") and polite decline text instead of guilt-tripping. It shows a popup at most once per session, waits 7 to 30 days after a dismissal, excludes checkout flows and converted visitors, and gives typical conversion rates by type. It ends with common setups for e-commerce, B2B SaaS, content sites and lead generation, and a long list of experiments. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/popups"
        },
