      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/review-pr",
      "name": "review-pr",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/review-pr",
        "installs": 29200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [660, 613, 930, 499, 371, 369, 126, 94],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a pull request from a local annotated diff and writes severity-labelled comments to review.json for a CI workflow to post.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A CI job has checked out a pull request and you want the agent's review as a file to publish, not comments posted directly"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reviews the lines the PR changes for correctness, security, error handling, performance, comment quality and tests, and labels each finding by severity"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "review.json: an APPROVE or REJECT verdict, a review body with an overview, issue counts and a recommendation, and inline comments, with suggestion blocks for code changes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "A line-annotated pr_diff.txt and pr_description.txt from your workflow, and Python 3 to run its bundled validator"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Built for an automated review step. The workflow checks out the PR and supplies pr_diff.txt, with every line annotated by its old or new line number, plus pr_description.txt. The agent puts correctness, security, error handling and real performance issues first, and treats comment and test quality as equal priorities, judged by the repo's own conventions. Every comment starts with a label: CRITICAL, IMPORTANT, SUGGESTION, or NIT, which is allowed only with a suggestion block. Line numbers are copied from the annotations; feedback on untouched code goes in the review body. Suggestion blocks must replace exactly the lines they target and keep brackets balanced. Before choosing a verdict, it lists every comment and test the diff adds and checks each against the repo's guidelines. It applies a repo's review-pr-local skill when one exists and, when spec_context.md is present, checks the code against the spec with a check-impl-against-spec skill if available. review.json must pass the bundled validator before it's done.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/review-pr"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:github",
          "code-review",
          "ci"
        ],
        "note": "Expects a workflow to supply pr_diff.txt with line annotations; it writes review.json and never posts to GitHub itself.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/spec-driven-implementation",
      "name": "spec-driven-implementation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/spec-driven-implementation",
        "installs": 29000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [648, 595, 930, 492, 362, 358, 112, 83],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a spec-first workflow for large features: product spec, tech spec when needed, then code, with specs kept current in the same PR.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Starting a large, risky or ambiguous feature that an agent will build, such as one around 1k+ lines"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Decides whether specs are worth it, files a Linear issue if there's none, then has the agent write the product spec, the tech spec when warranted, and the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "PRODUCT.md and TECH.md under specs/<ticket>/, usually in the same PR as the code, kept current so they describe what ships"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Linear MCP server, and the write-product-spec, write-tech-spec and implement-specs skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Warp's process for features where written specs will improve an agent's work and the review. Specs are for substantial work: product or architectural ambiguity, around 1k+ lines, deep or cross-cutting changes, risky behavior changes. Small bug fixes, straightforward refactors and narrow UI tweaks usually skip them. Specs live in specs/<Linear ticket>/, and if no Linear issue exists the agent creates one first with the Linear MCP tools, asking you when the team or labels aren't obvious. write-product-spec writes PRODUCT.md, the user-facing behavior, and the agent asks for a Figma mock when there's UI. write-tech-spec writes TECH.md when the work spans subsystems or has trade-offs worth reviewing, possibly after an end-to-end prototype. implement-specs builds from them once they're approved. For large features it may offer PROJECT_LOG.md and DECISIONS.md to record explored paths and decisions. When the implementation drifts, the specs are updated with the code, in the same PR whenever practical, and the work is verified against them with unit and integration tests, and walkthrough recordings or screenshots for UI-heavy work.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/spec-driven-implementation"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:mcp",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "stack:linear",
          "planning",
          "requirements",
          "implementation",
          "issue-tracking"
        ],
        "note": "Needs the Linear MCP server: it creates a Linear issue before writing specs when none exists.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-product-spec",
      "name": "write-product-spec",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/write-product-spec",
        "installs": 29000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [648, 593, 944, 496, 375, 365, 121, 90],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a PRODUCT.md spec describing a feature's behavior from its user's side as numbered, testable rules, before implementation starts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a feature's behavior pinned down in writing before an agent builds it, as a product spec or PRD"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks you for missing context and for a Figma mock when there's UI, then writes the behavior as numbered rules that can each be tested"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "specs/<id>/PRODUCT.md: a short summary and an exhaustive Behavior section, with no implementation details and no test plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Next step",
              "text": "write-tech-spec, which plans the implementation and the tests from these rules"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A product spec here describes behavior only from the side of whoever uses the feature: the person in the app, code reading a data model, callers of an API, or a developer running a CLI. It names no internal types, modules or algorithms. The Behavior section carries the spec: the happy path, every visible state and transition, all inputs, empty, error, loading and cancelled states, edge cases such as offline, timeouts, races and focus loss, keyboard and accessibility, and what must never regress. Everything else stays thin: a summary of one to three sentences, and Problem, Goals, Figma or Open questions only when they add something. Testing is left out on purpose; it belongs in TECH.md, which write-tech-spec writes from these rules. The folder is named after a Linear ticket, a GitHub issue or a short feature name, and a new ticket is filed only if you ask. Length follows the feature: no spec for trivial fixes, about 30 to 60 lines for a small feature. It's written for Warp, and its worked example specifies Markdown tables in Warp's block list.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/write-product-spec"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "requirements",
          "planning"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-tech-spec",
      "name": "write-tech-spec",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/write-tech-spec",
        "installs": 28900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [646, 594, 950, 493, 371, 367, 109, 89],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Researches the codebase and writes a TECH.md implementation plan for a feature, with commit-pinned code links and tests mapped to the product spec.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A feature spans several modules, has real architectural trade-offs, or reviewers should see the plan before or with the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the product spec and the relevant code, then plans the changes and maps each important rule of the spec to a test or check"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "specs/<id>/TECH.md next to PRODUCT.md, with file references linked to GitHub at the commit it inspected"
            },
            {
              "label": "Comes after",
              "text": "write-product-spec, whose PRODUCT.md it prefers to have first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The step after a product spec. Before writing, the agent reads PRODUCT.md if there is one and the code involved, and records the commit SHA of each repository it inspected, so file references link to GitHub at that commit and line range. The spec has four parts. Context: what's being built, how that area works today and the key files. Proposed changes: modules, new types, APIs and state, data flow, ownership, and the trade-offs when there's more than one reasonable path. Testing and validation: each important rule from PRODUCT.md mapped to a test or manual check. Parallelization, only when the agent has a run_agents tool: which sub-agents to run, local or remote, in which worktrees and branches, and how their work lands, or why it isn't worth it. Diagrams, an end-to-end flow, risks and follow-ups appear only when they add something. When the implementation is still too uncertain, an end-to-end prototype comes first and the spec is written from what it showed. A single-file change gets no tech spec or under 40 lines; multi-module work runs about 80 to 150. TECH.md is updated in the same PR as the implementation changes. It's written for Warp's codebase.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/write-tech-spec"
        },
