    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/update-skill",
      "name": "update-skill",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/update-skill",
        "installs": 28700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [639, 580, 925, 489, 361, 356, 104, 79],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides writing or revising a SKILL.md: frontmatter, a description that says what and when, structure, and when to split content into references.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're creating a skill, or reworking an existing one's frontmatter, structure or guidance"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets the rules: a kebab-case name, and a third-person description that starts with a verb and says what the skill does and when"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A SKILL.md kept under about 200 lines, with detailed material moved to a references folder"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike skill-creator",
              "text": "That one runs evals to test and improve skills; this one gives authoring rules, and testing means trying the skill on similar tasks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Warp's house guide for skill files. Frontmatter needs a kebab-case name and a description that starts with an action verb, is written in the third person, says both what the skill does and when to use it, and includes the terms that should trigger it. The body usually has a title and summary, an optional overview, the steps or instructions, and optional best practices and example PRs. Past about 200 lines, detail moves into a references folder linked from SKILL.md. Its best-practices reference covers loading in stages (name and description always, the body when the skill triggers, references only when needed), keeping references one level deep, cutting what the agent already knows, naming the language of code blocks, numbered steps with checklists for ordered work, gerund names such as processing-pdfs, and anti-patterns like vague descriptions, too many options and dated advice. To revise a skill, it says to watch where the agent struggles, fix those sections, then try the skill on similar tasks. Checking frontmatter with the skills-ref library is optional. Its examples point to Warp skills, such as add-feature-flag, that aren't in this repo.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/update-skill"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "skill-authoring"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/diagnose-ci-failures",
      "name": "diagnose-ci-failures",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/diagnose-ci-failures",
        "installs": 28600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [641, 578, 925, 489, 362, 355, 108, 76],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Diagnoses a pull request's failing GitHub CI checks from their logs and writes a fix plan for review, without changing code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A pull request's CI is red and you want to know why before anyone touches the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds the branch's PR with gh, sorts its checks into passed, failed and running, pulls the failed logs and groups the errors"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A plan: the failing checks, the errors and where they are, the fix for each group, and the commands to verify it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The GitHub CLI, gh, and a create_plan tool for the plan document"
            },
            {
              "label": "Tuned for",
              "text": "Rust projects: its error groups and check names assume cargo fmt, Clippy, WASM builds and macOS, Linux and Windows test runs"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A fixed sequence for red CI. The agent finds the PR for the current branch with gh, offering to open one with create-pr if there's none, and reads the status of every check. If some are still running, it reports what has passed and failed so far and suggests waiting. For each failed check it pulls the failed logs and extracts error messages with their files and lines, lint names, failing tests and stack traces, then groups them: formatting, linting, compilation, tests, and platform-specific failures such as WASM or one operating system. The plan, made with a create_plan tool, states the problem, what failed where, the fix for each group and the commands to verify it, and points to the fix-errors skill for how to fix each kind of error. It never edits code itself. It sets GH_PAGER=cat on every gh call, and treats tests that fail locally but passed in CI as likely flaky or specific to the environment.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/diagnose-ci-failures"
        },
