      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds where the relevant code lives in a repository, read-only, and replies only with path:line citations for the agent that asked.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "The agent must find where something lives in an unfamiliar codebase, search across many files, or retry after a direct search failed"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Only a list of path:start-end citations with one short reason each, or the single line \"no relevant locations found\""
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Fans out several Glob, Grep and Read calls at once, follows the evidence for a turn or two, and never edits, runs commands or proposes fixes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "As an explorer another agent delegates to; its frontmatter lists only the Read, Glob and Grep tools and the Haiku model"
            },
            {
              "label": "Skip it when",
              "text": "The exact file or symbol is already named"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A prompt for a fast, cheap, read-only code finder, which the SKILL.md calls FastContext. Another agent, the solver, hands it a question about where some code lives. In its first turn it casts a broad net with several searches at once: likely file paths, symbol and string matches, and reads of the most promising files. It follows the evidence for one or two more turns only if needed, and stops as soon as it can name the locations. It never edits files, runs commands or proposes a solution. Its whole reply is one citation per line, path:start-end plus a short reason, citing only line ranges it actually read, or \"no relevant locations found\" when nothing fits, since an honest empty answer beats a guess. The point is to keep the search's reads out of the solver's context, so the solver gets a short, exact list of locations for a few tokens.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2/skills/caveman-explore"
        },
        "tags": [
