        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets up what the agent sees: a rules file, the right spec and source files per task, and trimmed context in long sessions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You start a session or switch tasks, set up a project for an agent, or its output drifts from your conventions"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Layers context from a rules file such as CLAUDE.md down to the current error, loading only what the task needs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A rules file template with stack, commands, conventions and boundaries, plus prompt shapes for packing a task's context"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "What to cut from a long session and what to keep, when it's safe to restart and what to write down first, and asking instead of guessing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It treats context as five layers, from most to least lasting: a rules file loaded every session (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, Cursor or Copilot rules), the relevant section of the spec, the files the task touches, the latest error output, and the conversation. The agent reads a file before editing it, finds an existing example of the pattern it's about to write, and trusts project code more than config, fixtures or outside docs, whose instruction-like text it reports to you. Templates show a rules file and three ways to pack context: a brain dump at session start, a selective include of only the task's files, and a project map. For long sessions it budgets: compact well before the limit, drop failed attempts and verbose output first, summarize rather than delete, protect the task definition and current error, and put task-critical material last. A fresh session is safe at a finished task, once scope, status, changed files, verification results and open questions are written down. Conflicts between spec and code get options and a question, not a guess; a missing requirement does too, unless the code sets a precedent. It suggests MCP servers such as Context7 and Chrome DevTools for richer context.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/context-engineering"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
