        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Routes each piece of work to the right skill of Addy Osmani's pack, and sets rules every skill shares.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have this pack installed and want the agent to pick the right skill at the start of a session or a task"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Maps the task to a phase, from define to ship, and names the skill to use, or a sequence of skills for a whole feature"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also sets",
              "text": "Six rules for every task: state assumptions, stop when confused, push back, keep it simple, stay in scope, verify before calling it done"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The pack's entry point. A decision tree sends each task to one skill: interview-me when you don't yet know what you want, spec-driven-development for a new feature, debugging-and-error-recovery when something broke, shipping-and-launch for a deploy, and so on across the pack's 24 other skills. For a whole feature it gives a typical sequence, from interview and spec through planning, building slice by slice, testing, review, simplification, commits and docs to launch, and notes that a bug fix might need only three skills. It also sets behaviors that hold under every skill: list assumptions before non-trivial work, stop and ask when something is unclear or contradictory, push back on approaches with clear problems instead of agreeing, prefer the boring simple solution, touch only what you were asked to, and don't call anything done without evidence such as passing tests. Its rules tell the agent to check for an applicable skill before starting work and to follow each skill's steps in order.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/using-agent-skills"
        },
