        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Prepares a production launch: a pre-launch checklist, feature flags, a staged rollout with rollback thresholds, monitoring and a written rollback plan.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to deploy to production and want to know what must be in place first"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Walks a pre-launch checklist across code quality, security, performance, accessibility, infrastructure and docs, then plans a staged rollout"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rollout",
              "text": "Deploy with the flag off, enable for your team, then 5%, 25%, 50% and 100% of users, advancing only while error rate and latency hold"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A rollback plan with triggers, steps, database considerations and expected time to roll back"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "First-hour checks after launch, error reporting, what to monitor, feature flag cleanup and an error-budget release gate"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Launches should be reversible, observable and incremental. The pre-launch checklist runs through code quality (tests, build, lint, review, no stray console.log), security (secrets, dependency audit, validation, auth, headers, rate limits, CORS), performance, accessibility, infrastructure (environment variables, migrations, DNS and SSL, logging, a health check) and documentation. Features ship behind flags, each with an owner and an expiry date, removed within two weeks of full rollout and tested both on and off in CI. The rollout moves from staging to production with the flag off, then to the team, a 5% canary and wider steps, with a table of thresholds deciding whether to advance, hold or roll back: for example, roll back when errors double or p95 latency rises more than 50%. It lists what to monitor across the application, infrastructure and client, shows error reporting in a React error boundary and an Express handler, and gives a first-hour check after launch. An error budget, the share of failures your service level objective allows, gates releases: under 20% left means slow rollouts only, none left means a feature freeze. Every deploy needs a written rollback plan before it happens.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/shipping-and-launch"
        },
