        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks framework-specific code against the official docs for your exact versions, and cites the source for every pattern it uses.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want framework or library code that follows current documented practice instead of the agent's possibly outdated memory"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads your dependency files for exact versions, fetches the specific official doc page, writes code that follows it, then cites it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code with full source URLs in comments and replies, and anything it couldn't find documented flagged as unverified"
            },
            {
              "label": "Trusts",
              "text": "Official docs and changelogs, then MDN, web.dev and caniuse; never Stack Overflow, blog posts or its own training data"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The process is detect, fetch, implement, cite. The agent first reads package.json, pyproject.toml, go.mod or the like and states the versions it found, asking you when they're missing, since the version decides which pattern is right. It then fetches the one documentation page for the feature at hand, such as a specific React hook's reference, not a homepage or a general search, and notes deprecations and migration advice. Sources rank from official docs, to official blogs and changelogs, to web standards references, to compatibility tables. It writes code the way the docs show, uses the new way where the docs have one, and when the docs disagree with your existing code it shows you both options instead of picking one. Every framework-specific decision gets a citation with a full, deep-linked URL, quoting the passage when the choice isn't obvious. Fetched pages are treated as data: it takes API signatures, examples and deprecation notes from them, ignores anything addressed to the model, and won't hardcode a telemetry or other outside endpoint from an example without telling you. It skips the process for version-independent changes or when you ask for speed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/source-driven-development"
        },
