    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/deprecation-and-migration",
      "name": "deprecation-and-migration",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/deprecation-and-migration",
        "installs": 68500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3892, 3588, 3781, 4824, 5459, 5119, 4611, 4014],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-30"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Retires old systems, APIs or features safely: replacement first, consumers migrated one by one, old code removed once nothing uses it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You replace or sunset a system, API, library or feature, or rename or drop a database column in production"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks whether the old system still earns its keep, builds and documents the replacement, migrates each consumer, then removes the old code once usage is zero"
            },
            {
              "label": "For schemas",
              "text": "Expand and contract: add the new column, write to both, backfill in batches, switch reads, and drop the old column in a later deploy"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "Advisory versus forced deprecation, the strangler, adapter and feature-flag migration patterns, and zombie code nobody owns"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It starts from the view that code is a liability: every line costs upkeep, and with enough users every behavior gets depended on, so removal needs active migration, not just an announcement. Five questions decide whether to deprecate: does it still provide unique value, who depends on it, does a replacement exist, what will migrating cost each consumer, and what does keeping it cost? Deprecation defaults to advisory, with warnings and docs; a hard deadline is for security risks or unsustainable upkeep, and needs migration tooling. The process is to build a replacement proven in production, publish a deprecation notice with a migration guide, migrate consumers one at a time, verifying each, and remove the old code, tests, docs and config only once metrics show zero use. If you own what's being deprecated, migrating its users is your job. Patterns include running old and new side by side while shifting traffic (the strangler pattern), an adapter that keeps the old interface over the new code, and per-user feature flags. Database changes use expand and contract across separate deploys, each with a tested down migration. Zombie code, depended on but unowned, gets an owner or a removal plan.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/deprecation-and-migration"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "migration",
          "databases"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/interview-me",
