      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Profiles a React Native app's re-renders and CPU use through Argent, ranks the slowest commits and components, and re-profiles after each fix.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A React Native screen re-renders too often or stutters, or you want before-and-after numbers for a fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Records React commits and CPU samples, with native profiling alongside, while the agent or you use the app, then analyzes the recording"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A ranked report of hot commits and the functions behind them, drill-downs to the root-cause file, and a fix only if you want one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks you",
              "text": "After each report and each drill-down round whether to dig further, fix and re-profile, or stop"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Argent MCP server and a Hermes app on a simulator, emulator or Android phone; physical iPhones aren't supported"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of Software Mansion's Argent toolkit, which lets the agent control simulators and emulators; argent-react-native-optimization calls it for measurement. First the agent agrees a success metric with you, such as total render time or one component's render count. It starts the React and native profilers together, then drives the interaction itself or waits while you do, annotating every tap and swipe with the server's timestamps so the report lines up with what happened. The analysis ranks hot commits with the JavaScript functions that ran in each; when both profilers ran, a combined report links native hangs to React commits. Query tools then trace a slow commit to what triggered it, or a slow component to its hot function and source. Earlier sessions can be reloaded to compare before and after. After a fix it re-profiles the same scenario and says honestly whether the metric improved, stayed flat or regressed, reverting a fix with no net gain. It warns that dev builds render about three times slower, that live data and 10-15% run-to-run noise skew results, and it won't propose memoization for components React Compiler already handles.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/software-mansion/argent/tree/b965c94827d6167fd2912a7ca5ad5505040ae4e0/packages/skills/skills/argent-react-native-profiler"
