      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies REST and GraphQL design principles, such as resource naming, versioning, pagination and error formats, when designing or reviewing an API.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing a new REST or GraphQL API, reviewing an API spec, or setting API standards for a team"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives the agent principles for resources, HTTP methods, GraphQL schemas, versioning, pagination, rate limits and errors"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "APIs designed along those rules; it adds no steps or files of its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "Common pitfalls, such as breaking changes, inconsistent error formats and APIs that mirror the database schema"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It's a reference, not a workflow. For REST, resources are plural nouns and actions are HTTP methods; it spells out what GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE mean and which are idempotent, and asks for correct status codes, stateless requests, pagination of large collections, rate limits and OpenAPI docs. For GraphQL, it designs the schema before the resolvers, uses DataLoaders to avoid N+1 queries, validates at schema and resolver level, returns structured errors in mutation payloads, paginates with cursors and retires fields with @deprecated. It shows three ways to version an API, with an example each: in the URL, in a header or as a query parameter. A separate file holds detailed patterns and worked examples.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/backend-development/skills/api-design-principles"
        },
