        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Removes the tells that make a web app feel like a website on a phone, such as sticky hover, tap flashes and input zoom.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A web app or PWA looks fine in desktop Chrome but feels like a website, not an app, on a phone"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Matches what you see to a symptom table and applies the fix, usually a single CSS declaration or meta tag"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The fixes in your code, plus a few lines on what was wrong, what changed and what still needs a real phone to confirm"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike animate-expo",
              "text": "That skill is for React Native apps; this one is for web apps in a mobile browser, and leaves motion design to animate"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Emil Kowalski's checklist for the platform layer of the mobile web: viewport, touch, scroll, safe areas and browser chrome. Each symptom maps to a fix and its reason. Hover styles that stick after a tap go behind a hover-and-fine-pointer media query; the gray tap flash is turned off; 100vh becomes 100dvh for app shells and 100svh for heroes; inputs get 16px text so iOS stops zooming in; taps get touch-action: manipulation and press feedback on :active; overscroll-behavior stops pull-to-refresh; viewport-fit=cover and safe-area insets handle the notch; controls stop selecting their label on long press; carousels claim their swipe axis; and theme-color is set per color scheme. Its hard rules: never disable zoom, detect touch with media queries rather than user agents, write for touch and mouse at once, and never call something fixed from device emulation. It also gives a baseline of meta tags and CSS for a new mobile-facing app and a never-ship table to check before finishing. When it can't test on hardware, it says which fixes it verified from code and which you must check on a phone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833/skills/mobile-native"
        },
