    {
      "id": "jakubkrehel/skills/skills/better-colors",
      "name": "better-colors",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jakubkrehel/skills/better-colors",
        "installs": 39500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2429, 2297, 3061, 2722, 1748, 2021, 2922, 2596],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds and audits color systems: generates ramps with a color library, maps steps to semantic tokens, converts notation and measures contrast.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a palette and color tokens, or checking existing ones for broken roles and failing contrast"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds one-hue ramps with a color library, gives every step a role, and names tokens in two tiers: by hue, then by job"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Tokens in your project's notation, OKLCH for a new system, and a measured contrast value for each rendered pair"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Change a failing color unless asked: it reports the pair, its measured value and the threshold it misses"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The color part of Jakub Krehel's better-* interface skills. A color system here is ramps, not colors: one neutral, one accent and only the status ramps the product actually renders, each holding one hue, built with a color library rather than by eye, and with no step that no role uses. Primitive tokens name a value, like --blue-600, and are never used in components; semantic tokens name a job, like --color-text-secondary, and are the only ones components reference, so a theme can repoint colors without touching them. A token is never borrowed for another role because its value happens to match. Hues within 15° in OKLCH count as the same color, so each color keeps one meaning, and color never carries meaning alone. Where filled color marks the primary action, only one action per view gets it. Contrast is measured against the background the text actually renders on, never estimated. A checklist catches common slips, such as white text on a Tailwind 500 fill, a dark theme made by reversing the light ramp, and vivid OKLCH colors with no sRGB fallback.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/better-colors"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "design-system",
          "accessibility"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "jakubkrehel/skills/skills/better-layout",
      "name": "better-layout",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jakubkrehel/skills/better-layout",
        "installs": 37600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2550, 2327, 3042, 2675, 1687, 2001, 2864, 2592],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets grouping, alignment, reading order and responsive structure in UI code, then checks it survives resizing, translation and right-to-left mirroring.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A layout has to hold up at narrow widths, at 200% zoom, in longer languages or mirrored for right-to-left"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Groups with space before lines, aligns to shared edges, keeps visual order equal to source order and writes direction-dependent CSS as start and end"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Fixes in your project's own spacing tokens, or a review whose findings each show a clip, overlap, broken mirror or misread group"
            },
            {
              "label": "Starting values",
              "text": "8px within a group and 16px or more between groups, used only when the project has no spacing scale"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The layout part of Jakub Krehel's better-* interface skills. It sets structure, then stress-tests it by resizing, translating and mirroring. Groups are made with space first, background shapes second and separator lines last, with gaps between groups at least twice those within. Controls get a shape, border, underline or fixed zone so they never read as static text, and each view gets one primary action, with secondary actions moved into a menu past three. Direction-dependent layout uses logical properties such as margin-inline-start, so it mirrors for right-to-left languages, and content is never reordered away from its source order. Hidden or scrolled-off content gets a visible cue, and breakpoints come from the content, with container queries for components. Text containers use max-width and min-height rather than fixed sizes, since translated strings, short labels most of all, grow; it suggests testing with pseudo-localization and German. A checklist catches traps such as 100vw causing a horizontal scroll, 100vh hidden under mobile toolbars, and safe-area insets that stay zero without viewport-fit=cover. A value that merely differs from its starting points is not a finding.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/better-layout"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "jakubkrehel/skills/skills/better-interface",
      "name": "better-interface",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jakubkrehel/skills/better-interface",
        "installs": 36700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2401, 2504, 2956, 2508, 1624, 1875, 2748, 2503],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a screen, flow or whole interface by running the six better-* domain reviews, then merges their findings into one ranked report.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a screen, flow or whole interface reviewed for accessibility, layout, copy, type, color and polish at once"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Loads each better-* skill in a fixed order, from accessibility to polish, and merges their findings into one table ranked by user impact"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One read-only report of at most 15 findings, each citing file and line, with the scope covered, what was verified and a verdict"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The six better-* skills installed; a domain whose skill is missing is marked Not reviewed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike interface-review",
              "text": "That one reviews only what a branch, pull request or uncommitted change introduced; this one reviews what exists"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The orchestrator of Jakub Krehel's better-* interface skills: it owns no design rules of its own. It first settles the scope, narrowing a request too large to inspect to one complete flow and saying what it left out. It identifies the framework, styling system, tokens and supported viewports, and reads the project's own interface docs, such as CONTRIBUTING.md, AGENTS.md or a design-system doc, so fixes are written in the project's idiom and documented conventions settle matters of taste. Then it loads better-accessibility, better-layout, better-writing, better-typography, better-colors and better-ui in that order, so basic failures aren't hidden behind polish. Thirteen escalation triggers, such as a control with no accessible name, body text failing contrast or an error with no way to recover, are always high severity. For each finding it proposes the cheapest fix that works: delete, use the platform, reuse the project's tokens, correct a value, and only then add something. One root cause is one finding listing every place it occurs. It runs the project's safe checks, marks what it couldn't check as Not verified, and edits no code unless you also ask for the fixes. Asked about a branch or pull request, it sends you to interface-review.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/better-interface"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "accessibility",
          "code-review"
        ],
        "note": "Loads the six better-* skills from the same repo; without them, it marks their domains Not reviewed.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "jakubkrehel/skills/skills/better-accessibility",
      "name": "better-accessibility",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jakubkrehel/skills/better-accessibility",
        "installs": 36300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2379, 2182, 2926, 2588, 1619, 1931, 2809, 2536],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews and fixes keyboard and focus behavior, ARIA, accessible names, forms, announcements, motion and zoom, citing the WCAG 2.2 criterion each failure breaks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building or reviewing UI and want it to work by keyboard and screen reader and meet WCAG 2.2 AA"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Walks each flow keyboard-only, then as a screen-reader user, checking names, roles, states, focus, forms, announcements, motion and zoom"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Fixes in your project's styling, or findings that each cite a WCAG criterion by number, ending in Block or Approve"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rule of thumb",
              "text": "Native elements over ARIA, and when unsure, remove ARIA rather than add it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The accessibility part of Jakub Krehel's better-* interface skills. Only a failure of a WCAG 2.2 Level A or AA criterion, or a task an assistive-technology user can't complete, counts as a finding; AAA criteria, ARIA pattern conventions and the larger 44px and 40px targets are recommendations, never high severity. Native elements come first: a button for actions, a link for navigation, never a clickable div. It covers focus rings on :focus-visible with 3:1 contrast, focus never hidden behind sticky headers, keyboard support following the ARIA Authoring Practices key maps, dialogs that trap focus and return it on close, focus moved on client-side route changes, 24px minimum targets, a single-pointer alternative to every drag, labelled inputs with autocomplete and paste allowed, errors checked on submit and announced, accessible names, a second cue beside color, reduced motion, pause controls for anything moving longer than five seconds, live regions for updates, alt text by purpose, and reflow at 320px width and 200% zoom. It decides which contrast ratio applies, while better-colors measures it. With a browser, it tabs through each flow, reads names and roles from the accessibility tree and runs an automated audit.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/better-accessibility"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "accessibility"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "jakubkrehel/skills/skills/better-writing",
      "name": "better-writing",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jakubkrehel/skills/better-writing",
        "installs": 35500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2310, 2160, 2854, 2520, 1579, 1826, 2688, 2398],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes and reviews interface copy, such as buttons, errors, empty states and confirmations, in the product's existing voice and vocabulary.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing or reviewing the words in an interface: labels, buttons, errors, empty states and confirmations"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "First lists the nouns, verbs and capitalization the product already uses, then writes or checks copy against them and its rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Copy in the product's voice, or findings by severity with file, before and after, ending in Block or Approve"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Flag a deliberate brand voice, or rewording that only suits its taste"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The copy part of Jakub Krehel's better-* interface skills. Before writing, it finds where strings live, from translation calls and locale files to a CMS, and lists the word used for each object and action, so new copy says \"Archive\" wherever the menu already does. Tone follows the stakes: warm for success and empty states, calm and plain for errors and destructive actions. Buttons start with a verb, links name their destination instead of \"Click here\", toggles describe what happens when they're on, and errors sit beside the field and say how to fix it, with no \"Oops\" and no exclamation marks. It prefers undo for frequent reversible actions, and confirms irreversible ones with a title naming the action and the object, verb-and-object buttons, and, for an account, workspace or repository, typing its name. Empty states say what the place is for and offer one next step. Strings are built whole with placeholders and the locale's plural rules, never stitched around a variable, and dates and numbers go through Intl. A checklist flags words such as \"successfully\" and \"Please\" in routine copy.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/better-writing"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "category:writing",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "writing",
          "design"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill/cli/assets/skills/design-system",
      "name": "design-system",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill/design-system",
        "installs": 52200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2132, 1999, 1986, 2291, 2654, 3485, 3926, 3553],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-25"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "b7d5353e0130feb8c5574ef1251f8a1db1873ccb",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds three-layer design tokens with scripts that generate and validate them, plus token-based HTML slide decks with Chart.js charts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're setting up design tokens or component state specs, or want an HTML pitch deck that sticks to them"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Layers tokens as raw values, purpose aliases and per-component values, and runs scripts that turn a JSON config into CSS variables and flag hardcoded values"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A tokens CSS file, and for slides, HTML decks that import it, draw charts with Chart.js and navigate by keyboard, click and progress bar"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "brand, a sibling skill whose brand colors and fonts become the primitive tokens"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of the design skills, written by ClaudeKit, that ship next to ui-ux-pro-max in the UI UX Pro Max repo. Tokens come in three layers: primitives hold raw values such as --color-blue-600, semantic tokens alias them by purpose such as --color-primary, and component tokens such as --button-bg point at those, so a theme can switch without touching components. generate-tokens.cjs turns a JSON config into CSS variables, validate-tokens.cjs scans your source for hardcoded values, and a starter JSON template is included. References cover each layer, component specs, states and variants, and Tailwind integration. The slide half builds persuasive HTML decks: a BM25 search over bundled CSV data picks a deck structure with an emotional arc, then for each slide a layout, type scale, color treatment and, if needed, a background image; the agent writes the HTML on the tokens and checks it with a validator script. Every slide must import the tokens CSS, use only CSS variables, draw charts with Chart.js and include navigation.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill/tree/b7d5353e0130feb8c5574ef1251f8a1db1873ccb/cli/assets/skills/design-system"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:tool",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design-system",
          "slides"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill/cli/assets/skills/design",
      "name": "design",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill/design",
        "installs": 50600,
