    {
      "id": "dietrichgebert/ponytail/skills/ponytail-review",
      "name": "ponytail-review",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/dietrichgebert/ponytail/ponytail-review",
        "installs": 69884,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2591, 2486, 3221, 8440, 10867, 6013, 5776, 4529],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-12"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96608e078b2d5ea607c5c3b8b5a54d1c1fba6846",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a code change like the developer who'll be paged when it breaks: bugs, security, load, missing tests, speed and needless code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a diff, branch, pull request or your last commit reviewed for what will actually break, not for style"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the change and the code around it, such as callers, tests and the README, then checks correctness, safety, load, tests, speed and needless code, in that order"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Numbered plain-English findings under Must fix, Should fix and Nice to have, each with what the code does, the problem, the fix and the cost of skipping it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Ends with",
              "text": "A verdict, such as \"Ship.\" or \"fix 1 and 3 first\", and how many lines could go"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike ponytail-audit",
              "text": "That one runs the same checks across a whole repo instead of one change"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Change code, or report style taste or a finding without a concrete failing case"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Ponytail is a skill set that makes coding agents write the least code that fully solves a task; this is its reviewer, modelled on the senior developer who gets paged when a change breaks. With no target named, it reviews uncommitted changes, or the last commit if there are none. It reads past the diff, to the callers of every changed function, the functions it calls, the tests and the README, and greps every caller when a signature or return value changes. It looks in the repo for the expected load, from one person running a script to many users at once, and says which it assumed. Findings rank by correct, safe, holds under load, tested, fast, lean: missed edge cases and broken callers, injection and data loss, races and a query per item, risky logic with no test, real slowdowns, and code to delete, reuse, merge or split. Each needs a concrete case that goes wrong, and a shortcut marked with a shortcut: comment naming its limit counts as a decision. Findings are numbered so you can say \"fix 2 and 5\", and written in short everyday sentences for a reader who's never seen the code.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail/tree/96608e078b2d5ea607c5c3b8b5a54d1c1fba6846/skills/ponytail-review"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "code-quality"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "dietrichgebert/ponytail/skills/ponytail-audit",
      "name": "ponytail-audit",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/dietrichgebert/ponytail/ponytail-audit",
        "installs": 57500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2370, 2293, 3020, 4843, 5407, 5602, 5460, 4302],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96608e078b2d5ea607c5c3b8b5a54d1c1fba6846",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits a whole repo for bugs, security holes, what breaks under real load, untested risky code, slow paths and code to delete.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've inherited a codebase, or want to know what in yours to fix or delete first"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Maps the repo first, from README, deploy config and dependencies to entry points and tests, then traces the main data flows end to end"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Up to 20 ranked findings in plain English, each with the problem, the fix and the cost of skipping it, and a verdict on what to fix first"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also reports",
              "text": "How many lines and dependencies could go, and which parts it didn't read or couldn't run"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike ponytail-review",
              "text": "That one checks one change and the code it touches"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Change any code; it's a one-shot report"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Ponytail is a skill set that makes coding agents write the least code that fully solves a task; this skill audits a whole repo, or a folder or package you name, like the senior developer who just inherited it. It reads the README, build and deploy config, dependency list, entry points and tests, works out the expected load and says what it assumed, and traces how data comes in, is stored and goes out, reading the riskiest paths in full: user input, money, auth, data writes, background jobs and anything shared between processes. On a big repo it goes deep where a mistake costs most and names the parts it skipped. Findings rank by correct, safe, holds under load, tested, fast and lean, and lean findings name what to delete, reuse, merge or split, such as an interface with one implementation or a dependency doing what a few lines could. Before calling code unused, it greps the whole tree, including tests, config and dynamic references. Every finding needs a concrete case that goes wrong, and they come grouped as Must fix, Should fix and Nice to have, numbered so you can pick which to fix.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail/tree/96608e078b2d5ea607c5c3b8b5a54d1c1fba6846/skills/ponytail-audit"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "code-quality"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
