        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits a site's performance, accessibility, SEO, best practices and agent readiness from live measurements, then makes or recommends fixes by severity.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want one review of a site's quality across speed, accessibility, search, security and how well AI agents can use it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Records a performance trace and runs Lighthouse audits, uses the failures to find the code responsible, then fixes or recommends fixes and re-runs the checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report with an evidence table, issues from critical to low with file, impact and fix, a fix order, and what's still unverified"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Chrome DevTools MCP if available, otherwise the Lighthouse CLI, PageSpeed Insights and manual checks; a bundled script gives a quick static scan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike its siblings",
              "text": "It's the umbrella of Addy Osmani's web quality skills; performance, core-web-vitals, accessibility, seo and best-practices each go deep on one area"
            },
            {
              "label": "Install with",
              "text": "The repo's performance skill: it reads that skill's measurement workflow file"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It starts by settling the scope: which URLs, states and user journeys, logged in or not, mobile or desktop. With a runnable page, it collects a small live baseline before searching the codebase, and lets runtime failures point to the source, keeping measured findings apart from hypotheses found only in code. Performance goes through a browser trace, since the Lighthouse tool in Chrome DevTools MCP leaves performance out; accessibility, SEO, best practices and agentic browsing go through Lighthouse, plus an accessibility-tree snapshot and trying the UI. The checklist covers Core Web Vitals thresholds, images, scripts, fonts and caching; WCAG basics such as alt text, contrast, keyboard access and labels; crawlability and page tags; HTTPS, CSP and deprecated APIs; and agentic browsing, meaning whether an AI assistant can find and operate the page's controls, with WebMCP and llms.txt treated as optional. It warns that an aggregate score isn't proof of quality. Findings are ranked critical (fix now), high (before launch), medium (this sprint) or low. It ends with checklists for every deploy and for weekly and monthly reviews.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/web-quality-skills/tree/afa8da942115f2961fdbfa80807ea0b232ff6c00/skills/web-quality-audit"
