      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a website page by page in a loop: each run generates one page in Google Stitch and writes the next page's prompt.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an agent to build a whole website with Google Stitch, Google's AI tool that generates UI screens from text, one page per run"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the next page's prompt from a \"baton\" file, generates the screen in Stitch, adds its HTML to your site and wires up the navigation"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A growing static site in site/public, an updated sitemap in .stitch/SITE.md, and the next page's prompt written for the following run"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Once per call; you, a CI job or another agent re-run it to build each next page"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Stitch MCP server, plus .stitch/DESIGN.md (the design system, which the repo's design-md skill can write) and .stitch/SITE.md (vision, sitemap, roadmap)"
            },
            {
              "label": "Optional",
              "text": "Chrome DevTools MCP, to screenshot each built page and compare it with Stitch's render"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The \"baton\" is .stitch/next-prompt.md: a page name and a prompt that must carry the design system block from DESIGN.md, so every page shares one look. Each run reads it, checks SITE.md so it doesn't rebuild an existing page, and generates the screen in Stitch, creating the Stitch project on the first run and recording project and screen IDs in .stitch/metadata.json. It downloads the screen's HTML and screenshot, asking before it replaces local copies, moves the HTML into site/public, fixes asset paths and turns placeholder links into real navigation. With Chrome DevTools MCP, it serves the site locally and compares a screenshot with Stitch's. It then ticks the page off in SITE.md's sitemap, picks the next page from the roadmap, from SITE.md's list of ideas, or invents one that fits the site, and writes that prompt to the baton. Skipping that last step breaks the loop, so the skill insists on it. How runs are triggered is up to you: a GitHub Action when the baton changes, a developer reviewing each iteration, or one agent dispatching the next.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/google-labs-code/stitch-skills/tree/0337446dadde6f8c94210444e2aa9d546126480f/plugins/stitch-utilities/skills/stitch-loop"
