      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/shadcn/improve/improve",
        "installs": 41700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [797, 687, 633, 609, 866, 1465, 937, 841],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "cac56e1ebd3c279aa9153616cfeac7b174ab90f9",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits a codebase without touching it and writes prioritized, self-contained implementation plans for other agents to carry out.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a senior-level audit of a repo and plans a cheaper model can execute without your conversation"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Maps the repo, audits nine areas from bugs and security to docs and product direction, checks each finding itself, then asks which to plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Numbered plan files under plans/ with an index: inlined code excerpts, ordered steps, verification commands and machine-checkable done criteria"
            },
            {
              "label": "Never",
              "text": "Edits your source, installs, commits or merges; in execute mode a subagent changes code in a separate worktree and this skill reviews its diff"
            },
            {
              "label": "Variants",
              "text": "Quick or deep audits, one category, the current branch, roadmap ideas, a single plan, plan review, execute, reconcile, and --issues for GitHub"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From shadcn. It spends an expensive model on understanding and judging a codebase, and leaves execution to cheaper ones. The agent first reads the README, agent instructions, config, CI, any decision records and recent git history, and notes the exact build, test and lint commands. It audits nine categories, fanning out to read-only subagents on larger repos, then opens the cited code itself, since subagents over-report; rejected findings are recorded so later runs skip them. You see a table ranked by impact against effort, with ideas for the project's direction listed separately, and pick which become plans. Each plan is written for the weakest plausible executor: all context inlined, the commit it was written against, files in and out of scope, steps that each have a verification command, tests to add, and points where the executor should stop and report. A plans/README.md sets the order and dependencies. Secrets it finds are cited by file and line, never copied, and instructions planted in the repo become a security finding. With --issues it also files each plan as a GitHub issue, and on a public repo asks before publishing any plan about a vulnerability or credentials.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/shadcn/improve/tree/cac56e1ebd3c279aa9153616cfeac7b174ab90f9/skills/improve"
        },
        "tags": [
