        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Helps build trading-strategy backtests that avoid look-ahead, survivorship and overfitting bias, using walk-forward testing and realistic trading costs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a backtester, or checking whether a trading strategy's historical results can be trusted"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Names the biases that inflate backtests (look-ahead, survivorship, overfitting, cherry-picking, ignored trading costs) and how to avoid each"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Backtests that split history into training, validation and test sets and test walk-forward over rolling windows"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also has",
              "text": "Python for an event-driven and a vectorized backtester, walk-forward optimization, Monte Carlo analysis and performance metrics"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference for trading-strategy backtests. Each bias comes with its fix: point-in-time data against look-ahead, delisted securities against survivorship, out-of-sample testing against overfitting, pre-registration against cherry-picking strategies, and realistic cost models for trading costs. History is split three ways: develop on the training set, choose parameters on the validation set without peeking, and touch the test set only for the final estimate; walk-forward analysis repeats that over rolling windows. Its rules: limit parameters, never optimize on the full history, handle adjusted prices with care, use Monte Carlo analysis to understand uncertainty, and account for capacity and market impact. A separate file holds the implementations in Python with pandas and NumPy: an event-driven backtester, a faster vectorized one, walk-forward optimization, Monte Carlo analysis, and a function computing returns, volatility, Sharpe, Sortino and Calmar ratios, maximum drawdown, win rate and profit factor.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/quantitative-trading/skills/backtesting-frameworks"
