        "description": "Writes a phased implementation plan file of atomic, ID-numbered tasks, with files, tests, risks and status, for another agent or person to execute.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a feature, refactor, package upgrade or infrastructure change planned in a file an agent can execute without asking questions"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Fills a mandatory template: requirements and constraints, phases with goals and task tables, alternatives, dependencies, files, tests, risks and assumptions"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A Markdown file in /plan/, named like feature-auth-module-1.md, with front matter and a status badge: Planned, In progress, Completed, On Hold or Deprecated"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "Runs grep commands so each REQ-, TASK- or other identifier is declared only once, renumbering duplicates until the checks come back empty"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike create-specification",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, defines what a component must do; this one sets out the tasks to build it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The plan is written for machines first: deterministic language with nothing left to interpret, tables and lists that parse cleanly, and tasks that name exact file paths, functions and implementation details so none needs a human decision. Each phase has a GOAL- and measurable completion criteria, and its TASK- rows track completion and date; tasks within a phase can run in parallel unless a dependency is stated. Every section of the template is required, headers must match exactly and no placeholder text may remain. Before finishing, the agent runs three grep checks over the file: two that must return nothing, catching an identifier declared twice in a task table or a bullet line, and a broader scan for awareness only, since references to an ID are allowed. The file name starts with its purpose: upgrade, refactor, feature, data, infrastructure, process, architecture or design. The checks need grep, sed, sort and uniq, or Windows equivalents.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/create-implementation-plan"
        },
