      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns threat models and business needs into specific, testable security requirements, user stories and test cases, mapped to compliance frameworks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have a threat model or business security goals and need requirements, security user stories or acceptance criteria a team can build and test against"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Traces each requirement from a business need to a security requirement to a technical control, typed as functional, non-functional or constraint, with priority and risk level"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Requirements with acceptance criteria and test specifications, security user stories grouped into epics, and mappings to PCI DSS, HIPAA, GDPR and OWASP controls"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Threat models whose threats carry STRIDE categories, the six kinds of threat from spoofing and tampering to elevation of privilege, which its templates turn into requirement patterns"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference for the step after threat modeling. It sorts security requirements into functional (what the system must do), non-functional (how well it must do it) and constraints (limits such as approved crypto libraries), and gives each one a link to the threat or compliance rule behind it, a way to test it, a priority and a risk level. Its templates, written in Python and kept in a separate file, model requirements and sets of them, turn STRIDE-labelled threats into requirement patterns (spoofing becomes authentication and session rules, tampering becomes input validation and integrity checks), set priority from a threat's impact times its likelihood, map requirements to PCI DSS, HIPAA, GDPR and OWASP controls with a traceability matrix and a gap analysis, and write security user stories and epics with acceptance criteria and a definition of done. The rules: trace every requirement to a threat, make it specific enough to test, define when it's done, map to compliance early, and never write \"be secure\" as a requirement.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/security-scanning/skills/security-requirement-extraction"
