      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies Clean Architecture, Hexagonal Architecture and Domain-Driven Design to backend services, with layer rules, ports, aggregates and in-memory test adapters.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing a backend service, untangling business logic from ORM models or HTTP code, splitting a monolith into bounded contexts, or breaking dependency cycles between layers"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives the agent the rules of Clean and Hexagonal Architecture and the strategic and tactical patterns of Domain-Driven Design, with Python examples"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A layered structure where dependencies point inward, interfaces at each boundary, and use cases you can test without a database"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "Troubleshooting, such as circular imports between layers, ORM annotations on domain entities, overgrown controllers and one context importing another's entities"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference for structuring backend code. Clean Architecture's layers (entities, use cases, interface adapters, frameworks) depend only inward. Hexagonal Architecture keeps the domain core behind ports, abstract interfaces, and plugs adapters such as a PostgreSQL or Stripe implementation in at the edges. From Domain-Driven Design it takes bounded contexts, a separate model per subdomain, and a ubiquitous language shared with domain experts, plus entities, value objects, aggregates, repositories and domain events. The sign it's done right: every use case runs in a plain unit test against an in-memory repository, with no database, Docker or network. Examples are in Python. Two more files hold worked implementations and advanced patterns: a multi-service project tree, Onion versus Clean Architecture, an anti-corruption layer between contexts, context maps, dependency injection wiring, aggregate design heuristics, domain events through a transactional outbox, and finding dependency cycles with pydeps.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/backend-development/skills/architecture-patterns"
