              "text": "A mitigation report: executive summary, defense-in-depth coverage, critical gaps, recommendations and an implementation roadmap"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also has",
              "text": "Python templates for a control library tied to STRIDE threat categories (spoofing, tampering, repudiation and so on), budget-limited control selection and control effectiveness tests"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The step after finding threats: deciding what stops them. Controls are sorted by what they do, preventing an attack (firewall, input validation), detecting one in progress (intrusion detection, log monitoring) or recovering from it (incident response, backup restore), and by layer, from network and application to data, endpoints, and people and process. Defense in depth means each threat meets several layers and kinds of control, so no single control is a point of failure. Its Python templates model threats, controls and coverage; hold a library of standard controls, such as multi-factor authentication, with cost, effectiveness and compliance references; compute risk reduction and critical gaps; choose controls within a budget; generate a roadmap and a report; and test whether controls work. The rules: leave no threat unmapped, layer and mix control types, weigh cost, test controls, review them regularly, and don't rely on technology alone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/security-scanning/skills/threat-mitigation-mapping"
