            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference, not a step-by-step workflow. RAG, retrieval-augmented generation, means fetching passages from your own documents and putting them in the model's prompt, so answers rest on them rather than on the model's memory. The SKILL.md lists four parts and the choices for each: a vector database to store document embeddings, an embedding model to turn text into vectors (with a 2026 table of models and their dimensions), a retrieval strategy (dense, keyword such as BM25, hybrid, multi-query, or HyDE, which searches with a hypothetical answer), and reranking to reorder results. Its quick start builds a two-step LangGraph graph, retrieve then generate, with Claude, Voyage embeddings and Pinecone. A reference file holds worked code for hybrid search with rank fusion, multi-query retrieval, contextual compression, parent-document retrieval and HyDE; chunking by characters, tokens, meaning or Markdown headers; setup for five vector stores; metadata filtering, MMR (which balances relevance with variety), cross-encoder and Cohere reranking; prompts that cite sources; structured answers; and a function that scores retrieval precision and recall, and answer quality with an LLM judge.",
