      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits web pages against WCAG 2.2 with a criterion-by-criterion checklist, automated axe-core, pa11y and Lighthouse checks, and fixes for common violations.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're auditing a site for accessibility, fixing WCAG violations, or preparing for ADA, Section 508 or VPAT requirements"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Ranks common violations by impact, and its reference file gives a checklist of 38 Level A and AA criteria, grouped by principle, about a third of them with code examples"
            },
            {
              "label": "Automates",
              "text": "axe-core inside a Playwright test, plus axe, pa11y and Lighthouse from the command line; it says automation finds only 30–50% of issues"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fixes",
              "text": "Before-and-after code for missing form labels and insufficient color contrast, and a custom dropdown made keyboard-operable"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference for accessibility audits against WCAG 2.2, the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines. Conformance comes in three levels: A is the legal baseline, AA what most regulations require, AAA enhanced accessibility. Every criterion falls under four principles, known as POUR: perceivable, operable, understandable and robust. The SKILL.md sorts common violations by impact: critical ones block users (missing alt text, no keyboard access, unlabeled form fields, autoplaying media without controls), serious ones include low contrast and missing skip links, moderate ones a missing language attribute or a broken heading order. Its reference file is a checklist of 38 Level A and AA criteria from 1.1.1 to 4.1.3, including the WCAG 2.2 addition Focus Not Obscured and noting that 4.1.1 Parsing is obsolete, each with checkboxes and about a third with HTML, CSS or JavaScript examples. It then shows axe-core running in a Playwright test, CLI checks with axe, pa11y and Lighthouse, and fixes for three common violations. Because tools catch only part of the issues, it asks for manual testing and testing with disabled users, native HTML before ARIA, visible focus outlines, zoom left on and never color alone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/accessibility-compliance/skills/wcag-audit-patterns"
