      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Rewrites Chinese or English prose to remove AI tone while keeping the author's meaning and voice, with modes for release notes, replies and tweets.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A draft in Chinese or English reads as AI-written, or you need release notes, a maintainer reply or a social post"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Cuts AI tells such as repetition, summary tone and em dashes, keeping the meaning, structure and the author's own voice"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The edited text, or a scoped diff for files in a repo; your own finished draft gets light fixes plus a list of every changed sentence"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also runs",
              "text": "A bundled punctuation checker for the output language before returning any text"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike phrase-by-phrase rewording",
              "text": "This one mostly cuts, and counts over-editing as a failure as bad as under-editing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Write treats its rules as a catalog of AI smells, not a checklist. It reads the text first, asks about the audience only when it can't be inferred, and loads a Chinese or English reference, plus bilingual and localization guides when copy spans languages. The default is a line-level rewrite in place: no reordered sections, no deleted links or images without saying why, and no invented anecdotes when writing as you. Banned-phrase lists are examples, so a flagged word that reads naturally stays. Modes cover release notes, public issue or PR replies, installation steps and error text, long drafts that need structural work (proposed first), comparing two versions of a text, document review with a privacy scan, paragraph coherence and social posts. When asked to write rather than edit, it counts the material it actually has before choosing a length, and researches, asks or writes shorter instead of padding. It never outputs em or en dashes. Code comments and commit messages are out of scope.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/tw93/waza/tree/58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c/skills/write"
        },
        "tags": [
