      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Researches an unfamiliar topic from primary sources through six phases, from collecting to self-review, into notes or a publish-ready article.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need to understand an unfamiliar domain, or turn a pile of materials into one reference"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works through six phases, collect, digest, outline, fill in, refine and self-review, starting at the phase your mode needs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Research notes, or a publish-ready draft that you read through yourself before publishing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "Deep research, quick reference (notes only), write to learn from materials you already have, or one canonical article that covers a topic"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "read to fetch sources and write to strip AI tone; without them it warns once and falls back to native fetch and a manual scan"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Learn is for research across many sources that ends in something new; a single page to fetch belongs to read. Collecting uses primary sources only, such as papers, official blogs, builders' posts and from-scratch repos, not explainers: 5 to 10 for a blog post, 15 to 20 for a technical survey. Digesting means building a mental model, not summarizing: a claim reaches the outline only if it passes at least two of three tests, such as showing up in more than one context and not being generic wisdom. Every outline section needs a source behind it, and a section that's hard to write sends it back to digest that sub-topic. Refining only edits, then strips AI patterns. The last phase is yours: you read the whole draft yourself, at least twice. Contradictions between sources stay visible, and it never posts or uploads anything unless asked. It can also distill conversations or review reports into durable rules, keeping only patterns with repeated evidence.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/tw93/waza/tree/58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c/skills/learn"
        },
