      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews mobile and desktop app designs against Apple's Human Interface Guidelines, citing its bundled copy, and flags generic, templated looks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want screenshots, mockups or UI code of an iOS, macOS, Flutter, React Native, Tauri or Electron app reviewed"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Loads 8 to 12 of its 123 HIG pages for what's on screen and audits accessibility, platform conventions, craft, interaction and writing, in that order"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report rated from Excellent to Critical issues, each finding tagged by severity, citing file and heading, with a concrete fix in your framework"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike emilkowalski's apple-design",
              "text": "That one brings Apple's motion and gestures to web code; this one reviews whole app designs against Apple's guideline text"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also",
              "text": "An improvement mode that plans colors, type, layout and one signature element, and critiques the plan before proposing changes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent plays two roles at once: a reviewer who knows Apple's Human Interface Guidelines and a studio design lead who wants the app to have a point of view. The guidelines ship with the skill as 123 Markdown pages pulled from developer.apple.com, plus a curated Liquid Glass guide and a file that translates Apple's terms for Flutter, React Native, Tauri and Electron. It must open a page before citing it, quoting it as file and heading, and labels anything else as its own judgment. A review first pins down platform, framework, audience and the screen's single job, then applies Apple's eight design principles and five lenses in order. Accessibility failures, such as small text below 4.5:1 contrast, are Critical, and findings come with numbers rather than adjectives. The craft lens names three looks that dominate generated interfaces, such as warm cream with a serif and a terracotta accent, and asks what the design will be remembered by. Specialized modes cover app icons, dark mode, Liquid Glass, Apple Pay buttons, iPhone Duo layouts, widgets, onboarding and code audits. Web and Android-only apps get the principles and foundations, not Apple's platform conventions.",
