      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Tests an application against the current OWASP Top 10 with Strix, exploiting each category it can and reporting coverage honestly.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need an assessment mapped to OWASP Top 10 categories"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs Strix against each OWASP Top 10:2025 category and maps every proven finding back to its category"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Findings grouped by category, with a frank note of what a runtime scan cannot assess"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "Categories like logging/alerting marked not-testable rather than reported as passed"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Treats the OWASP Top 10 as a risk taxonomy, not a test suite: it exercises each category against the real application and reports only what is actually exploitable. It defaults to the current OWASP Top 10:2025 (SSRF folded into A01, a new software-supply-chain category and a new exceptional-conditions category) and asks before using an older edition. A coverage table states honestly which categories a runtime scan handles strongly (access control, misconfiguration, injection, auth), which only partially (crypto, insecure design, integrity, supply chain), and which it cannot test from outside (logging and alerting). For full coverage it wants both source and a running instance plus credentials at two privilege levels, and runs a deep scan. The report groups findings by category, labels the edition, and states what could not be assessed. It also points APIs to the api-security-testing skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/usestrix/strix/tree/06015506625a78b981ae0b34be40239fc1b389be/skills/owasp-top-10-testing"
