      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a white-box Strix security review of a codebase, reasoning about data flow and authorization, then exploiting findings in a sandbox with a PoC.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a security review of a repo, working tree or PR that reports proven issues, not scanner noise"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the source to model routes, sinks and authorization, then exploits what it finds in a live sandbox"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A short list of proven findings with file, line, PoC and remediation, plus SARIF"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike pattern-matching SAST tools",
              "text": "This one validates exploitability instead of listing potential hits"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A white-box Strix review: the agents read the source to build a model of routes, sinks and authorization checks, then attempt real exploitation, so the output is a short list of proven issues rather than hundreds of potential hits. It runs against a local tree, a GitHub repo, a monorepo subtree, or just a branch's diff, mounting local code writable in the sandbox. Two things sharply improve results: adding a running instance so findings are confirmed against live behavior (static-only findings are flagged unconfirmed), and scoping to the risky subtree while telling the agents the tenancy model and trust boundaries they cannot infer. Results come with file, line, PoC and SARIF. It is exploit-validated review, not an inventory, so keep a dependency scanner and secret scanning alongside it. Remediate with the fix skill; for PRs use the CI skill.",
