      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5e95aaebb84541742686e56352b7569acf4306f1",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies Feature-Sliced Design v2.1 to frontend code: where each file belongs, which layers to use, import boundaries and cross-import fixes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're structuring or reorganizing a frontend project with Feature-Sliced Design, or deciding where a piece of code should go"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Places code with a five-step decision tree, enforces the import and public-API rules, and resolves cross-imports between slices"
            },
            {
              "label": "Bias",
              "text": "Pages first: code moves down to features or entities only when it's reused now, changes for its own reasons and has a focused job"
            },
            {
              "label": "Layers",
              "text": "App, pages, widgets, features, entities and shared, highest to lowest; most projects can start with only app, pages and shared"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "Migrating from FSD v2.0 or a non-FSD codebase, Next.js, Nuxt, Astro and other frameworks, auth, API calls, Redux and TanStack Query"
            },
            {
              "label": "Official",
              "text": "The Feature-Sliced Design project's own skill, based on the v2.1 docs at fsd.how"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Feature-Sliced Design is a methodology for organizing frontend code into layers, which divide into slices by business area, which divide into segments by technical purpose: ui, model, api, lib and config. A module may import only from layers below it, and only through a slice's index.ts. The skill leans hard toward simplicity: duplication across pages is acceptable, the widgets layer is discouraged, plain CRUD is infrastructure rather than an entity, auth tokens live in shared, and files are named for their domain (user.ts) rather than their role (utils.ts). For cross-imports between entities it prefers merging their boundaries over the @x notation, a last resort, and for features it offers four strategies, from merging slices to composing them from the page. It recommends Steiger, the official FSD linter, and reads each of its nine reference files only when a task calls for it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/feature-sliced/skills/tree/5e95aaebb84541742686e56352b7569acf4306f1/feature-sliced-design"
        },
