        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a branch, pull request or uncommitted change for the interface problems it introduced or regressed, marking each finding Introduced, Regression or Pre-existing.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know whether a branch, pull request or uncommitted change made your interface worse"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds the change's scope, reviews the screens its files render in, reads removed lines for regressions and marks each finding Introduced, Regression or Pre-existing"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A read-only report: the scope, findings with their status, at most three pre-existing ones, and better-interface's coverage, verification and verdict"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike better-interface",
              "text": "That one reviews what exists; this one reports what a change caused and stays mostly quiet about what it merely touched"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "better-interface and the six better-* skills it loads; without better-interface it reports only the scope and file list"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it, as in /interface-review pr 482"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The change-review entry point to Jakub Krehel's better-* interface skills. With no target, it reviews the branch's commits past the merge base plus any uncommitted work, or the uncommitted work alone; with nothing to review, it asks instead of falling back to the last commit, offering the open pull request, the last commit, a target you name or a whole-repository audit. It drops lockfiles, snapshots, generated and vendored code, then expands changed files to the surfaces that render them: direct importers, a second hop for tokens, themes and shared primitives, at most five in all, saying how many it skipped. It reads the removed side of every hunk for lost signals such as a deleted aria-label or reduced-motion guard, and blames lines against the base to settle each finding's status. It also holds the change to its pull request and commit messages, looking for missing hover, loading or error states and untranslated strings. It never checks out or switches branches, reading pull request refs in place, and renders the change only when you ask or a cheap preview exists.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/interface-review"
        },
