      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds three deliberately different versions of a UI piece behind a picker on the real page, then keeps the one you choose.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing one piece of UI, such as a metric card, and want to compare real alternatives before committing"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks one axis, such as structure, density, emphasis, type or voice, and builds three variants at different positions on it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The variants on the real page behind a picker set by a ?__variant= URL parameter, and a table of when each is right and what it costs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Then",
              "text": "On your pick, it builds that variant properly and deletes the others, the picker and the page's imports of them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of Jakub Krehel's interface skills, it produces candidates rather than verdicts. It scopes the run to one piece of UI, then reads the project's styling system, component library, tokens, density and voice so each variant could ship tomorrow; with no project, it uses neutral grays, one accent and the system font stack, and says so. It names the set before writing code, with names such as Quiet, Editorial or Dense, never Option A: three variants, or up to five when asked. Every variant first clears the same floor, from accessible names and visible focus to 320px reflow and errors that name a way to recover; a direction that only works by breaking it is dropped. Variants render one at a time, full size, with real copy and the item count the page will carry. It flips through them once in a browser if one is at hand, otherwise hands you the URL. It never marks a favourite; asked directly, it answers from how often the piece is seen and the product's personality. Asked for another round, it keeps the picker and tries new positions around the one you leaned toward.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/variant"
        },
