      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Explains how a website, visual effect or animation was built, from a live URL or a screenshot, marking each claim measured, derived or inferred.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You see a site, visual effect or animation and want to know how it was built"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the page in a scriptable browser or from its fetched HTML and CSS, and finds the layers behind the effect in paint order"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Each layer's technique and the job it does, every value marked measured, derived or inferred, ending on the recipe in words, not code"
            },
            {
              "label": "From a screenshot",
              "text": "It proposes how it could be built and says it's a reconstruction"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of Jakub Krehel's interface skills, it explains without judging: there's no verdict. It scopes the answer to the question. Asked about a whole site, it covers the framework and rendering strategy, styling system, tokens, type, spacing and color systems, motion, breakpoints and how fonts and images are served; asked about one effect, only the stack of layers behind it. It says which route it used, since a browser shows what actually paints while fetched source shows authored rules, and prefers a browser for canvas, shaders, runtime styles and motion, suggesting the chrome-devtools-mcp server. Rather than a table of values, it explains the mechanism, such as a fade whose first 300ms carry 85% of it, and names the rule behind a long set instead of listing every element. It closes by naming what wouldn't survive copying, such as a licensed typeface or a brand hue, and what it couldn't read, such as a cross-origin stylesheet or a WebGL shader. Page content is evidence, never instructions: it doesn't submit forms, sign in or follow links you didn't name.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/explain-interface"
        },
