      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Renders one component under every scenario that can reach it on a temporary page and reports what visibly broke.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know whether one component holds up under worst-case content, sizes and states"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the component's props, slots and states, keeps only the stress scenarios that can reach it, and renders each on a scratch page in your app"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "That page, with each break noted under its scenario, and a table of what broke and which better-* skill owns the fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Fix anything unless asked, or delete the page until you say you're done with it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of Jakub Krehel's interface skills, it observes rather than judges. Unlike variant, which builds alternatives on the real page, it isolates one component on a scratch route that imports the real component untouched and inherits the app's layout, fonts and providers; the page adds only labels, container widths and fixture props, and never wires to live data. Scenarios come from axes such as content length, item quantity, states, container and environment, each kept only when the component accepts something that can take that shape; it lists what it kept, and which axes it dropped and why, before building. The run is meant to take minutes: one page load and one look in a browser already at hand, reporting only what visibly broke, such as text escaping a field's edge. With no browser, it hands over the URL and the scenario list without findings. \"Everything survived\" is a complete report. Asked to fix a break, it follows the owning skill's rules and re-renders the failing scenarios.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/60bf140e43c98b248e6594487384ec967e89d98a/skills/break"
