      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns your project's website or app into a 15–25 second launch video with music, sound effects and share copy, rendered with Hyperframes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've built something and want a short video to post about it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the project's code, plans an angle and a storyboard, then hands a composition brief to Hyperframes to build, check and render"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A brag-output/ folder with the plan, the brief, brag.mp4 with its best frame as the thumbnail, brag.jpg and share-copy.txt"
            },
            {
              "label": "Options",
              "text": "Seven tones from polished to yc-parody, or your own direction; landscape, vertical or square; no music or effects; opt-in voiceover"
            },
            {
              "label": "On Opus 5.5",
              "text": "It hands the run to its bundled brag-slim unless you ask for the full workflow or voiceover"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Hyperframes CLI and skills, and FFmpeg"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A launch-video director that leaves the animation to Hyperframes. It reads the project itself, not a live URL or screenshots, and must answer a nine-question planning rubric first. The plan commits to one creative angle and a beat-by-beat storyboard of scenes, text, timing, transitions and sound cues, timed to 15–25 seconds, and can line cuts up with the bundled music's cue points. Its creative laws: hook in the first two seconds, show real UI or copy from the product, no generic SaaS language, hold every line long enough to read, and humor only from the project's own absurdity; the usual shape is hook, reveal, two or three highlights and a punchline. Hyperframes owns the composition structure, exact timing and rendering, and npx hyperframes check must pass with zero errors; then it opens a preview and renders once you approve. Output goes to brag-output/, or to a timestamped folder when one already exists. Narration, with Kokoro through Hyperframes, is off unless you pass --voice.",
