      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds and verifies Nature/CNS-family literature supporting manuscript claims, maps each claim to a source and exports a reference-manager file.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need references from Nature, Science or Cell family journals to support specific claims in a manuscript"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Splits the text into segments, turns each claim into searches, grades how well each paper supports it, and exports one reference file"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Sources for each segment with a support grade and where to insert them, one RIS, EndNote or Zotero RDF file, optionally an HTML browser"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python and internet access for its bundled search script"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A citation finder restricted to one journal family: Nature Portfolio, CNS (Cell, Nature, Science and their sister journals) or flagship journals only, as you ask. It splits your text into citable segments, extracts each core claim and its type, and turns it into two to four English search queries, then searches with a bundled Python script against Crossref, and PubMed for biomedical topics, with optional year limits. It grades each candidate on a conservative scale, from strong to partial to background to contradictory, and never cites a metadata-only hit without checking the abstract or publisher page. Before writing the single reference-manager file it checks that every author list is complete, refetching by DOI or PMID rather than exporting surname-only entries, and it will not invent missing fields. Long texts are run in batches of about ten segments and merged, with an HTML browser to filter and pick candidates. Notes can be in Chinese while queries stay in English. For unrestricted sources use a broader literature search instead.",
