          "text": "A literature-search router covering six workflows: multi-source search across Crossref, PubMed and arXiv; verifying citations extracted from a document; building a MeSH (PubMed subject heading) search strategy; converting .nbib, .ris and .bib files; reference management such as BibTeX, related articles and ID conversion; and a strict citation-impact audit. That audit separates independent citations from self, team and close-collaborator ones, builds article-level citation tables, and identifies high-profile citers and how they cite the paper. It states which workflows it picked, loads only those, and ranks sources by reliability, such as PubMed first for clinical work, Crossref for cross-disciplinary and arXiv for preprints, with fallbacks. Its tools come from an academic-search MCP server that ships with it, with an install script; without the server, stdlib-only scripts search OpenAlex and export citations. Converting complete records you supply needs no network. Optional Semantic Scholar, NCBI and Elsevier keys raise rate limits or add Scopus and ScienceDirect, and keys stay in local config. Use nature-citation when you only want Nature or CNS-family supporting references.",
