      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Retrieves academic full text lawfully through open access, publisher APIs, CNKI and your authenticated institutional browser session.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need to batch-download papers you have lawful access to, Chinese ones through CNKI"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Routes each paper to a publisher API, open access, CNKI or your institution's logged-in Chrome session, and verifies each file"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "PDFs or native full text, optional supporting information, and a manifest of route, format, checksum and failures"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node.js 22+ and Python 3; institutional routes need your logged-in Chrome, the web-access skill and your library URL"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "Your cookies and passwords untouched, and hands secret-bearing checks such as OTP or QR login to you"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A download orchestrator that picks a lawful route per paper. Chinese literature always goes through CNKI. Elsevier, Springer Nature and IEEE articles try a publisher API with your configured key first, then legitimate open access (PMC, Unpaywall, arXiv); other English articles check open access first, then use Web Access, your institution's authorized access in your own browser. After an API and open access both fail it asks before switching to Web Access. It drives your already-authenticated Chrome through remote debugging, via the separate web-access skill's proxy, and never exports cookies, passwords or session files, nor bypasses paywalls, DRM or two-factor. It asks once whether you also want Supporting Information, confirms the paper list, and writes a manifest recording route, access mode, format, checksum and typed failure states. A batch script, which can also collect papers by Web of Science topic, keeps large pages and bytes out of the agent context. It tries slider puzzles and Turnstile or reCAPTCHA checkboxes, at most twice per tab, then hands off to you. API keys are stored locally with owner-only permissions and kept out of logs and manifests.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/yuan1z0825/nature-skills/tree/d774add241c65e89ed17a4da573b472b0972fd42/skills/nature-downloader"
        },
