      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets up BMad in a project and recommends what to run next, from the help of the BMad modules you have installed.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You start BMad in a project, want to update or repair it, or wonder which BMad skill to run next"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Creates and maintains the project's _bmad/ folder, and answers questions only from the installed modules' own help, never from memory"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A working BMad setup, an active initiative once you pick or create one, and a recommended next skill with the reason; it can run that skill for you"
            },
            {
              "label": "Speaks as",
              "text": "BMad, a direct, opinionated advisor who introduces itself as the BMad Agent"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv; updates run npx skills update"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The hub of the BMad Method. Every other BMad skill runs scripts from the project's _bmad/ folder and sends you here when it's missing. Setup creates that folder with uv and asks each installed module's configuration questions; on later runs the same flow checks each module's version, runs npx skills update, and offers to remove retired skills, duplicate copies and leftovers of a v6 install, always asking before it deletes anything. It can also migrate a project's documents to a newer version of a module, following a plan you approve first. Work is grouped into initiatives, a folder per body of work under the output folder, and the skill shows, switches, creates or clears the active one. Everything else is help: where the project stands, which installed skill comes next and why, or the trade-offs of an approach, all taken from the help files the installed modules ship. When one skill is the clear next step it suggests running it in a fresh context, offers to run it here; asked for a sequence of skills, it runs them one at a time, checking each result with you.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad"
