          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "multi-agent",
          "verification"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-brainstorming",
      "name": "bmad-brainstorming",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-brainstorming",
        "installs": 15800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [971, 1251, 2399, 2609, 1492, 1669, 1438, 1488],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a brainstorming session on any topic, rotating creative techniques to push past 100 ideas, then helps you narrow them down.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want far more and better ideas on a topic than you'd come up with alone"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs creative techniques one after another, logs every idea, and aims past 100 ideas before narrowing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Three stances",
              "text": "Facilitator only asks and never supplies ideas, Creative Partner trades ideas with you, Ideate for me runs the session by itself"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A log of every idea, plus your pick of an HTML keepsake of the session or a short intent document for planning skills"
            },
            {
              "label": "Picks techniques",
              "text": "On a local HTML page it tries to open in your browser, where you compose the session and paste a prompt back, or in chat"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first; subagents to write the keepsake and other artifacts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A creative coach for divergent thinking. You say what you're brainstorming and why, then choose a stance and a batch of techniques, usually on a local HTML page: you compose the session there and paste the generated prompt back. In chat, three or four techniques is the suggested batch, and the agent can also pick techniques or invent new ones. In dialogue it asks one question per message, offers no multiple-choice menus, and moves to a new technique every five to ten turns. Every idea, insight and decision goes into a memlog, a running file of one line per entry that lets a session resume after an interruption. When a batch is spent you can run another, converge to narrow and decide, or wrap up. Wrap-up reflects a sampling of your ideas back, asks what you see, then adds connections you might have missed. Artifacts are opt-in, except in Ideate for me, where the HTML keepsake comes automatically: the keepsake, a short intent document for planning skills such as bmad-agent-pm, or other formats such as a one-pager.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-brainstorming"
        },
