    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-architecture",
      "name": "bmad-architecture",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-architecture",
        "installs": 15800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [979, 1282, 2455, 2693, 1487, 1689, 1439, 1496],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-15"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Coaches you through the architecture decisions that keep separately built parts of a system consistent, and records them in a short document.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Separately built parts of a system, or several agents, must agree on the same design rules before they're built"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Interviews you by default, or drafts fast with [ASSUMPTION] tags for you to correct, logging every decision as it goes"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An architecture \"spine\": numbered decisions, each with what it binds, the divergence it prevents and its rule, plus diagrams and what it defers"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps minimal",
              "text": "Structural detail such as the stack or file tree, which the code owns once it exists"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also",
              "text": "Updates an existing spine, or validates one and reports in HTML"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first; subagents for its review gate"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The spine fixes only invariants: the design paradigm, boundary and dependency rules, how state changes, who owns shared data, the calls a later builder can't read off the code. Its test for each decision: could two units built independently choose incompatibly, and is the call non-obvious and a real trade-off? If not, it goes under Deferred. It starts from a ticket file, the initiative's or epic's spec, plus any existing architecture document or codebase, whose conventions it ratifies rather than reinvents, and an epic's spine inherits its parent's decisions as read-only. In the default coaching path it asks open questions and, for big calls such as paradigm, stack or starter, lays out the alternatives and lets you choose; for a new or beginner project it recommends a current, well-known starter, checked on the web. Decisions land in a memlog, an append-only log the spine is distilled from at the end, so a run can resume. Before handoff a lint script and parallel reviewer subagents check the spine, and it offers an extra piece for people, such as an HTML deck or a C4 set. Next it points you to Sofia, BMad's product owner, to adopt the spine on the ticket.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-architecture"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "planning",
          "interview"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-agent-pm",
      "name": "bmad-agent-pm",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-agent-pm",
        "installs": 15800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [966, 1238, 2370, 2602, 1437, 1674, 1431, 1486],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-17"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a867e1604b365d48ea03cbd109da1066ba0c84bc",
        "updated": "2026-11-08",
        "skew": 263
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns the agent into John, BMad's product manager, who coaches PRDs, briefs, business cases and other planning documents out of you.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need a product document written, updated or validated: a PRD, brief, business case, charter, pitch, SRS, BRD, experiment or launch plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works out where the document sits in BMad's ticket tree and which templates fit there, then hands the coaching to bmad-facilitate"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also offers",
              "text": "A prototype plan instead of a PRD for hobby projects or ideas to prove out, and a light PRD if you still want one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Persona",
              "text": "John, a relentless detective's \"why?\", who stays on until you dismiss him"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first, and the repo's bmad-facilitate skill, which writes every document"
            }
          ],
          "text": "John is BMad's product manager persona, and what he adds is placement. BMad keeps work in a ticket tree: an initiative ticket, epics below it, stories below those, with a program above several initiatives. John works out the altitude a document sits at, what it should state there, and which of his templates fit: a PRD at any level; for a program, a charter or narrative; for an initiative, an opportunity assessment, brief, business case, pitch, SRS or launch plan; an agent spec for a feature outside the tree; an experiment at any level. Documents for sponsors stay in prose and end with a section listing the stable ids that requirements will cite. The writing itself, create, update or validate, runs in bmad-facilitate, which he calls with his persona, the templates that fit and the next steps to name, usually Sofia, the product owner skill who turns a document into the initiative's requirements, then bmad-ux and bmad-architecture. A first message that belongs elsewhere is redirected, such as an express build to bmad-build or a PRFAQ to bmad-prfaq. With no person present he runs headless and asks nothing.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-agent-pm"
        },
        "tags": [
