          "design-system",
          "accessibility",
          "requirements"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-review",
      "name": "bmad-review",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-review",
        "installs": 15300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [954, 1227, 2369, 2618, 1449, 1661, 1413, 1475],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a diff, PR or document through several lenses, from adversarial critique to edge cases, test gaps, structure and prose, and reports the findings.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You ask for a review of code or a document and actually say \"review\"; it never starts on its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs every review pass, or lens, that fits the content, each with its own method and stance, in parallel subagents when it can"
            },
            {
              "label": "Lenses",
              "text": "Adversarial (at least ten findings), edge-case hunter, verification gap for untested code, and editorial structure and prose for documents"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One list of findings, each with location, problem, fix and consequence, in chat as JSON and Markdown, with no severity ranking"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike bmad-code-review",
              "text": "This one only reports: it doesn't rank the findings or fix anything"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A general review skill that other BMad skills also call, for example to polish documents. It stages the content once as a file: a branch, uncommitted work or a commit range becomes a unified diff in the temp folder, and any description of the change, such as its commit messages, is staged separately for the edge-case lens alone. It classifies the content as code or docs and runs every lens that applies, or exactly the ones you name. Each lens has its own stance: the adversarial lens must find at least ten concrete problems, most others may come back empty, and the editorial lenses keep the content as it is and critique only how it's organized and expressed, against the Microsoft Writing Style Guide by default. Lenses never see each other's findings, except the prose lens, which runs after the structure lens, on its findings. Every finding names where it is, what triggers the problem, the fix or guard, and what goes wrong if it ships. Teams can add or replace lenses, and set a file to write the report to.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-review"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "docs-writing",
          "writing"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-advanced-elicitation",
      "name": "bmad-advanced-elicitation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-advanced-elicitation",
        "installs": 15300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [946, 1210, 2324, 2516, 1435, 1615, 1386, 1466],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Pushes a draft past its first version: cold-reader subagents critique it with chosen methods, and you accept or reject one merged revision.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A draft, plan or choice needs pushing past the agent's first, most predictable answer, and you or a skill name advanced elicitation"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Briefs fresh subagents, each with one critique method and none of the conversation, then merges their findings into one proposed revision"
            },
            {
              "label": "Picks methods",
              "text": "The ones you name, your team's defaults, or three pushes from different directions, one of them a wildcard"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A numbered list of changes with before and after; nothing changes until you apply all, some or none"
            },
            {
              "label": "Then",
              "text": "Another round on the accepted version, in a direction not yet pushed, or done"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first; subagents, or it runs at most two pushes itself"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Advanced elicitation treats a first draft as the most likely draft: centered, agreeable with its own prompt, biased toward adding. The target is whatever you or a calling skill point at, at its own size, or else the last answer in the conversation. Each push gets a brief with only the target, its purpose, what's already settled so it isn't reopened, the method and the shape of its answer, so the author's reasoning can't anchor it. Methods come from a catalog; one with several voices, such as an expert panel, gets one subagent per voice, and a voice can take an installed BMad agent's persona. For a single choice or a few lines, one push is the agent's own: an open challenge with no method. It then composes one revision, sets a finding aside only on a stated ground, and shows a finding that rejects the target's premise as a decision for you. Each round goes into a report in the session folder. Applied changes edit a file target in place; a target that exists only in chat is staged to target.md. Methods can be named in the call or set as team defaults with bmad-customize.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-advanced-elicitation"
        },
