        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Adds end-to-end and API tests for a feature that already works, following your project's existing test setup, and reports bugs they find.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A working feature has no end-to-end or API test coverage yet"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Lists the user-visible scenarios for you to confirm, learns the project's test harness, writes one test per scenario and runs them twice"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "New tests in the project's own runner and layout, and a chat report of what's covered, what isn't and why, bugs found and the command to run them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "A test that finds a bug, marked as an expected failure, rather than weakening it; flaky tests get their cause fixed, not retries"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Unit tests on work in progress, which bmad-build writes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "BMad's QA skill adds tests for behavior that already works, so later changes can be checked against it. It finds the feature from your request, a ticket in review in BMad's ticket tree or the current branch, or asks, offering to list the features with no end-to-end coverage by how much a regression would hurt. It writes each scenario as one sentence a product person would recognize, marks those already covered, and waits for your confirmation. It then reads how the project already tests: the runner, where tests live, how the app starts, test data, authentication and the CI job, and runs the existing suite once; with no harness at all, it proposes one and asks before adding any dependency. Each test goes through the UI by role, label or text, or through the public API, asserts the visible outcome, waits on conditions rather than time, creates its own data and fails for one reason. Product code changes only where the harness needs it, such as a test id, and each change is listed in the report.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-qa-generate-e2e-tests"
        },
