    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-build-auto",
      "name": "bmad-build-auto",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-build-auto",
        "installs": 15200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [950, 1206, 2318, 2561, 1503, 1659, 1442, 1494],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-30"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Implements one story, feature or bug fix without asking anything: plans, codes, reviews with subagents, commits locally and reports a final status.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An orchestrator or script hands the agent one ticket to build with nobody watching"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "bmad-build's plan, implement, review and commit steps, but never asks: where bmad-build would stop for you, it halts with a status"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A local commit and the plan file marked built, or marked blocked with the reason, such as an intent gap or a dirty tree"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when invoked by name"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first; subagents, which it must use, or it halts as blocked"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The unattended twin of bmad-build, called by name, usually by an orchestrator. It takes one ticket from BMad's ticket tree, a plan file or a description, and never moves on to another. It follows the same path: check that the working tree is clean, investigate, write a plan file with acceptance criteria and verification commands, implement in the session for a change of about 100 lines or fewer or through a fresh subagent for a larger one, run the verification commands, have a reviewer subagent read the diff (four for a thorough review), verify each finding, patch what this change caused, defer older problems in the plan and commit locally without pushing. The invocation can pin the route or the review level, including no review, and can ask it to halt after planning. Wherever a person would be asked, it stops instead: an intent gap, a plan that fails its readiness standard, failing verification or a dirty tree each end the run as blocked, with the reason written to the plan file. A built or done plan handed back to it gets a fresh review pass.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-build-auto"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "code-review",
          "agent-workflows",
          "multi-agent"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv, BMad set up in the project and subagents; it halts as blocked without them.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-project-context",
      "name": "bmad-project-context",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-project-context",
        "installs": 15200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [958, 1225, 2372, 2590, 1453, 1642, 1402, 1461],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-03"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes or refreshes a small, verified block of agent instructions in your repo's AGENTS.md, and records agent mistakes as pitfalls.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You set up a repo's agent instructions, take over an existing AGENTS.md, or an agent keeps making the same mistake"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scans the repo with subagents, verifies every command and path it will state, and asks you only what no scan can answer"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A managed block inside AGENTS.md with a date and commit stamp, shown to you in full before it's written; it never commits"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "Every existing instruction accounted for in a ledger: kept, rewritten, moved, automated or deleted, each with its reason"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "setup, adopt an existing file, refresh, record a mistake, audit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when invoked by name"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv, and subagents for the scan; it also works in a project without BMad's setup"
            }
          ],
          "text": "BMad's skill for AGENTS.md keeps agent instructions small and true. It reads the existing instruction files, docs and notes, and opens a ledger entry for every existing instruction, so nothing is silently lost. It asks for rules the repo can't show, such as governance, security, standards or frozen areas, and for outside documents. Parallel subagents then check CI, config, source and history; it path-checks every file it names, reads the script behind every command it describes, and leaves out what package.json, a Makefile or CI already states. The interview covers only what scans can't reach: what agents get wrong, what's off limits, why a constraint exists, in batches of at most eight questions. For each candidate rule it first asks whether a hook, lint rule or CI check would enforce it better. You approve the block and the ledger together, and weakly grounded deletions need approval line by line. Record captures one observed agent mistake and adds a line once it recurs or proves costly. Refresh re-checks against git history since the stamped commit; an audit only ever ends smaller or equal.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-project-context"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "docs-writing",
          "agent-workflows",
          "self-improvement"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-party-mode",
      "name": "bmad-party-mode",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-party-mode",
        "installs": 15200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [941, 1193, 2302, 2511, 1416, 1584, 1368, 1454],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-12"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a round-table where BMad's agent personas, or personas you define, argue with each other and with you until you call it done.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want several perspectives on a topic at once, argued out like a group chat rather than filed as reports"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Voices the installed BMad agents, a saved group, or a cast you name inline, and pulls you into the conversation"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "The clash: voices push back, take sides and aren't reconciled into a tidy consensus"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "One mind voicing every persona by default; real subagents every round or only when independent thinking matters; or a persistent agent team in Claude Code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An open-ended discussion; at the end, the best takeaways and an optional HTML keepsake; the default room remembers past sessions, a saved group only if set to"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also",
              "text": "Creates custom parties, such as a focus-group panel distilled from customer data"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent becomes the orchestrator of a round-table. A script supplies the roster: the default party if one is set, otherwise the installed BMad agents. A cast you name inline takes over for the session, and you can switch groups or summon any member by name midway. Agents whose skills aren't installed are still voiced from their persona. The bar is a group chat, not a panel: short turns, distinct and unequal voices, disagreement left unresolved, characters who talk to you and build running jokes and grudges, staging played as written, and no summaries unless you ask. It runs round after round until you signal you're done; only an explicit --non-interactive run closes by itself. Then it reads back the best takeaways, tops up the party's memory when it keeps one, offers a self-contained HTML keepsake laid out by persona, and offers to save walk-on characters to your party settings. The built-in members include code reviewers such as a security engineer, an adversary and an edge-case hunter.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-party-mode"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:mode",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "memory"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-deep-recon",
      "name": "bmad-deep-recon",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-deep-recon",
        "installs": 15100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [940, 1198, 2316, 2527, 1450, 1638, 1410, 1457],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Researches the question behind a decision, on the web or in your codebase, and writes a report where every claim cites a source.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A decision depends on research: entering a market, picking a stack, comparing competitors, reading literature, or why your code is built a certain way"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs parallel web-research subagents, drafts a prompt for your own deep-research tool, or processes a report you already have"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report that leads with the decision, with findings, contrary evidence, recommendations, a source appendix and when each claim should be re-checked, plus an HTML briefing page"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "Conclusions to evidence found during the run: never training data alone, and never your project's own documents"
            },
            {
              "label": "Research types",
              "text": "Market, domain, technical, competitive, user voice and academic literature; teams can add their own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first; web access to run research here, or it offers Draft and Process instead"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Deep Recon acts as a research director: every run serves a decision and is shaped by it. You choose how the research gets done. Run does it here through parallel web searches; Draft writes a prompt for a deep-research tool you subscribe to, often cheaper; Process turns that tool's report into the same cited summary; and a codebase mode answers questions about your repository from its code and git history. Two rules bind every subagent: nothing is concluded from training data alone, and project context such as tickets, briefs or code shapes what to ask but never counts as evidence. Each claim carries its publisher, publication date and access date, and thin data is reported as thin. Every digest and extract is written to the run folder as it lands, so a run can resume, and a memlog records each decision and claim. Before it finishes, a script checks every citation marker against the appendix and a fresh subagent checks that each source says what the text claims. A select mode compares candidates, and Refresh or Deepen update a finished run.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-deep-recon"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "category:browser",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "research",
          "web-search"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-forge-idea",
      "name": "bmad-forge-idea",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-forge-idea",
        "installs": 15000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [937, 1194, 2299, 2502, 1433, 1625, 1395, 1460],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-22"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a867e1604b365d48ea03cbd109da1066ba0c84bc",
        "updated": "2026-11-08",
        "skew": 2
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Pressure-tests a half-formed idea, one question at a time and with personas probing weak spots, until you act on it or drop it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have an idea, product or not, and want its weak points found while changing your mind is still cheap"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks one question at a time, checks your claims against an existing project's files, and lets you say \"attack this\", \"defend this\" or \"switch roles\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Voices",
              "text": "Each turn pairs an installed BMad agent or your own persona with a freshly invented outsider, such as a competitor or finance reviewer"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An HTML report stamped Hardened, Killed (an \"Idea Death Certificate\") or Clarified, and for a hardened idea a very short file for planning skills"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike bmad-brainstorming",
              "text": "That one generates many ideas; this one tests one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The goal is better thinking, not a document: making an idea stronger, killing it, or understanding it better are all complete outcomes, and it doesn't steer toward building. It first confirms the idea, what you want from the session (clarify it, test it or improve it) and whether it changes an existing project, whose files it treats as the source of truth over your summary. It works one question at a time in dependency order, offers its own best guess when that helps you answer, and names fuzzy terms, such as user, buyer and payer, until you pick one. Praise is treated as noise. Decisions, assumptions, cracks, kills and locked ideas each go into a memlog, an append-only log file, so the session can resume. If a BMad persona was already active, it leads in its own voice. The forged idea file, written only for a hardened idea, holds just the decisions, rejected options and reasons that matter downstream, and can go to Sofia, BMad's product owner skill, as input for requirements.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-forge-idea"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "interview",
          "planning",
          "requirements"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv and BMad set up in the project: it runs scripts from the _bmad/ folder that the bmad skill's setup creates.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bmad-code-org/bmad-method/skills/bmad-customize",
      "name": "bmad-customize",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/bmad-customize",
        "installs": 15000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [926, 1177, 2264, 2482, 1425, 1579, 1402, 1486],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-22"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a867e1604b365d48ea03cbd109da1066ba0c84bc",
        "updated": "2026-11-08",
        "skew": 538
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes TOML overrides that change installed BMad agents and workflows, such as a persona, template or extra steps, for your team or yourself.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a BMad agent or workflow to behave differently: a new standing fact, a swapped template, a different output path"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Lists what's customizable, picks the right layer (one workflow, or every workflow an agent runs) and drafts only the fields that change"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A TOML file in _bmad/custom/, either a committed team file or a gitignored personal one, shown and confirmed before it's written"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "Runs BMad's resolver afterwards so you see the merged result"
            },
            {
              "label": "Can't change",
              "text": "Step logic or central config; it says so and suggests a feature request or bmad-toolsmith"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "BMad skills read their defaults from a customize.toml, merged with a team file and a personal file under _bmad/custom/; this skill writes those overrides. A specific change to a named skill goes straight to drafting, while a vague request starts from a scripted list of customizable agents and workflows, showing which already have overrides. For a change that could live in several places, it walks you through the choice: an agent override applies to every workflow that agent runs, a workflow override to one. Overrides stay sparse, holding only the changed fields, and follow BMad's merge rules: single values replace, lists append, and menu items with a code or id replace their match. For a template swap it offers to copy the default template for you to edit. It shows the full file, or a diff when one exists, writes only after a yes, then verifies by running the resolver and reworks the override if it didn't land.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-customize"
        },
