      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a branch's changes for maintainability against a very high bar, pushing for restructurings that delete complexity instead of moving it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a harsh review of a branch's code quality, not its bugs: abstractions, sprawling files, ad-hoc conditionals, needless wrappers"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Audits the current branch's changes against eight standing rules, hunting for \"code judo\": restructurings that keep behavior but make whole branches, helpers or layers disappear"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A few high-conviction findings, structural regressions first and no flood of cosmetic nits, in a direct tone, with no approval just because the code works"
            },
            {
              "label": "Blocks on",
              "text": "A file pushed past 1,000 lines, special-case branches scattered into shared code, or a clear simplification left on the table, unless the author justifies it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it; the agent never starts it on its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Part of",
              "text": "Cursor's Thermos plugin, alongside a bug and security review and a thermos skill that runs both reviews as parallel subagents"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A review prompt from Cursor's plugins repo with one goal: keep the codebase simple. Called by name, the agent audits the current branch's changes and asks of each one whether a \"code judo\" move, a restructuring that preserves behavior but makes branches, helpers, modes or layers disappear, would make it dramatically simpler. Eight standing rules back that up: be ambitious about structural simplification; don't let a PR push a file from under to over 1,000 lines without a strong reason; no ad-hoc conditionals in unrelated flows; push for the cleaner design, not just working code; prefer direct, boring code over magic or thin wrappers; question casts, any, unknown and needless optionality; keep logic in its canonical layer and reuse existing helpers; and treat needlessly sequential or half-applied updates as design smells. Findings come ordered from structural regressions down to legibility, few and high-conviction rather than a flood of nits, in a direct but not rude tone, with sample phrasings. Working code isn't enough to approve: the listed problems are presumptive blockers unless the author justifies them.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/2776ddd2355d6d6c8747d3964600f28daf62f049/thermos/skills/thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review"
