      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies motion design principles, from emotional intent to timing, easing and choreography, to UI animations in any animation system.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're making UI animations, transitions, micro-interactions or loading states and want them to feel intentional rather than default"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs an 8-step checklist before any animation: emotional target, one of four motion personalities, property, duration, easing, hero element, supporting layers and its 1/3 rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Animations whose durations, curves and staggers come from its tables, such as 120–180ms for a button press and entrances 30–50% longer than exits"
            },
            {
              "label": "Never",
              "text": "Linear easing for movement, opacity alone for an important state change, or motion across more than a third of the screen without a keyframe"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "CSS, Framer Motion, GSAP, Lottie, springs or any other animation system"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike emil-design-eng",
              "text": "That one asks whether to animate and how fast; this one starts from the feeling an animation should give, with personalities, an emotion map and Disney's animation principles"
            }
          ],
          "text": "LottieFiles' guide has the agent think like a motion director before writing animation code. Every animation must answer three questions first: what the viewer should feel, what micro-story it tells, and what makes it believable. The project gets one of four personalities, Playful, Premium, Corporate or Energetic, each with its own durations, easing and overshoot; Corporate is the default for UI, Playful for illustrations. The rest is tables: which property to animate for each effect, durations by element from 80–120ms tooltips to 400–600ms page transitions, easing by direction (ease-out to enter, ease-in to exit) and by material, weights, stagger budgets that keep a sequence under 500ms, and an emotion-to-motion map. Each animation should have three layers, a primary action, secondary detail and ambient life, and follow 1/3 rules on how far things travel and how many move at once. Recipes cover a button press, a card entrance, a success state and an error shake, and a troubleshooting table maps symptoms such as \"looks robotic\" to fixes. Deeper files on Disney's principles, choreography and patterns load when needed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/lottiefiles/motion-design-skill/tree/f9a8a041b85185ee4881b3471d3415e939aac772/skills/motion-design"
