      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e94f22214528d69899d09c627b3315ddc3544816",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews code for exploitable security vulnerabilities, reporting only high-confidence findings after tracing where each input really comes from.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a security review of a file, diff or component: injection, XSS, authentication, authorization, cryptography, SSRF and the like"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Loads reference guides for the kind of code and its language, researches the whole codebase to trace each input, and reports only what it confirms is exploitable"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report of findings rated Critical to Low, each with location, impact, evidence and fix, plus a separate Needs verification list"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't flag",
              "text": "Test files, dead code, constants, server-side settings, code reachable only after authentication, or framework patterns that already escape or parameterize, unless they're bypassed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Language guides",
              "text": "Python and JavaScript; other languages rely on its general references and the framework's protections checked in the code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Sentry's security review hunts only for vulnerabilities an attacker could actually exploit. It reports on the file, diff or code you give it, but researches the whole codebase first: where each input really comes from, whether it's validated elsewhere, and what configuration and the framework already protect. Values from settings, environment variables or constants count as server-controlled, and auto-escaped templates or ORM queries are flagged only when bypassed, as with mark_safe, dangerouslySetInnerHTML, v-html or raw SQL. For each kind of code, such as endpoints, templates, file uploads, crypto, serialization or CI, it loads matching reference guides based on OWASP's cheat sheets, plus a Python or JavaScript guide and, for Dockerfiles, a Docker guide. Only high-confidence findings are reported; medium-confidence ones go under Needs verification, and theoretical or best-practice issues are left out. Some patterns are always flagged, such as eval or pickle on user input, SQL built from strings and hardcoded secrets. If nothing qualifies, it says \"No high-confidence vulnerabilities identified.\" For Django access control and GitHub Actions workflows, it uses the same repo's django-access-review and gha-security-review when they're installed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/getsentry/skills/tree/e94f22214528d69899d09c627b3315ddc3544816/skills/security-review"
        },
