              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A DESIGN.md in a fixed seven-section format, with named hex colors and exact values, for Stitch to follow when it generates screens"
            },
            {
              "label": "Bans",
              "text": "Inter, generic serifs, pure black, purple neon glows, three equal cards in a row, emojis, invented metrics and copy like \"Elevate\" or \"Seamless\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Access to Google Stitch to use the file; the Stitch MCP server is optional"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A skill from Google Labs' Stitch repo, which turns anti-slop frontend rules into the DESIGN.md that Stitch, Google's tool for generating UI screens from text, reads as its design system. The agent first sets the project's density, variance and motion on 1–10 scales, defaulting to creativity 9, variance 8, motion 6 and density 5 and adjusting to the vibe you describe. It then writes seven sections: atmosphere, a palette of named hex colors with one accent under 80% saturation, typography, component behavior, layout, motion and banned patterns. The rules are strict: Geist, Outfit, Cabinet Grotesk or Satoshi instead of Inter, no serifs in dashboards, no centered hero when variance is high, no three equal cards in a row, skeleton loaders instead of spinners, spring physics and perpetual micro-animations, and a single column below 768px. It also bans emojis, invented statistics, fake names such as John Doe, and copy such as \"Elevate\" or \"Seamless\". Everything is written in plain descriptive language with exact values in parentheses, the way Stitch interprets design.",
