      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scans React code with the React Doctor CLI for security, performance, correctness and architecture issues, scores it, and fixes what regressed.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've changed React code and want to catch regressions before committing, or want a cleanup pass, a UI design audit or a runtime performance trace"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs npx react-doctor scans scoped to your changes or the whole codebase and fixes issues by severity, errors first"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A 0–100 health score, findings by rule with file and line, and fixes in your working tree; it never commits or opens PRs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Traces",
              "text": "Slow interactions in an isolated Chrome profile while you reproduce them, highlighting components as they render"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "After React changes on its own, or as /doctor for a full triage"
            }
          ],
          "text": "React Doctor is a CLI that scans React codebases for security, performance, correctness and architecture issues and scores them from 0 to 100. The skill tells the agent which scan fits. After React code changes, it scans only what changed against the base branch and fixes any drop in the score before committing. For a cleanup, it scans the whole codebase and fixes errors before warnings. A design subcommand runs only the UI rules for composition, typography, interaction, accessibility and motion. For slow interactions, scan with a URL opens an isolated Chrome profile, records a DevTools trace while you reproduce the problem and outlines components as they render; the trace stays on your machine unless you allow an upload. Typing /doctor fetches the maintainers' current triage playbook from react.doctor and follows it: scan, filter, triage, fix and validate, editing the working tree but never committing or opening PRs. It can also explain a rule and disable or tune it in your config, report only findings that are new against a saved baseline scan, and give GitLab CI a separate exit code for warnings.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/millionco/react-doctor/tree/9791c32f61fb1477a4f8dd596ff80184ad26b2e3/skills/react-doctor"
