      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Searches code by its structure instead of its text, turning a plain-language request into an ast-grep rule it tests before running.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Text search can't express what you're looking for, such as async functions without a try-catch or console.log calls inside class methods"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes a small example of the code to match, drafts an ast-grep pattern or YAML rule, tests it on the example, then searches the codebase"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The matches, as text or as JSON with file and line, and the rule that found them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Debugs",
              "text": "A rule that matches nothing by simplifying it, adding stopBy: end and dumping the syntax tree to check node kinds"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The ast-grep CLI"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ast-grep searches code by its syntax tree rather than its text, so it finds structures plain search can't, such as functions that await but have no try-catch. This skill has the agent translate your request into an ast-grep rule step by step. It asks about the pattern, language and edge cases when they're unclear, saves a small example of the code to match to a temporary file, and drafts a rule: a simple pattern first, then node kinds, relational rules such as has or inside, and all, any or not only as needed. It tests the rule on the example, inline or from a YAML file. When nothing matches, it simplifies the rule, adds stopBy: end to relational rules, and uses --debug-query to see how the code and the pattern were parsed. Only then does it search the codebase, with run for single-node patterns or scan for full rules, optionally as JSON that it can filter with jq. A reference file covers the full rule syntax, and ast-grep's online docs are the last resort.",
