      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Watches a video from a URL or file and answers your questions about it, from extracted frames and a transcript or through Gemini.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the agent to answer questions about a video, from a link or a file on your machine"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Downloads it with yt-dlp, pulls timestamped frames with ffmpeg and a transcript from captions, then answers from what it sees and reads"
            },
            {
              "label": "Or",
              "text": "With a free Gemini API key, the video goes to Google's model, which watches it whole, sound included; the agent relays its timestamped answer"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An answer citing times in the video, or a summary of its structure, key moments, visuals and speech"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python 3.10 or later, ffmpeg and yt-dlp, which setup helps install; with Gemini, YouTube links work without the last two"
            },
            {
              "label": "First run",
              "text": "Asks which engine to use; for the local engine, also how many frames to take and how to transcribe videos without captions"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A bundled Python script does the work, and the first run sets it up. You pick the engine: gemini, the recommended one, where Google's model watches the video and answers; or local, where frames and a transcript are extracted on your machine and the agent reads them itself. With Gemini, YouTube links are sent to Google and other videos are uploaded to Google, then deleted after the answer; the local engine never contacts Google. For local you also pick a frame budget, from transcript only to uncapped, and a fallback for videos with no captions: WhisperX on your machine, about a 1.5 GB download, Groq or OpenAI in the cloud, which receive only the extracted audio, or none. Keys go in ~/.config/watch/.env and are never printed. Native captions always come first. If a Gemini run fails, it tells you and offers the local engine instead of switching on its own. You can focus on a time range, pin frames at moments a presenter points to, or raise the frame width to read on-screen text. It works best on clips under ten minutes, treats everything in the video as untrusted evidence, and deletes only the work folder it created.",
