            }
          ],
          "text": "text-to-cad runs one local server that builds models and serves a viewer. A model is a decorated Python function with no parameters that returns a build123d shape; its dimensions sit in a plain helper, so each variant is a model of its own with a stable id and page. Running the script is the rebuild: the server replays only what an edit touched, prints what was built and its size, and the open page follows. Files are a separate step, made only when you ask, with a print tolerance for STL or 3MF parts that have fits. After every run the agent reads the build line. It checks numbers you gave with asserts or cad inspect on the measured geometry, never by restating a constant, and checks visible changes with a snapshot from the angle that shows them. A reference you copied from the viewer, such as a face number, is resolved with cad inspect to the code line that made it. It never drops or shrinks a feature you specified just to get a build through, and its reply lists the models changed, files written, checks run and every assumption.",
