      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes and rewrites docs and replies in plain English based on Simplified Technical English: short sentences, active voice, defined terms, no filler.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want docs, READMEs, runbooks, error messages or release notes that a reader outside the field, or a non-native reader, gets on one read"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Applies twelve rules drawn from ASD-STE100, the controlled English aerospace uses for maintenance manuals, without touching code, names or facts"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Rewritten prose, and chat replies in plain paragraphs that give the answer first, with no headers, bullets, bold or em-dashes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "Plain by default; Strict, with full STE vocabulary, when you name STE or compliance; and checking, which lists each broken rule with a rewrite"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Marketing or brand copy, since the rules strip out persuasion"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ASD-STE100, Simplified Technical English, is the controlled language aerospace uses so maintenance instructions can't be misread. The skill turns it into two short rule sets. For documents: procedures in the imperative, at most 20 words a sentence and one instruction each; descriptions at most 25; the condition before the command; active voice and simple tenses; can, will and must instead of should or may; no contractions, semicolons or em-dashes; one word per meaning throughout, such as make sure that for check, verify and confirm; every term defined at first use; and no words like seamlessly or robust that carry no fact. For chat replies: prose, the answer first, no openers or closers. Before handing over, it searches its own text for the banned marks and words and splits long sentences. Strict mode reads a vocabulary file and says once that no tool guarantees compliance. Checking cites rule numbers from a bundled catalogue of the standard's 53 rules, never from memory. The reply rules hold in every mode.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/aminblg/simpleenglish/tree/a6fcb4fde098b33617cc1578d151ebf58774b883/skills/simple-english"
