      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits an iOS app's code and configuration for App Store rejection risks and returns a prioritized report, with draft notes for Apple's reviewers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to submit an iOS app and want to know what could get it rejected"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads Info.plist, entitlements, the privacy manifest, StoreKit code and login, paywall and content flows, and checks them against App Store Review Guidelines topics"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A summary, a risk table from P0 blocker to P3, findings with test steps, a reviewer-experience checklist and draft App Review Notes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Changes",
              "text": "No code on the first pass; afterwards it offers patches, wording for permission prompts and paywalls, or a pre-submission checklist"
            },
            {
              "label": "Evidence",
              "text": "Each finding cites a file, symbol, setting or screen; a check that needs something outside the repo is marked Unverified"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent plays an App Store reviewer looking at your repository. It first works out the app's purpose, its top three user flows and what a user needs to get in, such as an account, permissions or a purchase. Then it flags the usual rejection causes: missing or vague permission usage strings, undisclosed data collection or tracking, in-app purchases without restore or with misleading pricing, login walls with no explanation or demo path, third-party sign-in without Sign in with Apple, unsubstantiated medical, financial or safety claims, and incomplete screens. Some checks run only when they apply: rules for user-generated content, such as terms accepted before sign-in, acting on reports within 24 hours and blocking that hides the blocked user's content at once; pricing words like \"Free\" in metadata or screenshots; copycat and spam apps; and Live Activities used for messaging. It verifies current guideline wording online when it can, keeps assumptions apart from verified checks and invents no features. The draft App Review Notes are ready to paste into App Store Connect, with placeholders for test credentials.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/apple-appstore-reviewer"
