              "text": "The updated Markdown plan, with front matter, a status badge and REQ-, TASK- and other prefixed IDs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike create-implementation-plan",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, writes a new plan and runs grep checks for duplicate IDs; this one revises an existing one without those checks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The companion to create-implementation-plan: the same template and rules, applied to a plan that already exists. You give it the plan file and the new or updated requirements. The plan is written for machines first: deterministic language, tables and lists that parse cleanly, and tasks that name exact file paths, functions and line numbers so none needs a human decision. Phases are atomic, each with a GOAL- and measurable completion criteria; tasks within a phase can run in parallel unless a dependency is stated, and each task row records whether it's done and when. Every section of the template is required, headers must match exactly and no placeholder text may remain. The front matter's status, also shown as a badge in the introduction, is Completed, In progress, Planned, Deprecated or On Hold. Plans live in /plan/, named by purpose, component and version, such as upgrade-system-command-4.md.",
