        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Cuts one method's cognitive complexity to the threshold you set by extracting helper methods, then checks the tests report zero failures.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A method is flagged as too complex and you want it split up without changing what it does"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds nesting, long if-else chains, repeated blocks and complex conditions, extracts validation and case-specific logic into helpers, and flattens the main method"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The method rewritten as a high-level flow of small helpers, with inputs, outputs, exceptions and error messages unchanged"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "Runs the related tests and reads the output for failed=0 instead of assuming a pass, fixing and rerunning until it gets there"
            },
            {
              "label": "Takes",
              "text": "The method's name and the target complexity threshold"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Cognitive complexity is a score of how hard code is to follow; the skill lists its sources as nested conditionals, long if-else or switch chains, repeated blocks, loops with conditions and complex boolean expressions. The agent extracts validation into Validate* methods, type- or case-specific processing into handler methods and repeated operations into utility methods, each with one responsibility, made static when it needs no instance state and kept close to where it's used. The main method then reads as a high-level flow, with guard clauses early, less nesting and switch statements where they dispatch more cleanly. Behavior must not change, including validation, exception types and error messages. Afterwards the agent must run the related tests and read the summary line itself: any count other than failed=0 is a failure, which it states, analyzes, fixes and reruns. It finishes with a checklist: the code compiles, the tests show failed=0, complexity is at or below the target, and the project's conventions are kept. It names no tool for measuring complexity.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/refactor-method-complexity-reduce"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "refactoring",
          "code-quality",
          "verification"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
