      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reads your uncommitted changes, decides whether they belong on a Git Flow feature, release or hotfix branch, then creates and switches to it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've started changes and want them on a properly named branch under the Git Flow model"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads git status and the diff, classifies the work as a feature, release or hotfix, and runs git checkout -b from develop or master"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A new branch such as feature/user-registration-api, release-2.1.0 or hotfix/auth-security-patch, with its reasoning, alternative names and next steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike git-commit",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, commits your changes; this one only creates the branch they go on"
            },
            {
              "label": "Assumes",
              "text": "The nvie Git Flow model, with long-lived develop and master branches"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Git Flow keeps two long-lived branches, master for production and develop for integration, and branches features from develop while releases and hotfixes merge back into both. The skill maps your changes onto it with a short decision tree: critical production fixes, such as security holes, outages or data corruption, get a hotfix branch from master; version bumps, release notes and other release preparation get a release branch from develop; everything else defaults to a feature branch from develop. Names are lowercase with hyphens, include a ticket number when there is one, and get a suffix or an alternative if the name is taken. For mixed changes it picks the most significant type or suggests splitting them, and with no changes it tells you so. It checks that the source branch exists, creates the new branch and switches to it, then reports its analysis with two or three alternative names in case you disagree.",
