      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a coding standards document from the existing style of files or a folder you name, flagging or fixing inconsistencies it finds.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A project has a de facto code style but no written standard, and you want one drawn from real files"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Analyzes indentation, naming, comments, nesting and quoting in the files you name, fetches the language's public style guide, and fills a verbose or minimal template"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A new standards file, named with the first free name from a list that starts CONTRIBUTING.md, STYLE.md; or a README section or chat output if you ask"
            },
            {
              "label": "Watch out",
              "text": "Given one file, it also edits it to match the majority style by default; with several files or a folder it only lists inconsistencies"
            },
            {
              "label": "Options",
              "text": "Settings you name in your request, such as addToREADME, useTemplate=minimal or addStandardsTest, override its defaults"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Written as a configurable prompt: a list of settings with defaults, which you override by naming them in your request. You pass a file name, or a folder whose files it treats as one sample, and optional extra instructions. The agent sorts the code's indentation, line breaks, comments, conditional and function nesting and string quotes into categories, counts each variant, and treats whatever disagrees with the majority as an inconsistency. By default it also fetches a published style guide for the language from a built-in list of 32, such as PEP 8 for Python or Google's shell guide, as extra context. The standards follow a verbose template, with a naming table, formatting, comments, error handling, commit and review practices and tests, or a minimal one, or whichever fits best. Output goes to a new file by default; addToREADME inserts it into README.md at a position you choose instead, outputSpecToPrompt prints it, and addStandardsTest also writes a test that checks the files follow it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/write-coding-standards-from-file"
