      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Advises on Power BI report design: chart choice for your data, page layout, color, typography, navigation, slicers, mobile layout and accessibility.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're planning a Power BI report and want its charts, layout and interactions chosen for its audience"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks about the business problem, audience, decisions, KPIs, data and constraints, then recommends visuals, layout, colors, type sizes, navigation, filters and a mobile layout"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A design specification: summary and rationale, a page-by-page visual plan, interaction design, a four-week build order, a QA checklist and success metrics"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules of thumb",
              "text": "Pie or donut charts for 5 to 7 categories at most, 4.5:1 text contrast, no red-green-only distinctions, 44px touch targets, at most 7 tabs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works from",
              "text": "The context you describe: it recommends a design, and none of its steps open or build the report"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A design consultation for Power BI report visuals; most of its advice is general data visualization design. It starts with three sets of questions: business context (the problem, audience, decisions supported, KPIs, how the report is viewed), data (types, volume, hierarchies, key comparisons, drill-downs) and technical needs (performance, accessibility, brand colors, mobile, integrations). Chart choice follows the relationship in the data: bars and bullet charts for comparison, lines and sparklines for trends, stacked bars, treemaps and waterfalls for composition, histograms, box plots, scatter plots and heat maps for distribution. Executive, analytical and operational reports each get their own patterns. The design pass covers a layout with header KPIs above a main visual, a semantic color map with hex values, a type scale from 20-24pt titles to 9-10pt footnotes, tab, drill-through and button navigation, slicer and filter strategy, and which visuals work on phones. It closes with a quality checklist and a usability test protocol with targets such as task completion above 80% and time to insight under two minutes.",
