            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A feature's PRD and implementation plan are written and you want the work broken into GitHub issues, estimated and ordered"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Lays out an Epic > Feature > Story/Enabler > Task hierarchy, fills an issue template per level, assigns priorities and estimates, and maps blocking dependencies"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "project-plan.md and issues-checklist.md in /docs/ways-of-work/plan/{epic-name}/{feature-name}/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Reads",
              "text": "The feature PRD at {epic-name}/{feature-name}.md, and technical-breakdown.md and implementation-plan.md in {epic-name}/{feature-name}/, all under /docs/ways-of-work/plan/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Gaps",
              "text": "breakdown-feature-prd saves the PRD as prd.md inside the feature's folder, and no breakdown step writes technical-breakdown.md, so point it at your files"
            },
            {
              "label": "Includes",
              "text": "A P0-P3 priority and value matrix, Fibonacci story points and t-shirt sizes, a sprint template, Kanban columns, custom fields and two sample GitHub Actions workflows"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Working as a project manager and DevOps specialist, it takes a feature's planning documents and lays out its work as a Mermaid hierarchy, from the epic through features, user stories and technical enablers (infrastructure or architecture work supporting the stories) down to tasks and tests. It fills a Markdown issue template for each level: description, acceptance criteria, Definition of Done, labels, parent links and an estimate. Priorities run from P0, blocking the release, to P3, nice to have, crossed with business value; stories get Fibonacci points from 1 to 8, with 13 or more meaning epic-level work to split, and epics and features t-shirt sizes from XS to XL. It maps blocking, related, prerequisite and parallel dependencies, and adds a sprint capacity and goal template, a six-column Kanban board with custom fields, and two sample GitHub Actions workflows, one that creates a feature issue and one that moves issues as pull requests open and merge. The issues checklist walks through creating the epic, feature, story and enabler issues. Its own output is the two Markdown files; the issues are drafted there, not filed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/breakdown-plan"
        },
