      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Saves a lesson you give it as a succinct instruction in a per-domain memory file that persists across VS Code sessions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "The agent keeps making the same mistake, or you found a pattern worth keeping, and you want it applied in future sessions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Call it as",
              "text": "/remember [>domain [scope]] lesson, such as /remember >clojure prefer passing maps over parameter lists; the scope is global, the default, or workspace"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads your existing memory and instruction files, matches the lesson to a domain or starts a new one, and writes it as a general, positively worded rule"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A new or updated <domain>-memory.instructions.md, or memory.instructions.md for universal lessons, with applyTo patterns for the files it concerns"
            },
            {
              "label": "Saves to",
              "text": "Your VS Code user prompts folder, applying to every project (global, the default), or the project's .github/instructions/ (workspace or ws)"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Memories here are GitHub Copilot instruction files: each domain, such as clojure or git-workflow, gets its own <domain>-memory.instructions.md, with applyTo glob patterns in its front matter saying which files it applies to, a '# <Domain> Memory' heading, a tagline and one level-2 heading per lesson. When you call /remember, the agent globs the memory and instruction files in the chosen scope to see which domains exist, classifies the lesson as a new gotcha, an addition to a section, a best practice or a process improvement, and matches it to a domain, asking you when the domain is unclear or the name you gave looks like a typo. It reads that domain's files so the new memory complements rather than repeats them, then writes the lesson succinctly, generalized beyond the case at hand so others could use it, phrased as what to do rather than what to avoid, with code examples when they help. It tracks its steps in the agent's todo list. memory-merger, from the same repo, later folds mature lessons into the domain's main instruction file.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/remember"
