      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Adds teaching comments to a code file, explaining the why behind its syntax, idioms and design at the knowledge level you set.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a code file turned into a learning resource, for yourself or for students"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Adds comments explaining the concepts, idioms and design choices the code shows, keeping its encoding, line endings, indentation and build intact"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The same file grown to 125% of its length, at most 400 new comment lines (300 for files over 1,000 lines), numbered Note 1, Note 2 and on"
            },
            {
              "label": "Settings",
              "text": "Comment detail, repetitiveness, your general and language knowledge, each from 1 to 3, plus the domain focus, note numbering and nesting"
            },
            {
              "label": "Run again",
              "text": "On a file it already commented, and it refines the existing notes instead of adding more"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Point it at a file, or it asks for one and offers a numbered list of close matches. Defaults: detail 2, repetitiveness 2, general knowledge 2, knowledge of the language 1, Computer Science as the focus, with note numbers and nested comments on; you can change any of them inline, as in Comment Detail = 1, and it reads obvious typos such as 'Line Numer'. It plans which lines best illustrate the language or platform, then writes comments pitched at that level: foundations for beginners, practical insight for intermediate readers, performance, architecture and internals for advanced ones, suggesting improvements only where they teach something. It uses only standard keyboard characters, no emojis, leaves imports, namespaces and encoding headers working, and checks formatting, encoding and syntax afterwards. Note numbers let related comments refer to each other. You can give it URLs of references to draw on; PEP 263, on Python source encodings, is in its default list.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/add-educational-comments"
