      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a technical implementation plan for a feature from its PRD: Mermaid architecture and database diagrams, API design, frontend components and state.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A feature's PRD is written and you want the technical design worked out before anyone codes"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets out the goal and requirements, a layered system diagram, database schema with indexes, API endpoints with TypeScript types, a component tree and state flow"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "implementation-plan.md in /docs/ways-of-work/plan/{epic-name}/{feature-name}/, with no code beyond pseudocode"
            },
            {
              "label": "Assumes",
              "text": "A monorepo of apps, services and packages, with tRPC, Stack Auth, shadcn/ui, Zustand or React Query, and Docker"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike create-implementation-plan",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, writes atomic task tables for an agent to execute; this one designs the architecture, data model, API and UI"
            }
          ],
          "text": "You give it the content of a feature PRD, and it writes the plan engineers build from, acting as a veteran engineer at a large SaaS company. The plan states the feature's goal in three to five sentences and its detailed requirements, then the technical considerations. A Mermaid system diagram shows the frontend, API, business logic, data and infrastructure layers as subgraphs with labelled data flows, followed by the technology choice for each layer, integration points, Docker deployment and scaling. The database section has a Mermaid entity-relationship diagram with field types and constraints, indexes, foreign keys and a migration strategy. The API section specifies endpoints, request and response formats as TypeScript types, authentication with Stack Auth, error handling, rate limiting and caching. The frontend section lays out a component hierarchy built on shadcn/ui, with a recipe library page as its example, plus a state flow diagram and Zustand or React Query for state, and a last section covers security and performance. The folder layout follows what it calls Epoch's monorepo structure, so expect to swap parts of the stack for yours.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/breakdown-feature-implementation"
