        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Updates an existing specification file for new requirements or code changes, keeping create-specification's AI-ready template of numbered requirements and criteria.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Requirements changed or the code moved on, and a spec file in /spec/ needs to catch up"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Revises the spec file you point it at against the new requirements or code, keeping every section of the template filled"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The updated Markdown spec with front matter, in /spec/, its name starting with its purpose: schema, tool, data, infrastructure, process, architecture or design"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike create-specification",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, writes a new spec from a purpose you give; this one updates an existing one, with the same template"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The update counterpart of create-specification. You give it a spec file, and it brings it in line with new requirements or changes to the existing code. The spec is written for AI readers: precise, explicit language, requirements kept apart from constraints and recommendations, no idioms or metaphors, every acronym defined, examples and edge cases included, and a document that stands on its own. The template's sections are purpose and scope, definitions, requirements, interfaces and data contracts, acceptance criteria, test automation strategy, rationale, dependencies, examples and edge cases, validation criteria and related specs. Requirements carry prefixed IDs such as REQ-001, SEC-001, CON-001, GUD-001 and PAT-001, acceptance criteria use Given-When-Then where it fits, and dependencies say what is needed, such as an OAuth 2.0 library, rather than a package and version. Front matter holds the title, version, creation and update dates, owner and tags. Like create-specification, the test section suggests MSTest, FluentAssertions and Moq for .NET apps and GitHub Actions for CI.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/update-specification"
        },
