      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns shorthand, pseudo-code and loose descriptions, including ()=> lines in your files, into working code, correcting wrong terms or approaches along the way.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You sketch what you want as rough notes, pseudo-code or half-right code and want the agent to write the real thing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Shorthand",
              "text": "Lines starting with ()=> between start-shorthand and end-shorthand comments, which it replaces with code and always deletes"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Judges how much you know, keeps your goal but replaces a method it is confident is wrong, and asks when your intent is unclear"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code edited into your file in the project's existing style, with error handling and validation you didn't mention"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A convention for writing code by sketch. You mark a section of a file with start-shorthand and end-shorthand comments in that file's comment syntax, and write lines starting with ()=>, mostly intent with a little structure, such as \"()=> check email format is correct\". The agent reads the whole section, works out the goal and replaces it with code in the target language, removing every ()=> line. It also acts on clear shorthand without markers, and on plain-language requests. How far it trusts you depends on your apparent expertise: with an expert it fixes typos and builds what you described; with a beginner it corrects the terminology and picks the right libraries and patterns. Its rules: when over 90% sure your method is wrong or not best practice, use a better one; put the goal before the method; ask when unsure; follow the project's existing style; don't over-engineer. If you insist on your own approach, it builds that and explains the trade-offs. A shorthand line can also ask for something other than code, such as running a command or saving fetched data to a file, and it does that before removing the line.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/quasi-coder"
        },
