      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a time-boxed spike document in docs/spikes for one technical decision, with research questions, tasks, success criteria and a place for the recommendation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A technical unknown, such as an API's limits or an architecture choice, has to be settled before development can go on"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One Markdown file per spike, named like api-copilot-integration-spike.md, with status, priority, timebox and owner in its front matter"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Frames one primary question, research tasks, success criteria and constraints, and leaves room for findings, a recommendation and follow-ups"
            },
            {
              "label": "Research strategy",
              "text": "Three phases for working the spike: search docs, the codebase and outside sources; prototype and experiment; then record a recommendation and follow-up tasks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A spike is a short, time-boxed investigation that answers one question blocking development. This skill sets up its document: a file in docs/spikes, or a folder you give, whose front matter holds the title, category, status, priority (High by default), timebox (one week by default) and owner. The body states the objective and why it matters, the primary and secondary questions, checklists of research tasks and success criteria, and the related components, dependencies and constraints. Lower sections hold the investigation results, prototype notes, links, the recommendation with its rationale and implementation notes, follow-up actions and a status history from Not Started to Complete. It lists spike categories, from API integration and architecture to performance, platform, security and user experience, and a three-phase research strategy: gather information from docs, the codebase and outside resources; build prototypes and run targeted experiments; then write up a recommendation and create follow-up tasks. Its rules: one question per spike, evidence before it's marked complete, and every spike ends in an actionable decision.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/create-technical-spike"
