              "text": "plans/{feature-name}/implementation.md, with checkboxes, a verification checklist and a STOP & COMMIT point after each step"
            },
            {
              "label": "Next",
              "text": "structured-autonomy-implement follows that file one step at a time"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The middle step of Structured Autonomy. It reads plans/{feature-name}/plan.md for the feature name, branch, steps and affected files, then runs one research pass through a subagent, without pausing: the project's type, stack and versions, folder layout, naming conventions, build and test commands, existing error-handling, logging and helper patterns, how components interact, and the official docs of its major libraries, with version details and known gotchas. From that it writes plans/{feature-name}/implementation.md, meant to leave nothing to decide. It opens with the goal and a prerequisite to be on the feature branch, created from main if missing. Each step then gets checkbox instructions with complete code to paste into named files, with no placeholders or TODO comments, a verification checklist such as no build errors and UI checks, and a STOP & COMMIT point where the agent waits for you to test, stage and commit.",
