        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds which of a GitHub repo's direct and transitive dependencies you can sponsor, and lists verified funding links grouped by maintainer.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to give back to the open source maintainers your project depends on, and need to know who accepts sponsorship"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Resolves a repo's direct and transitive dependencies through the deps.dev API, then looks up each one's funding links and maintenance score"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One report of who to sponsor, sorted by how many of your dependencies each covers, with the dependency names and a verified link for each"
            },
            {
              "label": "Start with",
              "text": "/sponsor and a GitHub owner/repo, such as /sponsor expressjs/express"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The GitHub MCP server to read repo files, plus web fetch and web search; it never clones the repo or runs shell commands"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "Every funding link by fetching it before it's shown, and drops the dead ones"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Sponsor Finder turns a repository's dependency tree into a short list of people and organizations to fund. The agent reads the repo's manifest, such as package.json, requirements.txt, Cargo.toml, go.mod, Gemfile or pom.xml, to find the package and version, then asks the deps.dev API for every direct and transitive dependency in one call. For each one, deps.dev gives the source GitHub repo and its OSSF Scorecard Maintained score from 0 to 10, shown as actively maintained, partially maintained or possibly unmaintained. Funding links come from the npm registry's funding field, the repo's .github/FUNDING.yml or else the owner's default .github repo, and a web search for the ten unfunded dependencies with the most transitive dependents. Each GitHub Sponsors, Open Collective, Ko-fi, Patreon, Tidelift or custom link is fetched to confirm it's live. The report leads with \"Ways to Give Back\": a numbered list grouped by maintainer, with the sponsorships covering the most dependencies first and GitHub Sponsors preferred, then a short coverage summary. Unfunded projects appear only as a count and a few names, framed as not having funding set up yet. If deps.dev fails or rate-limits, it falls back to the registries or reports partial results.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/sponsor-finder"
