      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Randomizes the order of objects in a JSON array, first checking they share the same properties and that the output stays valid.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have a JSON file of same-shaped records, such as a list of facts, and want them in random order"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks the JSON is valid, every object has the same property names and nothing is nested, then shuffles the objects"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The same records in random order, in the original encoding and formatting, or a report of the inconsistency and no shuffle"
            },
            {
              "label": "Options",
              "text": "ignoreProperties, requiredProperties and nesting, written after the command, change which properties must match and whether nested objects are allowed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Start with",
              "text": "/shuffle-json-data and a file; without one, it asks for it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A small command for reordering data without corrupting it. By default it works at the object level: the input should be a list of objects that all share the same property names, with no nested objects, and the first object's properties count as the required set. Before changing anything, the agent checks that the file is valid JSON and passes those structure checks. If any check fails, it stops and reports the inconsistency rather than modifying the data. Otherwise it randomizes the order of the objects, keeps the JSON valid and preserves the original encoding and formatting. Variables after the command override the defaults, as in /shuffle-json-data #file:funFacts.json ignoreProperties = \"year\", \"category\"; requiredProperties = \"fact\": they set which properties to ignore, which are required, whether nesting is allowed and which collections to shuffle, and closely related names are read sensibly. Without such instructions it won't shuffle nested or mismatched data.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/shuffle-json-data"
