      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a Python pipeline that screens credit data before pre-loan risk modeling, flagging sparse, weak, unstable and redundant features in an Excel report.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're preparing raw credit data for a pre-loan risk model and need to choose which variables are worth keeping"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs a bundled Python script through 11 steps, from per-organization bad rates to missing-rate, IV, PSI, noise and correlation screens"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One Excel report, its file and sheet names in Chinese, with a sheet per step listing each month or feature flagged for removal and why; your data is only read"
            },
            {
              "label": "Expects",
              "text": "Feature columns whose names start with i_, plus the date, label, organization and key columns you name"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks you",
              "text": "At the terminal for paths, column names, out-of-sample organizations and every threshold, each with a default"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python with pandas, toad, LightGBM, scikit-learn, joblib and openpyxl, which the script imports but the SKILL.md doesn't list"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Credit-risk models start from wide tables of candidate variables, many too sparse, weak or unstable to keep. This skill runs a bundled Python script through that screening. It loads a parquet, CSV, Excel or pickle file, treats -1, -999 and -1111 as missing, and counts samples and bad rates per organization, the column naming each sample's source institution. It sets aside the out-of-sample organizations you name and flags months with too few bad or total samples. Then, for every feature, it computes missing rates overall and per organization and flags those over a missing-rate threshold (default 0.6); with low information value (IV, how well a feature separates good from bad borrowers); with an unstable population stability index (PSI, how much its distribution drifts month to month); that look like noise under Null Importance, which compares LightGBM gain on real and shuffled labels; and, among highly correlated features, those with lower gain. Each step runs on its own copy of the data rather than on the previous step's result, so the screens can be compared. IV and PSI calculations run in parallel across CPU cores, and the report, 数据清洗报告.xlsx, goes to the output folder you name.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/datanalysis-credit-risk"
        },
