        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 2014
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans and blocks out game levels from the player's movement metrics, then paces, gates and playtests them before any art goes in.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're laying out a level and want it to play well before any art exists, or a level feels flat, confusing or unfair"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sizes every gap and ledge from the character's jump and run numbers, blocks the level out in grey primitives, paces tension and rest, then playtests"
            },
            {
              "label": "Blockout means",
              "text": "A rough version of the level built from untextured boxes at the right scale, cheap to change, and only dressed with art once it plays well"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Level geometry sized in player units; its patterns add the pacing as data and a room-graph check that the goal can be reached"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Generating levels by algorithm, to procedural-gen, and painting tiles in the engine, to the engine tilemap skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection, engine-neutral with GDScript examples. Its seven steps: derive metrics from the character controller (max jump height and distance, run speed, reach); block the whole level out in primitives at the right scale, with Godot CSG boxes, Unity's ProBuilder, Unreal's Modeling Mode or a grey tileset; mark the critical path from start to goal and the golden path most players take; alternate tension and rest; teach each mechanic somewhere safe before testing it under pressure; gate with keys, abilities and one-way drops while guiding the eye with light, lines and landmarks; and fix the blockout by watching real players. Its patterns compute comfortable and hard gap widths from the same constants the controller uses, write the level's pacing as a list of beats with an intensity, and check a graph of rooms and gated exits for soft-locks, where the goal needs a key no reachable room grants. Pitfalls cover dressing before it plays, flat pacing and unsignposted one-way drops.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/level-design"
