        "skew": 2210
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds what makes a game slow with the engine profiler, fixes that one bottleneck, and reports measured frame times before and after.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Your game's frame rate is low or uneven, it stutters, or it misses a target such as 60 FPS"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets a frame-time budget, profiles a repeatable worst-case scene, decides whether the CPU or the GPU is the bottleneck, fixes the biggest cost, then measures again"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One targeted fix at a time, such as object pooling, fewer draw calls or no per-frame allocations, with before and after numbers"
            },
            {
              "label": "Profilers",
              "text": "Godot 4.7's debugger tabs, Unity 6.3's Profiler and Frame Debugger, and Unreal 5.8's stat commands and Insights"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rule",
              "text": "Never optimize what you haven't measured, and profile a release build on target hardware, since editor and debug builds mislead"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection: a measurement method, not a bag of tricks. It turns \"feels slow\" into a budget, 16.67 ms for the whole frame at 60 FPS, split into slices for scripts, physics, rendering and UI, then reads the profiler's split between CPU and GPU time, because fixing the wrong side changes nothing. It prefers algorithmic wins, such as doing less work, caching, partitioning space or running less often, over polishing a hot line. Its shared fixes are a pool that hides and reuses bullets and enemies instead of creating and freeing them every shot; fewer draw calls through atlases, shared materials, instancing, static batching and baked lighting; and no allocations in Unity's Update, so the garbage collector doesn't stall frames. It ends by setting per-subsystem and asset budgets, adding a performance check to verification and reporting the measured numbers, or saying when it could only measure in the editor. Physics jitter and timestep problems go to the repo's physics-tuning.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/performance-optimization"
