    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/game-ai",
      "name": "game-ai",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/game-ai",
        "installs": 12500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [388, 452, 455, 564, 764, 1668, 2008, 1851],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 2075
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds enemy and NPC behavior in three layers: a state machine or behavior tree decides, pathfinding routes, and steering moves.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing enemy or NPC logic, such as patrol, chase, flee or guard, or need a path to the player"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks a decision model by complexity, keeps decisions apart from motion, paths on a grid, waypoint graph or navmesh, and steers along the waypoints"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "AI code with a state machine or behavior tree, A* or the engine's navmesh, and seek and arrive steering, tuned by drawing paths and states on screen"
            },
            {
              "label": "Decision models",
              "text": "A state machine for two to five clear states, a behavior tree for many prioritized behaviors, utility scoring for weighing options"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Engine navmesh and behavior-tree APIs, to the Unity, Unreal and Godot skills, and deeper trees and utility AI, to ai-behavior-trees-utility-ai"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection, teaching engine-neutral algorithms with GDScript and Python examples. Its rule is to separate deciding what to do, routing around the map and moving there: a behavior tree picks a target, the pathfinder produces waypoints, steering follows them. Patterns show a state machine in which each state returns the next one, instead of a pile of if-flags; behavior-tree sequence and selector nodes that return success, failure or running; seek and arrive steering scaled by frame time; and A* heuristics that never overestimate, Manhattan for four directions and octile for eight, so paths stay shortest. Paths are recomputed on a timer or when the goal moves a tile, never every frame. Pitfalls include leaves that never return running, so the tree restarts long actions every tick, agents that steer straight at the goal and hug walls, and no stuck check, which leaves agents grinding into walls. Its references hold the full A* loop and behavior-tree decorators and blackboards.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/game-ai"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:games",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "architecture"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/camera-systems",
      "name": "camera-systems",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/camera-systems",
        "installs": 12500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [377, 447, 467, 561, 780, 1682, 2014, 1843],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1067
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds game cameras that follow smoothly with a deadzone, look-ahead and level bounds, plus third-person orbits with wall collision and first-person look.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A camera should follow the player smoothly and stay inside the level, or it jitters, snaps, shows past the edge or makes players queasy"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets the camera's rules by genre, smooths the follow independently of frame rate, adds a deadzone, look-ahead and bounds, and pulls 3D cameras in off walls"
            },
            {
              "label": "Deadzone means",
              "text": "A box the player can move in without moving the camera, which stops twitchy games from shaking the view"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Camera code, checked at low and high frame rates, in corners, against walls and at level edges"
            },
            {
              "label": "Code examples",
              "text": "Godot 4.7's Camera2D and SpringArm3D and a hand-rolled follow, with Unity Cinemachine 3 equivalents"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection. It starts from what the camera serves: a platformer leads the jump, top-down centers with a deadzone, third-person orbits with collision, first-person only looks. The follow uses exponential smoothing or a spring rather than a fixed lerp per frame, whose feel changes with frame rate, and runs after movement and physics resolve, avoiding a one-frame lag. Look-ahead offsets the target in the direction of travel; bounds keep the whole view, not just its center, inside the level. In 3D, yaw and pitch turn a pivot, pitch is clamped, and a spring arm or ray pulls the camera in when geometry blocks it. Screen shake is an offset added on top of the follow; how strong it is and when it fires belong to the repo's game-feel skill. Pitfalls include Godot mouse sensitivity that changes with window size, unclamped pitch flipping the camera, and teleports that whip it across the level. Its reference covers multi-target framing, split-screen and cinematic blends.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/camera-systems"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "category:games",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "animation"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/save-systems",
      "name": "save-systems",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/save-systems",
        "installs": 12400,
        "weeklyInstalls": [390, 438, 451, 558, 770, 1660, 2017, 1862],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1391
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs game save and load: plain-data snapshots with a version number, crash-safe writes, migrations for old saves, slots and throttled autosave.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're adding save and load, slots or autosave, or old saves break after a patch"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Saves data rather than engine objects, stamps a schema version, writes to a temporary file then renames it, migrates old saves step by step, and autosaves to its own slot"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Save code that survives a crash mid-write, loads saves from earlier versions, and falls back to a backup when a file won't parse"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checked by",
              "text": "Saving, quitting, relaunching and loading, then loading a save from the previous version"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Roblox cloud saves, to roblox-datastores, and the data model itself, to the engine resource skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection. Its premise: writing bytes is easy; the hard parts are choosing what to save, writing it so a crash can't corrupt it, and reading old saves after you ship a patch. It saves plain data, such as health, position, seed and flags, and rebuilds objects on load instead of serializing live nodes. Every save carries a version number; loading reads it, runs one migration function per version up to the current one, validates the result, and refuses saves from a newer build. Writes go to a temporary file renamed over the real one, keeping the last good save as a .bak, and it says where that falls short: on Windows, and on power loss, since nothing calls fsync. Autosave runs on safe boundaries such as checkpoints, at most once a minute, into its own file. Pitfalls include Godot's JSON turning every number into a float, locale-dependent decimals, save files that run scripts when loaded, and trusting client saves in multiplayer. Examples are GDScript and Python, starting with JSON.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/save-systems"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:games",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "migration"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/input-systems",
      "name": "input-systems",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/input-systems",
        "installs": 12100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [371, 423, 444, 530, 735, 1636, 1975, 1825],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1906
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a game input layer of named actions with per-device bindings, rebinding with conflict checks, stick deadzones, input buffering, coyote time and accessible controls.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're setting up controls, adding gamepad or touch support or a rebinding menu, or making jumps feel forgiving"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Maps keys and buttons to named actions, binds each per device, filters sticks with a radial deadzone, buffers early presses and builds a rebinding screen that catches conflicts"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Input code that gameplay reads by action name, with saved rebinds, a reset to defaults and prompts that switch with the device"
            },
            {
              "label": "Coyote time means",
              "text": "A short window after walking off a ledge in which a jump still works; buffering keeps an early jump press alive until landing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Code examples",
              "text": "Godot 4.7's InputMap, with the matching Unity Input System and Unreal Enhanced Input calls"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection. Its rule is never to wire gameplay to raw keys: gameplay asks whether jump is pressed, and bindings are data, which gives rebinding, multiple devices and accessibility almost for free. It separates pressed-this-frame checks for discrete actions from held checks for movement, applies a radial deadzone that rescales past its edge so diagonals aren't clipped, and keeps the response curve apart from sensitivity. Buffering and coyote windows last about a tenth of a second. Rebinding captures the next input, rejects a binding another action already uses, binds keyboard keys by physical position so WASD stays put on AZERTY or Dvorak, binds pads for every device rather than pad 0, and persists through the repo's save-systems skill. It checks every device, and a rebind mid-game. Pitfalls include accessibility gaps: required simultaneous presses, no remapping, hold-only actions and fixed sensitivity.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/input-systems"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:design",
          "category:games",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "accessibility"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/procedural-gen",
      "name": "procedural-gen",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/procedural-gen",
        "installs": 12100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [367, 435, 442, 528, 733, 1616, 1966, 1796],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1826
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Generates game levels, terrain and loot from a seed: deterministic random numbers, layered noise heightmaps, room-and-corridor dungeons and weighted drop tables.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want maps, dungeons, terrain or item drops you don't author by hand, reproducible from a seed for debugging, daily challenges or shared worlds"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Threads one seeded random generator through all generation, picks noise, room placement or weighted tables by content, fills a plain grid, then checks the result is playable"
            },
            {
              "label": "Seed means",
              "text": "One number that reproduces the same world every time, as long as nothing else random leaks into generation"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Generation code that fills a data grid, separate from drawing, with reachability checks that reject or repair broken maps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Painting the grid, to the engine tilemap skills, and hand-paced levels, to level-design"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection, built on determinism. Generation code never calls the global random function, which makes worlds impossible to reproduce, and the seed goes in the save. Terrain sums several octaves from a real noise library, normalized and reshaped, with elevation and moisture on different seeds so biomes don't line up in bands. Dungeons place non-overlapping rooms, with capped attempts, and carve corridors between them. Loot rolls against relative weights. Before the player sees a map, it checks every room is reachable, the spawn is safe and the exit has a path. It tunes with the seed fixed, then sweeps many seeds to check the spread. Pitfalls include Perlin noise that is flat at whole-number coordinates, Python set order leaking into choices, and library generators whose sequences change between versions; for those it suggests shipping your own small generator with a test, or saving the result instead of the seed. Examples are Python with Godot and Unity equivalents; its references hold BSP, caves and wave function collapse.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/procedural-gen"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:games",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "generative-art",
          "implementation"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/create-game-assets",
      "name": "create-game-assets",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/create-game-assets",
        "installs": 12200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [399, 474, 516, 611, 863, 1778, 2200, 2023],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-08"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 705
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Directs and produces cohesive game art: a locked visual target, an asset manifest, families generated or sourced from it, then normalized and checked in-game.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need sprites, tilesets, backgrounds, icons, UI art, textures or 3D asset briefs that look like one game, not a pile of one-off generations"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Records the technical frame and a visual system, approves one hero asset at game scale, produces related assets as families, normalizes them with ordinary tools and imports them"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A filled-in art-direction brief and asset manifest, engine-ready files, contact sheets, and a record of each file's source and license"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "An image-generation skill or tool when one is installed, following it for the actual call; without one, licensed assets or labelled greybox placeholders"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python 3.10+ and Pillow for its two bundled QA scripts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection that treats image generation as one tool inside a controlled pipeline, never as proof an asset can ship. It first inspects existing art and import settings, then locks the technical frame: view, native size, palette, filtering, frame counts and budgets. It names the visual system in concrete properties, such as shape language, value structure and edge treatment, never a living artist's name. One representative asset is approved against a gameplay background before the rest are made as families that reuse it as a reference, keeping palette, view, proportions and lighting fixed. A prompt template covers role, view, art direction, game-scale read, technical output, locks and exclusions. Outputs are cropped, anchored, sliced and inspected with ordinary tools and two scripts: a report on size, transparency and color count, and a nearest-neighbor contact sheet. Quality gates cover cohesion, gameplay read, technical fit, animation, seams, 3D checks and rights, and nothing counts as production-ready from a prompt result alone. Sound goes to audio-design.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/create-game-assets"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:media",
          "category:design",
          "category:games",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "image",
          "design",
          "ai-generation"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/physics-tuning",
      "name": "physics-tuning",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/physics-tuning",
        "installs": 11800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [357, 422, 428, 507, 712, 1582, 1940, 1763],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1899
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Fixes jittery, tunneling or floaty game physics with a fixed timestep, render interpolation, continuous collision, body tuning and collision layers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Motion jitters, fast objects pass through walls, stacks explode, or movement feels floaty, sticky or laggy"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Moves simulation into the fixed physics step, interpolates rendering between ticks, turns on continuous collision for fast bodies, tunes mass, drag and materials, and checks layers and masks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Tunneling means",
              "text": "A fast, small object skipping past a thin collider between two physics steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Physics settings and code changes, checked at low and high frame rates by throwing fast objects at thin walls and shoving stacks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Code examples",
              "text": "GDScript for Godot, with the Unity equivalents in comments"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection. Its premise: most bad physics isn't an engine bug but a mismatch between the fixed-rate simulation and the variable-rate render loop, or untuned settings. Forces, velocities and collision reads go in the physics callback with its fixed step, and rendering interpolates between the last two physics states, preferably with the engine's built-in interpolation. Tunneling gets continuous collision detection and a speed cap, with the rule that one step's travel should stay under the thinnest wall. Mass sets weight in collisions, not fall speed, so feel comes from drag, gravity scale and physics materials. Stacks and joints settle with modest mass ratios, more solver iterations and sleeping. Pitfalls include one-sided layer and mask setups, huge time-step spikes after a hitch, teleports smeared by interpolation, and Godot damping that drifts after raising the physics rate. Jump buffering and coyote time are left to input-systems and the platformer skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/physics-tuning"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:games",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/shader-programming",
      "name": "shader-programming",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/shader-programming",
        "installs": 11800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [354, 419, 429, 509, 708, 1564, 1957, 1772],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1421
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes game shaders in GLSL, with HLSL equivalents, from the vertex and fragment stages to effects such as dissolve, scrolling UVs and rim light.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing a vertex or fragment shader, building an effect such as dissolve, outline, rim light or vignette, or porting a shader between GLSL, HLSL and engines"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Tracks which stage and coordinate space it's in, drives effects with UVs and time, prefers mix and smoothstep over branches, and validates the source before testing visually"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Shader code, checked with glslangValidator or naga when installed, then looked at on the hardware it ships to"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Each engine's shader syntax and built-ins, to godot-shaders or the engine's material docs, and particle systems, to unreal-niagara"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection: the shader concepts that carry across engines, where only the dialect and built-in names change. Its snippets are desktop GLSL 330 that pass glslangValidator, with notes for GLSL ES in WebGL2, three.js and PixiJS, and HLSL names in comments. Patterns cover sampling and tinting a texture, scrolling UVs driven by a real time uniform so speed stays steady, a dissolve that thresholds a noise map with a glowing edge and no stray specks at zero, and a fresnel rim light that brightens silhouettes. It explains why GPUs pay for both sides of a branch that differs between neighbouring pixels, and why texture samples dominate cost. Pitfalls include mixing coordinate spaces, unnormalized interpolated normals, V flipped on some engines, discard defeating early depth tests on mobile, reversed smoothstep edges, shimmer at low precision, GLSL-to-HLSL differences such as mod and fmod, and raw GLSL rendering nothing under three.js's WebGPURenderer. A reference holds outline, vignette and color-grading shaders and the full GLSL-to-HLSL table.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/shader-programming"
        },
