        "skew": 1123
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds branching game dialogue as a graph of lines, choices, conditions and variables, in Ink, Yarn Spinner or a small custom runner.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're adding NPC conversations, choice menus or story flags that change later dialogue"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Chooses between Ink, Yarn Spinner and a custom runner, defines what a node can yield, keeps variables apart from flow, uses line IDs for translation and walks every branch"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A dialogue graph as data and a runner that hands lines and choices to your UI, or the same scene in Ink or Yarn script"
            },
            {
              "label": "Ink and Yarn",
              "text": "Ink is prose-first and suits writer-heavy branching stories; Yarn Spinner is node-based, with explicit commands for engine hooks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Text boxes and choice buttons, to engine UI skills, and saving story variables, to save-systems"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection that models conversations as a graph: nodes hold lines, choices branch, conditions gate options and variables remember what the player did. The first decision is build or buy, and for a custom runner it says to build a graph, not a language: a JSON graph whose lines are string-table IDs, with conditions and variable writes on choices, and a GDScript runner that shows lines, offers only the choices whose condition passes, and waits for the player. The same guard-bribe scene is shown in Ink (once-only and sticky choices, diverts, global variables) and in Yarn Spinner 3 (options, declare and set, jumps), noting that an undeclared Yarn variable compiles with only a warning, which hides typos. Writes go on the transition, so revisiting a node doesn't charge the player twice. It checks that every branch reaches an end or a valid jump. Pitfalls include hardcoded display strings that make translation a rewrite, inventing a scripting language for a simple tree, and dead-end nodes that stall silently.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/dialogue-systems"
        },
