        "skew": 1833
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans an RPG's core systems, stats and leveling, inventory, quests, combat, dialogue and saves, with design knobs, example formulas and pitfalls.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building an RPG, JRPG or action RPG, or designing its leveling curve, damage formula, inventory or quest states"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Frames every system around the loop of explore, encounter, reward and grow, and lists the eight systems an RPG needs, with the knobs that tune each"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Stats, items and quests defined as data, with combat stats recomputed from base attributes and gear applied as removable modifiers"
            },
            {
              "label": "Composes",
              "text": "dialogue-systems for conversations, save-systems for persistence, game-ai for enemies and game-ui-ux for menus; Godot and Unity bindings are examples, not requirements"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A genre playbook from a game-development collection: it ties systems together and points to the repo's other skills for the parts it doesn't teach. Its core loop is explore, encounter, earn rewards, grow and take on harder content, and every system should feed that sense of getting stronger and shaping a character. The eight must-haves are stats and leveling, inventory and equipment, combat, quests, dialogue, save and load, economy with power gating, and UI. A table of design knobs covers the XP curve, stat scaling, damage formula, variance and crits (±10% and about 1.5× crit as safe defaults), drop rates and choice consequences. Its pseudocode derives combat stats from base attributes every time, levels up on a quadratic XP curve, and advances quest objectives from game events. Pitfalls include editing base stats for buffs, multiplying stacked percentages, hardcoding content instead of data, saves with no version, and choices that reconverge at once. It works with custom engines too, binding the concepts to the project's own data, events, UI and saves.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/genres/rpg"
