      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1110
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Greyboxes one game mechanic within a hard timebox to answer whether it's fun, then makes a keep, kill or refactor call.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know whether a mechanic or idea is fun before committing real time, or need something rough and playable fast"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes the one question, decides throwaway or keep before coding, sets a 30 to 90 minute timebox, builds only the core action in grey shapes and playtests it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A filled-in prototype brief, a playable spike in its own folder, and a keep, kill or refactor decision against criteria written in advance"
            },
            {
              "label": "Greybox means",
              "text": "Plain boxes, circles and built-in fonts instead of art, so no time goes to anything the question doesn't need"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike game-jam",
              "text": "There's no deadline or submission; the output is a decision, not a product"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A workflow skill from a game-development collection. A prototype's output, it says, is a decision, not a product, so it optimizes for learning speed. Before any code it fills a brief: the question, ideally yes or no; the single action the player repeats; whether the code is throwaway; the timebox; and observable keep and kill signals. Only the core action is real. Everything else is primitives, stubs or absent, and on-screen debug text shows the numbers being judged, such as speed or a timing window. You play it, then hand it to one other person without explaining the controls. Keep means a fresh player does the core action on purpose within about 30 seconds and repeats the loop unasked; kill means it only feels good once explained, needs far bigger systems, or you're adding art to avoid admitting it's flat. The spike lives in a separate folder or on a throwaway branch, and a keep authorizes a rewrite in the real project, never a copy of the spike.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/workflows/prototype-fast"
        },
