      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1734
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides building a turn-based roguelike: an energy turn scheduler, connected procedural dungeons, symmetric field of view, depth-scaled loot and permadeath.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're making a turn-based, grid-based dungeon crawler where death ends the run and the world regenerates, as a roguelike or a roguelite"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets out the systems to build, the design knobs to tune and the bugs to avoid, with engine-neutral pseudocode"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A roguelike built around eight must-have systems, from an energy-based turn scheduler to a message log"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Noise, tilemaps, saving and monster AI, to the repo's procedural-gen, engine tilemap, save-systems and game-ai skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A genre playbook from a game-development collection. It anchors the design in the Berlin Interpretation, a community list of what makes a game feel roguelike, such as random levels, permadeath, turns, a grid and many interacting items and monsters. Its core loop: take a turn, let the world resolve its turn, then descend, loot or die and restart in a fresh dungeon. Patterns cover one seeded random generator per run, so a dungeon can be replayed or a crashed run resumed; an energy scheduler, where faster actors act more often, granting energy in its own pass so no actor is starved of turns; and field of view with explored memory, using symmetric shadowcasting so that if you can see a monster, it can see you. A table of knobs covers dungeon size, monster and loot curves by depth, FOV radius, scarcity and permadeath versus meta-progression. Pitfalls include unreachable rooms, real-time loops posing as turn-based ones, save-scumming and wiping meta-unlocks along with the run. It's a compositional skill: it names the repo's other skills to build each primitive from instead of teaching them.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/genres/roguelike"
        },
