        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1735
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides building a first-person shooter: hitscan or projectile shots, damage falloff, time-to-kill balance, learnable recoil and spread, and hit feedback.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a game whose core verb is aim and shoot, or tuning time-to-kill, recoil, spread or aim feel"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets out the controller, shooting model, weapons, health, enemy AI and feedback to build, with a recommended default for each design knob"
            },
            {
              "label": "Defaults",
              "text": "Headshots at about 1.5–2× damage, full damage to about 20 m, a learnable recoil pattern and an accurate first shot"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "The 3D body, raycasts, input and enemy AI, to the repo's engine, input-systems and game-ai skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A genre playbook from a game-development collection. It treats the aim-and-fire micro-loop as the whole game: responsive look, clear hit feedback and a death that reads instantly. Its eight must-have systems run from the first-person controller and camera to objectives beyond shooting. Patterns, in engine-neutral pseudocode, cover a hitscan ray from the camera that excludes the shooter and scales damage by range and headshot, a projectile that despawns once it has covered its range, and time-to-kill worked out from health, damage per shot and fire rate together. Pitfalls include look sensitivity multiplied by frame time, a view that can flip over unless pitch is clamped to about ±89°, shots stopped by the shooter's own collider or by trigger volumes, recoil written into the aim so that recovery fights the player, hitscan pistols that snipe across the map and trusting the client in multiplayer. It insists on FOV and sensitivity options, against motion sickness.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/genres/fps-shooter"
