      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1702
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides building match-3, sokoban and other grid puzzles: a board model, rule resolution repeated until stable, exact undo and solvable levels.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're making a board the player changes with moves and that resolves by rules: match-3, sokoban, sliding or logic-grid puzzles"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets out the board model, move validation, resolution loop, undo, scoring and level progression, with engine-neutral pseudocode"
            },
            {
              "label": "Its rule",
              "text": "The board is the single source of truth and the view only draws it; input stays locked until the board is stable"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Tile rendering, level pacing and juice, to the repo's engine tilemap, level-design and game-feel skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A genre playbook from a game-development collection. The fun of a puzzle is the planning, so the engine's job is to resolve each move deterministically and show it clearly. Patterns cover match detection on a grid; a resolve loop that clears, drops and refills pieces and scores each step of a chain until nothing changes; and undo by snapshot, or by storing commands that can be inverted on large boards. Undo must restore everything, random state included, or players learn to reroll refills by undoing. Pitfalls include resolving only once and missing cascades, unseeded refills, generated boards with no solution (a sliding puzzle filled by a random shuffle is unsolvable half the time), match-3 boards left with no valid move, and difficulty spikes from introducing several mechanics at once. A table of knobs covers grid shape, move or time limits, cascade scoring, undo depth and deadlock handling.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/genres/puzzle"
        },
