      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 2045
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides building card games and deckbuilders: cards as data, deck, hand and discard zones, reshuffling, turn phases and one interpreter for all effects.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a game whose objects are cards moving between zones: a deckbuilder, TCG or CCG, solitaire or roguelike deckbuilder"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets out card data, zones, draw and reshuffle, turn phases, resources and effect resolution, with engine-neutral pseudocode"
            },
            {
              "label": "Its rules",
              "text": "A card lives in exactly one zone, effects are data rather than code per card, and a play is validated before anything is paid"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "Card assets, hand UI, saves and opponent AI, to the repo's engine data and UI skills, game-ui-ux, save-systems and game-ai"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A genre playbook from a game-development collection. Depth comes from the combinations a hand allows; the engine's job is to resolve them unambiguously. Patterns cover drawing with an automatic reshuffle of the discard pile and a deck-out case; a card defined as data whose effects one small interpreter applies, after checking cost and targets so that a failed play leaves zones and resources untouched; and turns as a fixed list of phases (untap, draw, main, combat, end) where triggers fire. Pitfalls include a card in two zones at once, an undefined order for simultaneous triggers, unseeded shuffles that break replays, an off-by-one in the shuffle's random index that biases the deck (engines differ on whether a random range includes its top value), and threats with no answer in the card pool. A table of knobs covers hand size, deck size, the resource curve, rarity and reshuffle rules.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/genres/card-game"
        },
