      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 2347
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides building 2D platformers around a jump that feels right: gravity derived from height and apex time, coyote time, jump buffering and variable height.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're making a Mario- or Celeste-like, or a jump feels floaty, unresponsive or unfair"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sets out the controller, collisions, level geometry, hazards, checkpoints, camera and juice, and tunes the jump by outcome instead of raw numbers"
            },
            {
              "label": "Coyote time means",
              "text": "A jump still works for about 0.1 s after running off a ledge; a jump buffer keeps a press made just before landing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Starting points",
              "text": "3–4 tiles of jump height, 0.30–0.40 s to the top of the jump, and fall gravity 1.5–2× the rising gravity"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "The character body, tilemaps and level layout, to the repo's engine movement, tilemap and level-design skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A genre playbook from a game-development collection. A platformer lives or dies on one jump repeated thousands of times, so it tightens the controller first and treats everything else as content. Patterns, in pseudocode, solve gravity and jump velocity from the height and time to apex you want; combine coyote time, a jump buffer, a cut to upward speed when the button is released early and heavier gravity on the way down; and use each engine's one-way platform setting (Godot, Unity and Phaser are named) with a short window to drop through. Its knob table adds apex hang, ground acceleration, corner correction and wall slide and wall jump. Pitfalls include jump height that varies with frame rate unless the controller runs on the fixed physics tick, platform velocity added twice when leaving a moving platform, wall jumps that use up the double jump, catching on tile seams, tunnelling through floors at speed and a camera that snaps.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/genres/platformer"
        },
