      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "25eec57919aec2cfd87d4ef9ae5661832302585e",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 1777
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans a game jam entry to the deadline: a one-sentence concept, scope by jam length, a 48-hour schedule, feature cuts and a submission checklist.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're entering a timed jam such as Ludum Dare, GMTK Jam or Global Game Jam, or asking what you can build in a weekend"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Takes you from the rules and the theme to one concept, a scope that fits the clock, an early playable loop and a build submitted before the deadline"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A one-sentence concept (\"You [verb] to [goal] while [constraint]\"), a scope budget, a 48-hour timeline in blocks of hours and a pre-submission checklist"
            },
            {
              "label": "Its rule",
              "text": "Keep the last ~20% of the clock for shipping; under deadline pressure, the default answer to a feature is cut"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "The game code and the upload, to the repo's engine and genre skills and to itch-publish"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A planning and scope-control workflow from a game-development collection: its win condition is something submitted and playable, not the game you imagined. Before the theme drops, it checks the jam's rules (length, deadline and timezone, teams, assets, generative AI policy, platforms, where to submit, whether uploads lock) and preps an empty project that already builds and exports. It then brainstorms ten ideas in 15 minutes and commits to one, picks one core mechanic and one hook, and gets a 30-second loop from start to restart running early, before content. A budget table scales mechanics, levels, art, audio and the ship buffer for 48-hour, 72–96-hour and one-week jams, and a 48-hour timeline schedules sleep, a playtest, an art and audio pass and a bug-fix freeze. Feature triage asks three questions of every feature. The checklist tests the build from a clean extracted copy and confirms the entry is attached to the jam, not just the game's page. It also summarizes the formats of Ludum Dare, GMTK Jam and Global Game Jam, with a reminder to re-check the live rules.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/workflows/game-jam"
        },
        "tags": [
