      "skillMd": {
        "version": "74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks a resume against applicant tracking systems: formatting that breaks their parsers, keyword match with a job posting, and where to add missing terms.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Your applications get no replies, or you want to know whether a resume will get through automated screening for a job"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks formatting that breaks resume parsers, then scores keyword match: required keywords found divided by those in the job description, aiming for 80% or more"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An ATS compatibility report: overall score, formatting issues, found and missing keywords, before-and-after rewrites and an estimated new match score"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike resume-formatter",
              "text": "That one covers layout alone; this one also matches your resume's keywords against a specific posting"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An applicant tracking system (ATS) is the software employers use to read resumes into structured data and rank them by keywords before a person sees them. This skill first checks for what breaks that reading: tables, columns, text boxes, headers and footers, images, unusual fonts, and section names such as \"My Journey\" instead of \"Experience\". It then pulls the job description's keywords in three groups, hard skills, soft skills and industry terms, and checks each for exact phrases, synonyms, how often it appears and where. Missing keywords get suggested placements: five to eight of the most important in the summary, the posting's exact phrasing in the skills section, and the rest worked into experience bullets, with critical terms appearing two to four times and no keyword stuffing. It adjusts for tech, finance, healthcare and marketing resumes, for career changers, recent graduates and executives, and for employment gaps, then re-scores the resume after the suggested changes.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/paramchoudhary/resumeskills/tree/74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d/skills/resume-ats-optimizer"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:careers",
