            }
          ],
          "text": "A guide to a LinkedIn profile written for job searching. It starts from how LinkedIn differs from a resume: longer, more conversational, keyed to the whole industry rather than one employer, and always current. The headline, up to 220 characters and the part it treats as most important for search, follows a role, expertise and value formula. The About section opens with a hook in the first lines that show before \"see more\", then covers who you are, key achievements, what you're looking for, a keyword list and how to reach you. Each experience entry gets a short description, four to six achievement bullets and media where possible. It also covers the photo and banner, using all 50 skill slots, the Featured section, five to ten recommendations, Open to Work visibility, posting at least weekly, and what to keep the same, expand or adjust when syncing the profile with your resume. You make the changes on LinkedIn yourself, from its checklist.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/paramchoudhary/resumeskills/tree/74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d/skills/linkedin-profile-optimizer"
        },
