        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scores how well you match a job posting, separates must-haves from nice-to-haves, spots warning signs, and recommends whether and how to apply.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're asking \"should I apply to this job?\" or \"am I qualified?\" before spending time tailoring a resume"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Weights required skills 70% and preferred ones 30% into a match score, then sorts each gap as critical, major or minor"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A job analysis report: match score and recommendation, strengths to lead with, gap strategies, resume changes, cover letter talking points, red flags and a timeline"
            },
            {
              "label": "Thresholds",
              "text": "75-89% is an excellent fit, 60-74% good, under 50% a skip unless it's a dream job, and 90% or more overqualified"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The step before tailoring a resume. Its own advice is to apply to 10-15 jobs at a 70-90% match rather than 50 blindly. It splits the posting into required qualifications, preferred ones and culture signals, using wording cues (\"must have\", \"bonus if\") and how often each comes up, and pulls out hard skills, soft skills and domain terms. The match score weights required skills at 70% and preferred at 30%. Each missing requirement is a critical gap (don't apply), a major one (address it in the cover letter) or a minor one (downplay it or stress related skills); dealbreakers include a required license, clearance, degree or location you can't meet. It scans for red flags in workload, culture and pay language, such as \"wear many hats\", \"like a family\" or \"competitive salary\" with no range. The report also lists checks to run on Glassdoor, LinkedIn and Levels.fyi, an application timeline and the reasons for and against applying. Vague, multi-role, internal and reposted listings get their own handling. resume-tailor, from the same repo, says to run it first.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/paramchoudhary/resumeskills/tree/74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d/skills/job-description-analyzer"
        },
