    {
      "id": "paramchoudhary/resumeskills/skills/cover-letter-generator",
      "name": "cover-letter-generator",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/paramchoudhary/resumeskills/cover-letter-generator",
        "installs": 9500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [390, 311, 334, 425, 440, 419, 406, 488],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a 250-400 word cover letter from your resume and the job description, opening with a specific hook and addressing any gaps.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need a cover letter for a specific job"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Opens with a hook (company knowledge, a mutual connection, their problem, an achievement or an industry insight), then matches your strongest experience to their top need"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The letter, two alternative openings, and talking points from it to expand on in the interview"
            },
            {
              "label": "Avoids",
              "text": "\"I am writing to apply\", \"please find my resume attached\" and \"I look forward to hearing from you\""
            }
          ],
          "text": "A cover letter should add what a resume can't: why you, why now, why here. This skill writes one in three or four paragraphs. The opening hooks with something specific, never \"I am writing to apply\" or a first word of \"I\". The first body paragraph ties your strongest experience to their top requirement with a result; the second adds more value and handles gaps head-on, such as SQL you're still learning, set against related strengths. The close names a contribution you'd make and asks to talk. Tone shifts by industry: technical detail for engineering, some creativity for marketing, formality for finance and consulting, scrappiness for startups. It covers unknown hiring managers (no \"To Whom It May Concern\"), referrals, being under- or overqualified, and career changes, and checks each draft against a ten-point list that includes a metric and the word count. It suggests analyzing the job description first; its output header has a slot for the match score from job-description-analyzer, in the same repo.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/paramchoudhary/resumeskills/tree/74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d/skills/cover-letter-generator"
        },
