      "skillMd": {
        "version": "74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes VP and C-suite resumes around leadership brand and business transformation: an executive profile, career highlights, P&L scope and board roles.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're applying for director, VP or C-suite roles and need a resume that reads as strategic, not tactical"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes bullets as leadership action, strategic initiative and business outcome at scale, with company context, reporting lines and P&L for each role"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An executive resume (profile, competency grid, career highlights, experience, board roles, education) plus notes on your narrative and differentiator"
            },
            {
              "label": "Length",
              "text": "Two pages for VP and SVP, two to three for the C-suite"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Executive resumes sell a leadership brand and organizational change rather than skills and tasks, for readers such as executive recruiters, board members, investors and CEOs. The structure opens with an executive profile instead of a summary: a title line, a statement of who you are as a leader, your track record with scope and scale, and what you're known for. Core competencies are framed as leadership themes, and four to six career highlights with metrics come before the chronological experience. Each role starts with company context (revenue, headcount, stage), reporting lines and P&L, followed by strategic initiatives and results such as margins, EBITDA, acquisitions, team growth and IPO preparation. Board and advisory roles get their own section. It covers transformation stories (inherited, implemented, achieved), hinting at how you lead, framing short and long tenures, and keeping confidential figures to percentages or ranges. Its tone rules: strategic verbs, no first person, no tactical words like \"managed\" or \"assisted\", and achievements that will survive fact-checking.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/paramchoudhary/resumeskills/tree/74ae19e7c62b0516d1c298328e5544976c12da5d/skills/executive-resume-writer"
        },
