              "label": "Unlike a cover letter",
              "text": "It reads like a note from a colleague: no achievement lists, no \"I would love the opportunity to\""
            }
          ],
          "text": "Cold email for job seekers, built on one idea: it should sound like a smart person who noticed something specific about the company, not a candidate broadcasting credentials. Before writing, the agent gathers one specific angle from the company's website, job description or About page (asking for the site, or fetching it, when you only give the job description), the recipient's name from LinkedIn, a team page or AngelList, your most relevant projects, and any real overlap in tech stack. The email opens with that hook (a stat, a product decision or a technical insight, never \"I love what you're building\"), says early and casually if you're based in another country, briefly acknowledges an experience gap without apologizing, then gives your current role, past context and one or two projects with a proof point. A sentence bridges to what the role needs, followed by a portfolio link and a low-pressure close. It runs 200 to 300 words, 400 at most, and comes with a full worked example.",
