      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sizes a market's TAM, SAM and SOM with top-down, bottom-up and value-theory methods, cross-checked for a pitch or business plan.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need market size figures for a pitch deck or business plan, or to decide whether a market is worth entering"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Defines the market, gathers sources, calculates total, serviceable and obtainable market with up to three methods, then cross-checks the results"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "TAM, SAM and SOM with the method, sources and assumptions behind each, laid out for investors or for strategy"
            },
            {
              "label": "Terms",
              "text": "TAM is all revenue at 100% share, SAM the part your product can serve, SOM the share you can realistically win in 3–5 years"
            },
            {
              "label": "Red flags",
              "text": "A TAM under $1B for a VC-backed startup, an SOM above 10% in five years, or methods more than 50% apart"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A market sizing method that needs no tool or data service. The SKILL.md defines the three tiers and when each method fits; a reference file holds the formulas and steps. Top-down starts from an industry report's category size and narrows it by geography and segment. Bottom-up multiplies the customers in each segment by their yearly revenue, the method it calls most credible to investors. Value theory prices the solution at 10–30% of the value it creates, for new categories with no market data. The six steps define the market (problem, customers, category, geography, time horizon), gather sources such as analyst reports, census data, Crunchbase or customer interviews, calculate TAM, filter it down to SAM, take 2–5% of SAM as SOM, then triangulate: top-down and bottom-up results should land within 30% of each other. It gives TAM formulas for SaaS, marketplace, consumer and B2B services markets, outlines for presenting to investors (lead with bottom-up) or for strategy, and five common mistakes, such as confusing TAM with SAM or relying on top-down figures alone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/startup-business-analyst/skills/market-sizing-analysis"
        },
