      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies RAII, smart pointers, ownership and bounds checking in C, C++ and Rust to prevent use-after-free, leaks, overflows and data races.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing or reviewing C, C++ or Rust and want to rule out leaks, dangling pointers, buffer overflows and data races"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives the agent a table of six memory bug types and how each is prevented, with code patterns for each language"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code written along those patterns; it adds no steps or files of its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Terms",
              "text": "RAII ties a resource to a scope, so it's freed when the scope ends; ownership is Rust's compile-time rule of one owner per value"
            },
            {
              "label": "Debugging tools",
              "text": "Commands for AddressSanitizer, ThreadSanitizer, Valgrind and Rust's Miri"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference, not a workflow. It places languages on a spectrum from manual memory management in C, through smart pointers in C++ and ownership in Rust, to garbage collection in Go and Java: safer toward the end, with less control. A reference file holds six patterns with code: RAII classes in C++ for files, locks and a transaction that rolls back unless committed; unique_ptr, shared_ptr and weak_ptr, with make_unique over raw new; Rust's moves, borrowing, lifetimes, interior mutability and Rc and Arc for shared ownership; cleanup functions and the GCC/Clang cleanup attribute in C, which has no RAII; bounds checking with containers, at() and std::span in C++ and slices in Rust; and data-race prevention with atomics, mutexes and read-write locks in both. Its rules include avoiding raw pointers except at C boundaries, never returning references to locals, keeping Rust's unsafe to a minimum and never assuming thread safety.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/systems-programming/skills/memory-safety-patterns"
