          "text": "A reference, not a workflow. Its framework has four parts: visibility (cost allocation tags, budget alerts, dashboards), right-sizing (downsizing, auto-scaling, removing idle resources), pricing models (reserved capacity, spot or preemptible instances, savings plans, committed use discounts) and architecture (managed services, caching, data transfer, lifecycle policies). For each provider it lists the main options: AWS Reserved Instances, Savings Plans and Spot; Azure reserved VMs, Hybrid Benefit for existing Windows Server and SQL Server licenses, and Advisor; GCP committed and sustained use discounts and preemptible VMs; OCI flexible shapes, commitments, compartment budgets and preemptible capacity. Four architecture patterns follow: serverless first, databases sized per environment, storage tiered from S3 Standard to Deep Archive, and auto-scaling. A reference file sets the required tags, how each provider uses them for billing, and rules such as enforcing tags in CI and auditing them weekly. It names cost tools per provider and across clouds, such as Cost Explorer, Azure Advisor, Kubecost and CloudHealth, and points to terraform-module-library for provisioning.",
