          "text": "A reference with worked examples, not a step-by-step workflow. It sorts data quality into six dimensions, completeness, uniqueness, validity, accuracy, consistency and timeliness, each with a matching Great Expectations check, and a testing pyramid of schema, column and cross-table tests. Its quick start installs Great Expectations and builds a first expectation suite. The reference file holds six patterns: an orders suite with schema, key, allowed-value, range, freshness, row-count and mean checks; a checkpoint that stores results, updates data docs and posts to Slack on failure; dbt tests for keys, relationships and accepted values, with dbt_utils; custom generic and singular dbt tests in SQL; a data contract in the datacontract.com format with PII flags, SodaCL checks and an SLA; and a Python pipeline that validates several tables and builds a Markdown report. Its rules: test source data before transforming it, add tests as you find issues, alert on failures, version contracts, focus on critical columns, and use dynamic baselines rather than hard-coded thresholds.",
