          ],
          "text": "A reference for the protection mechanisms an analyst meets during authorized reverse engineering, and how to neutralize each. It opens with an authorized-use banner and an input/output contract: you give a binary, platform and goal; it produces named protections with locations, a strategy per layer, an analysis report and code artifacts. A reference file covers Windows anti-debugging (API checks, PEB flags, timing, exceptions), Linux ptrace checks, anti-VM detection (hardware fingerprints, registry and file artifacts, timing), and obfuscation (control-flow flattening, opaque predicates, string and API obfuscation). A second reference file goes further into packers and unpacking, virtualization-based protection and anti-disassembly tricks. The SKILL.md's troubleshooting notes platform differences (x86 vs ARM), calibrating timing thresholds against false positives, and adjusting a comparison rather than a jump to avoid crashes. It points to the binary-analysis-patterns, memory-forensics and protocol-reverse-engineering skills.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/reverse-engineering/skills/anti-reversing-techniques"
