      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Combines vector similarity and keyword search for retrieval, fusing the two result sets, for RAG and search when neither alone has enough recall.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Pure vector search misses exact terms like names or codes, and you want keyword matching too"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs vector and keyword search in parallel and fuses their rankings into one result list"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A design and, in its reference file, templates; it names fusion methods and when each fits"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fusion methods",
              "text": "Reciprocal Rank Fusion for general use, a weighted linear sum for tunable balance, a cross-encoder rerank for quality, cascade filtering for efficiency"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short reference for hybrid retrieval, combining semantic vector search with keyword search so a query gets both meaning and exact matches. The SKILL.md diagrams the flow — the query fans out to a vector search and a keyword search, whose candidates are fused — and tabulates four fusion methods: Reciprocal Rank Fusion (general purpose, works without tuning), a weighted linear sum of scores (tunable), a cross-encoder reranker (highest quality) and a cascade that filters then reranks. Its advice: tune weights on your own data, start with RRF, add reranking for a quality jump, log both scores for debugging, A/B test real impact, and handle edge cases like empty results and single-word queries without over-fetching. The templates and worked examples are in a reference file.",
