          "text": "A practical guide to testing with assistive technology. The SKILL.md tabulates the major screen readers (JAWS ~40%, NVDA ~31%, VoiceOver ~15%, TalkBack, Narrator) with their platform and browser, recommends a minimum matrix (NVDA+Firefox, VoiceOver+Safari on macOS and iOS) and a comprehensive one, and explains the three interaction modes. It points to separate reference files for VoiceOver (macOS and iOS), NVDA and JAWS/TalkBack setup, commands and checklists to read before testing on each. It then gives common test scenarios with accessible HTML and JavaScript: a dialog with focus trap and restore, polite and assertive live regions and progress and log roles, and a tab interface with arrow-key navigation. A debugging snippet logs an element's role, accessible name and ARIA state. Do's and don'ts: test with real screen readers not simulators, semantic HTML before ARIA, test both modes, start keyboard-only, and don't assume one reader is enough.",
