              "text": "Cosine, Euclidean, dot-product or Manhattan distance; a flat (exact), HNSW (graph) or IVF+PQ (quantized) index, with their speed and recall trade-offs"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short reference for production similarity search. The SKILL.md tabulates four distance metrics and when each fits — cosine for normalized embeddings, Euclidean for raw vectors, dot product when magnitude matters, Manhattan for sparse vectors — and three index families with their complexity and recall: flat exact search (O(n), 100% recall, small data), HNSW (O(log n), 95–99%, medium to large) and IVF+PQ (O(√n), 90–95%, very large). Its advice: use HNSW for most cases, tune ef_search or nprobe for the recall/speed balance, add keyword search for hybrid retrieval, pre-filter to shrink the search space, measure recall before optimizing, start flat and scale up, watch P99 latency, and remember storage cost. The templates and worked examples are in a reference file.",
