          "text": "A compliance reference for developers. It groups PCI DSS's 12 requirements under secure networks, protecting cardholder data, vulnerability management, access control, monitoring and security policy, and gives the four merchant levels by yearly transactions. Full magnetic-stripe data, CVV and PIN may never be stored; card number, name and expiry only when encrypted. Python examples mask card numbers in logs to the first six and last four digits, raise an error on prohibited fields, charge a card and attach it to a Stripe customer through tokens, so card details never reach the server, build a token vault with Fernet for when you must store cards, encrypt data at rest with AES-256-GCM, and enforce HTTPS and secure cookies in Flask. A reference file adds a decorator that restricts and logs access to card data, audit logging, input validation, the three main questionnaires (SAQ A for hosted payment pages, A-EP for embedded forms, D for anyone who stores or processes cards, from about 20 to about 300 questions) and a checklist across network security, data protection, vulnerability management, access control, monitoring and policy.",
