      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets up mutual TLS between services for zero-trust networking in Istio or Linkerd, with cert-manager or SPIRE certificates, rotation and handshake debugging.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want services in a Kubernetes service mesh to prove their identity to each other, or a TLS handshake between them is failing"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Explains the mTLS handshake, where each side checks the other's certificate, and a certificate chain from a root CA down to short-lived workload certificates"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Istio PeerAuthentication and DestinationRule YAML, cert-manager and SPIRE configs, Linkerd policies, and commands to check certificates and debug handshakes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Key advice",
              "text": "Move from permissive to strict mode gradually, keep workload certificates to 24 hours or less and automate rotation"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Mutual TLS means both sides of a connection present a certificate, not only the server, so each service can verify who is calling. The SKILL.md shows the handshake between two sidecar proxies and a hierarchy of a long-lived root CA, intermediate CAs per cluster or across clusters, and workload certificates. Its rules: start permissive and tighten to strict, alert on expiry, rotate the CA, log TLS errors; never disable mTLS for convenience, ignore expiry, use self-signed certificates instead of a proper CA hierarchy or skip chain verification. A reference file has the templates: Istio strict mode mesh-wide with a permissive override for a legacy namespace and a per-port exception for metrics, DestinationRules for mTLS inside the mesh, one-way TLS to an external API and mutual TLS to a partner's, a cert-manager issuer with 24-hour certificates, a SPIRE server and agent, and Linkerd's automatic mTLS with server policies and skipped ports. It ends with commands to read a proxy's certificate, force rotation by restarting a deployment and debug handshakes with istioctl, kubectl logs and linkerd viz.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/cloud-infrastructure/skills/mtls-configuration"
        },
