            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "IPFS metadata JSON with traits, on-chain metadata with a placeholder SVG, EIP-2981 royalties capped at 10%, soulbound tokens, NFTs that level up and ERC721A for cheaper batch mints"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Templates for the two NFT standards. ERC-721 gives every token its own ID: the example caps supply at 10,000, charges a price per mint, limits mints per transaction, sets an IPFS token URI and lets the owner withdraw the proceeds. ERC-1155 holds many token types in one contract: the example defines a sword, a shield and a potion with separate supply caps, owner-only single and batch minting, and burning. Metadata can live off-chain as JSON on IPFS with name, description, image and attributes, or be generated on-chain as Base64 JSON with an SVG image, whose drawing is left as a stub. Royalties follow EIP-2981, which tells marketplaces who receives what share of a resale. A reference file adds soulbound tokens, which can be minted and burned but never transferred; dynamic NFTs whose level rises with experience, with the metadata that follows their state left as a stub; and ERC721A, a library that makes minting several tokens at once cheaper in gas.",
