    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/agent-teams/skills/parallel-debugging",
      "name": "parallel-debugging",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/parallel-debugging",
        "installs": 10500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [189, 221, 268, 216, 187, 185, 171, 158],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-05"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Debugs hard bugs by having parallel investigators test competing hypotheses, then weighing their cited evidence to name the root cause.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A bug has several plausible causes, first attempts failed, or it spans several modules and you want to avoid fixing the first theory that fits"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Draws hypotheses from six failure types, has investigators gather evidence for each, then sorts them into confirmed, plausible, falsified or inconclusive"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A root cause backed by file:line evidence and a confidence level, a compound cause, or new hypotheses when none holds up"
            },
            {
              "label": "Terms",
              "text": "Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH) tests several explanations side by side instead of chasing one"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A method for running several lines of investigation at once, written for a team of agents, though it names no particular tool. Hypotheses come from six failure categories: logic errors, data issues, state problems such as races and stale caches, integration failures, resource exhaustion and environment differences. Evidence is graded as direct (the code at a line), correlational (errors rose after a commit), testimonial (\"works on my machine\") or absence (no null check on the path), and is always cited with file:line. Confidence is high above 80%, with several direct pieces and nothing contradicting, medium from 50% and low below. Once every investigator reports, each hypothesis is labelled confirmed, plausible, falsified or inconclusive, and confirmed ones are ranked by confidence, amount of evidence, strength of the causal chain and lack of contradiction. One clear winner is the root cause, a tie may be a compound issue, and no winner means new hypotheses from what was learned. Before the bug counts as fixed, the fix must address the root cause, break nothing new, pass the original reproduction, cover related edge cases and come with tests.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/agent-teams/skills/parallel-debugging"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "debugging",
          "multi-agent"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/agent-teams/skills/parallel-feature-development",
      "name": "parallel-feature-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/parallel-feature-development",
        "installs": 10400,
        "weeklyInstalls": [190, 216, 277, 223, 194, 186, 171, 159],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-05"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Splits a feature across parallel implementer agents with one owner per file, shared interface contracts and a vertical, horizontal or hybrid integration plan.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A feature is big enough for two or more agents to build at once and you need to keep them out of each other's files"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Assigns file ownership by directory, module or layer, and has implementers code against contract files that none of them edit"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A split into vertical slices, horizontal layers or a mix, and a branch strategy: one shared branch or a sub-branch per implementer"
            },
            {
              "label": "Troubleshoots",
              "text": "Implementers blocking each other, merge conflicts in index files, early finishers, a wrong split and tests failing on drifted contracts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Rules for dividing one feature among several implementer agents. The cardinal rule is one owner per file. When a file needs changes from several implementers, one owns it and the others send change requests, or the shared part moves into a separate interface file. Interface contracts, such as a TypeScript types file owned by the team lead, let implementers build against each other's APIs before those exist. Vertical slices, where each implementer builds UI, API and tests for one feature, need little integration; horizontal layers, one implementer on all UI and another on all endpoints, keep each layer consistent but add integration points; a hybrid pairs a feature slice with shared infrastructure. A single branch suits two or three implementers with clear boundaries, sub-branches suit four or more. Its fixes: move shared code into a lead-owned contract, give barrel and index files one owner, write stubs so a finished implementer unblocks the others, stop and redistribute when the split is wrong, and broadcast before changing a contract. It points to team-composition-patterns and team-communication-protocols.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/agent-teams/skills/parallel-feature-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:agents",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "implementation",
          "git"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/agent-teams/skills/task-coordination-strategies",
      "name": "task-coordination-strategies",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/task-coordination-strategies",
        "installs": 10300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [187, 214, 272, 215, 202, 189, 176, 163],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-05"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Breaks work into tasks for an agent team, sets their dependencies, writes briefs with owned files and acceptance criteria, and rebalances workload.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're splitting a large task among teammates, deciding which tasks block which, or a team's workload has become uneven"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Splits work by layer, component, concern or file ownership, and keeps dependency graphs wide and shallow so more runs in parallel"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Task descriptions with objective, owned files, requirements, interface contracts, acceptance criteria and out-of-scope items, from a template"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Claude Code's Agent Teams task tools, which it uses to set blockers and reassign work"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Planning advice for a lead agent. It names four ways to split work: by layer for full-stack features, by component for modular systems, by concern for reviews and audits, and by file ownership to avoid conflicts. Dependency graphs should be wide and shallow, with blockedBy used only where truly needed and no cycles; the longest chain, the critical path, sets the minimum completion time. It draws three patterns, independent tasks feeding an integration task, a sequential chain and a diamond, and shows the TaskCreate and TaskUpdate calls that make an integration task wait for two others. Every task description carries six parts, shown in a template for an authentication API task. For workload, it reads the signals (a teammate idle while others are busy, one stuck on a task, everything blocked, one carrying three times the others' load) and rebalances: check TaskList, find idle or overloaded teammates, reassign with TaskUpdate, tell those affected with SendMessage and watch throughput.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/agent-teams/skills/task-coordination-strategies"
        },
        "tags": [
