          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "needs:claude-code",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "agent-workflows"
        ],
        "note": "Written for Claude Code's experimental Agent Teams feature, which the agent-teams plugin's README turns on with CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS=1.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/agent-teams/skills/team-composition-patterns",
      "name": "team-composition-patterns",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/team-composition-patterns",
        "installs": 10100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [185, 213, 268, 213, 180, 182, 168, 149],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-05"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans a Claude Code agent team: how many teammates, a preset such as review, debug or feature, each role's agent type and display mode.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to spawn a Claude Code agent team and need to choose its size, roles and agent types"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sizes the team by complexity, one or two agents for a simple job up to four or five for a full-stack or systemic one, as small as covers the work"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Seven preset teams (review, debug, feature, fullstack, research, security, migration) with roles and default focus, and a table of which agent type has which tools"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "tmux, iTerm2 or in-process display modes in settings.json, rules for custom teams and troubleshooting"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Team design for Claude Code's Agent Teams. Each preset names its size, agents and use: three reviewers on security, performance and architecture; three debuggers on competing hypotheses; a lead with two implementers for a feature, or three for full-stack work; three general-purpose researchers searching code and the web; four security reviewers on OWASP, auth, supply chain and secrets; and a migration team of a lead, two implementers and a reviewer. Agent types decide tools: general-purpose can edit files, Explore and Plan are read-only and must never get implementation work, and the plugin's team-reviewer, team-debugger, team-implementer and team-lead agents add the team task and messaging tools. The display mode, set as teammateMode in ~/.claude/settings.json, puts each teammate in a tmux pane or an iTerm2 tab, or keeps them all in one process for CI. Custom teams need a coordinator, roles matched to agent types, no duplicate roles, clear boundaries and two to four teammates. Troubleshooting covers read-only agents asked to write, teams too big to coordinate, tmux panes not showing, overlapping reviewers and teammates spawned without full context, since they start with no conversation history.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/agent-teams/skills/team-composition-patterns"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "needs:claude-code",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "agent-workflows"
        ],
        "note": "Its presets use the agent-teams plugin's team-lead, team-reviewer, team-debugger and team-implementer agents, which installing the skill alone doesn't add; the plugin's README also turns on the experimental Agent Teams feature with CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS=1.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/claude-plugins-official/plugins/claude-code-setup/skills/claude-automation-recommender",
      "name": "claude-automation-recommender",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/anthropics/claude-plugins-official/claude-automation-recommender",
        "installs": 11100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [215, 138, 112, 203, 418, 598, 741, 677],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-21"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "c07c748f1c680734a813eefd909bc073f8a9103f",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scans your codebase and recommends the Claude Code hooks, subagents, skills, plugins and MCP servers that would help it most, without changing files.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're setting up Claude Code on a project, or want to know which of its automation features would pay off there"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Inspects the project's manifests, dependencies, folders and existing .claude config, then picks the top one or two automations of each type"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report with a codebase profile and, per category, each recommendation with why it fits and where it goes, such as .claude/settings.json"
            },
            {
              "label": "Read-only",
              "text": "It creates and changes no files; you build the recommendations yourself or ask the agent separately"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Anthropic's claude-code-setup plugin. It looks at what the project is built with: package.json, pyproject.toml and similar files, frameworks and libraries such as React, Prisma, Supabase or Stripe, test and CI setup, issue-tracker references, and any existing .claude folder or CLAUDE.md. It maps those signals to Claude Code's five extension types. MCP servers connect outside tools, for example context7 for live library docs, Playwright for browser testing or the GitHub MCP server for issues and PRs. Skills package repeatable tasks, such as a create-migration or release-notes skill, with a note on whether you, Claude or both should invoke each. Hooks run on tool events, like auto-formatting after an edit or blocking edits to .env and lock files. Subagents are specialized reviewers, such as a security reviewer or test writer. Plugins bundle skills to install. It recommends one or two per category, three to five if you ask about one type, skips categories that don't apply, and uses web search to go beyond its reference lists. The report ends by offering more options or help implementing them.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/claude-plugins-official/tree/c07c748f1c680734a813eefd909bc073f8a9103f/plugins/claude-code-setup/skills/claude-automation-recommender"
        },
