      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a Semgrep security scan over your codebase with third-party rulesets, after you approve the plan, and merges the findings into one SARIF file.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a first-pass static security scan of a codebase, before or alongside code review"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Detects languages, checks for Semgrep Pro, proposes rulesets and a mode, waits for your explicit yes, then runs them through its bundled script"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Per-scan output and one merged results.sarif in an output folder, plus a report that lists any ruleset that failed or was skipped"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "\"run all\" reports everything; \"important only\" keeps security findings of medium-to-high confidence and impact"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Semgrep CLI, jq and uv; Semgrep Pro is optional and adds cross-file taint tracking"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Trail of Bits' Semgrep skill, from its static-analysis plugin. It always adds third-party rulesets from Trail of Bits, 0xdea and Decurity when the language matches, because they catch bugs the official registry misses. Every command runs with --metrics=off so the scan sends no telemetry, and its run-scans.sh script, not the agent, writes the semgrep commands, runs them in parallel batches and records what ran, failed or was skipped in scans.json. The approval step is a hard gate: asking for a scan isn't approval, and the agent may not add rulesets you didn't approve. With Semgrep Pro it tracks taint across files; without it, analysis stays within single files. Cloned rule repos are deleted at the end. The plugin also ships a /static-analysis:semgrep-scan command that runs the same scan without stopping for approval, and for writing your own rules it points to the semgrep-rule-creator skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/static-analysis/skills/semgrep"
        },
        "tags": [
