        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a PR, commit or diff for security risks using git history, blast radius and test coverage, and writes a markdown report of findings.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're checking a change for vulnerabilities, a re-introduced old bug, what else it could break, or modified code no test covers"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Rates each changed file's risk, git-blames removed code, counts callers to size the blast radius, checks test coverage and models attackers for high-risk changes"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A markdown report file with findings tied to line numbers and commits, concrete attack scenarios and stated coverage limits"
            },
            {
              "label": "Scales",
              "text": "Reads everything on small codebases, one hop of dependencies on medium ones and only critical paths on large ones"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Trail of Bits' security review for code changes. How risky a change is, not how few lines it touches, decides how closely it is examined: authentication, crypto, external calls, value transfer and removed validation count as high risk, and a refactor is treated as high until shown otherwise. Blast radius is the number of callers a changed function has, counted rather than guessed. Removed code from commits about security, CVEs or fixes, dropped access-control modifiers and validation removed without replacement trigger adversarial analysis even in a quick triage. Its detailed methods live in separate methodology, adversarial, reporting and patterns files, and its plugin's adversarial-modeler agent can build exploit scenarios for high-risk changes. On large audits it pairs with the audit-context-building skill for baseline context. It isn't meant for greenfield code with nothing to compare against, or for documentation and formatting-only changes, and it always writes the report to a file rather than only to chat.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/differential-review/skills/differential-review"
        },
