      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scans a codebase for vulnerabilities with CodeQL data-flow and taint analysis: builds and checks the database, models project APIs and outputs SARIF.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want interprocedural security analysis of Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, Go, Java/Kotlin, C/C++, C#, Ruby or Swift code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds a CodeQL database, checks it really extracted code, adds data-extension models for project-specific sources and sinks, then runs an explicit query suite"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An output folder with the database, build log, extensions, and raw and final results.sarif"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike semgrep",
              "text": "That one is meant for fast pattern matching or code with no build; this one tracks data flow across functions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The CodeQL CLI, jq and uv on PATH; it stops before building if any is missing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Trail of Bits' CodeQL skill, from its static-analysis plugin. It is built around results you can trust: a database that builds may still have extracted nothing, so a quality script runs after every build, and zero findings is a reason to investigate rather than a clean bill. Pack names are never passed straight to the analyzer, whose default suites can silently drop queries; it generates an explicit suite and verifies it resolves to a non-zero query count. Run-all mode imports both security-and-quality and security-experimental; important-only keeps high-precision results. It uses Trail of Bits and community query packs where available, and data extensions describe the project's own wrappers around databases, request parsing and shell calls that CodeQL doesn't model. If databases already exist it asks whether to reuse one or build new. On Apple Silicon it works around arm64e build failures before falling back to build-mode=none. The plugin also ships a /static-analysis:codeql-build command that drives the database build unattended.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/static-analysis/skills/codeql"
