        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes, reviews and debugs property-based tests that check a rule across all inputs, from encode/decode roundtrips to Solidity contract invariants.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Code has an algebraic shape, such as encode/decode pairs, parsers, normalizers, sort order or contract state, that example tests only spot-check"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks the strongest property the code supports from a catalog, and loads its guide for writing, refactoring, reviewing or reading a failure"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Property tests in the project's existing library, a review of existing ones, or a verdict that the code suits example tests better"
            },
            {
              "label": "Catches",
              "text": "Tests that restate the implementation, and assume() filters that let nearly every case pass untested"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Hypothesis, fast-check, proptest, jqwik, rapid, and Echidna or Medusa for Solidity"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. An example test checks one input; a property states a rule, such as decode(encode(x)) == x, and lets a generator hunt for a counterexample. Its catalog covers roundtrip, inverse, oracle (matching a reference implementation), idempotence, invariants, easy-to-verify outputs, commutativity, associativity and identity, ranked from weakest (no crash) to strongest (roundtrip or oracle). When a calculation is buried in I/O or mutation, it looks for a refactor that exposes the property before concluding there is nothing to assert, and calling code a poor fit for property tests is a valid result. It also covers adding cases to existing suites, judging whether property tests assert anything real, and telling a wrong property from a genuine bug once a counterexample has shrunk. If the project has no property-testing library, it offers one once, with the property it would write, and leaves the choice to you. It isn't for coverage-guided fuzzing, mutation testing or end-to-end UI tests.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/property-based-testing/skills/property-based-testing"
        },
