      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Maps what each function in unfamiliar code assumes, guarantees and depends on, before any bug hunting, and writes a dossier of the results.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're starting an audit, threat model or architecture review on code you don't know yet"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Dispatches a workflow that analyzes each function in its own subagent and returns compact records, keeping the long analysis out of your context"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "audit-context/DOSSIER.md plus one file per function under audit-context/functions/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Doesn't",
              "text": "Name vulnerabilities, suggest fixes, write proofs of concept or rate severity; that's the bug-hunting phase that follows"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Trail of Bits' first pass on an audit: build understanding, not verdicts. For a codebase it runs the plugin's audit-context command; for a single function, its function-analyzer agent. Each record lists what must always be true, with the line that shows it, what the function takes on faith and what establishes it, what it needs from each function it calls, and what is still unclear. The dossier adds rules that span several functions, who can reach what, and where complexity clusters. Its core rule is to follow the calls: read each called function and every path through it, cite a line for every claim, and write \"nothing found\" when nothing makes an assumption true. Those unenforced assumptions and the open questions are what it hands the bug-hunting phase. Where two records disagree, both are quoted rather than reconciled. The same format covers smart contracts, C and C++, decompiled firmware and web services.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/audit-context-building/skills/audit-context-building"
