        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Keeps your workplace's people, projects and shorthand in CLAUDE.md and a memory folder, so the agent decodes requests like a colleague.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the agent to understand your workplace's nicknames, acronyms and project codenames without asking every time"
            },
            {
              "label": "It keeps",
              "text": "A short CLAUDE.md of your top people, terms, active projects and preferences, and a memory/ folder with a full glossary and per-person and per-project files"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Decodes each request from CLAUDE.md, then the glossary and memory files, then by asking you, and saves what you tell it to remember"
            },
            {
              "label": "For example",
              "text": "\"ask todd to do the PSR for oracle\" becomes a request to Todd Martinez, Finance lead, for the Pipeline Status Report on the Oracle Systems deal"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "On its own when the agent sees a fit; user-invocable: false hides it from the slash menu"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Workplace memory from the productivity plugin of Anthropic's knowledge-work plugins, in plain Markdown files in the current folder. Working memory is CLAUDE.md, kept to about 50 to 100 lines of tables: you, about 30 people you deal with most, about 30 common terms, 5 to 15 active projects, and your preferences. Deep memory is a memory/ folder: glossary.md, the full list of acronyms, internal terms, nicknames and codenames, plus people/, projects/ and context/ files with profiles, project details, teams, tools and processes. Before acting on a request, the agent decodes it from CLAUDE.md first, then the glossary and memory files, and asks you about anything still unknown. When you say \"remember this\" or \"X means Y\", it files the item in the glossary or a person or project file, and in CLAUDE.md if it's used often. Frequent items are promoted to CLAUDE.md and stale ones demoted to memory/ only. To bootstrap memory by scanning your chat, calendar, email and documents, it points you to /productivity:start.",
