              "text": "PreMortem-[product-name]-[date].md, with Tigers marked launch-blocking, fast-follow or track, and a mitigation, owner and due date for each blocker"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike strategy-red-team",
              "text": "That one, its sibling in the same plugin, attacks the plan's assumptions now instead of narrating a failure"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From the PM Skills marketplace's pm-execution plugin. The agent plays a veteran product manager. It reads the PRD or plan you give it, searching the web for the competitive landscape or market when relevant, then assumes the launch has failed: customers didn't adopt it, revenue missed, reputation took a hit. Each cause it finds becomes a Tiger, a real problem backed by evidence, experience or clear logic; a Paper Tiger, a concern others might raise that it judges unlikely or overblown, written down to align stakeholders; or an Elephant, something the team isn't discussing enough that deserves investigation. Tigers are then ranked launch-blocking (solve before launch), fast-follow (within 30 days of launch) or track (watch after launch). Every launch-blocking Tiger gets a concrete mitigation, an owner and a decision or completion date. When unsure, it calls a risk a Tiger, and it draws on engineering, design and go-to-market perspectives. It suggests revisiting the pre-mortem two to three weeks before launch to check the mitigations are on track.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/phuryn/pm-skills/tree/2a12295a7717569baa7be181b4cfebe3ca38a6bb/pm-execution/skills/pre-mortem"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "phuryn/pm-skills/pm-execution/skills/prioritization-frameworks",
      "name": "prioritization-frameworks",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/phuryn/pm-skills/prioritization-frameworks",
        "installs": 10200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [107, 93, 84, 81, 78, 130, 104, 90],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "9af7bfd0bc47fdad43c2eaa7a43cd13918e7aaef",
        "updated": "2026-11-09",
        "skew": 33
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Explains nine prioritization frameworks, from Opportunity Score and ICE to RICE, Kano and MoSCoW, with formulas and when to use each.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're choosing how to prioritize, comparing RICE and ICE, or learning how a framework works"
            },
            {
              "label": "Core rule",
              "text": "Prioritize customer problems, not features, and never let customers design the solution"
            },
            {
              "label": "Recommends",
              "text": "Opportunity Score, importance × (1 − satisfaction), for customer problems, and ICE for quick prioritization of ideas"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Formulas for Opportunity Score, ICE and RICE, a table of all nine frameworks with what each suits, and links to templates"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "As a reference only: no steps and no file"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference card from the PM Skills marketplace's pm-execution plugin. It starts from one principle: rank problems (opportunities), not features. Opportunity Score, from Dan Olsen's The Lean Product Playbook, surveys customers on how important each need is and how satisfied they are, normalized to 0–1; importance × (1 − satisfaction) finds the biggest gaps, plotted on an importance versus satisfaction chart. ICE multiplies impact (Opportunity Score × customers affected) by confidence and ease, scored 1–10. RICE splits reach out of impact, takes confidence as a percentage and divides by effort in person-months, for larger teams. A table places all nine: the Eisenhower Matrix for personal tasks, Impact vs Effort for quick triage, Risk vs Reward when uncertainty matters, Opportunity Score for customer problems, Kano for understanding expectations rather than ranking, the Weighted Decision Matrix for stakeholder buy-in, ICE, RICE, and MoSCoW for requirements, with a caution about its project-management origin. It links Google Sheets and Slides templates for Opportunity Score, ICE and RICE.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/phuryn/pm-skills/tree/2a12295a7717569baa7be181b4cfebe3ca38a6bb/pm-execution/skills/prioritization-frameworks"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "phuryn/pm-skills/pm-execution/skills/outcome-roadmap",
      "name": "outcome-roadmap",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/phuryn/pm-skills/outcome-roadmap",
        "installs": 10200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [108, 86, 82, 74, 75, 132, 95, 86],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "2a12295a7717569baa7be181b4cfebe3ca38a6bb",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Rewrites a feature roadmap as outcome statements, \"Enable [segment] to [outcome] so that [business impact]\", with success metrics and dependencies.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Your roadmap lists features and you want it to say what each will change for customers and the business"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks of each initiative what outcome it serves, which customer problem it solves and which metric moves, then rewrites it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Format",
              "text": "Enable [customer segment] to [desired customer outcome] so that [business impact]"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The roadmap by quarter or phase, each initiative beside its outcome statement, success metrics and sequencing notes, plus strategic context"
            },
            {
              "label": "Writes",
              "text": "Outcome-Roadmap-[year].md when the result is substantial"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From the PM Skills marketplace's pm-execution plugin. Feature-list roadmaps, it argues, create false precision and align teams on output instead of results. The agent reads your current roadmap and, if you mention strategy documents or company objectives, searches the web to see how the roadmap should line up with them. For each initiative it names the planned output, asks why it's being built and what changes for customers or the business, considers whether there's a better way to reach the same outcome, and rewrites it as an outcome statement. Its example turns \"build advanced search filters\" into \"enable customers to find products 50% faster through intuitive discovery\". The result lists the original initiatives by quarter or phase beside their outcomes, the metrics that will show success, and dependencies, then adds how the outcomes align with company strategy, key assumptions about customer needs, and flexible release windows in quarters rather than dates. Outcomes should be testable and measurable; when it's unclear what a feature drives, it keeps asking \"so what?\" until it reaches real customer or business value.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/phuryn/pm-skills/tree/2a12295a7717569baa7be181b4cfebe3ca38a6bb/pm-execution/skills/outcome-roadmap"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:command",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "phuryn/pm-skills/pm-execution/skills/strategy-red-team",
      "name": "strategy-red-team",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/phuryn/pm-skills/strategy-red-team",
        "installs": 9600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [137, 115, 113, 98, 103, 163, 134, 111],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-05"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "2a12295a7717569baa7be181b4cfebe3ca38a6bb",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Red-teams a PRD, roadmap or strategy, attacking its load-bearing assumptions and giving each the cheapest test and a kill criterion.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're stress-testing a plan, challenging its assumptions or preparing a document for executive review"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Lists what the plan claims, keeps the load-bearing claims that would kill it if false, and attacks the strongest version of each"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Three to five kill-assumptions ranked by impact × likelihood × cheapness to test, each with how it fails, evidence to get this week, a kill criterion and the cheapest test"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also says",
              "text": "What's well reasoned and why, and what it couldn't assess"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike pre-mortem",
              "text": "That one imagines the launch already failed; this one attacks the assumptions now, while the cheapest test can still run"
            },
            {
              "label": "Optional",
              "text": "On request, and when another model such as Codex or Gemini is reachable, runs the plan past it and flags where they disagree"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From the PM Skills marketplace's pm-execution plugin. The agent plays a sharp but fair adversary. It extracts every claim the plan makes about users, market, constraints, mechanism and timeline, and separates load-bearing claims from cosmetic ones. For each load-bearing claim it first states the strongest case for it, then attacks that case, writing each failure mode as a concrete, falsifiable \"Fails if ___\". It ranks them by impact if wrong × likelihood of being wrong × cheapness to test, so the top of the list is what to test this week. It treats a risk as real unless the plan already cites evidence against it, but says plainly when a claim is well reasoned and never invents a weakness. Each surviving assumption gets the evidence to collect this week, the threshold at which you'd stop or change course, and the smallest experiment that would move the belief. The output is a Markdown reply, not a saved file, meant to be screenshotted, with no generic risk lists, and it ends on what to do rather than what to fear.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/phuryn/pm-skills/tree/2a12295a7717569baa7be181b4cfebe3ca38a6bb/pm-execution/skills/strategy-red-team"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "shopify/shopify-ai-toolkit/skills/ucp",
      "name": "ucp",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/shopify/shopify-ai-toolkit/ucp",
        "installs": 7800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [307, 315, 322, 325, 320, 339, 266, 261],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-15"
