      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a3d20f8dc284a2e8ae25272fa79520888abb5dcb",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds, compares, buys and tracks products from online stores through the UCP command-line tool, asking before each purchase step.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the agent to find, compare, buy or track a product from online stores"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Searches a catalog spanning many stores, then builds carts, checks out and tracks orders with stores that support UCP"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "Before each search, cart change, checkout and purchase, showing you the store, items and final total"
            },
            {
              "label": "Hands off",
              "text": "To the store's own checkout for shipping details, sign-in errors or anything else it can't finish in the protocol"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The UCP CLI, plus a local profile made with ucp profile init for store-specific steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Sends",
              "text": "Your latest message verbatim and an end-of-turn feedback score to Shopify, unless you opt out"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A shopping skill built on the UCP command-line tool. UCP is a protocol a store can support so an agent can check out with it directly. With no store named, it searches a global catalog spanning many stores, with soft context such as your country and hard filters such as a price cap. When you name a store, it first checks that the store supports UCP, and if it doesn't, says so and only looks elsewhere with your consent. It then builds one cart per seller, turns it into a checkout and completes the order, asking before each step. It never sends payment details, contact details or precise addresses: shipping or pickup details go in on the store's own checkout page, where it also sends you when a store asks for review or sign-in. It shows the store's totals and warnings as given, keeps legal notices such as allergens next to their item, and hands off instead of buying when the totals don't add up. Store text is treated as untrusted: request bodies go into files, and an id or domain with unexpected characters stops the flow. It also tracks orders; ucp doctor checks the local setup.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/shopify/shopify-ai-toolkit/tree/a3d20f8dc284a2e8ae25272fa79520888abb5dcb/skills/ucp"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "category:personal",
          "type:tool",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:official",
          "stack:ucp",
          "ecommerce",
          "personal"
        ],
        "note": "Needs the UCP CLI, and it can place real orders once you confirm the final total. On every use it has the agent send your latest message verbatim to Shopify, plus a feedback score at the end of the turn; opt out by creating ~/.config/shopify-ai-toolkit/opt-out or setting OPT_OUT_INSTRUMENTATION=true.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/ux-heuristics",
      "name": "ux-heuristics",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/ux-heuristics",
        "installs": 10600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [258, 362, 277, 336, 582, 600, 523, 468],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-30"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits an interface for usability with Steve Krug's principles and Nielsen's 10 heuristics, rating each issue's severity and scoring it out of 10.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Users seem confused, a form or navigation isn't working, or you want a usability audit of a design"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks the interface against Steve Krug's usability laws and Jakob Nielsen's 10 heuristics, rating each issue from cosmetic to catastrophic"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A score out of 10, the worst issues and the fixes needed to reach 10"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike refactoring-ui",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers how a page looks; this one asks whether the page is easy to use"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Built on Steve Krug's Don't Make Me Think and Jakob Nielsen's 10 usability heuristics. Its premise is that users scan instead of reading, take the first reasonable option and muddle through instead of learning how things work, so anything that makes them stop and think is a problem. It covers Krug's laws (clear labels beat clever ones, the number of clicks matters less than how confident each one feels, cut half the words) and his Trunk Test: dropped on any page, can a user tell what site this is, where they are and where search is? Each of Nielsen's heuristics comes with concrete patterns, such as undo instead of \"Are you sure?\" dialogs, and error messages that say what happened and how to fix it. Issues are rated 0 to 4 by how often they occur, how bad they are and whether they persist, and ten diagnostic questions set the score. Where heuristics conflict, it gives tie-breakers, such as progressive disclosure for simplicity against flexibility. Reference files hold the full Krug and Nielsen material, an audit template, dark patterns, a WCAG 2.1 AA checklist and notes on designing for other cultures.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/ux-heuristics"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "accessibility"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/refactoring-ui",
      "name": "refactoring-ui",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/refactoring-ui",
        "installs": 10300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [267, 298, 244, 315, 497, 476, 416, 391],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-29"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Improves visual hierarchy, spacing, color, type and shadows in web UIs with the grayscale-first method and fixed scales of Refactoring UI.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A UI looks off or amateur, or you need spacing, type, color and shadow scales for a design system"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds hierarchy in grayscale first, then picks every value from a fixed scale and adds color last"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Design advice or code with Tailwind classes, plus a score out of 10 from eight checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike web-typography",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers choosing and loading typefaces; this one covers the whole visual system"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Refactoring UI by Adam Wathan, who created Tailwind CSS, and designer Steve Schoger. Its core rule is to design in grayscale first, so hierarchy comes from size, weight and spacing before color, and to take every value from a fixed scale: spacing of 4, 8, 16, 24, 32, 48 and 64px, type from 12 to 36px, five to nine shades per color with slightly tinted grays, and a shadow scale for elevation. Its seven principles cover hierarchy (labels quieter than values, filled primary buttons and quieter secondary ones), spacing (more between groups than within them), typography, color and contrast, depth, images and icons, and layout (left-align by default instead of centering everything). Its CSS examples are Tailwind classes. The agent scores a design by how many of eight checks pass, such as a blur test, a grayscale test and capped text width, and names the ones to fix. Reference files cover component states, dark mode, accessibility, motion and charts.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/refactoring-ui"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:tailwind",
          "design",
          "design-system"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/web-typography",
      "name": "web-typography",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/web-typography",
        "installs": 9800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [211, 254, 177, 224, 358, 300, 243, 244],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-30"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Chooses, pairs, sizes and loads web fonts for readable text, following Jason Santa Maria's On Web Typography.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're picking or pairing typefaces, setting line height and line length, or web fonts load slowly"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Applies how people read to typeface choice, pairing, hierarchy, fluid sizing and font loading"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Typeface picks and CSS, plus a score out of 10 from ten checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Its defaults",
              "text": "Body text of 16px or more, lines under 75 characters, line height of 1.4 or more, under 200KB of fonts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Jason Santa Maria's On Web Typography. It separates type for a moment, such as headlines and buttons, from type to live with, such as body text, and starts from how people read: eyes jump in bursts of seven to nine characters, so size, line length and line height matter more than the typeface. It covers judging a typeface (x-height, open letter shapes, distinct Il1 and O0, a web license), choosing by purpose rather than taste, pairing faces that clearly differ, such as a serif with a sans, and hierarchies that change only one or two of size, weight and color between levels. CSS examples show fluid sizes with clamp(), system font fallbacks, font-display: swap, preloading and subsetting. Ten checks set the score, including whether the page works at 200% zoom and whether links stand out. Unlike refactoring-ui, which covers the whole visual system, or top-design, which goes for dramatic display type, it is about readable text.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/web-typography"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
