          "performance"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/clean-architecture",
      "name": "clean-architecture",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/clean-architecture",
        "installs": 7900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [206, 232, 197, 206, 356, 307, 222, 221],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Structures code so business rules don't depend on frameworks or databases, following Robert C. Martin's Clean Architecture and SOLID principles.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Deciding which layer code belongs in, keeping business logic apart from the database or framework, or untangling module dependencies"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Applies the Dependency Rule: code depends only inward, from frameworks to adapters to use cases to business entities"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Architecture advice and a score out of 10 that names each failed check and the dependency to invert"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike domain-driven-design",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, models the business itself; this one arranges layers and dependencies"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Robert C. Martin's Clean Architecture. It pictures a system as rings: business entities at the center, then use cases (one per application operation, such as PlaceOrder), then adapters such as controllers, presenters and repositories, with frameworks and databases on the outside. Source code may only depend inward, so business rules never import the web framework or the ORM, and data crosses each boundary as plain objects shaped for the inner ring. Interfaces are defined inside and implemented outside, and one entry point, Main, wires the concrete parts together. It also covers six principles for grouping classes into deployable components, such as no dependency cycles, the five SOLID principles with examples, and where to draw boundaries, including the Humble Object pattern that keeps hard-to-test code thin. Seven diagnostic questions, such as whether business rules can be tested without a database or web server, set the score. Reference files work through each topic with code.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/clean-architecture"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "code-quality"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/top-design",
      "name": "top-design",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/top-design",
        "installs": 7700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [185, 215, 158, 178, 309, 264, 222, 208],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-02"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Pushes websites toward award-winning agency craft: dramatic type, choreographed motion, scroll storytelling, and a weighted design score out of 10.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a portfolio, brand or landing page to look like an Awwwards winner rather than a template"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scores the design on typography, composition, motion, color and details, then pushes each toward agency-level craft"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A weighted score out of 10 and the changes needed to reach 10"
            },
            {
              "label": "Insists on",
              "text": "Display type at least ten times body size, custom easing, smooth scrolling, no Inter as the main font, no pure black, custom cursors only if asked"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike refactoring-ui",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, is for clean, systematic UI; this one is for showpiece sites"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It draws on agencies that win Awwwards and FWA awards, such as Locomotive, Studio Freight and AREA 17. Seven pillars: type as the design itself, with display type at least ten times body size and distinctive fonts instead of Inter or Roboto; asymmetric layouts that break a strong grid on purpose; motion with custom easing curves and a timed page-load sequence; colors invented for the project, never pure black or white; scroll as storytelling, with a smooth-scroll library and pinned sections; performance targets such as near-zero layout shift and the main content loading within 2.5 seconds; and crafted details like selection colors, focus states and error pages. Its process starts from a concept and the one moment people would screenshot, then the display typeface, with motion prototyped alongside the visuals. Twelve diagnostic questions map to the score. It sets limits too: respect reduced-motion settings, no scroll hijacking, no parallax on text, and custom cursors only with your approval. Reference files hold animation code, layout systems, its recommended libraries and agency case studies.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/top-design"
        },
