    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/microinteractions",
      "name": "microinteractions",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/microinteractions",
        "installs": 7300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [197, 233, 178, 181, 317, 261, 203, 241],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs small product moments such as toggles, loading states and form feedback with Dan Saffer's trigger, rules, feedback and loops structure.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An interface feels dead, or you're designing a button, toggle, loading state or form validation"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Splits each small interaction into what starts it, what happens, what the user sees, and how it behaves over time"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Design advice and a score out of 10 from eight checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules of thumb",
              "text": "Feedback within 100ms and scaled to the event, few modes, hints that fade as users learn"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike refactoring-ui",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers a page's overall look; this one designs one interaction at a time"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Dan Saffer's Microinteractions. A microinteraction is one small, contained moment, such as flipping a setting, choosing a password or pulling to refresh. Each has four parts: a trigger that starts it and must show that it exists, what it does and what state it's in; rules that decide what happens, including edge cases like zero, the maximum and a double tap; feedback that shows what's happening, ideally by animating the element itself rather than adding a toast; and loops and modes, which decide how it changes with use and when the same control acts differently. It adds signature moments, one or two distinctive interactions that become part of a product's identity, like the Facebook Like, and reduction: smart defaults and single actions instead of settings. Each section draws an ethical line, such as no fake progress bars or pre-checked marketing opt-ins. Reference files hold case studies and more on each part.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/microinteractions"
        },
        "tags": [
