      "skillMd": {
        "version": "9504989f162a92b1996853eccbb3d8a86e73df68",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Keeps interfaces usable for everyone: contrast, keyboard and focus, states, zoom and real controls, with a script that computes WCAG contrast ratios.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "The agent builds or edits UI and you want it usable by people with different eyes, hands and setups"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Lists accessibility failures as Tell, Why and Fix: grey-on-grey text, removed focus outlines, mouse-only patterns, color-only feedback, divs posing as buttons, unlabelled fields"
            },
            {
              "label": "Contrast checker",
              "text": "A bundled Python script, or the WCAG formula and a reference table when it can't run, so contrast is computed rather than guessed"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A checklist run alongside the core's Delivery Gate"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The core antislop skill loaded with it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "antislop's accessibility skill, for the parts of a UI that exclude people. Contrast entries cover text over photos and gradients checked across the whole area, grey-on-grey text, and 3:1 non-text contrast for component edges and status indicators. Keyboard and focus entries cover removed outlines, mouse-only patterns, broken tab order, weak focus indicators and color-only feedback. It also covers missing empty, loading and error states, text that can't zoom to 200%, the mobile keyboard covering a form, and interaction affordances: a pointer cursor on every clickable control, native elements instead of divs, names for icon-only buttons, hover, active and disabled states, motion that respects reduced-motion settings, real field labels, and page-level semantics such as lang, landmarks and one h1 for whole documents. The contrast script takes two hex colors and prints the ratio with a pass or fail for normal and large text; it compares unrounded ratios, so 4.498 fails 4.5. Reference files hold copy-ready markup for patterns such as focus traps and skip links, and every WCAG 2.2 success criterion by level.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/miqdadbadjuber/anti-slop/tree/9504989f162a92b1996853eccbb3d8a86e73df68/skills/antislop-human"
