              "text": "For Excel and Google Sheets, such as SUMIFS or VLOOKUP, with each argument explained and where to put it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Limits",
              "text": "By its own account it can't run code on your data, and large datasets may need sampling"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Claude Office Skills. You share a spreadsheet and say what you're after: an overview, an answer to a specific question such as revenue by region or whether two columns correlate, a chart, or a formula. The agent answers in the skill's templates. A dataset overview lists rows, columns and date range, each column's type, completeness and sample values, and flags missing values and likely duplicates. A statistical summary gives mean, median, standard deviation and range with findings, and an insight report opens with an executive summary, then key metrics, trends and recommendations. Chart choices follow a table: line for trends, pie for part of a whole, bar for comparisons, histogram for distributions, scatter for correlation, map for geography. It also specifies pivot tables and cohort tables, and lays out sales, customer and financial analyses as sequences of questions. Its best practices: clean the data first, define each metric, consider seasonality and benchmarks, label charts, don't truncate axes misleadingly, and allow for colorblind readers. Its frontmatter lists spreadsheet, chart and pivot-table tools of the repo's office-mcp server, but its instructions never call them, and every output is a Markdown write-up.",
