      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns an idea into a /goal-ready package: an interview and fact review in Plannotator, then a codebase plan you approve.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have an idea or objective and want it pinned down into testable outcomes and a plan before an agent runs it with /goal"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Restates your goal, interviews you with one bundle of questions in Plannotator's browser UI, has you review the resulting facts, then explores the code and writes a plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A goals/<slug>/ folder with facts.md, plan.md and goal.md, plus JSON of your answers, ready to launch with /goal"
            },
            {
              "label": "Facts",
              "text": "Plain, testable statements of what the finished work does, each marked for automated verification or not"
            },
            {
              "label": "Plan approval",
              "text": "The plan opens in Plannotator as a gate; if you deny it, the agent revises from your notes until you approve"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Plannotator CLI"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of Plannotator's extra skills. Plannotator is a local, browser-based review surface for agents' plans and code. Setup goal starts by restating what you want in two or three sentences and waits for you to confirm. For a vague goal with many linked decisions it suggests, and runs on request, an optional grilling pass first: one question at a time, adapted verbatim from Matt Pocock's grill-me skill. The interview bundle holds only questions that need your judgment, each with a recommended answer and options; anything the codebase can answer, it looks up instead of asking. If your answers carry doubts or questions, it settles them in chat before writing facts. Facts you accept, edit or remove in the UI are applied as given, and those marked for automated verification need concrete automated checks in the plan. The plan lists the approach, ordered steps with the files each touches, a check for each step, and risks. It waits as long as you take in each browser session, and stops if you close one.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/backnotprop/plannotator/tree/b5bab995016a0f8443c7c4fb5d82b78fbcf0dba3/apps/skills/extra/plannotator-setup-goal"
