      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs polished web pages, prototypes, slide decks and dashboards in HTML, checking a design system and a rough first draft with you.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a web page, dashboard, prototype, HTML slide deck, animation or UI mockup that looks designed rather than generated"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks facts about named products, asks only what the brief lacks, gathers brand assets, declares a design system and waits for your OK before coding"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "HTML files built in stages: a v0 with placeholders for early correction, then the full build, with a Tweaks panel for live settings and variants"
            },
            {
              "label": "When you're unsure",
              "text": "It proposes three contrasting design directions, each tied to a named designer or studio, instead of a list of taste questions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Avoids",
              "text": "AI clichés such as purple-to-pink gradients, emoji icons and Inter headlines unless your brand uses them, and never invents stats or testimonials"
            },
            {
              "label": "Browser QA",
              "text": "Only when you ask for acceptance testing, QA or responsive checks; otherwise a code and design self-check"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent works as a design engineer whose output is always HTML, CSS and JavaScript or React. It verifies facts about any named product before designing, then decides how much to ask: a vague brief gets questions, a detailed one gets built. Context comes in priority order, from your screenshots, Figma files or code down to a named style such as \"Linear-style\", which loads one of its style recipes. For branded work it insists on the real logo and product images, listed in a brand-spec.md file, and stops to ask when it can't find a logo. Before coding it sums up the brief as a design read with five 1–10 dials (variance, motion, density, asset dependence, brand fidelity) and declares colors, type, spacing, radius, shadows and motion. Three checkpoints follow: confirm the system, review the v0, and confirm non-trivial decisions during the build. On request it critiques a design on five dimensions scored 0–10, with fixes and quick wins. It also sets rules for prototypes, 1920×1080 slide decks, Chart.js or D3 dashboards and animations.",
