      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Searches your past agent conversations, indexed by episodic-memory, for earlier decisions, fixes and context before the agent guesses or starts over.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A task resembles past work, or you say \"last time\", \"we discussed\" or \"why did we\", and the answer lives in an earlier conversation"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "In Claude Code, hands the search to episodic-memory's search-conversations subagent; elsewhere it calls the search and read tools itself"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A synthesis of the top 2–5 matching conversations, with pointers to the originals so you can check them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "On its own and often: it's told to search before saying \"I don't know\", guessing, or treating any topic as new"
            },
            {
              "label": "Skips",
              "text": "Questions about the current code, which it explores directly, and anything already in the conversation"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "episodic-memory's MCP server, from its Claude Code, Codex or opencode plugin"
            }
          ],
          "text": "episodic-memory makes past agent conversations searchable; this skill tells the agent when and how to use it. It announces \"Searching past conversations for [topic]\" and, in Claude Code, dispatches the search-conversations subagent with a focused prompt about the decisions, patterns, gotchas or code examples it needs. The subagent searches, reads the top 2–5 results and returns a 200–1,000-word synthesis with sources, which the skill says saves 50–100 times the context of loading raw conversations. In Codex and opencode it uses that agent if present, or calls the search and read MCP tools directly and reads only the most relevant conversations or line ranges. It triggers broadly: on historical cues, when the agent is stuck, when a task resembles earlier work or needs a workflow repeated, and before answering from uncertainty. Unlike memory skills that save notes you pick, it only reads back what earlier conversations actually said.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/episodic-memory/tree/7e06519357777badd7a115d2014a7ef845904310/skills/remembering-conversations"
        },
