      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Looks up docs, release notes, issues and pull requests for a library or API in Firecrawl's developer index, citing the passages it finds.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A question names a library version, an upgrade, a pasted error, or how to configure or migrate a specific library, API or CLI"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Searches Firecrawl's index of documentation, READMEs, and issues and pull requests from public repositories, choosing its approach by the kind of question"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An answer that quotes the matched passages and cites their URLs, telling a merged fix apart from the release that ships it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Firecrawl CLI, its MCP tools or its HTTP endpoint; the index works without an API key, which only raises rate limits"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike firecrawl-research-index",
              "text": "That one finds research papers; this one covers developer sources and leaves comparisons and opinions to web search"
            },
            {
              "label": "Misses",
              "text": "Private repositories: for those, the agent reads the code directly"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Firecrawl's skill for its developer index of documentation pages, READMEs, and issues and pull requests from public repositories. It aims to answer from the primary source: the issue that reported a bug, the merged pull request that fixed it, or the page that states the contract, not a blog post. There's no fixed recipe. A literal error string is searched in issues and pull requests, then retried without paths and IDs if nothing matches. A how-to question goes to docs and READMEs. A known bug is traced from its issue to the fixing pull request, and to a release containing it before it's called the user's behavior. An API contract is checked in READMEs and docs. Searches start broad and are narrowed by type, repository or documentation source once the hits show where to look. Comparisons, opinions, news and unindexed projects go to Firecrawl's web search and scrape. For documentation questions, a reference file sets the steps: search with the CLI, match the exact version, and cite what was read. An identical copy ships in firecrawl/cli.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/firecrawl/skills/tree/458d9d0331fb31121fd38e0e09ae5036f952784b/skills/core/firecrawl-developer-index"
        },
