      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Renders every state of one component on a throwaway page, with mock data and a switcher, while you work on its visuals.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're working on how one component looks and want each of its states, such as loading, empty, error or a locked feature, one keypress away"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds the states the component's code can reach and feeds each mock data from the page, through props or the app's own data seam"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A scratch page with the real, untouched component and a switcher where every state is a link, plus the state list and the switcher keys"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike break",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, stacks worst-case scenarios on one page for a single look; this one is a workbench that stays up while you change the visuals"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Add a branch to show a state the code can't reach, edit the component without asking, or change how a state looks unasked"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of Jakub Krehel's interface skills. It scopes the run to one component, then reads its code and data hooks for every branch: data states such as loading, empty, error, one item or a list long enough to scroll; account states such as plan tier and role; feature states such as flags, trials and limits. It writes the set down before building, grouped and named the way the product talks, and lists states the design shows but the code lacks instead of rendering them. The page sits in one folder inside the app, so it inherits the app's layout, fonts and providers; in Next it's a client page, so fixtures survive the server boundary. Mock data looks like the product, with real-shaped names, amounts and counts, and loading states stay pending until you switch. The switcher is styled deliberately outside your design system, sets a __state URL parameter, steps through states with the arrow keys and hides with H for screenshots. It checks every state once in a browser if one is at hand, and again after each visual change. On your word, it deletes the folder and searches for leftover route and fixture names.",
