      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Autonomously critiques a page's design in a visible browser, pinning five to eight annotations to its elements with the Agentation toolbar.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an agent to review a page's design and leave its feedback pinned to the elements while you watch"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Opens the page in a headed agent-browser, works top to bottom and writes a two-to-three-sentence critique into Agentation's annotation dialog for each element it picks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Five to eight annotations on the page unless you ask otherwise, each naming the design principle at stake, one or two concrete alternatives and a comparable product"
            },
            {
              "label": "Full self-driving",
              "text": "With Agentation's MCP server connected, annotations go to a second agent session that edits the code to address each one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Agentation toolbar on the page and the agent-browser CLI; it stops and tells you if the toolbar isn't found"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A design review you watch happen. Agentation is a feedback toolbar mounted in your own page, whose annotations a coding agent can read; this skill makes the agent the reviewer. It checks that agent-browser is installed, opens the page headed, never headless, confirms the toolbar is there and expands it, with Block page interactions on. For each annotation it scrolls an element into view, reads its position, clicks its center with coordinate-based mouse events, since ordinary element clicks don't open the annotation dialog, fills the dialog and checks that the annotation count went up. It covers the hero, navigation, demos, content sections, key taglines, calls to action and footer, looking at hierarchy, grouping, spacing rhythm, typography and conversion weight. Each critique is specific, such as stacking a command below a subheading at 16px, names a principle like Gestalt grouping, and points to how products such as Stripe, Linear or Vercel handle it. Fixing is left to a second session running agentation_watch_annotations, when the MCP connection is on. Tables cover troubleshooting and agent-browser pitfalls, such as commands that crash on element references.",
