        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Creates Excalidraw diagrams that argue visually, rendering each to PNG and fixing what it sees until the layout is clean.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a workflow, architecture or concept drawn as a diagram that teaches, not a grid of labelled boxes"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks simple or comprehensive depth, researches real formats and event names for technical diagrams, maps each concept to a visual pattern, then writes the JSON"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A .excalidraw file and a PNG render, checked against a 27-point quality list"
            },
            {
              "label": "Renders",
              "text": "Each draft to PNG with a bundled Playwright script, views it and fixes clipped text, overlaps and stray arrows, usually in 2–4 rounds"
            },
            {
              "label": "Brand it",
              "text": "All colors live in references/color-palette.md; edit that one file to restyle every diagram"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and Playwright's Chromium for rendering, set up once with uv sync"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its rule is that a diagram should argue, not display: with the text removed, the structure alone should still carry the concept. Technical diagrams must include evidence artifacts, such as real code snippets, JSON payloads, event sequences or UI mockups, and work at three zoom levels: a summary flow, labelled sections, and detail inside them. It favors free-floating text and lines over boxes, keeping under 30% of text inside containers, and draws on patterns such as fan-out, convergence, tree, cycle and assembly line. Large diagrams are built section by section in hand-written JSON, never through a generator script. Elements follow set defaults: font family 3, roughness 0 for a clean look, full opacity. Unlike a skill that only writes the JSON, it can't finish until it has rendered the diagram, looked at the PNG and repaired what it saw, until the result matches its plan.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coleam00/excalidraw-diagram-skill/tree/8646fcc9f74f38539c6cdb4c969723336a96ddcd"
