            }
          ],
          "text": "A bridge between a coding-agent session and the chat apps on your phone. In Claude Code, setup is a wizard that asks one question at a time: which channels to enable, then each credential, masked when read back, then the runtime (Claude, Codex, or auto, which falls back to Codex when the Claude CLI is missing), working directory, model and mode (code, plan or ask). It writes ~/.claude-to-im/config.env, restricts it to your user and checks each token before you start. In Codex, which can't ask questions this way, it shows the example config for you to fill in. Channels differ: Telegram gets a streaming preview and permission buttons, Discord access control by server, channel and user, and Feishu streaming cards, tool progress and buttons; QQ supports private chats only and WeChat one linked account, both approving tools with text /perm commands. Telegram and Discord reject every message until you allow a chat, user or channel. Feishu needs two publish rounds with admin approval, the second while the bridge runs. doctor runs a health-check script and suggests fixes, such as reinstalling dependencies or rebuilding the daemon; logs shows the last 50 lines by default.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/op7418/claude-to-im-skill/tree/536908f5e9bd65a151ca4cb4b08d3fedc1a43b4d"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:tool",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:account",
          "by:community",
          "stack:claude",
          "stack:codex",
          "stack:telegram",
          "stack:discord",
          "stack:lark",
          "stack:qq",
          "stack:wechat",
          "messaging",
          "agent-workflows"
        ],
        "note": "Leaves a bridge daemon running in the background until you stop it. Each platform needs its own bot or app credentials, and a Feishu app also needs admin approval.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
