        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns reference screenshots or URLs into a Design DNA JSON of tokens, style and visual effects, then generates new designs from it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have screenshots or URLs of a design you like and want its look captured as a reusable spec, or applied to your own content"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Fills every field of a three-part schema: design tokens, qualitative style such as mood and composition, and visual effects such as WebGL, particles or scroll animation"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A Design DNA JSON with no empty fields and, from it, a self-contained HTML page with inline CSS and JavaScript by default"
            },
            {
              "label": "Measures colors",
              "text": "From image references with a bundled script instead of guessing hexes by eye, then scores a screenshot of its output against them, PASS or FAIL per color"
            },
            {
              "label": "Phases",
              "text": "Show the schema, analyze references, generate a design; use any one alone or chain them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node.js, to install and run the measurement scripts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Design DNA splits a visual identity into three parts. The design system is what you can measure: color, type, spacing, layout, shape, elevation, motion and components. The style is what you feel: mood, visual language, composition, imagery, interaction feel and brand voice. Visual effects are what CSS alone can't express, such as WebGL and 3D scenes, particles, shaders, cursor effects and glassmorphism. To analyze, the agent extracts or infers a value for every schema field from your references, noting the dominant pattern when they conflict, then asks whether to adjust anything. For an image it measures the palette first and uses those hexes verbatim, keeping the measurement settings in the JSON so verification can reuse them. To generate, it turns tokens into CSS custom properties, lets the style fields guide judgment calls, fetches real assets from the source URL rather than recreating them, and picks technology by weight, from CSS and SVG up to Three.js and GLSL shaders. When the DNA holds a measured palette, it scores a screenshot of its output, fixes failing colors and verifies again, rather than asking you to judge by eye.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/zanwei/design-dna/tree/593e39bc9e3652734653bd75544a333d7d43615e"
        },
