      "skillMd": {
        "version": "734998e5fdbb16cd96dbaafe2da24eee6fb0d291",
        "updated": "2026-11-11",
        "skew": 79
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scores a page's content for E-E-A-T, readability, thinness and AI citation readiness, and cleans AI-typical phrasing from your own drafts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know whether a page's content deserves to rank or be quoted in AI answers"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs Google's who, how and why test, then scores E-E-A-T: experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust, the criteria Google's raters use"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A content quality score out of 100, an E-E-A-T breakdown weighted toward trust, an AI citation readiness score, issues and fixes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also does",
              "text": "On request (\"humanize this\", \"remove watermarks\"), strips invisible Unicode characters it calls watermarks from your own drafts and swaps AI-typical phrases, using a plugin script"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike seo-page",
              "text": "This one leaves technical metadata aside and looks only at the content"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It checks word count against floors per page type, such as 500 for a homepage and 1,500 for a blog post, while saying word count isn't a ranking factor, along with readability, keyword use without a density target, heading structure, internal and external links, images and video, and freshness. AI-assisted content is judged by quality markers, not by who wrote it, and it follows Google's line that optimizing for AI search is still SEO: no special AI files or chunking. Its E-E-A-T weights (trust 30, expertise and authoritativeness 25 each, experience 20) are its own model, and reports must say its scores are heuristics, not Google signals. The draft cleanup removes zero-width, directional and hidden tag characters, which it calls watermarks, keeps emoji intact, and makes conservative swaps such as \"delve into\" to \"explore\". It says no tool removes statistical watermarks like SynthID, and declines to strip provenance from content you don't own. With DataForSEO connected, it pulls real keyword volume, difficulty and intent.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/AgriciDaniel/claude-seo/tree/6be93f45064ffbadced572b5d5c95f55fd263af0/skills/seo-content"
