        "skew": 272
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits a page's images for alt text, size, format, dimensions and lazy loading, and converts files to WebP or AVIF with SEO metadata.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Images slow a page down, lack alt text, or don't show up in Google Images"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks alt text, file size by image role, format, responsive srcset, lazy loading, dimensions, file names and CDN use"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An image audit table, a list sorted by biggest savings and recommendations; /seo images optimize also converts and tags the files"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "exiftool, cwebp, ImageMagick or FFmpeg to optimize files, and DataForSEO to compare with image search results"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also checks",
              "text": "The IPTC label Google Merchant Center requires on AI-generated product images, and adds it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Size thresholds depend on the image's role: thumbnails should stay under 50 KB, content images under 100 KB and hero images under 200 KB. It recommends AVIF and WebP with a JPEG fallback in a picture element, fetchpriority=\"high\" on the main hero image and never lazy-loading it, and width and height on every image to stop layout shift. A plugin script tells native lazy loading apart from JavaScript lazy-loaders, so a missing loading=\"lazy\" isn't flagged as a fault. Optimizing converts to WebP or AVIF, makes 400, 800 and 1,200-pixel variants, writes the IPTC and XMP creator, credit and copyright fields Google Images can display, and generates the picture HTML. For AI-generated assets it asks you to confirm the source type and writes the matching DigitalSourceType value. With DataForSEO it compares your images with those in a keyword's search results, when that data exists. It is clear that IPTC fields affect display, not ranking.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/AgriciDaniel/claude-seo/tree/6be93f45064ffbadced572b5d5c95f55fd263af0/skills/seo-images"
