        "skew": 24
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes SEO content briefs from the top-ranking competitors: an outline with word counts per section, keyword placement, meta tags and content gaps.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A writer needs to know what a page must cover to outrank the current top results, for a new page or one you already have"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads your site and sitemap, scores the top 5 real competitors on depth, formatting, SEO and UX, and finds topic, depth and quality gaps"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A brief in a fixed format: intent, competitor table, gaps, an outline with word counts and a keyword per section, meta tags and internal links"
            },
            {
              "label": "Holds to",
              "text": "Only topics your site can credibly deliver, one specific piece of new value no ranking page has, and no fixed keyword density"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also does",
              "text": "An outline-only version on request, without the competitor table, gaps, new-value and E-E-A-T sections"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike seo-content",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, scores a page's content as it stands; this one tells a writer what to write or add"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Given a URL, it works in improve mode: it keeps what's strong, marks each section keep, strengthen or add, and avoids recommending a rewrite when targeted changes would win. Given a keyword, it reads the site only for context and briefs a new page. Competitors exclude Wikipedia, Reddit, Amazon, YouTube, directories, SEO tool pages and other non-rivals, and each scores out of 40; gaps are ranked by impact times competitive advantage over effort. It classifies intent as informational, commercial, transactional or navigational, notes which format Google rewards, then applies a template for the page type. Every suggested heading must be something the site can actually deliver, and a hub page's outline covers every category the site has, no more and no less. The primary keyword goes in the title, H1, URL slug, meta description and first 100 words; titles run 50 to 60 characters and descriptions 130 to 150. The brief lists the trust signals the content needs (E-E-A-T: experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust), names no researchers or tools, and uses DataForSEO or Ahrefs data when their MCP servers are connected.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/AgriciDaniel/claude-seo/tree/6be93f45064ffbadced572b5d5c95f55fd263af0/skills/seo-content-brief"
