      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Snapshots a page's SEO elements as a baseline and diffs later states against it, to catch regressions after deploys.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to deploy, or traffic dropped, and want to know what changed in a page's SEO"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "baseline stores the title, meta tags, canonical, robots, headings, JSON-LD, Open Graph, status code, Core Web Vitals and hashes; compare checks 17 rules against it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A JSON diff of every triggered rule with old and new values, severity and action, an optional HTML report, and a history per URL"
            },
            {
              "label": "Severities",
              "text": "Critical for SEO-breaking changes to fix now, Warning to look into within a week, Info for changes that may be intentional"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "Every baseline and comparison in a local SQLite database at ~/.cache/claude-seo/drift/baselines.db"
            },
            {
              "label": "Next step",
              "text": "Points each finding to the skill that goes deeper, such as seo-schema for changed schema or seo-technical for a new noindex"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its own pitch is Git for SEO. Baseline fetches the page through the plugin's fetch script, which blocks private and internal addresses, parses it, adds Core Web Vitals from PageSpeed Insights unless you skip them or have no key, hashes the HTML and the schema, and saves the snapshot. URLs are normalized first, with UTM parameters, default ports and trailing slashes removed and query parameters sorted, so the same page always matches. Compare loads the latest baseline, or one you name by ID, fetches the page again and classifies each change by severity. A page that returns an error is still captured, since the status code is one of the tracked fields. Typical uses are a baseline before a deploy and a compare after, another compare weeks later for ongoing monitoring, and history to find when something changed during a traffic drop.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/AgriciDaniel/claude-seo/tree/6be93f45064ffbadced572b5d5c95f55fd263af0/skills/seo-drift"
