      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Rewrites AI-sounding text so it reads like a person wrote it, keeping every claim and adding no facts, from Wikipedia's Signs of AI writing.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A draft, post, PR description or doc reads as if a chatbot wrote it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks the text against 26 AI-writing patterns, strongest first, such as not-X-but-Y contrasts, one-line closers, forced triads, dashes and stock AI words, then restates each point plainly"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A draft, a short list of the tells that remain, and the final rewrite; given a file, only the final text, written to that file"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "Every supported claim, your voice from a sample you give, and code, commands, paths, metadata and link targets unchanged"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Add a fact, name, number, date, quote or citation that isn't in your text or from you, except in fiction"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Built on Wikipedia's Signs of AI writing guide. It explains each tell as a model's default choice, made to suit the widest range of readers: staging a point instead of stating it, rhythm by rule, inflated significance, decoration on every item, chatbot leftovers, and replies that re-explain what the reader already knows. Patterns one to five justify an edit on one sighting; those marked weak alone need company. The agent reads the whole text, marks the tells, drafts a rewrite that keeps the information, then checks it: reads it aloud, looks for added or dropped facts, and searches again for the tells that most often survive, such as contrasts, closers, triads, dashes and bold labels. The final version has no em or en dashes unless your sample uses them. With a writing sample it matches your sentence length, words and punctuation; without one it takes the voice from the kind of text. It leaves watched phrases alone inside quotations, titles and names. Another skill can call it for a PR, commit message or document and get back only the final text.",
