      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a finished BMad epic from its tickets, plans, diffs and commits, and writes a retrospective with sourced findings, action items and a verdict.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An epic in BMad's ticket tree is built and you want to know what it really produced and whether it met its own criteria"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Inventories the epic's tickets, plans, diffs and commits, looks for defects no single ticket could show, and runs bmad-review on the whole diff"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "epic-<slug>-retrospective.md in the epic folder, with sourced findings, owned action items and an accepted, accepted-with-open-items or rejected verdict"
            },
            {
              "label": "Proposes, not applies",
              "text": "Fixes and spec changes become action items for you; besides decision records (ADRs) you approve, the document is all it writes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Headless",
              "text": "With -H and a named epic it asks nothing and records every assumption in the document"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv and BMad set up in the project: run the bmad skill's setup first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "BMad's retrospective reads the record an epic left behind rather than anyone's memory of it. It resolves the epic from a folder, id or slug, or offers the finished ones, and warns when tickets are still unfinished, which forces a rejected verdict unless you override it. It then lists what exists and what is missing, using a bundled script to derive each plan's diff range and commits. The analysis looks for problems that only show across the whole epic: changes to the dependency structure, the same problem solved twice, files that grew too large session by session, diverging patterns and gaps between spec and code. It hands the diff to bmad-review for adversarial, edge-case and verification-gap lenses, and exercises changed behavior end to end. A finding without a file, line, commit or log reference is dropped. On request, bmad-party-mode lets BMad's personas discuss the findings. It proposes a decision record (ADR) for each hard-to-reverse choice in the plans and writes only those you approve. The verdict judges the epic against its own requirements and done-when criteria. It also checks whether the previous retro's action items landed, and resumes from its document if interrupted.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bmad-code-org/bmad-method/tree/e734d3ac3c0f4ea31cc2c2bbbe79e86e54f2cae6/skills/bmad-retrospective"
        },
