      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs, builds, reviews and debugs UI motion, from springs and gestures to scroll effects, and reverse-engineers an animation from a screen recording into code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're adding or tuning animation, an animation feels off, you want to know where an interface should animate, or you want to copy a motion from a recording"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Decides whether something should animate and why, picks easing and duration from its defaults, and loads the recipe or technique reference for the component"
            },
            {
              "label": "Reviews",
              "text": "Motion code against ten standards with a Before/After/Why table, ending in a Block or Approve verdict"
            },
            {
              "label": "Reverse-engineers",
              "text": "A recording: extracts frames, tracks motion, fits spring or cubic-bezier curves, and emits CSS, Motion, SwiftUI, React Native or UIKit code with a handoff spec"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "Naming a motion you describe vaguely, and sparse interface sound"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Nothing for building and reviewing; ffmpeg plus Python with OpenCV, NumPy and SciPy for measuring recordings"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A motion design guide with workflows. Its core rules: animate for feedback, orientation, continuity or deliberate delight, keep focus and repeated navigation instant, prefer interruptible CSS transitions, and move things with transform and opacity only. Its principles include continuity over teleporting, overlays that emerge from their trigger, confirming a result on the control that caused it, and delight that shrinks as frequency grows. It gives a duration and curve table for buttons, popovers, modals, drawers and pages, and maps UI patterns to transition recipes such as container morphs, dropdowns, odometer rolls and error shakes. Validation asks for evidence: grep the diff for animated layout properties and transition: all, retoggle components to confirm they retarget, slow the DevTools Animations panel to 10%, test touch on real devices, and honor reduced motion, including in Motion's JavaScript animations. A discovery sweep reports which moments deserve motion and which don't. The reverse-engineer branch runs bundled scripts, measures opening and closing separately, and validates by recording its own output and comparing contact sheets.",
