            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes GATES.md first, one observable outcome per gate with a CHECK command and the output to EXPECT, then works until every gate passes"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report with re-measured counts of met, unmet and abandoned gates, never called done while one is unmet; a gate it can't meet is marked abandoned and handed to you"
            },
            {
              "label": "Depth Tree",
              "text": "For a big build or review, a PLAN.md splits the work into leaves with exact file ownership, launched as agents in waves and re-checked by the parent"
            },
            {
              "label": "Safety",
              "text": "Its checker runs only commands you've approved, and treats inherited gate files and command output as untrusted"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node 16+; native subagents only for orchestrated mode; the Stop hook only in Claude Code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The skill's ledger is a list of gates: each states one observable outcome and, where a command can decide it, a CHECK line and an EXPECT pattern. A gate counts as met only when its command exits zero, the output matches, and the recorded evidence matches the gate's current definition; changing a gate needs approval again. A linter flags gates that can't fail. Solo mode uses one GATES.md. Orchestrated mode, for builds and deep reviews, writes a contract and a Depth Tree under .unlazy/: each leaf gets a narrow contract, its own ledger and its files, each branch gets integration gates, and leaves whose dependencies are verified launch as agents in waves. Each leaf is worked in four passes: build it fully, redo the cheap parts as an expert would, hunt defects, then polish until a pass finds nothing. The parent re-runs a returned leaf's checks rather than trusting its report, and the final report re-measures every claim. Approvals are kept under ~/.unlazy/approved. In Claude Code it can offer a Stop hook, installed only with your consent, that blocks stopping while gates are unmet and releases after six blocks without progress.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/leonxlnx/unlazy/tree/16671491f6679ad9378f52604d3bc2415b4120c7"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
