              "text": "In plain words with no dashes or hyphens, and never writes code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The design step of a workflow with one skill per phase: scope, audit, architect, develop, check, test, document, sync and debug. It works in four modes: a new feature, a new project's stack, improving something that exists, or a standard across the whole codebase. Before asking anything it fetches from git to warn you if teammates pushed changes, reads AGENTS.md, finds the matching feature in docs/scope/, and lists earlier specs, offering to update or supersede one that overlaps. Then it runs a staged design conversation from a file it must read first, sorting each question into infer, ask or recommend, and never offers a neutral menu. Cheap subagents only read a large codebase or fetch current facts from the web; the main thread writes the spec. A simple decision gets one file; a heavy one gets a folder with index.md for the builder and rationale.md for the reasoning. It always asks whether another model should cross-check the spec for values the builder would otherwise invent. It also ratifies \"Assumed\" specs that develop records when you build before deciding.",
