      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a daily literature search that scores papers on six dimensions, reads the best, posts a digest to chat and archives notes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want new papers in your field found, ranked and summarized for you every day"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "On a schedule, pulls candidates from arXiv, OpenAlex, Crossref and Semantic Scholar, scores them on six weighted dimensions and reads the top few"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A digest in Feishu, Telegram or another chat, with rank, score, methods, key results and commentary, plus de-duplicated notes in a local archive"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike nature-academic-search",
              "text": "That one searches once when you ask; this one keeps watching a topic"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "An agent scheduler, such as Hermes cron, on a machine that stays on, and a messaging target"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An automated literature-monitoring pipeline from the Nature skills repo. You tell the agent your research area, keywords, delivery target and archive path, then ask it to set up a daily job, such as 08:30 Beijing time with 30 candidates and the top 5 delivered. Each run searches arXiv, OpenAlex, Crossref and Semantic Scholar, falling back to the others when Semantic Scholar is down. A six-dimension score filters the candidates: topic match weighs 35, methodology 20, journal quality 15, and network relevance, applied value and archival value 10 each, with each dimension capped and totals recalculated. The top papers are read at full-text, abstract or metadata level, and each is tagged with which. The digest, whose template targets a Feishu group with Chinese field labels, gives rank, title, journal, score, a one-line takeaway, methods, key results and commentary. Archiving de-duplicates by DOI, arXiv and OpenAlex IDs, sorts papers into tiers you define and writes notes to a raw/ folder only, never to your wiki without approval. Keywords, weights, tiers, target and path all come from its config template.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/yuan1z0825/nature-skills/tree/d774add241c65e89ed17a4da573b472b0972fd42/skills/nature-literature-pipeline"
        },
