      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews every changed line on your branch for security vulnerabilities, bugs and quality issues, then reports evidenced findings by severity without changing code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the changes on your current branch checked for bugs and security holes before you merge"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the full diff against the default branch, maps each file's inputs, queries, auth checks and external calls, then runs an 11-item security checklist on every file"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Findings ranked security first, each with file and line, severity, problem, evidence and a concrete fix, or a plain statement that nothing significant turned up"
            },
            {
              "label": "Doesn't",
              "text": "Edit code or report style and formatting issues; you decide what to fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike Sentry's security-review",
              "text": "That one researches the whole codebase for exploitable vulnerabilities only; this one covers just the branch diff, bugs and quality included"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "git and the GitHub CLI, which it uses to find the default branch"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A five-phase branch review from Sentry. It gets the whole diff from the default branch to HEAD, reading changed files one by one if the output is truncated, and lists every modified file. It then maps each file's attack surface: user inputs, database queries, authentication and authorization checks, session and state operations, external calls and cryptography. Every file goes through the same checklist: injection, XSS, authentication, authorization and IDOR, CSRF, race conditions, sessions, cryptography, information disclosure, denial of service and business logic. Each candidate issue is then verified: is it handled elsewhere in the change, does a test cover it, does the surrounding code confirm it. Before reporting, the agent must list every file it read in full, the result of each checklist item and anything it couldn't verify. Findings put security vulnerabilities before bugs before code quality, skip style, cite OWASP or RFCs where relevant, and it is told not to invent issues when there are none.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/getsentry/skills/tree/e94f22214528d69899d09c627b3315ddc3544816/skills/find-bugs"
        },
