          "text": "A reprint rebuilds a printed CLI from scratch under the current Printing Press instead of patching the old code. You name the CLI and, if you like, why. It resolves the name against the public library's registry and compares your copy with the public one: with no local copy it invokes printing-press-import, with a newer public copy it offers to import it, and with unpublished local work it stops. It refreshes the CLI's patch records, the post-publish hand-fixes that printing-press-amend records, and when the CLI predates research.json it asks whether to go on with what is then essentially a fresh print. It shows the research's age (under 30 days, reuse looks safe; over 120, redo is recommended) and the press version that made it, and lets you reuse it, redo it or look first. From the prior scorecard it offers spec enrichments for weak MCP, auth or sync scores, one question each, never applied unasked. It then invokes printing-press with your reason verbatim, the research choice, accepted enrichments and a summary of the patches, framed as a watch-list rather than a checklist to reapply.",
