          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to read how a desktop Electron app, such as Codex or VS Code, is built"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds the app's app.asar bundle on macOS or Windows, unpacks it, then rebuilds sources from .js.map files or runs Prettier on minified code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A folder in ~/Downloads with the raw contents, the restored source tree when maps allow, and a JSON report"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Bun or npx; it fetches asar and Prettier through npx on first run"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves alone",
              "text": "node_modules, and any non-empty output folder unless you pass --force"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A wrapper around one bundled script. You give it an app name or a path to the .app, install folder or .asar file; it searches /Applications and ~/Applications on macOS and the usual program folders on Windows, and asks you to choose when several match. On Linux you pass the .asar path yourself. A dry run prints what it found without writing anything. The output folder, ~/Downloads/<AppName>-electron-extract/ by default, holds extracted/ with the raw bundle, extracted.unpacked/ with native modules and large assets when present, restored/ with the original source tree when a source map embeds its sources, and extract-report.json with counts and warnings. Where no usable map exists, JavaScript and CSS are formatted in place with Prettier instead. Restored paths are kept inside restored/, and it refuses to write to /, your home folder or the current directory.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jimliu/baoyu-skills/tree/ca703c2d7c2c4399a1dc0cf82224f8551e05a96d/skills/baoyu-electron-extract"
        },
