      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Looks up current documentation and code examples for any library, framework or SDK through the Context7 CLI, instead of relying on training data.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "The agent writes or debugs code against a library and its training data may be out of date"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Resolves the library to a Context7 ID, then asks its docs one focused question, with at most three tries per question"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Current code snippets and explanations, for a specific version if you name one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Triggers",
              "text": "On any question that names a library, even one the agent thinks it knows; its description says to prefer it over web search"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node.js, to run npx ctx7@latest; a login or API key only raises the rate limit"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Context7 indexes documentation for libraries, frameworks and services, and this skill sends the agent there before it answers from memory. It runs the ctx7 CLI through npx, so nothing has to be installed globally. First it resolves a name such as Next.js or Prisma into a library ID like /vercel/next.js, choosing among matches by name, description, number of code snippets, source reputation and benchmark score, and using a version-specific ID when you mention a version. Then it queries that ID with one specific topic per query, optionally ranking examples in your programming language first. Results mix titled code snippets with prose explanations. It keeps API keys, credentials, personal data and proprietary code out of its queries. When the monthly quota runs out it tells you, suggests logging in, and only then answers from training data, saying it may be outdated.",
