      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans how to earn backlinks and third-party mentions that lift rankings and AI citations, from finding the link gap to outreach drafts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Rankings stall on authority, or you want your product mentioned on the pages AI assistants cite"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks the page itself and its page-level link gap first, then picks tactics by effort, qualifies prospects and drafts outreach"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A plan: diagnosis per target page, assets, three or four tactics, a prospect list, outreach drafts and 30- and 90-day targets"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Send outreach without your approval of the list and the copy, or recommend paid links that pass ranking credit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Ahrefs, Semrush, DataForSEO or Moz if you have them; Search Console and search operators otherwise"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Every placement is judged twice: does it pass authority to a page that needs it, and is it on a page AI engines cite for your category, where a mention helps even without a link. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), then asks for target pages, queries, rankings, linkable assets, budget, tools and link history. Before any outreach it confirms links are the bottleneck by comparing referring domains to the target page against the pages that outrank it, and sends content or intent problems to seo-audit. Tactics start with the cheap, high-yield ones: reclaiming unlinked mentions, partner and integration pages, and fixing moved or broken links, before best-of lists, resource pages, guest articles and handoffs to PR. It follows Google's spam policies: paid or exchanged placements carry rel=sponsored or nofollow, and it never uses private blog networks, paid insertions into old articles or automated link tools. Anchor text is left to the linking site.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/link-building"
