        "skew": 11
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Drives and inspects a live Chrome through the Chrome DevTools MCP server, for debugging pages, automating interactions, profiling performance and testing extensions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want the agent to debug a web page, click through it, read its network traffic or check its performance in a real Chrome"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Navigates, waits for content, takes a text snapshot whose element uids the click and fill tools need, then acts"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The chrome-devtools-mcp server connected to your agent, not in its --slim mode"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also does",
              "text": "CSS cascade inspection, logpoints instead of code edits, and unpacked extension tests when the server runs with --categoryExtensions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike chrome-devtools-cli",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, runs the same browser tools as shell commands"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The Chrome DevTools team's own guide to its MCP server. The browser starts on the first tool call with a persistent Chrome profile. Page tools take a pageId from list_pages or new_page, and the agent finds elements in a text snapshot of the page, taking a fresh one when an element has gone. The usual order is navigate, wait for known content, snapshot, then click or fill by uid; calls can run in parallel as long as that order holds. To keep context small it writes large screenshots, snapshots and traces to files, pages and filters lists, and skips snapshots after input unless it needs them. Screenshots are for visual checks, get_css_styles for matched rules and CSS variables, evaluate_script for other runtime data, and set_logpoints for logging values at source lines, source maps included. With --categoryExtensions it installs an unpacked extension, opens its popup or side panel, evaluates code in its service worker and checks its content scripts; without the flag it asks you to change the server config and restart. Memory tools need --memoryDebugging.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/chromedevtools/chrome-devtools-mcp/tree/0b37364dc0a88a804c6d73d3335e776adc7f33c4/skills/chrome-devtools"
