      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews and hardens API endpoints: token verification, per-object authorization, input validation, rate limiting, password storage and safe logging.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You add a protected endpoint, review who can reach which objects, or investigate an API abuse path"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Follows the request from caller identity through authorization and validated input to storage and response, keeping your existing identity provider"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The route policy, the failure cases reproduced, the exact test command and output, and the gaps left untested"
            },
            {
              "label": "Examples in",
              "text": "Node, with Express, jsonwebtoken and Zod, as sketches to check against your installed versions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Isn't",
              "text": "A penetration test or certified audit; it tests with synthetic identities in a test environment"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Security guidance for one API boundary at a time, starting from the failing route when there's a concrete defect rather than deploying new security infrastructure. It covers verifying JWTs against a fixed algorithm, issuer and audience, and rotating refresh tokens in one transaction so that reuse revokes the session family; authorizing each object and tenant inside the database write instead of checking first; parsing input once, rejecting partial numbers, unknown properties and oversized bodies, with notes on HTML sanitizing, outbound URLs and uploads; rate limits with shared stores, correct proxy trust and accurate Retry-After headers, without claiming DDoS protection; Argon2id for new password storage; and logs that never hold tokens, passwords or request bodies. A worked example takes a PATCH /users/:id endpoint through missing tokens, malformed IDs, an extra role field and cross-tenant access. It says plainly that passing structural checks doesn't prove an API secure, and wants separate authority before production scans, account writes or messages.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/sickn33/agentic-awesome-skills/tree/828257c3037dc7c3c3ec987decc5a603025a0ce5/skills/api-security-best-practices"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:security",
