      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Explores a mobile app on iOS or Android with agent-device to find bugs and UX issues, writing a report with reproducible evidence.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an app dogfooded, bug-hunted or QA'd on an iPhone, Android phone, simulator or emulator"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Opens the app, maps its navigation, works through main flows and edge states, and records evidence for each issue as it finds it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "./dogfood-output/report.md with severity counts and, per finding, repro commands, expected and actual behavior, and screenshots or video"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "agent-device 0.14.0 or later, on your PATH or through an exact-version npm command you approve; it won't install or upgrade it on its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Judges",
              "text": "Only what the running app does, never its source code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike agent-device",
              "text": "That one is the repo's skill for driving an app; this one runs a whole QA pass and writes the report"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short router: the SKILL.md checks the agent-device version, then reads the CLI's own guide with agent-device help dogfood, so the workflow matches the installed CLI. That guide has the agent find the target app, sign in or ask you for a code if needed, capture a baseline snapshot and screenshot, map the top-level navigation, then work through main flows and edge states: forms, empty, error and loading states, offline and retry, permissions, settings, accessibility labels, orientation and keyboard, and obvious stalls. Each issue gets its evidence on the spot, step screenshots and usually a video for interactive bugs, one screenshot for static ones, and a severity from critical to low. React Native error overlays count as findings or blockers, and Expo Go and dev-client URLs are handled. It aims for 5 to 10 well-evidenced issues, reports the ground covered and the remaining risk if it finds none, keeps every artifact, and closes the session, shutting down any simulator it booted unless you want it kept. Its allowed tools are limited to agent-device commands.",
