      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes firmware for microcontrollers such as STM32 and ESP32, bare-metal or on FreeRTOS, within tight memory, timing and power budgets.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing drivers, interrupt handlers, RTOS tasks or low-power code for a microcontroller"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Starts from the chip's memory, timing and power limits, designs tasks and interrupts, then writes drivers and application code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Hardware setup, drivers and interrupt handlers, RTOS tasks or a main loop, and a flash, RAM and power summary"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "Builds with -Wall -Werror, runs static analysis such as cppcheck, checks register bits against the datasheet, and measures stack use and interrupt timing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its rules come from resource-constrained work: volatile for hardware registers and variables shared with interrupts, short interrupt handlers that hand work to tasks, a watchdog timer, proper locking around shared resources, no blocking calls in interrupts, no unbounded dynamic allocation, and care with floating point on chips without hardware support. Timing is verified with a logic analyzer or oscilloscope and stack headroom with FreeRTOS's high-water mark, and worst-case load must miss no deadline; any failure sends it back to the validation step, with its warning-free build and static analysis. References cover FreeRTOS patterns, bare-metal register programming, sleep modes and battery life, I2C, SPI, UART and CAN, and code size and RAM. Examples include an STM32 UART interrupt with a critical section, a periodic FreeRTOS sensor task and a register-level timer blink.",
