      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds real-time messaging with WebSockets or Socket.IO: authenticated connections, rooms, presence and Redis-backed scaling across servers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building chat, live updates, presence or other server push that must hold up under many connections"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sizes connections and message volume, designs pub/sub and failover, builds the server, tests it locally with wscat, then scales"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A server setup, connect, message and disconnect handlers, a client that reconnects, and a scaling plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Scales with",
              "text": "A Redis adapter once a pub/sub round trip works, sticky sessions confirmed with test connections across instances, then load balancing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its examples are Socket.IO in Node.js: a JWT checked in middleware before a connection opens, rooms to scope messages, presence kept in Redis, and a client that reconnects with exponential backoff and jitter and queues messages while offline. Rules: sticky sessions behind a load balancer, heartbeats to detect dead connections, cleanup of presence and room membership on disconnect, no large in-memory state without a clustering plan, and load tests before production, since connection spikes behave differently from HTTP traffic. Its last step sets up tracking of connection counts, latency, throughput and error rates, with alerts on connection spikes and error-rate thresholds. References cover the WebSocket protocol (handshake, frames, close codes), scaling, room and broadcast patterns, security such as rate limiting and CORS, and when server-sent events or long polling fit better.",
