      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs mobile app screens step by step, applying rules for typography, 60/30/10 color, an 8-point grid and memorable success moments.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing or improving a mobile app screen, onboarding flow or navigation, often as a React or HTML prototype"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works out the app, user and main action, maps the flow, applies its visual rules, designs the key moments, then polishes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules",
              "text": "One font family with at most 4 sizes and 2 weights, 60/30/10 color, an 8-point spacing grid, main actions in the bottom third"
            },
            {
              "label": "Peak-end rule",
              "text": "People remember a task's most intense moment and its ending, so it designs both, with celebratory feedback and a closing summary"
            },
            {
              "label": "Builds with",
              "text": "Tailwind, Lucide icons and Recharts, at a 375px baseline width"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It models its advice on apps such as Airbnb, Duolingo, Spotify, Revolut and Phantom, and starts every screen from three questions: what the user is trying to do, how it should make them feel, and what they should notice first. The rules are specific: 60/30/10 means 60% neutral base, 30% complementary and 10% accent; secondary text uses 60-70% opacity instead of a new color, shadows are tinted to the background, cards get 24-32px of padding, tap targets are at least 44pt, and empty, loading, error and success states are all designed. Patterns adapt screens to new, returning and power users, fill search screens with recent and popular items, show order status as a visual timeline and offer tappable choices instead of typing. A list of anti-patterns rules out random spacing, actions out of thumb reach and labels bigger than their values. A reference file covers conventions by industry, such as finance, health or crypto. When you mention publishing to an app store, it suggests the author's separate ASO Shots skill for screenshots.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/ceorkm/mobile-app-ui-design/tree/0ad61f025fc647417d4b8fb0ca786cb493c5156a"
