      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs REST APIs as a linted OpenAPI 3.1 spec, tested against a mock server, with consistent errors, pagination and a versioning plan.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing a new REST API or its contract, before building the server"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Models resources from your domain, defines endpoints, writes an OpenAPI 3.1 spec, lints it with Redocly, then serves a Prism mock to try the contract"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A resource model, endpoint list, an OpenAPI YAML spec, an error catalog, auth flows, and pagination and versioning strategies"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules",
              "text": "Nouns not verbs in URIs, one naming convention, RFC 7807 errors, pagination on every collection, a deprecation policy, rate limits considered"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node, to run the Redocly and Prism CLIs with npx"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It works as a senior API architect. It sketches resources and their relationships before writing any spec, and the spec must pass `npx @redocly/cli lint` before it moves on; `npx @stoplight/prism-cli mock` then serves the spec so you can try the contract before anything is built. It ships a copy-paste OpenAPI 3.1 starter with cursor pagination, a reusable error schema, bearer JWT auth and 400, 401, 404 and 429 responses, plus an error example with field-level validation errors. RFC 7807 is the standard \"problem details\" JSON shape for HTTP errors: a stable type URI, a title, a status and a readable detail. Its last step plans versioning, deprecation and backward compatibility, and breaking changes need a migration path. Reference files cover REST patterns, versioning, cursor, offset and keyset pagination, error handling and OpenAPI. Its description also names GraphQL, but its steps, templates and references are about REST.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jeffallan/claude-skills/tree/078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3/skills/api-designer"
