      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits code and infrastructure for vulnerabilities, with scanners like Semgrep and Gitleaks and a manual review, and reports each finding's severity and fix.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a security audit of a codebase or its infrastructure, such as a dependency or secrets scan or a compliance check"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Maps the attack surface, runs code, dependency and secret scanners, reviews auth, input handling and crypto by hand, then rates each finding"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An executive summary, a findings table by severity, and each finding with its file and line, impact and fix, plus prioritized recommendations"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "For written authorization and rules of engagement before it starts and again before active testing, and confirms findings with you before the report is final"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike code-reviewer",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, is a broad one-pass review; this one covers security alone and runs scanning tools"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The scanners it runs, such as semgrep, bandit, gitleaks, npm audit and trivy"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It works as a security analyst who runs automated tools first and still reviews by hand, since tools miss context. Its example commands are `semgrep --config=auto`, `bandit`, `gitleaks detect`, `npm audit` and `trivy fs`. Severity runs from Critical to Info by CVSS, a standard 0 to 10 score of how serious a vulnerability is, and low-severity findings aren't ignored. Exploits stop at a proof of concept; it won't test production without authorization, go outside the agreed scope, cause disruption or data loss, or share detailed exploits publicly. Critical findings are reported at once. Its example finding is a SQL injection with file and line, impact, the parameterized-query fix and CWE and OWASP references. Reference files cover scanning tools, vulnerability patterns such as SQL injection and XSS, secret scanning, penetration testing, infrastructure and cloud security with compliance, and a report template.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jeffallan/claude-skills/tree/078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3/skills/security-reviewer"
