          "text": "It acts as a testing specialist that isn't tied to one framework: its example is a Jest unit test, and the same structure applies to pytest and others. When a test fails, it first decides whether the assertion or the environment is at fault; flaky tests get their ordering dependencies isolated and async handling checked, and are quarantined and fixed rather than re-run until green. Its rules: test error paths and edge cases such as empty, null and boundary values, mock external dependencies in unit tests, write descriptions that read as plain-English specifications, assert specific outcomes rather than truthiness, use fixtures instead of production data, keep each test runnable on its own, and test observable behaviour rather than internal calls. Coverage targets are checked before it closes, with gaps flagged. Ten reference files load by topic, from unit, integration and E2E testing to k6 performance tests, a security test checklist, report templates, QA methodology and automation frameworks; the TDD and anti-pattern files are adapted from obra/superpowers.",
