        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Defines SLOs and error budgets for a service, then builds burn-rate alerts, runbooks, toil automation and chaos experiments to test recovery.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're setting reliability targets for a service, writing an error-budget policy, handling incidents, planning capacity or cutting repetitive ops work"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reviews architecture, incidents and toil, sets SLOs, confirms they match what users expect, builds monitoring and alerts, automates toil, then runs chaos experiments"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "SLO definitions with their measurements and targets, Prometheus alerting rules, automation scripts, runbooks and a note on the reliability impact"
            },
            {
              "label": "SLO means",
              "text": "Service level objective: a target such as 99.9% availability, measured by an SLI (service level indicator); the error budget is the failure that target leaves room for"
            },
            {
              "label": "Toil means",
              "text": "Repetitive manual operations work that automation should take over"
            },
            {
              "label": "Changes systems",
              "text": "Its last step runs chaos experiments against your systems and checks that recovery meets its targets for recovery time and data loss (RTO and RPO)"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its examples show the arithmetic of a 99.9% SLO (43.2 minutes of downtime a month), a policy that freezes non-critical releases when half the budget burns in a quarter of the window, multiwindow burn-rate alerts in Prometheus, PromQL for the four golden signals (latency, traffic, errors and saturation), and a Python script that restarts a Kubernetes deployment when its error rate passes 5%. Its rules: quantitative SLOs justified by user impact, blameless postmortems for every incident, toil measured and given an automation plan once it passes half the work, no alert without an actionable runbook, no deploy without capacity planning, no ignoring an exhausted error budget, and systems that degrade gracefully. Five reference files load by topic: SLOs and SLIs, error-budget policy, monitoring and alerting, toil automation, and incident response with chaos engineering.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jeffallan/claude-skills/tree/078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3/skills/sre-engineer"
        },
