        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Splits a system or monolith into services along domain boundaries, then designs their communication, data ownership, resilience, tracing and deployment.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing a distributed system or breaking a monolith into services"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds service boundaries with domain-driven design, then picks communication, data, resilience, tracing and deployment patterns, checking each step against a rule"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A service boundary diagram, communication patterns, a data ownership and consistency model, resilience patterns for each integration point, and deployment requirements"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "Each service owns its data and deploys alone, no schema is shared, every external call has a timeout, retry budget and fallback, and one ID traces each request"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike architecture-designer",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers system design in general with decision records; this one is only about systems made of separate services, new or split from a monolith"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its six steps each end on a checkpoint. Bounded contexts, the parts of a domain that each keep their own model, set the service boundaries; each service gets a clear API contract. Long-running or cross-aggregate operations go async, and only query and command pairs that must answer within 100 ms stay synchronous. Data follows database-per-service, with event sourcing and eventual consistency. Resilience means circuit breakers, retries, timeouts, bulkheads and fallbacks; observability means distributed tracing, correlation IDs and central logging; deployment needs health and readiness probes and a documented canary or blue-green rollout. It avoids distributed monoliths, shared databases and chatty interfaces. Examples include Express middleware that propagates a correlation ID, a Python circuit breaker with pybreaker, a TypeScript saga runner that undoes completed steps when one fails, and Kubernetes probes. Five reference files cover decomposition, REST versus gRPC and messaging, resilience patterns such as sagas and bulkheads, data with event sourcing and CQRS, and observability.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jeffallan/claude-skills/tree/078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3/skills/microservices-architect"
        },
