      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs and builds command-line tools in Node, Python or Go, with subcommands, help text, shell completions, progress output and Ctrl+C handling.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a command-line tool, or adding subcommands, flags, interactive prompts, progress bars or shell completions to one"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Maps user workflows and the command tree, lists every command's expected --help output before coding, builds it, polishes it, then smoke-tests it on each platform"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The entry point and subcommands, configuration from files, environment variables and flags, error handling, completion scripts, and notes on UX choices"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "commander, yargs and inquirer in Node.js, click, typer and argparse in Python, and cobra, viper and bubbletea in Go"
            },
            {
              "label": "Targets",
              "text": "Startup under 50 ms, Windows, macOS and Linux, and both interactive and non-interactive use"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its rules come from what makes a CLI pleasant both to use and to script: --help and --version on every tool, consistent flag names, early input validation, clear and actionable error messages, graceful Ctrl+C, logs and diagnostics on stderr so piped output stays clean, colour only when output is a terminal, flag or environment-variable fallbacks so CI never waits on a prompt, no hardcoded paths, and never shipping without shell completions. Renaming a flag or subcommand counts as a breaking change. Its quick-start is a Node.js tool built with commander, and it shows terminal detection in Node.js, Python and Go. Five reference files load by topic: design patterns, CLIs in Node.js, Python and Go, and UX patterns such as progress bars, colours and help text.",
