      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies backend architecture patterns, such as repository and service layers, N+1 prevention, caching, retries, RBAC and structured logging, in Node.js and Next.js APIs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building or reviewing API routes and their data access in a Node.js, Express or Next.js backend"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives the agent patterns for REST URLs, repository and service layers, middleware, queries, transactions, caching, errors, auth, rate limits, job queues and logging"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Server code shaped by those patterns; it adds no steps or files of its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Examples in",
              "text": "TypeScript with Next.js route handlers, Supabase, Redis, Zod and jsonwebtoken"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike ECC's api-design",
              "text": "This one leaves the HTTP contract to that one and covers the code behind the endpoints"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference of server-side patterns from ECC, each shown as TypeScript code. It starts with resource-based REST URLs and query parameters for filtering and pagination, a repository interface that hides data access, with a Supabase implementation, a service layer for business logic, and middleware that wraps handlers with auth. For the database, it selects only the columns it needs, batch-fetches to avoid N+1 queries, and runs transactions as a Postgres function called through Supabase. It puts a Redis cache in front of a repository and shows the cache-aside pattern, a central error handler that turns API errors and Zod validation failures into responses, retries with exponential backoff, JWT verification, and role-based permission checks that wrap route handlers. Rate limits must use a shared store such as Redis, a gateway or the platform's limiter, never per-process counters that reset on deploy and split across replicas. It ends with a simple in-process job queue and a structured JSON logger that tags each request with an ID.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/backend-patterns"
        },
