      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies baseline coding conventions, such as descriptive names, immutability, KISS, DRY and YAGNI, with TypeScript and React examples, when writing or reviewing code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing, reviewing or refactoring code and want a shared floor of naming, readability and quality rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "States KISS, DRY and YAGNI, then shows good and bad TypeScript for naming, immutability, errors, async code, types, React components and APIs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code held to those conventions, plus a list of code smells to catch in review"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike backend-patterns and api-design",
              "text": "This one defers to those ECC skills for server layers and endpoint design"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's shared floor of coding conventions, meant for when no narrower framework skill applies. It starts with four principles: readability first, KISS, DRY and YAGNI. Most of it is pass and fail TypeScript examples: descriptive variable names and verb-noun function names, the spread operator instead of mutation, error handling around fetch, Promise.all for independent calls, and real types instead of any. A React section covers typed components, custom hooks, functional state updates and conditional rendering without nested ternaries. It also sets REST URL conventions, one response shape with Zod validation, a Next.js-style project layout and file naming, comments that explain why rather than what, JSDoc for public APIs, memoization and lazy loading, and arrange-act-assert tests with descriptive names. It ends with code smells to flag: functions over 50 lines, deep nesting that early returns would flatten, and magic numbers instead of named constants.",
