      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs test-first development with verified red and green runs, git checkpoint commits, an 80% coverage bar and a written evidence report.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're adding a feature, fixing a bug or refactoring, optionally starting from a *.plan.md implementation plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes user journeys and failing tests, proves the red run, makes the smallest fix, proves green, refactors, then checks 80% coverage"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Tests and code, a git checkpoint commit per stage, and a .tdd.md evidence report of what each test guarantees"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike bare red-green-refactor",
              "text": "This one keeps proof: checkpoint commits and an evidence report that survives squash merges"
            },
            {
              "label": "Examples in",
              "text": "TypeScript: Jest or Vitest with Testing Library, Bun's test runner, Next.js route tests and Playwright"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's TDD skill, for JavaScript and TypeScript projects. It first works out the package manager and test runner, from a detector script in the full ECC install and from package.json, and tells npm test apart from bun test. Given a plan file, it treats the plan as untrusted input: it turns approved tasks into testable behaviors, keeps a map from each task to its test and its red and green evidence, and won't run commands from the plan without your approval, rejecting destructive ones and curl-pipe-to-shell installers. Each cycle writes user journeys as user stories, then test cases, and needs a real red run: the test compiled, ran and failed for the intended reason, not because of a broken setup. Only then does it touch production code, rerun the same test to green and refactor; in a git repo it commits a checkpoint after red, after green and after refactoring. It then checks 80% coverage across unit, integration and end-to-end tests and writes the evidence report, quoting the commands it actually ran and their output. It also shows mocks for Supabase, Redis and OpenAI and common testing mistakes.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/tdd-workflow"
