        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Points you to Claude Code's /context or Codex's /status for live usage, and on request estimates your installed skills' and tools' context overhead.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You ask how much context is left, or want to know how much context your installed skills, agents and MCP tools take up"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "By default, only names the native meter, /context in Claude Code or /status in Codex, without reading files or running tools"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "On an explicit audit request, a short read-only report of estimated always-loaded overhead by component, the heaviest entries and up to three optimizations"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike file-scanning estimators",
              "text": "Those estimate usage by scanning files; this one never guesses live usage, and its audit reads only the install root you name"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Claude Code and Codex; with any other harness it says it can't read live state and asks which one you use"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Most context questions don't need a scan, and this ECC skill is built around that. Asked how much room is left, the agent calls no tools: it names the harness's own meter, /context or /context all in Claude Code, /status in the Codex CLI or app, since a skill can't run a slash command for you. The installation audit runs only when you ask for it explicitly, with --audit or audit installed components. If the scope is unclear it asks which harness and install root to look at, then reads only that root and the harness's known config files, never the workspace, home directory or transcripts, and caps listings at 50 entries per class. It counts what loads up front: skill and agent descriptions, commands the harness discovers eagerly, always-loaded instruction files, and MCP tool schemas when a manifest exposes them. Full skill and rule bodies are reported separately as disk size, since they load on demand, and every figure is labelled an estimate of static overhead, not live context. A third, opt-in diagnostic runs a short Python snippet on a session JSONL file you pick and prints the share of its bytes taken by attachment records.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/context-budget"
