      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies WCAG 2.2 AA to web, iOS and Android UI, mapping labels, roles, focus, contrast and target sizes to ARIA, SwiftUI and Compose code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're specifying, building or auditing UI components for keyboard, screen reader and WCAG 2.2 AA support"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Takes each component from its native role through perceivable, operable, understandable and robust checks, with concrete thresholds"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Components with 4.5:1 text contrast, 24x24px web or 44x44pt native targets, visible and contained focus, plus a pre-ship checklist"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "HTML and ARIA, SwiftUI accessibility modifiers and Jetpack Compose semantics side by side, with an example for each"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike browser-qa",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, runs axe-core on live pages; this one is applied while the agent writes the UI"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's accessibility skill works from WCAG's four principles, perceivable, operable, understandable and robust, and turns them into five checks per component: use the most semantic native element before a custom role; give text 4.5:1 contrast (3:1 for large text and UI), add text alternatives and let content reflow to 400% zoom; make targets at least 24x24 CSS pixels, reachable by keyboard with a visible focus indicator, and give dragging a single-pointer alternative; keep navigation consistent, write error messages that suggest a fix and don't ask for the same data twice; and use correct name, role and value, with live regions for status updates. A table maps labels, hints, roles and live updates across HTML/ARIA, SwiftUI and Jetpack Compose, with a search form, an icon button and a toggle as examples. It warns against clickable divs, meaning shown by color alone, modals that don't trap focus or can't be escaped, and alt text starting with image of. A closing checklist covers target sizes, focus, focus return from modals and menus, form errors, icon labels and text scaling.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/accessibility"
        },
