        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Diagnoses Blender character and animation problems, like foot sliding, floor penetration or backwards imports, from extracted bone and frame data, not screenshots alone.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A Blender character looks twisted, mirrored, offset or foot-sliding, or you need to know whether a retargeted motion matches the expected pose"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Inventories the scene, maps the skeleton, works out facing axes, samples key frames and measures contact, sliding, crossover, twist and scale drift"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report of scene inventory, orientation, baseline integrity and per-frame findings with coordinates, ending in a pass/fail verdict and the fix needed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Blender, using its own Python (blender --background) or a Blender state exporter to pull the data"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "Your mesh, materials, armature and skinning untouched unless you ask for a repair"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Screenshots hide axis conventions, bone names, scale and per-frame contact, so this ECC skill has the agent pull structured facts out of Blender first and use renders only to confirm them. It lists meshes, armatures, helpers and bounding boxes, separating the character from proxy geometry; maps bones such as hips, knees and feet and flags missing left/right pairs; and works out forward and up from pelvis, spine, head and feet together, catching glTF Y-up versus Blender Z-up mix-ups and backwards imports. It samples first, middle, contact, airborne and extreme frames, more densely around landings and turns, then checks ground penetration, foot sliding on planted frames, crossed legs, limb twist and scale drift against the clean starting pose. Its thresholds: penetration over 1-2 cm is visible, a scale change above 5% points to a rig or constraint problem, and a heading jump over 30 degrees in one frame is suspect. The report separates confirmed failures, with frame numbers, bone names and coordinates, from visual suspicions. Two worked examples cover a sliding walk cycle and a character that moves opposite the way it faces.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/blender-motion-state-inspection"
