      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks the repo, npm, PyPI, MCP servers, skills and GitHub for existing solutions before coding, then adopts, extends or builds.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to add a feature, dependency, integration or utility that may already exist"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks which search channels it has, defines the need, searches registries, MCP servers, skills and GitHub, and scores the candidates"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A decision, adopt as is, extend with a thin wrapper, combine 2-3 small packages or build custom, then the install or minimal code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Inline as a quick five-question check, or for non-trivial needs as a subagent that returns a structured comparison"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike deep-research",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, writes cited reports on any topic; this one only looks for code to reuse before writing it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A research-before-coding habit from ECC, written as steps. The agent first checks which search channels it actually has, ripgrep, a package manager, gh, MCP or docs tools, a local skills folder, and says which it had to skip instead of reporting nothing found. It then defines the functionality and constraints, searches npm or PyPI, MCP servers, skills and GitHub, and scores candidates on functionality, maintenance, community, docs, license and dependencies. A decision matrix picks the outcome: adopt an exact, well-maintained, MIT or Apache match; extend a partial one with a thin wrapper; combine two or three small packages; or build custom code informed by what it found. For non-trivial needs it hands the search to a general-purpose subagent that returns a comparison with a recommendation. It lists usual candidates by category, such as linters, formatters, HTTP clients, validators and Markdown processors, and warns against wrapping a library until it loses its benefits or installing a large package for one small feature. Three worked examples each end in adopting an existing package.",
