      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides team git practice: branching strategies, conventional commits, merge versus rebase, pull requests, conflict resolution, releases and git configuration.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're picking a branching strategy, setting commit conventions, deciding between merge and rebase, or resolving conflicts"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Compares GitHub Flow, trunk-based development and GitFlow by team size and release cadence, then sets conventions for commits, branches, PRs and tags"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Commit, PR title and PR description templates, a code review checklist for reviewers and authors, and branch naming rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "Safe rebasing, undoing mistakes, syncing forks, stashes, semantic versioning, git config and aliases, and pre-commit and pre-push hooks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference of git practice from ECC. It sets out three branching strategies: GitHub Flow, recommended for most teams and for continuous deployment; trunk-based development, for experienced teams with strong CI and feature flags; and GitFlow, for scheduled releases and enterprise projects, with a table of which fits which team. Commits follow Conventional Commits: a type, a scope and an imperative subject of at most 50 characters, with a body that explains why. It explains when to merge and when to rebase, and never to rebase shared, protected or merged branches. Pull requests get a title format, a what, why, how and testing description template, and review checklists for reviewers and authors. Further sections cover resolving and preventing conflicts, branch naming and cleanup, stashes, semantic versioning, annotated tags and changelogs, recommended git config and aliases, a .gitignore, commands for common tasks such as starting a feature, syncing a fork or undoing a commit, and sample pre-commit and pre-push hooks. It closes with anti-patterns such as committing to main, committing secrets, giant PRs and long-lived branches.",
