      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Records architectural decisions as numbered ADR files in docs/adr/, with context, alternatives, consequences and an index, after you approve each draft.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've just chosen between frameworks, databases or patterns, or someone asks why the code is built the way it is"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Spots decision moments in the conversation, drafts a record with context, rejected alternatives and consequences, and writes it once you approve"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Numbered Markdown files in docs/adr/ and a README index listing each decision with its status and date"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "Before creating docs/adr/ and before writing each record; for decisions it only infers from the conversation, it suggests a record rather than starting one"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An ADR, or architecture decision record, is a short file saying what was decided, why, and what was turned down. This ECC skill catches those moments while you code. Explicit cues such as \"ADR this\" or \"we decided to…\" start a record; implicit ones, like comparing two libraries and settling on one, only make the agent suggest one. It drafts Michael Nygard's lightweight format: date, status, deciders, context, the decision, each alternative with pros, cons and why it lost, and positive, negative and risky consequences. Nothing is written until you approve the draft, and the first time it also asks before creating docs/adr/ with an index README and a blank template. It numbers each record after the existing ones and appends it to the index. When you ask why something was chosen, it searches the index and shows the matching context and decision, or offers to record one. It adds rules for good records, such as being specific, readable in two minutes and skipping trivial choices, and a lifecycle from proposed to accepted, deprecated or superseded.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/architecture-decision-records"
        },
