        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets rules for engineering work that agents carry out: evals before coding, 15-minute task units, model tiers by task, review focus and cost tracking.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Agents will do most of a piece of engineering work end to end, while humans keep control of quality and risk"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Has the agent set done criteria and evals first, split work into 15-minute verifiable units, and pick Haiku, Sonnet or Opus by task"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Work checked against evals before and after, with model, token estimate, retries, time and outcome tracked per task"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "When to continue, restart or compact a session, what to review in AI-written code, and how to read tool results before trusting them"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short operating guide from ECC for agent-driven engineering. Before any work, the agent defines what done means, writes a capability eval and a regression eval, runs a baseline, implements, then reruns them and compares. Work is cut into units of about 15 minutes, each verifiable on its own, with one main risk and a clear done condition. Model choice follows the task: Haiku for classification, boilerplate and narrow edits, Sonnet for implementation and refactors, Opus for architecture, root-cause analysis and invariants across files, moving up a tier only when a lower one shows a clear reasoning gap. Sessions continue for closely coupled units, start fresh after major phases, and compact after milestones rather than mid-debugging. Reviews of AI-written code focus on invariants, edge cases, error boundaries, auth assumptions and hidden coupling, not style. A longer section teaches reading a tool's result in order, transport error, then operation status, then document shape, so a failed read is never mistaken for a data mismatch and a write with an unknown outcome is never blindly retried.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/agentic-engineering"
        },
