      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits 12 layers of an agent or LLM app, from prompts and memory to tools and rendering, then ranks findings and orders code-first fixes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An agent or LLM feature works in the playground but misbehaves in your app and you don't know which layer is at fault, or before shipping one"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scopes the system, searches its code, logs, prompts and memory files for known failure patterns, and ties each finding to one of 12 layers"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Severity-ranked findings with file-and-line evidence and a confidence score, a diagnosis, and an ordered code-first fix plan in a set JSON shape"
            },
            {
              "label": "Looks for",
              "text": "Wrapper regressions, memory leaking across conversations, tools required only in prompt text, hidden retry or repair LLM passes, and output mangled in delivery"
            }
          ],
          "text": "This ECC skill, adapted from oh-my-agent-check, starts from the idea that the model is often fine and the wrapper around it is not. It splits an agent system into 12 layers that can each corrupt an answer: system prompt, session history, long-term memory, distillation, active recall, tool selection, tool execution, tool interpretation, answer shaping, platform rendering, hidden repair loops and persistence. The audit runs in four phases. Scope sets the target, entrypoints, models, symptoms and layers to check. Evidence collection reads the agent loop, tool router, prompt assembly, logs and memory files, with ripgrep searches for patterns such as tool rules stated only in prompts or LLM calls outside the main loop. Failure mapping records each finding's symptom, mechanism, layer, root cause, evidence and confidence. The fix strategy puts code before prompts: enforce tool requirements in code, make fallback agents explicit, cut duplicated context, rank user corrections above agent claims in memory, and pass output through untouched. Findings come first, critical to low, with no compliments, then the diagnosis and the fix plan.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/agent-architecture-audit"
        },
        "tags": [
