          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "agent-development",
          "debugging"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "affaan-m/ecc/skills/error-handling",
      "name": "error-handling",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/affaan-m/ecc/error-handling",
        "installs": 6000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [274, 172, 174, 255, 437, 431, 505, 474],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies error-handling patterns in TypeScript, Python and Go: typed error hierarchies, result types, API error envelopes, retries with backoff and safe user-facing messages.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing error types, adding retries, wiring API error responses or writing the messages users see when something fails"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives typed error classes and a Result type in TypeScript, an exception hierarchy with FastAPI handlers in Python, and wrapped sentinel errors in Go"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code where every catch handles, rethrows or logs, API errors share one code-and-message envelope, and users never see stack traces"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "A Next.js API error handler, a React error boundary, exponential backoff with jitter that skips client errors, and a pre-merge checklist"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's error-handling reference starts from five rules: fail fast and loudly, make errors typed values rather than strings, keep user messages apart from developer details, never swallow an error, and treat error codes as part of the API contract. In TypeScript it builds an AppError hierarchy carrying codes and HTTP statuses, for not found, validation, unauthorized and rate limited; a Result type for failures that are expected; a Next.js API handler that maps app errors and Zod validation errors to one response envelope; and a React error boundary. Python gets a matching exception hierarchy and FastAPI exception handlers that log the full error and return a generic message. Go wraps sentinel errors with context and unwraps them at the handler to pick the HTTP status. A shared retry helper uses exponential backoff with jitter and retries only what a predicate allows, not 4xx errors. A table maps error codes to plain user messages. The checklist to clear before merging covers silent catches, the envelope, server-side logging, async errors, retries and error boundaries.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/error-handling"
        },
