      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Defines capability and regression evals before the agent codes, grades them by code, model or human, and reports pass@k reliability.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want pass/fail criteria for an agent task before it starts, or proof that a prompt, agent or model change didn't break what worked"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Has the agent write capability and regression evals first, implement, run them with code, rule, model or human graders, then report"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Eval definitions and run history under .claude/evals/, and a report of pass@1 and pass@3 results with a status such as READY FOR REVIEW"
            },
            {
              "label": "Explains",
              "text": "pass@k means at least one success in k tries; pass^k means all k tries succeed, the bar for critical paths"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Eval-driven development treats evals as the unit tests of AI-assisted work. Before coding, the agent writes capability evals, things it should now be able to do, each with success criteria, and regression evals, existing behavior that must keep passing against a baseline commit. Then it implements, runs the evals and writes a report. Graders come in four kinds: code checks such as grep, the test suite or a build; rule checks of regex or schema constraints; a model grader that scores a change from 1 to 5 against set questions; and a flag for human review with a risk level, which it requires for security. Suggested thresholds are a pass@3 of at least 90% for capability evals and a pass^3 of 100% for release-critical regressions. Definitions, run history and baselines live under .claude/evals/, with release snapshots in docs/releases/. It warns against overfitting prompts to eval examples, testing only happy paths, ignoring cost and latency, and flaky graders in release gates. A section on ECC's local utilities describes hash-linked run records, replay and receipts, with running candidates disabled until an isolation backend exists.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/eval-harness"
