          "text": "Its one rule is that every investor document must agree with every other. Before drafting, the agent inventories the canonical facts (traction metrics, pricing and revenue assumptions, raise size and instrument, use of funds, team bios and titles, milestones), lists the missing assumptions, and resolves any conflicting numbers first. Then it picks the asset and drafts it with explicit logic. Decks follow a twelve-part order; one-pagers and memos state the company in one sentence, show why now, put traction early and make a precise ask; financial models get visible assumptions, bear, base and bull cases where useful, layered revenue logic, milestone-linked spending and sensitivity analysis; accelerator answers address the exact question and match the deck. It steers clear of unverifiable claims, market sizing without assumptions, inconsistent titles and revenue math that doesn't sum. A final gate cross-checks every number against the source of truth. For a web-based deck it suggests pairing with ECC's frontend-slides skill.",
