      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a one-line objective into a reviewed, multi-PR construction plan whose steps a fresh agent can each pick up cold.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A feature, refactor or migration will span several PRs or sessions; it says not to use it for work that fits in one PR"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the repo, splits the objective into one-PR steps with dependencies, parallel lanes, a model tier and a rollback each, then has a strongest-model subagent review the plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A Markdown plan in plans/, where every step has a context brief, tasks, verification commands and exit criteria"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike a to-do list",
              "text": "That covers one session; here each step is written so an agent with no prior context can execute it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Subagents for the review; git and the GitHub CLI are optional, and without them it plans direct edits with no branches"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Blueprint runs five phases. Research: it checks git, gh auth, the remote and the default branch, then reads the project structure, existing plans and memory files. Design: it breaks the objective into one-PR-sized steps, typically 3 to 12, and gives each dependency edges, serial or parallel ordering, a model tier (strongest or default) and a rollback strategy; steps that share no files or outputs can run in parallel. Draft: it writes a self-contained plan file to plans/, so a fresh agent can execute any step without reading the ones before it. Review: a strongest-model subagent, such as Opus, checks the plan for completeness, dependency correctness and anti-patterns, and every critical finding is fixed. Register: it saves the plan, updates the memory index and reports the step count and parallelism. With git and gh, every step comes with its branch, PR and CI workflow. A plan mutation protocol lets steps be split, inserted, skipped, reordered or abandoned, with an audit trail. You call it as /blueprint with a project name and the objective. ECC's version is inspired by antbotlab/blueprint.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/blueprint"
