      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Puts a hard decision to three fresh subagents, a Skeptic, a Pragmatist and a Critic, then weighs their views against its own.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A decision has several credible options and no obvious winner, such as ship now or hold, or a feature flag versus a full rollout"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes down the agent's own position first, then asks Skeptic, Pragmatist and Critic subagents in parallel, each given only the question and the context it needs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A short verdict with each voice's position, the consensus, the strongest dissent, a check on the question's premise and a recommendation"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike Warp's council",
              "text": "That one spreads one question across different models; this one uses fixed roles and keeps the agent as a fourth voice"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Subagents"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Council is for decisions under ambiguity, not code review, implementation plans, architecture design or factual questions. The agent first reduces the decision to one question with its constraints and what counts as success, asking you one clarifying question if it's vague. It gathers only the context that matters, then records its own position as the Architect, with three reasons and its main risk, before reading anyone else's. The other three voices start as fresh subagents without the conversation, which keeps them from anchoring on it: the Skeptic challenges the framing and offers the simplest alternative, the Pragmatist argues for speed and real-world execution, and the Critic looks for edge cases and failure modes. Each answers in under 300 words with a position, reasons, a risk and one thing the others may miss. In the synthesis, the agent must explain any view it dismisses, say so when a voice changed its recommendation, and always show the strongest dissent. It runs one round by default, and saves a decision only when it changes something real, through ECC's knowledge-ops skill, a saved session or the relevant issue.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/council"
