        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Hardens AI agents that sign transactions or trade, with spend limits, pre-send simulation, circuit breakers, wallet isolation and injection checks on outside data.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building or auditing an AI agent that signs transactions, places orders, swaps tokens or touches a treasury"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Layers independent controls: injection checks on outside data, hard spend limits, simulation before every send, circuit breakers and an isolated hot wallet"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Python patterns for each control and a nine-item pre-deploy checklist"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike ECC's security-review",
              "text": "That one is a general web-app checklist; this one covers agents whose mistakes turn straight into lost funds"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Trading agents have a harsher threat model than other LLM apps, since an injected instruction or a bad tool path becomes asset loss, so the skill treats prompt hygiene, spend policy, simulation, execution limits and wallet isolation as separate defenses. Token names, pair labels, webhooks and social feeds are checked against injection patterns, such as instructions to send or approve funds, before they reach a prompt that can execute. Per-transaction and daily spend limits are enforced in code, independent of the model's output. Each transaction is simulated with eth_call first, and refused without a minimum output or when the simulation falls short of it. In its example, a circuit breaker halts trading after three consecutive losses or a 5% hourly drawdown. Keys come from the environment or a secret manager, for a dedicated hot wallet that holds only the session's funds, never the main treasury. Against MEV it suggests a private RPC such as Flashbots, slippage caps per strategy and short deadlines. The checklist also asks for an audit log of every agent decision, not only successful sends.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/llm-trading-agent-security"
