        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets team process for codebases mostly written by agents: planning and evals over typing, behavior-focused reviews, agent-friendly architecture and a higher testing bar.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're setting process, review gates or ownership rules for a team whose code is largely written by agents"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Shifts the weight from typing to planning, from anecdotes to eval coverage, and from reviewing syntax to reviewing system behavior"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Rules for agent-friendly architecture, what reviews should check, hiring signals and a higher testing bar for generated code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Length",
              "text": "One short page of principles, with no steps or tools"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short operating model from ECC for teams where agents write much of the implementation. It asks for architectures agents can work in safely: explicit boundaries, stable contracts, typed interfaces and deterministic tests, without behavior spread across hidden conventions. Code review focuses on behavior regressions, security assumptions, data integrity, failure handling and rollout safety, leaving style to automation. Strong engineers in such a team decompose ambiguous work, define measurable acceptance criteria, write high-signal prompts and evals, and keep risk controls under delivery pressure. Generated code meets a higher testing bar: regression coverage for every touched domain, explicit edge-case assertions and integration checks at interface boundaries.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/ai-first-engineering"
        },
