      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Measures whether Claude Code actually follows a skill, rule or agent definition, by generating test scenarios, running them headless and scoring the tool calls.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've written a skill or rule and want to know whether the agent follows it, rather than assume it does"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Derives the expected steps from the file, writes scenarios at three prompt strictness levels, runs each with claude -p and classifies the tool calls"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A Markdown report with the spec, the prompts, a compliance score per scenario and a labelled timeline of tool calls"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Claude Code and uv; full runs spend tokens on every scenario, while a dry run stops after the cheaper model writes the spec and scenarios"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works on",
              "text": "SKILL.md files, rule files and agent definitions, the last only for whether the agent gets invoked when expected"
            }
          ],
          "text": "skill-comply tests instructions the way you'd test code. Given a skill, rule or agent file, its bundled Python script has a cheaper model, Haiku by default, extract the expected sequence of steps, then write scenarios whose prompts go from supportive to neutral to competing, so you can see whether the instruction holds even when the prompt doesn't back it. Each scenario runs in a fresh sandbox folder through Claude Code's headless mode, Sonnet by default, with its tool calls captured as stream-json. An LLM, not a regex, matches the calls to the expected steps, and their order is checked deterministically. The report holds the spec, the scenario prompts, each scenario's compliance score and the tool-call timeline with labels, and for steps with low compliance it suggests turning them into hooks. A --dry-run flag prints the spec and scenarios and skips the scenario runs, so only the generation model is called, and --model and --gen-model pick the models.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/skill-comply"
