    {
      "id": "affaan-m/ecc/skills/click-path-audit",
      "name": "click-path-audit",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/affaan-m/ecc/click-path-audit",
        "installs": 5700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [257, 163, 141, 235, 417, 407, 477, 448],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Traces each button's handler through its state changes to find calls that silently undo each other, races and stale closures in React apps.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Users report a button that does nothing though debugging found no bug, or after changing a shared state store"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Maps what every store action sets and resets, then traces each handler's calls in order against what its label promises"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Numbered findings with a severity, the bug pattern, a call-by-call trace, expected and actual state, and a fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Looks for",
              "text": "Sequential undo, async races, stale closures, missing state transitions, dead conditional paths and useEffect interference"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike general debugging",
              "text": "This one leaves API, styling and performance bugs aside and checks only the state flow behind each touchpoint"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Some bugs survive review because every function works on its own. The one behind this skill: a \"New Email\" button turned compose mode on, then cleared the selected thread, and clearing the thread reset compose mode, so the button did nothing. The audit starts by mapping each Zustand store or React context action: which fields it sets and which it resets as a side effect, flagging actions that clear state they don't own. Then, for every button, toggle and form submit in scope, it lists the handler's calls in order with what each reads and writes, and checks whether a later call undoes an earlier one, whether the final state matches the button's label, and whether async calls can resolve in the wrong order. You scope it to one page, to the consumers of a changed store, or to the whole app, which it splits across parallel agents once one agent has mapped the stores. It suggests running it after a systematic debugging pass and turning each finding into a test.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/click-path-audit"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
