      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Adds x402 crypto payments to agents and paid APIs, with per-task budgets, recipient allowlists, user confirmation and replay protection.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An agent has to pay for an API, a service or another agent itself, or your API should charge agents per call"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks agentwallet-sdk, OKX's Agent Payments Protocol or the upstream x402 packages by chain and side, then wires spending controls that fail closed"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Client code that refuses payments off its allowed networks, tokens, recipients or budget, and seller middleware that runs each paid request once"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also called",
              "text": "x402: a server answers HTTP 402 Payment Required, and the agent's payment tool checks the price, signs and retries"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A guide to agent payments over x402, the protocol that turns an HTTP 402 Payment Required reply into a payment an agent can negotiate, sign and retry with. A decision table picks the path: agentwallet-sdk as an MCP payment server for Base, OKX's Agent Payments Protocol skill for X Layer, or the upstream x402 fetch and axios wrappers for Solana and other networks, plus seller middleware for TypeScript, Python, Go, Rust and Java. Spending policy is set by the orchestrator before delegation, never by the agent: per-task and per-session budgets, allowlisted recipients and rate limits, with keys kept in the orchestrator's environment. Its long TypeScript example checks network, token, recipient and amount against the server's actual challenge before signing, allows only approved origins and no redirects, reserves the session budget before each call and asks you once to approve the caps. For sellers it covers creating the Solana token account payments go to, and a Redis claim that stops one signed payment from running a handler several times. It advises pinned versions and testnets first, and discloses that its facilitator section was written by someone at PayAI.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/agent-payment-x402"
        },
