      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes CodeTour .tour files that walk a chosen reader, such as a new joiner or PR reviewer, through real files and verified line anchors.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an onboarding, architecture, PR, root-cause or security tour a reader can step through file by file"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A .tours/<persona>-<focus>.tour JSON file in Microsoft's CodeTour format, which opens each step at its file and line, with no change to the source code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Explores the repo, picks a persona and step count, verifies every path and line, and sets the git ref so new files still open"
            },
            {
              "label": "Writes",
              "text": "Each step as situation, mechanism, implication and gotcha, along an arc from orientation to the core path to a next move"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike codebase-onboarding",
              "text": "That one, from ECC, is for broad onboarding without a tour file"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's code-tour writes guided walkthroughs as CodeTour files instead of Markdown notes. It explores the repo first: README, entry points, folder structure, config and, for a PR tour, the changed files. It then infers the reader and depth from your request, from a 5-8 step vibe check to a 14-18 step architecture tour, with personas for new joiners, PR reviewers, root-cause investigators, security reviewers, feature explainers and bug fixers. Steps can anchor to a directory, a file and line, a selection, a text pattern for code that moves, or a URI such as the PR itself. Each description follows what it calls SMIG: the situation, the mechanism, the implication for this reader, and the gotcha a smart reader would miss. Before finishing it checks that every path and line exists and that the tour tells a story rather than listing files. It also explains the ref field: a PR tour pinned to the base branch can't open files the PR adds, so it points ref at the PR branch or leaves it out.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/code-tour"
