        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a PRD, roadmap item or product discussion into an implementation-ready capability plan of constraints, states, interfaces, non-goals and open questions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Product intent is clear but the rules, invariants and edge cases engineers need still live in someone's head"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Restates the capability in one sentence, pulls out business rules, invariants, trust boundaries and rollout needs, and marks what's still undecided"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An SRS-style plan, kept in the repo's product docs such as PRODUCT.md, ending in a handoff: build, architecture review or product clarification first"
            },
            {
              "label": "Comes after",
              "text": "ECC's product-lens, which decides whether to build it at all"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The step between deciding to build something and writing code. It reads only what it needs: the PRD, issue or founder note, the relevant architecture docs, schemas and routes, any existing PRODUCT.md or RFCs, and delivery constraints such as auth, billing, compliance and backwards compatibility. It compresses the ask into one statement of who gains which capability and what changes, then extracts the constraints that must hold first: business rules, scope, invariants, trust boundaries, data ownership, lifecycle transitions, rollout and migration needs, and failure and recovery. The result is a plan with actors and surfaces, states and transitions, interfaces, data model implications, security, billing and policy constraints, observability needs, non-goals and blocking open questions. It never invents product truth: unresolved points stay marked open, and conflicts with the repo's existing constraints are called out. It ends by saying whether the work is ready to implement or needs architecture review or product clarification first, and which ECC skill should take it next.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/product-capability"
        },
