        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies HIPAA decision gates and guardrails to healthcare software, keeping PHI out of logs, prompts and vendors without a BAA.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A task mentions HIPAA, protected health information (PHI), covered entities or business associate agreements (BAAs) in US healthcare software"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks before PHI moves: is it PHI, is this a covered entity or business associate, does the vendor need a BAA, is access minimal and audited"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Designs that keep PHI out of logs, URLs, prompts and analytics, with outside vendors and LLM providers blocked until their BAA status is clear"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike healthcare-phi-compliance",
              "text": "That one, from ECC, holds the detailed PHI handling rules; this one adds HIPAA's own gates on top and sends the agent there"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "ECC's healthcare-phi-compliance and security-review skills and its healthcare-reviewer agent"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's HIPAA entry point stays deliberately thin. It treats HIPAA as an overlay: the agent starts from the healthcare-phi-compliance skill for concrete rules on handling protected health information (PHI), then applies HIPAA's own gates. Is this data PHI? Is the actor a covered entity or a business associate? Does a vendor or model provider need a business associate agreement (BAA) before it touches the data? Is access limited to the minimum necessary, and are reads, writes and exports audited? Its guardrails: no PHI in logs, analytics, crash reports, prompts, error strings, URLs, browser storage or screenshots; authenticated, scoped and audited access; opaque internal IDs instead of names or record numbers; and third-party SaaS, observability, support tools and LLM providers blocked by default until their BAA status is clear. Two examples show the pattern: checking that a summarization provider is covered by a BAA before any PHI reaches it, and blocking patient messages from an analytics stack unless the vendor is approved and the data minimized. Work that touches patient safety or clinical workflows goes to ECC's healthcare-reviewer agent.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/hipaa-compliance"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:security",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "privacy",
          "security"
        ],
        "note": "A thin entry point: it hands the detailed PHI rules to ECC's healthcare-phi-compliance skill and clinical reviews to its healthcare-reviewer agent, so install those alongside.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
