        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans a home or homelab network: gateway, switch and access point roles, non-overlapping IP ranges, DHCP reservations, home.arpa names and cabling.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're planning a home or lab network, outgrowing the ISP router, or fighting double NAT, flaky Wi-Fi or servers that change address"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Separates gateway, switch and access point roles, compares gateway options and lays out an IP plan that's ready for VLANs later"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A subnet per device group, DHCP reservation and local DNS conventions, and a list of mistakes to avoid"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keep in mind",
              "text": "It advises and gives example plans; it doesn't configure any device"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A planning guide for home and small-lab networks that should grow without a rebuild. It starts by separating roles: the modem, a gateway that handles NAT, firewall, DHCP and DNS, a managed switch, access points for Wi-Fi only, then servers and clients. A table weighs gateway options, from the ISP router through UniFi, OPNsense or pfSense and MikroTik to a Linux router. The IP plan avoids 192.168.1.0/24, which clashes with hotels, offices and ISP routers once you use a VPN, and gives each group its own range (trusted clients, IoT, servers, guest, management) with the gateway on .1, infrastructure on .2 to .49 and the DHCP pool from .50. Local names go under home.arpa, the name reserved for home networks. Anything you SSH into, bookmark or monitor gets a DHCP reservation, and a Pi-hole gets its address before DHCP points clients at it. It favours wired access point backhaul, PoE switches, labelled cables and UPS power, and walks through a beginner upgrade and a VLAN-ready plan.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/homelab-network-setup"
        },
