      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Lays out a persistent multi-agent setup in Claude Code: a routing CLAUDE.md, specialist agent files, slash commands, file-based memory and external cron.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want Claude Code to run as a lasting personal or project system with its own agents, memory and scheduled jobs, not one-off chats"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Lays out a CLAUDE.md \"kernel\" that routes requests to specialist agent files, plus slash commands, scripts and a data folder"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Templates for the kernel, an agent file, commands like /daily-sync, a daily log, and LaunchAgent, systemd or pm2 schedules"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "State in JSON and Markdown files under data/, with no database"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Claude Code, plus a LaunchAgent, systemd timer or pm2 for timed runs"
            }
          ],
          "text": "This skill treats Claude Code as a long-running system instead of a chat. The kernel is a short CLAUDE.md that makes the agent a coordinator, with a registry table of specialist agents (@dev, @writer, @researcher, @ops) and their trigger words, and routing rules: match the request, load that agent's file, hand off, then combine the results. Each specialist is a Markdown file with its identity, the files it reads and writes, its tools and its constraints. Slash commands in .claude/commands hold repeatable routines such as a morning briefing, research or logging a decision. Memory lives in data/: append-only daily logs, per-project context, decision records, an inbox and contacts, with a reflection written at the end of each session. Scheduled runs use macOS LaunchAgents, systemd timers or pm2 rather than Claude Code's own cron, which dies with the session. It warns against one agent doing everything, stateless sessions, keys in agent files, a database for one person's state and routing written in code, and ends with a checklist, such as keeping CLAUDE.md under 200 lines.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/agentic-os"
