      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits your installed Claude skills with subagents, giving each a keep, improve, update, retire or merge verdict with a reason.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Your ~/.claude/skills folder has grown and you want to know which skills still earn their place"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Inventories global and project skills, has subagents judge about 20 at a time for overlap, staleness and usefulness, and saves the verdicts"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A table with a verdict and reason per skill (keep, improve, update, retire or merge), then proposals you confirm one by one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "As /skill-stocktake: a quick scan of changed skills takes 5–10 minutes, a full stocktake 20–30"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Claude Code with subagents, plus Bash, Node.js and jq; its scripts and results cache expect it installed at ~/.claude/skills/skill-stocktake"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A slash command that reviews the skills in ~/.claude/skills and the current project's .claude/skills. A full stocktake runs a bundled scan script that lists every skill with its frontmatter and modification time, plus 7- and 30-day use counts when an optional observations log exists; without it, usage shows as unmeasured, never as zero. It then hands batches of about 20 skills to a general-purpose subagent with a checklist: overlap with other skills and with MEMORY.md or CLAUDE.md, whether the tools and APIs it names are still current (checked by web search), and usage. The judgment is holistic, on actionability, scope, uniqueness and currency, and each verdict needs a self-contained reason, such as which skill already covers the same need. Results go to a results.json cache after each batch, so an interrupted run resumes, and a later quick scan re-evaluates only skills that changed. It ends with a summary table and proposals: retirements and merges with their justification, specific improvements, updated content, and a proposal to compress MEMORY.md once it passes 100 lines. Archiving or deleting always waits for your confirmation.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/skill-stocktake"
