      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Searches installed, marketplace, GitHub and web skills before you create a new one, vets the matches, and offers use, fork or create.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to create a skill, or ask whether a skill for some job already exists"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Searches your installed and marketplace skills, then GitHub and the web, reads each outside match for risky commands, and ranks up to ten"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A ranked table of matches with what each one lacks, and a choice: use it, fork it or create a fresh skill"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike skill-stocktake",
              "text": "That one, from ECC, audits the skills you already have; this one checks what exists before you add one"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A search-before-you-build step for skills. The agent pulls the task, triggers, tools and three to five keywords with synonyms out of your request. It searches skill names and descriptions under ~/.claude/skills and ~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces first, then GitHub, for example with gh repo and code searches, and at most three web queries. Before recommending anything from outside, it reads the SKILL.md for unexpected shell commands, file writes, network calls, credential handling or package installs, and checks that the repo looks maintained. Candidates are ranked by name match, description match, local source, maintained GitHub repo and web-only mention, capped at ten. You get a table of matches with why each fits and where it falls short, and three options: use an existing skill, fork or extend the closest one, or create a fresh one. It creates nothing until you choose that path or the search finds no close match, and it skips the search when you say to.",
