      "id": "affaan-m/ecc/skills/content-hash-cache-pattern",
      "name": "content-hash-cache-pattern",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/affaan-m/ecc/content-hash-cache-pattern",
        "installs": 5600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [257, 157, 139, 232, 408, 398, 467, 441],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Caches slow file processing under a SHA-256 hash of each file's content, so moved files still hit and edited files reprocess.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A pipeline keeps re-parsing the same PDFs, images or text files, or a CLI needs a --cache/--no-cache option"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Keys each result by a SHA-256 of the file's content, stores it as its own JSON file, and wraps caching around the untouched processing function"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A cache that survives renames and moves, invalidates itself when content changes and needs no index file"
            },
            {
              "label": "Examples in",
              "text": "Python, with frozen dataclasses"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A design pattern for caching expensive file work such as PDF parsing, OCR or image analysis. Instead of caching by path, which breaks when a file moves, it hashes the file's content in 64 KB chunks with SHA-256 and uses that hash as the key, so a moved file is still a hit and an edited one is a miss. Each entry is saved as its own {hash}.json file, for direct lookup without an index, and a corrupt entry counts as a miss rather than an error. Caching sits in a separate service-layer function that checks the cache, calls the processing function on a miss and writes the result, so the processing code knows nothing about caching. It logs hits and misses with shortened hashes and serializes entries by hand rather than with dataclasses.asdict. It also says when not to use it: data that must always be fresh, very large entries, and results that depend on settings beyond the file's content.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/content-hash-cache-pattern"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
