          "text": "Give it the path to a photo or scan of an official document and it runs the whole job without stopping to ask. HEIC files are converted to PNG with macOS's sips, and the image is rotated from its EXIF orientation, or by 180 degrees if it reads upside down. It tries OCR engines in turn, macOS's Vision framework, then EasyOCR, then Tesseract, and identifies the kind of document: deposit, income, employment or retirement certificates, property certificates, business licences, ID cards, passports and others, listed with their Chinese names. The translation keeps the document's structure and every number, date and amount, and gives proper names in the original language with English in parentheses, Chinese names in pinyin. The agent then writes and runs a Python script with Pillow and reportlab that puts the rotated original on an A4 first page and the translation on the second, under a centred bold title, with a closing line calling it a certified English translation. The PDF is saved next to the source image.",
