      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Splits a large feature's RFC into dependent work units for several agents, each researched, built, tested and reviewed, then merged through a queue.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A feature is too big for one agent pass and you have an RFC to split among several agents"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Breaks the RFC into a graph of dependent work units, runs each through research, plan, build, tests and review, then merges them through a queue"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An execution log, a scorecard per unit, a snapshot of the dependency graph and an integration risk summary"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keep in mind",
              "text": "It's a one-page outline of stages and rules, with no scripts or prompts of its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An outline for running a large feature as many agent-sized pieces. The RFC is decomposed into a DAG, a graph of work units in which each unit lists what it depends on, its scope, acceptance tests, a risk level and a rollback plan. Units fall into three tiers, from isolated file edits with deterministic tests to schema, auth, performance or security changes. Each unit goes through research, an implementation plan, implementation, tests, review and a merge-ready report. Merges go through a queue: never merge a unit whose dependencies failed, always rebase unit branches on the latest integration branch, and rerun integration tests after each merge. A stalled unit is pulled from the queue, its findings saved, its scope narrowed, and it's retried with the new constraints. The run ends with a final verification of the whole system.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/ralphinho-rfc-pipeline"
        },
