      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Gates output before it ships: two independent reviewers grade it on one rubric, both must pass, and fixes loop up to three rounds.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Output will be published, deployed or seen by users, or must meet compliance, brand or accuracy rules; not for drafts or what tests already check"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Spawns two reviewer subagents with the same rubric and no shared context; if either fails the output, all flagged issues get fixed and fresh reviewers check again"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Output both reviewers passed or, after three failed rounds, an escalation to you with the open issues"
            },
            {
              "label": "Costs",
              "text": "About two to three times the tokens of generating alone; for batches of 100+ items it reviews a 10–15% sample instead"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike self-review",
              "text": "An agent reviewing its own work shares the blind spots that produced it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A verification layer named after its slogan: make a list, check it twice, and fix what's naughty until it's nice. The agent produces the deliverable as usual. Then two review agents each get the task spec, the output and the same rubric, never seeing each other's verdict, and return JSON with a pass or fail per criterion, critical issues and suggestions. The output ships only if both pass; an issue one reviewer catches counts as real. Otherwise every critical issue from both is fixed, with instructions to change nothing else, and two fresh reviewers start over, so earlier rounds don't anchor them. After three rounds it escalates to a human. The rubric is the key input: each criterion needs an objective pass condition, with defaults for factual accuracy, hallucinations, completeness, compliance, consistency and technical correctness, plus extensions for marketing, code and regulated content. Subagents are the recommended way; without them it falls back to inline reviews with context resets. It also covers failure modes such as rubber-stamping and metrics such as first-pass rate.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/santa-method"
        },
