      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks a deployed URL after a release for HTTP errors, console errors, failed requests, Web Vitals regressions, missing content, broken assets and SSE streams.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've just deployed, merged a risky PR or upgraded dependencies and want to confirm the live site didn't regress"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks eight things, from HTTP status and new console errors to LCP, CLS and INP, key page elements, API response times, static assets and SSE heartbeats"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A canary report: each check against its baseline, sorted into critical, warning and info by set thresholds"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "One pass by default, every N minutes for M hours in sustained mode, or as a staging-versus-production comparison"
            },
            {
              "label": "Alerts",
              "text": "A desktop notification, an optional Slack or Discord webhook and a line in ~/.claude/canary-watch.log"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "A browser tool the agent can drive for the console and performance checks; the skill names none"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A post-deploy watch for a live URL. It defines what to look at: HTTP status, new console errors, failed network calls and 5xx responses, LCP, CLS and INP against a baseline, whether key elements like the h1, nav, footer and call to action are still there, critical API endpoints within their SLA, JS, CSS, image and font requests with the expected content types, and event-stream (SSE) endpoints that connect and receive a first event or heartbeat. Thresholds sort findings: a non-200 status, more than five new console errors, LCP over 4 seconds, a 5xx API or a broken asset are critical; slower responses, layout shift, new console warnings and changed content types are warnings. Modes are a single quick check, a sustained watch at an interval for a set duration, and a diff of staging against production. Critical findings trigger a desktop notification, an optional Slack or Discord webhook and a log entry. It suggests pairing it with browser-qa before deploys, a hook after git push, or a CI step after deploy.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/canary-watch"
