      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Works a billing case in a tool like Stripe: finds the customer, classifies the problem, applies the safest reversible fix and drafts a follow-up.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A customer says billing is broken, wants a refund or can't cancel, or you're chasing duplicate subscriptions, failed renewals or churn risk"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds the customer from an email or Stripe ID, puts the case in one of five buckets, then prefers restoring self-serve control over refunding"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An operator handoff: customer and billing state, the decision and why, the action taken, a short customer message and the product gap to fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike payment API guides",
              "text": "This one is for handling real customers' billing, not writing billing code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Guardrails",
              "text": "Never shows secret keys, full card details or unneeded personal data, and never refunds before classifying the case"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A support workflow for subscription billing problems, built around four questions: who is this customer, what happened, what's the safest fix and what should we send them. It prefers a connected billing tool such as Stripe, with email, GitHub or issue trackers only as supporting evidence. It starts from the strongest identifier, such as an email, customer, subscription or invoice ID, and summarizes active and canceled subscriptions, invoices and anomalies like duplicate active subscriptions. Each case goes into one bucket before any action: duplicate personal subscription, real team purchase, failed or incomplete checkout, missing self-serve controls, or product failure. Actions follow a fixed order of preference: restore self-serve management, fix the broken billing state, refund only the affected charge, document why, then send a short follow-up. Annual, team and prorated plans get their contract checked first. When the pain comes from a missing billing portal, cancellation flow or duplicate-subscription guard, it reports that as a product gap rather than a one-off ticket.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/customer-billing-ops"
