      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds an app autonomously with planner, generator and evaluator agents; the evaluator tests the live app through Playwright and scores it until it passes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a whole app or a polished frontend built from a one-line brief and will spend hours and $50–200 on quality"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "A planner expands the brief into a spec, a generator builds it, and an evaluator clicks through the running app, scores it and sends feedback for the next round"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An app that cleared a weighted 7 out of 10 on design, originality, craft and functionality, or stopped at the iteration cap"
            },
            {
              "label": "Based on",
              "text": "Anthropic's March 2026 post on harness design for long-running apps; GAN refers to generative adversarial networks, where one model creates and another critiques"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Claude Code and the Playwright MCP server; its commands, script and agents ship with the full ECC plugin"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An agent harness that keeps building and judging apart, because agents grading their own work are too generous. A planner turns a one-line prompt into an ambitious spec of about 16 features over several sprints, with design direction and evaluation criteria. The generator implements it, agrees a sprint contract with the evaluator before coding, commits between iterations and reads each feedback file. The evaluator, told to be ruthlessly strict, uses Playwright to click through the live app, fill forms and hit endpoints, then scores design quality, originality, craft and functionality from 1 to 10 with weights; 7.0 passes, and it stops after 15 iterations by default. You run it with ECC's /project:gan-build or /project:gan-design commands, its gan-harness.sh script, or by hand with claude -p steps. Environment variables set the models, iterations, threshold, criteria, dev server and evaluation mode: playwright, screenshot or code-only. It advises stripping harness parts as models improve, and warns against lenient evaluators, generators ignoring feedback and endless loops.",
