              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code in Python with web3.py, TypeScript with ethers and Solidity, plus a cast command to check a token's decimals"
            },
            {
              "label": "Why",
              "text": "The same stablecoin can use different decimals on different chains, and the bug throws no error"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short rule set from ECC for a bug that fails silently: assuming a token's decimals. A symbol with 6 decimals on one chain can have a different count elsewhere, so it never hardcodes them. The agent queries decimals() at runtime, caches the result by chain ID and token address rather than by symbol, and does value math with Decimal, BigInt or another exact type instead of floats. For old or non-standard tokens whose decimals() call reverts, it falls back to 18 and logs a visible warning. In Solidity it normalizes amounts to 18 decimals (a WAD) before comparing or pricing. Examples cover a web3.py balance read and a cached decimals lookup, ethers' formatUnits in TypeScript and a Foundry cast call for a quick on-chain check. It also says to query decimals again after bridging or a wrapper change.",
