        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Diagnoses CRC errors, drops, flapping and duplex or speed mismatches on routers, switches and Linux hosts by comparing interface counters over time.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A host or VLAN has packet loss or intermittent reachability, or a switch port shows CRCs, drops, resets or flaps"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Captures a counter baseline, waits, captures again and compares the increments, on both ends of the link"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A likely cause from a counter table, such as a bad cable or optic, a duplex or MTU mismatch, or a congested uplink"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also has",
              "text": "A Python parser for show interfaces output that slices each interface block cleanly"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A diagnostic flow from ECC for problems that may sit in a physical link, switch port, cable, transceiver, duplex setting or congested interface. Counters are evidence, but the trend matters more than the total, so the agent records a baseline, waits a measurement interval and compares the increments: show interfaces and show logging on network gear, ip -s link and ethtool on Linux hosts. A counter table maps CRCs, input errors, runts, giants, input and output drops, resets and collisions to their usual causes. For CRCs it checks both ends, since receive errors point at the signal arriving on that side, then the cable, fiber or optic, then speed and duplex. For drops it separates input from output and proves congestion before tuning queues. It prefers auto-negotiation on both sides and never mixes fixed and auto settings. Worked examples cover CRCs on one switch port and slow internet with a healthy LAN. Anti-patterns include clearing counters before saving a baseline and blaming the cable for output drops.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/network-interface-health"
        },
