      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a planned home network change, such as VLANs, Pi-hole DNS or WireGuard access, and returns a staged plan with checks and rollback.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to split a flat home network into VLANs, point DHCP at Pi-hole or AdGuard Home, or add VPN access"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Takes an inventory of your edge, gateway, switches, Wi-Fi, subnets, DNS and DHCP, and how you'd recover, then looks for ways you could lock yourself out"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Trust zones with default policies, a staged change sequence, the validation evidence to collect, and a review checklist"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Give firewall, VLAN, DHCP or VPN commands until your platform and a rollback path are confirmed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike homelab-vlan-segmentation",
              "text": "That one gives UniFi, pfSense and MikroTik configuration; this one stays vendor-neutral"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A planning and review skill from ECC for home and small-lab networks, used before anything changes. Its first answer stays read-only: inventory, risks, a staged plan, validation and rollback. It asks about the internet edge, gateway, switch ports, SSIDs, subnets, who hands out DHCP leases and DNS, and how you'll reach your gear after the change. It proposes trust zones (trusted, servers, IoT, guest, management, VPN), each with a default policy, and first confirms your gateway, switch and access points can route between VLANs, tag ports and map SSIDs. A local DNS resolver comes in as a dependency with a reserved address and a fallback, tested on one client before every DHCP scope. For WireGuard-style access it decides what the VPN may reach before generating keys, and recommends port forwarding only once the endpoint is patched and keys can be revoked. The change sequence moves one test client, then one low-risk group, with default-deny rules between zones. It never exposes admin panels, DNS or SSH to the internet, and wants console access before touching management VLANs or trunks.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/homelab-network-readiness"
