        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks router and switch configs before deployment for dangerous commands, duplicate IPs, overlapping subnets, undefined references and weak management-plane security.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to paste a Cisco IOS-style config into a router or switch, or let automation push a generated one"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks destructive commands, management exposure, duplicate IPs and overlapping subnets, undefined ACLs or route-maps, then hygiene such as NTP and logging"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Findings from Python checks you can reuse as a pre-flight gate that fails closed on dangerous commands and credentials"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not",
              "text": "A device parser: a network engineer still approves intent, syntax and rollback"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A pre-flight review from ECC for network configuration, built on layered evidence rather than a full parser. The agent checks in a fixed order. Destructive commands: reload, erase, format, removing a routing process or an interface, aaa new-model and crypto key changes. Management exposure: VTY lines that allow Telnet or lack an inbound access-class or exec-timeout, read block by block so checks don't spill across sections. Addressing: duplicate interface IPs and overlapping subnets, with Python's ipaddress module. References: ACLs, route-maps and prefix-lists that are used but never defined. Hygiene: default or SNMPv2 communities, SSH version 1, enable password instead of enable secret, and missing NTP, timestamps, logging or banners. Most checks come as Python code. Before a change window it runs on the exact snippet and the full candidate config, and confirms rollback and out-of-band access. Before Netmiko, NAPALM or Ansible pushes a config, it blocks on dangerous commands and credentials and only warns about hygiene gaps outside the change.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/network-config-validation"
