              "text": "That one plans and reviews the change without vendor configuration"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A how-to from ECC for what it calls the most impactful security upgrade for a home network. On a flat network a compromised smart TV can reach your NAS, so it splits devices into VLANs: trusted (10), IoT (20), servers (30), guest (40) and management (99), each with its own /24 subnet. A worked example for a UniFi house shows SSID mapping, trunk ports to the router and access points, access ports for the NAS and Pi-hole, and the firewall matrix: IoT blocked from trusted devices and servers except Pi-hole DNS, guests internet-only. Configuration follows for the UniFi controller, pfSense or OPNsense (VLAN interfaces, DHCP per VLAN, ordered rules with the DNS exception above the private-range block) and MikroTik bridge VLAN filtering. It explains trunk versus access ports. Anti-patterns include VLANs without firewall rules, which isolate nothing, a native VLAN equal to the management VLAN, which allows VLAN hopping, and one Wi-Fi password for the IoT and trusted SSIDs. All its rules add isolation, and it says to test after each change by pinging a trusted device from the IoT VLAN.",
