      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a \"make it faster\" request into a bounded, measured loop that benchmarks one-hypothesis variants and promotes the fastest one that stays correct.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're asked to speed something up, try dozens of optimizations, or pick the best implementation by repeated measurement"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Pins down the operation, metric, correctness gate, baseline and search budget, then benchmarks variants that each test one hypothesis on the same input"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A table of variants with hypothesis, command, time and correctness, and the winner codified in a script, test, config or doc"
            },
            {
              "label": "Promotes",
              "text": "A variant only when correctness tests pass, the gain is repeated or explained, rollback is obvious and the change is in source control or a runbook"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's answer to requests like \"make it 20x faster\": keep the ambition, but make the loop bounded and measurable. Nothing gets optimized until five things exist: the operation, the correctness gate that must stay green, the metric (wall time, p95 latency, rows per second, cost per run, memory or error rate), the current baseline, and a budget of variants, time, spend and data impact. The agent then measures the baseline, finds bottlenecks from evidence, runs variants that each test one hypothesis on the same input shape, rejects any that fail correctness, safety or reproducibility, and promotes the fastest safe one. It codifies the winner and reruns baseline and winner to confirm the gain. For recursive or hyperparameter searches it logs every run to a ledger, compares against the prior accepted winner rather than the last run, keeps a holdout or replay check, and stops when gains fall within noise, correctness fails or the budget runs out. It calls the result the \"best measured safe variant\", not a global optimum, unless the search was exhaustive, and its final summary gives exact commands and measurements.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/benchmark-optimization-loop"
        },
