        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Optimizes realtime systems such as dashboards, market-data feeds, queues and caches by measuring each segment of the hot path, then verifies with live readbacks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "p95 latency, throughput or data freshness matters: realtime dashboards, market-data feeds, streaming agents, execution gateways, queues or caches"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Tracks p50, p95 and p99 latency, freshness age, queue depth and cache hit rate separately, and maps the path from source event to what the user sees"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fixes in order",
              "text": "Round trips first, then caching with freshness metadata, batching, moving compute closer, splitting hot and cold paths, and backpressure"
            },
            {
              "label": "Verifies with",
              "text": "Live readbacks of a deployed system: HTTP timings and headers, freshness timestamps, queue and cache state, browser checks and retry logs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Drop validation for speed, hide stale data behind fast cache hits, or run live orders or risky deploys without an approval gate"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short engineering guide from ECC for systems where speed means freshness as much as response time. It asks the agent not to collapse everything into \"fast\" but to track each metric on its own: latency percentiles, throughput, freshness age, queue depth, cache hit rate, provider response time, browser render time, correctness under load and retry behavior. The agent writes out the hot path, for example source event, provider API, ingest worker, queue, cache, edge route, client stream and browser render, and measures each segment. Optimizations follow a fixed order, ending with streaming only where it helps freshness and canaries for stale data, degraded providers and bad cache state. For market-data or execution-adjacent paths it also checks orderbook age, VWAP assumptions, provider status and kill-switch behavior before calling the path ready. It covers HFT-like infrastructure but says it doesn't authorize live trading or give financial advice, and it keeps secrets and private payloads out of logs and benchmark artifacts.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/latency-critical-systems"
        },
        "tags": [
