        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Speeds up slow backfills, ETL jobs and warehouse loads by finding the real bottleneck, benchmarking variants, and proving row counts and timestamps still agree.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An ingestion, backfill, export, ETL job, warehouse load or table sync is too slow and must get faster without losing data"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Separates extraction, transfer, load, transform and freshness, measures the backlog, then benchmarks batch sizes, worker counts, warehouse SQL and file grouping"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The fastest correct path codified as a CLI, scheduled job, workflow or runbook, and an accounting block of files, rows, remaining tail and runtime"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Delete raw data to improve a metric, skip failed files silently, or call a pipeline done before target tables and manifest agree"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ECC's skill for data that moves too slowly. Its goal is faster correct data in the right place, with proof. The agent first separates source extraction, network transfer, warehouse load, transform, serving-table freshness and the live tail still arriving while the job runs, since a fast pipeline can still look behind. It reads the source, target and manifest contracts, measures the backlog in files, manifest rows, raw and derived rows, timestamps and unprocessed counts, then runs a safe catch-up or sample benchmark. Variants compare batch size, worker count, warehouse SQL, file grouping, staging shape and manifest updates, guided by heuristics: move compute to the data, prefer warehouse-native scans and appends, skip completed files or partitions through manifests, batch small writes and make writes idempotent. Only a path that keeps counts and timestamps coherent is promoted, and the accounting is rerun after the codified path executes. For finance, healthcare, regulated or customer-impacting data it keeps replay evidence and approval gates.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/data-throughput-accelerator"
