      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Judges whether prediction-market prices can serve as a signal for a product, agent or dashboard, weighing liquidity, resolution rules and non-market sources.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're considering prediction markets as a data source, forecasting input or oracle-like signal for a product, agent, dashboard or decision"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Records market-implied probabilities with timestamps and links, rates liquidity, spread, age and resolution, and compares them with filings, news, polls or internal data"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report from decision context to caveats, with a verdict that the signal is usable, weak or unsuitable for that decision"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "Treat prices as objective truth or give investment advice or trading recommendations"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A research workflow for teams thinking of using prediction markets as an input rather than a bet. The agent starts from the decision the signal is meant to inform, finds the relevant markets, events and venues, and records each implied probability with a timestamp and source link. It then judges signal quality: liquidity, spread, market age, trader or incentive concentration where known, who resolves the market, and geography or account restrictions, calling out manipulation, thin liquidity, stale markets and ambiguous outcomes. Market signals are checked against non-market sources such as filings, news, polls, research, customer data or internal KPIs. It suggests ways to use a signal: context for a human analyst, a figure on a dashboard beside internal metrics, a time-stamped input to agent memory, threshold alerts, or scenario planning without automated trades. The report runs from decision context, market sources, signal quality and comparison sources to an integration recommendation and caveats, and ends with a line saying the signals are not investment advice. For on-chain or execution-linked systems it points to ECC's llm-trading-agent-security skill before granting any write authority.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/prediction-market-oracle-research"
