        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes, reviews or repairs agent skills for OpenClaw in five checked steps, then validates the SKILL.md frontmatter with a bundled script.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to write a new skill for OpenClaw, or review, repair or restructure an existing SKILL.md and its files"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Walks five steps, each closed by a \"done when\" check: list each distinct case the skill handles, pick how it's invoked, lay out its files, draft, validate"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A SKILL.md with references/, scripts/ and assets/ as needed, as a pending proposal in OpenClaw's skill_workshop tool or a diff in your repo"
            },
            {
              "label": "Validates with",
              "text": "A bundled Python script that checks the frontmatter: only allowed keys, a hyphen-case name, and a description of at most 1,024 characters without angle brackets"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike Anthropic's skill-creator",
              "text": "This one runs no evals: it's a short checklist built around OpenClaw's frontmatter fields and its skill_workshop tool"
            }
          ],
          "text": "OpenClaw's own guide to authoring skills in the AgentSkills format. It starts by pinning down the contract: each real branch of the workflow, as opposed to a synonym for another, gets a trigger, an expected outcome and a place where its result is saved. Then it picks how the skill is reached: found by the model through its description, run only by hand with disable-model-invocation, or dispatched straight to a tool without the model. Shared steps go in SKILL.md, each ending with a checkable completion criterion; branch-only detail goes to references/, helpers to scripts/, output resources to assets/ and UI metadata to agents/. A live workspace skill is drafted as a pending proposal in OpenClaw's skill_workshop tool, leaving the live files untouched until it's applied; a skill kept in a repository goes through that repo's normal edit and review. Last, it runs quick_validate.py on the skill folder, plus the focused test of every helper it touched. The validator accepts only the frontmatter fields OpenClaw supports, such as metadata, homepage, user-invocable and command-dispatch, and rejects any other key.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/tree/f99d785d2ac6269b37a1c88eb8d5ef2283b76b99/skills/skill-creator"
