      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Hands feature builds, PR reviews, refactors and issue-to-PR work from OpenClaw to background Codex, Claude Code or OpenCode workers in isolated git worktrees.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want OpenClaw to hand a feature build, PR review, large refactor or issue-to-PR job to Codex, Claude Code or OpenCode, running in the background"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Prepares an isolated git worktree from a freshly fetched base, writes the worker's prompt to a file, launches the worker and monitors it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The worker's session ID right away, then one completion or failure message from the worker in your chat"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "OpenClaw with this skill enabled in its config, and the claude, codex or opencode CLI, signed in"
            },
            {
              "label": "Watch for",
              "text": "Claude Code runs with --permission-mode bypassPermissions; Codex gets a separate login in ~/.codex-coding-agent, never your usual ~/.codex"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Simple edits, read-only code lookup, or work inside OpenClaw's own state directories"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A delegation playbook for OpenClaw. Every worker starts in the background and reports back itself, with openclaw message send, to a chat route captured before launch; the agent watches it with OpenClaw's process tool, reports only milestones, questions, errors and the finish, and respawns or asks rather than quietly coding the task itself if a worker fails. Before work that changes a git project, the agent proves the target repo, remote and base branch, fetches, creates a fresh worktree and branch, checks that its starting commit matches the fetched base, and pastes those facts into the worker's prompt. Code from untrusted contributors is never checked out outside the repo's own sandboxed review workflow, nor given to a permission-bypassed worker. For long jobs, a GitHub issue serves as the spec: the worker implements, tests, reviews until no actionable findings remain and opens the PR, checking just before pushing that its branch still sits on the latest base. Shared branches aren't rebased, reset or force-pushed unless you ask. A scratch mode runs Codex in a throwaway repo for work outside any project.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/tree/f99d785d2ac6269b37a1c88eb8d5ef2283b76b99/skills/coding-agent"
