        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits the security of a machine running OpenClaw, from SSH and firewall to backups and the gateway, and proposes staged, reversible hardening.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know how exposed the machine running OpenClaw is, and lock it down without cutting off your own access"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works out the host's setup, asking only for what's missing, runs read-only checks once you allow them, then asks which posture you want: convenience, balanced or strict"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report of the current posture, findings with severity and evidence, a staged reversible plan, commands, and what it couldn't check"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "OpenClaw's security audit, gateway status and doctor lint, plus listening ports, firewall, SSH, updates, disk encryption and backups on macOS, Linux or Windows"
            },
            {
              "label": "Changes",
              "text": "Nothing without your confirmation; SSH, firewall and remote access stay untouched until your access path is confirmed"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A security review for the host OpenClaw runs on, whether a personal workstation, a local assistant box or a remote server. The agent first works out what it can: the OS, whether it's in a container, its privileges, how you reach it (locally, over SSH, RDP or a tailnet), its network exposure, the OpenClaw gateway's bind and auth, backups, disk encryption and automatic updates, asking you only for what's missing. With your permission it runs read-only checks, from openclaw security audit --deep to the OS's firewall, listening ports and encryption status. It then reports and offers only the hardening items that apply, such as binding the gateway to loopback, LAN or tailnet on purpose, requiring auth for remote access, closing public ports, enabling updates and disk encryption, verifying backups, requiring SSH keys, and scheduling the audit. It prefers reversible steps with rollback notes, never prints secrets, only recommends when it doesn't know your role, and never claims OpenClaw manages the firewall, SSH or updates.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/tree/f99d785d2ac6269b37a1c88eb8d5ef2283b76b99/skills/healthcheck"
