        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Makes code readable with Robert C. Martin's Clean Code rules for naming, small functions, comments, error handling and unit tests.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A function is too long, names are unclear, or you're reviewing a pull request for readability"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Applies six disciplines from Clean Code: names that reveal intent, small functions, comments only for why plus consistent formatting, exceptions instead of error codes, clean tests and code smells"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Code advice and a score out of 10, with the changes needed to reach 10"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike refactoring-patterns",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, is a catalog of named transformations; this one sets the standard the code should meet"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Robert C. Martin's Clean Code. Its premise is that code is read far more often than it is written, so every choice should serve the reader, and the Boy Scout Rule applies: leave code cleaner than you found it. Names reveal intent, with no prefixes or encodings and one word per concept. Functions are small, do one thing at one level of abstraction, take no more than two arguments without good reason, avoid flag arguments, and either change state or return a value, never both. Comments explain why, never what, and commented-out code is deleted. Formatting is agreed once and automated, with high-level functions at the top of a file. Errors use exceptions that carry context rather than return codes, and nothing returns or passes null: empty collections, Optional or a Null Object take its place. Tests are kept as clean as production code, one concept per test, following F.I.R.S.T. (fast, independent, repeatable, self-validating, timely). A catalog of smells, such as feature envy and magic numbers, points to targeted fixes. Ten diagnostic questions, such as whether every function is under 20 lines, guide a review. Reference files cover naming, functions, comments, error handling, testing and smells.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/clean-code"
        },
        "tags": [
