    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/refactoring-patterns",
      "name": "refactoring-patterns",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/refactoring-patterns",
        "installs": 6800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [173, 175, 154, 163, 287, 232, 162, 173],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Restructures code without changing its behavior using Martin Fowler's named refactorings, matching each code smell to its fix in small tested steps.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Code smells, a method is too long, conditionals are tangled, or you need to clean up before adding a feature"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Names the smell, picks the refactoring Fowler's catalog prescribes for it, and applies it in small steps, running tests before and after each and committing when they pass"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A score out of 10 from eight checks, the smells pulling it down and the refactorings that fix them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike clean-code",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, says what good code looks like; this one is the catalog of moves that get you there"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Martin Fowler's Refactoring. Refactoring here means small changes that keep behavior the same, each backed by tests, never a big-bang rewrite. Code smells are the triggers, in five families: bloaters such as long methods, object-orientation abusers such as switch statements, change preventers such as shotgun surgery, dispensables such as duplicate code, and couplers such as feature envy. Each maps to named refactorings: Extract Method, which it calls the most important, Inline Method, Replace Temp with Query, Move Method, Extract Class, Hide Delegate, Replace Magic Number, Replace Data Value with Object, Decompose Conditional, guard clauses, Replace Conditional with Polymorphism and Special Case. The loop is fixed: tests green, one transformation, tests green, commit; if tests go red, revert instead of debugging. It refactors on the third copy of duplicated logic, cleans the spot where a feature will go before adding it, never refactors and adds features at once, and leaves alone code that will soon be deleted. Large changes in production use Branch by Abstraction and Parallel Change. Reference files give step-by-step mechanics with before-and-after code.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/refactoring-patterns"
        },
