      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs habit-forming product loops with Nir Eyal's Hook Model of trigger, action, variable reward and investment, checked against an ethics test.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Users sign up but don't come back, or you're designing notifications, streaks or engagement loops"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds the feeling that should bring users back, then checks each phase of the loop: trigger, action, reward and investment"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A score out of 10 from four checks, capped at 3 when the product fails its ethics test"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike improve-retention",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, makes the key action easier; this one designs the whole habit loop, from trigger to variable reward and investment"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also has",
              "text": "An onboarding checklist for the first pass through the loop"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Nir Eyal's Hooked. A habit forms through repeated passes through four phases. A trigger starts it: external, such as a notification, or internal, such as boredom, loneliness or FOMO. The action is the simplest thing done in anticipation of a reward, made easier by cutting time, money, effort and choices. The reward varies so it stays unpredictable, and comes from other people, from finding things or from personal mastery. Then the user invests something, such as data, content or reputation, that improves the next use and loads the next trigger; it asks for that only after the reward. The aim is to move users from external to internal triggers within about 30 days. A grid weighs how often a product is used against how much each use is worth, and a habit counts as formed once 5% of users come back unprompted. Eyal's Manipulation Matrix sorts products by whether they improve users' lives and whether the maker uses them. A product that does neither, or one that targets vulnerable users such as children, scores 3 at most. Each phase comes with copy patterns and an ethical limit.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/hooked-ux"
        },
        "tags": [
