        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs scalable distributed systems with Alex Xu's four-step method: requirements first, back-of-the-envelope estimates, standard building blocks and stated tradeoffs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing a service for scale, estimating capacity, or preparing for a system design interview"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scopes requirements, estimates traffic and storage, chooses load balancers, caches, queues and database scaling, then goes deep on the riskiest parts"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A design checked against eight questions, with a score out of 10 and a fix for each failure"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike clean-architecture",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers layers inside one codebase; this one designs the servers, caches, queues and databases around the code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Alex Xu's System Design Interview. Its rule is requirements before solutions, in four steps: agree on scope and scale (daily users, queries per second, storage), sketch a high-level design with APIs and data flow, go deep on the two or three hardest components, then name the tradeoffs and bottlenecks. Estimates use powers of two, standard latency numbers and simple formulas, such as daily users times actions divided by 86,400 seconds, rounded to an order of magnitude. Its building blocks are DNS, CDNs, layer 4 and layer 7 load balancers, cache layers and strategies, message queues and consistent hashing, each added only once its bottleneck appears. Databases scale up first, then add read replicas, then shard by hash, range or directory. It carries a library of known designs: URL shortener, rate limiter, news feed, chat, autocomplete, web crawler and unique ID generator. Operations count as design too: health checks, metrics, logs and traces, rolling, blue-green and canary deploys, recovery targets and autoscaling. Eight diagnostic questions, such as whether every component is redundant, set the score.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/system-design"
        },
