      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Makes product flows, copy and sales more persuasive with Robert Cialdini's seven principles of influence, within explicit ethical limits.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Copy isn't persuasive, trust signals are weak, or you're designing testimonials, onboarding or urgency messages"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks and layers Cialdini's principles: reciprocity, commitment, social proof, authority, liking, scarcity and unity"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Copy patterns and a score out of 10, capped at 3 for any fake proof, fake scarcity or hidden cost"
            },
            {
              "label": "Its ethics test",
              "text": "Would the tactic still work if the user knew the strategy?"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike negotiation",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers live deal-making; this one applies persuasion to products and copy"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Robert Cialdini's Influence. Its premise is that people decide through mental shortcuts, and each of seven principles names one: reciprocity (give first, then ask), commitment and consistency (small yeses lead to bigger ones), social proof (specific numbers from similar people), authority (credentials, and admitting a weakness first), liking (similarity, compliments, cooperation), scarcity (real limits on time or quantity) and unity (a shared identity, such as \"for remote-first teams\"). Each principle comes with product examples and copy patterns, and four carry an ethical limit, such as never inventing scarcity or using countdown timers that reset. The strongest persuasion layers several principles, shown for a SaaS landing page and a referral program. A seven-question diagnostic asks which principles are used and whether they are combined, whether claims are true, whether the tactic helps the user, whether it survives the user knowing the strategy, whether decisions can be reversed and whether it is safe for vulnerable users. Reference files cover each principle, copywriting, case studies, and an ethics guide on regulation and vulnerable users.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/influence-psychology"
        },
