      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds offers people find hard to refuse with Alex Hormozi's $100M Offers: the Value Equation, bonuses, guarantees, real scarcity and a memorable name.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Prospects say it's too expensive, or you're packaging a product, adding bonuses or designing a guarantee"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Raises the dream outcome and the buyer's confidence while cutting time and effort, then adds bonuses, a guarantee and real urgency"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An offer built in ten steps, from picking a market to naming it, and a score out of 10 from seven checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike obviously-awesome",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, decides which category you compete in; this one designs what you sell and at what price"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Alex Hormozi's $100M Offers. Its premise is that the offer matters more than the marketing: a Grand Slam Offer packs so much value into one bundle that it can't be compared on price. The Value Equation scores value as the dream outcome times the perceived likelihood of reaching it, divided by the time it takes times the effort and sacrifice. It starts from a starving crowd, a market with great pain, money to spend, easy reach and growth. It lists every obstacle between the customer and the outcome and turns each into something delivered, then trims costly low-value items and stacks cheap high-value ones until the bundle is worth ten times its price. Price follows value, not cost. Each bonus answers one objection and carries a dollar value, guarantees range from unconditional to performance-based, and the MAGIC formula names the offer by a reason, the audience, the goal, a time frame and a format word such as blueprint. Most sections set an ethical limit: dollar values must be what a part would really sell for, scarcity must be true, and guarantees must be easy to claim. Reference files hold a worksheet and six before-and-after redesigns.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/hundred-million-offers"
        },
