        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Positions a product with April Dunford's Obviously Awesome, from what customers would use instead to the market category that makes its value obvious.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Prospects don't get what you do, you're launching into a crowded market, or your team describes the product differently"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works through five steps: what customers would use instead, what only you have, the value it enables, who cares most, and the category to compete in"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A filled positioning canvas, from competitive alternatives to messaging, and a score out of 10"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike storybrand-messaging",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers the words; this one sets the context the words are judged in"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on April Dunford's Obviously Awesome. Its premise is that positioning is context, not messaging: it sets what customers compare you to and how they judge your value. Five steps build it. Competitive alternatives come from interviewing 15 to 20 happy customers about what they'd use if you vanished, often a spreadsheet, a manual process or doing nothing. Unique attributes must pass an \"only we\" test. Each goes through repeated \"so what?\" questions until it reaches value a customer cares about, grouped into two or three themes. Best-fit customers are defined by traits you can spot before talking to them, such as job title or company size, plus signs that someone isn't a fit. The market category can be an existing one, a subcategory or a new one, which means paying to teach the market what it is. Trends can support the positioning but shouldn't carry it. A ten-part canvas holds the result, and a team workshop runs the five steps so everyone describes the product the same way.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/obviously-awesome"
        },
