    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/lean-ux",
      "name": "lean-ux",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/lean-ux",
        "installs": 5900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [153, 161, 132, 150, 259, 209, 139, 136],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Replaces heavy UX deliverables with Gothelf and Seiden's Lean UX: declared assumptions, testable hypotheses, the cheapest useful experiment and whole-team design sessions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Design work is buried in specs and handoffs, or a team argues over a design instead of testing it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Turns assumptions into hypotheses with success criteria set in advance, then picks the cheapest experiment that can settle each"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Process advice and a score out of 10 from eight checks, with the fix for each failed one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike ux-heuristics",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, audits an interface's usability; this one changes how a team decides what to design"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Lean UX by Jeff Gothelf and Josh Seiden. Its premise is outcomes over outputs: a design is judged by the change in user behavior it causes, not by how polished its deliverables are. The team writes down its business and user assumptions together and tests the riskiest, least-known ones first. Each becomes a hypothesis in a fixed format, \"We believe [outcome] will happen if [persona] achieves [action] with [feature]\", with what counts as validated agreed before the test. Experiments start at the lowest fidelity that can answer the question, such as a paper prototype shown to five users, a landing page that counts sign-ups, or a pricing page shown before billing exists (a painted door), disclosed as a test. Design happens in Design Studio sessions where engineers, PMs and designers sketch alone, critique and converge, with a designer synthesizing the result. Research is small and weekly, discovery runs one sprint ahead of delivery, stories carry a hypothesis alongside their acceptance criteria, and invalidated items leave the backlog. Eight diagnostic questions set the score, including whether the team can point to a recently invalidated hypothesis. Reference files hold workshop protocols, experiment types, outcome metrics and case studies.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/lean-ux"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "category:planning",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "prototyping",
          "research"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/improve-retention",
      "name": "improve-retention",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/improve-retention",
        "installs": 5900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [144, 156, 128, 147, 265, 206, 137, 133],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Diagnoses why new users don't stick around with BJ Fogg's B=MAP behavior model, then makes the core action easier, better prompted and celebrated.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Users sign up but don't come back, activation is low, or onboarding loses people"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks whether motivation, ability and a prompt meet at each key action, and which of six factors, such as time or mental effort, makes the action hard"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A diagnosis by drop-off point and a score out of 10 that names each failed check"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike hooked-ux",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers habit loops and variable rewards; this one relies on making the action easier rather than on motivation"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on BJ Fogg's Tiny Habits and his Behavior Model, B=MAP: a behavior happens only when motivation, ability and a prompt come together. Motivation rises and falls in waves, so it designs for low-motivation moments and treats making the action easier as the reliable lever. Ability depends on the scarcest of six resources, the Ability Chain: time, money, physical effort, mental effort, social deviance (doing something unusual among peers) and non-routine. A prompt sent to someone not yet motivated and able is spam, so it prefers prompts tied to real events. From Tiny Habits it takes a recipe, \"After I [anchor moment], I will [tiny behavior], then I [celebration]\", and Starter Steps, such as filling in one field. Its five-step process starts from the user's aspiration, not the product's goal, lists and matches possible behaviors, shrinks the best to a starter step, then expands it. A table maps metrics, from low activation to notification fatigue, to causes. Six diagnostic questions set the score, such as whether a new user can do the core action in under 60 seconds. It rules out inventing a loss to drive a session. Reference files hold case studies of Instagram, Duolingo and Slack.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/improve-retention"
        },
