      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans and scores a five-day Google Ventures design sprint, from mapping the problem Monday to testing a prototype with five customers Friday.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A team is stuck debating a high-stakes product idea, or wants to test it with users before building"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Lays out the sprint day by day: map the problem, sketch, decide, build a prototype, then test it with five customers"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A plan for the week and a score out of 10 from ten checks, with the missing items"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike lean-ux",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, runs ongoing experiments within agile sprints; this one answers one big question in a single week"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Sprint by Jake Knapp, John Zeratsky and Braden Kowitz, the process Knapp created at Google and refined at Google Ventures. A Decider, who has the final say, and four to seven others spend five days from 10am to 5pm on one big question, led by a facilitator. Monday sets a two-year goal, lists the sprint's questions, maps the customer journey and picks one target customer and moment. Tuesday everyone sketches alone, including Crazy 8s, eight ideas in eight minutes, ending with an anonymous three-panel solution sketch. Wednesday the team marks the sketches silently, the Decider makes the final call, and a 10-15 panel storyboard is drawn. Thursday the team builds a prototype that looks real without working, testable in 5-15 minutes. Friday one interviewer runs five-act interviews with five target customers while the team watches and marks what worked and what didn't, then decides what to do next. The score is one point for each of ten checks, capped at 6 without a Decider, real users or a prototype built in a day. It also covers four-day and remote variants, and when not to run a sprint.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/design-sprint"
        },
