      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides data-system choices with Martin Kleppmann's Designing Data-Intensive Applications: data models, storage engines, replication, partitioning, transactions, streams and fault tolerance.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Choosing a database, planning replication or sharding, or chasing stale reads and data that differs across replicas"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Makes each data-layer trade-off explicit, from data model and storage engine to isolation level and failure handling"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Architecture advice and a score out of 10 from seven checks, with the improvements needed to reach 10"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike release-it",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, keeps services up through failures; this one focuses on the data layer"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Martin Kleppmann's Designing Data-Intensive Applications. Its premise is that data outlives code, so the data layer's correctness and ability to evolve matter most, and every choice should be a deliberate trade-off rather than habit. It covers seven areas: data models (relational for many-to-many relationships, document for self-contained records, graph for traversals); storage engines (LSM trees for heavy writes, B-trees for mixed reads and writes, column stores for analytics); replication with one, several or no leaders, and the anomalies replication lag causes; partitioning by key range or hash, and hot keys; transactions and isolation levels, warning that most databases don't default to serializable and so allow write skew, where two transactions read the same data and each write a different row; batch and stream processing, change data capture and event sourcing; and fault tolerance with timeouts, retries and consensus. Examples name systems such as PostgreSQL, Cassandra, Kafka and Flink. Seven diagnostic questions set the score, such as whether you know your database's default isolation level and have tested failover. Reference files go deeper on each area. It points to system-design for whole-system design and to release-it for resilience.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/ddia-systems"
        },
