      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds uncontested market space with Kim and Mauborgne's Blue Ocean Strategy: strategy canvases, the eliminate-reduce-raise-create grid and non-customers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Your market is crowded, you're stuck in a price war, or you're looking for a new category or overlooked buyers"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Plots what your industry competes on, then decides what to eliminate, reduce, raise and create so value rises while cost falls"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A score out of 10 from five checks and the moves needed to reach 10"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike crossing-the-chasm",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, takes a tech product to the mainstream; this one looks for a market nobody competes in yet"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Blue Ocean Strategy by W. Chan Kim and Renée Mauborgne. Red oceans are existing markets where rivals fight over the same customers; blue oceans are new market space where competition doesn't matter yet. The way there is value innovation: being different and cheaper at once. Its main tool is the strategy canvas, a chart of the factors an industry competes on and each player's level on them, where look-alike curves mean a red ocean. The Four Actions, or ERRC grid, ask which factors to eliminate and reduce, which cuts cost, and which to raise and create, which adds value, as Cirque du Soleil dropped animal acts and star performers and added themes. Six Paths look across alternative industries, strategic groups, the chain of buyers, complementary offerings, functional versus emotional appeal, and trends over time. It targets three tiers of non-customers rather than rivals' customers, and checks an idea in order: buyer utility, then price, then cost, then adoption. Five diagnostic questions set the score. Reference files hold worked cases such as Netflix, Yellow Tail and Nintendo Wii, canvas and workshop templates, and rollout guidance.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/blue-ocean-strategy"
        },
