        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs an outbound B2B sales system from Aaron Ross's Predictable Revenue: separate prospecting and closing, referral cold emails, ANUM qualification, pipeline math.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a B2B outbound sales team, your pipeline swings between feast and famine, or salespeople both prospect and close"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Splits sales into prospecting, closing and account roles, and fills the pipeline with short emails asking senior people who to talk to"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A sales process design, pipeline math worked back from a revenue goal, and a score out of 10 from five checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike one-page-marketing",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, covers a whole marketing plan; this one builds the outbound sales machine"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Predictable Revenue by Aaron Ross and Marylou Tyler, from the outbound process Ross built at Salesforce. It sorts leads into seeds (referrals and word of mouth), nets (marketing and inbound) and spears (outbound prospecting), and its first rule is that the people who prospect shouldn't also close. Sales development reps (SDRs) find and qualify opportunities, account executives close them, and customer success managers keep and grow accounts. Its outbound method, Cold Calling 2.0, emails someone above the decision maker asking only who the right person is, in under 100 words with no pitch, then follows up on days 3, 7, 14 and 30. Prospects are qualified with ANUM, for authority, need, urgency and money, before a structured handoff to the account executive. Pipeline math works back from the revenue goal to the deals, opportunities and emails needed, with benchmarks such as 9-15% response rates. It also covers hiring, ramping and paying SDRs, and which metrics to track, and says to follow spam laws and honor opt-outs at once. Five diagnostic questions set the score.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/predictable-revenue"
        },
