            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Teresa Torres's Continuous Discovery Habits. Its benchmark is at least one customer conversation every week by the product trio: a product manager, a designer and an engineer. An opportunity solution tree links a desired outcome to customer opportunities (needs, pain points and desires, framed from the customer's side), then to possible solutions and the experiments that test them, and is updated weekly. Experience maps show how customers reach a goal today, step by step, to surface opportunities. Interviews ask for stories about specific past events, \"Tell me about the last time…\", and each becomes a one-page snapshot the whole team can see. Before building, it lists the assumptions a solution rests on (that customers want it and can use it, that it can be built, that the business can sustain it) and tests the important ones with the least evidence first, in days. Opportunities are compared against each other rather than scored alone. To keep the habit, recruiting is automated and a recurring slot blocked. Seven diagnostic questions set the score, which starts at 3. Reference files cover each part, with case studies.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/continuous-discovery"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "research",
          "requirements"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
