    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/drive-motivation",
      "name": "drive-motivation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/drive-motivation",
        "installs": 5600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [133, 142, 123, 144, 280, 213, 137, 140],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs motivation into products and teams with Daniel Pink's Drive, replacing if-then rewards with autonomy, mastery and purpose.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Rewards or gamification aren't working, a team is disengaged, or you're designing progression into a product"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks a product or team for autonomy, mastery and purpose, and for if-then rewards that crowd them out"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A score out of 10 from six checks and the fixes needed to reach 10"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike improve-retention",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, makes key actions easier; this one is about why people want to do them"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Daniel Pink's Drive. Its premise is that for any work needing thought, if-then rewards (\"do X and get Y\") don't work or make things worse, and it lists seven ways they backfire, from killing interest and creativity to encouraging cheating and short-term thinking. Lasting engagement comes from three needs. Autonomy: choice over task, time, technique and team, so in products no forced linear tutorials, unskippable steps or mandatory notifications. Mastery: getting better at something that matters, with visible progress, challenge matched to skill so people reach flow, and failure framed as learning. Purpose: connecting actions to something larger, such as showing a user's or a community's impact. For gamification it contrasts opt-in challenges and meaningful milestones with points for everything and discouraging leaderboards, and warns that streaks can turn into fear of loss. For teams, it says to pay fairly enough to take money off the table, hand over the goal rather than the method, and prefer unexpected \"now-that\" recognition to promised bonuses. The score starts at 5, adds a point for each of five checks passed and loses 2 when if-then rewards do the motivating. Reference files cover each pillar, with case studies.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/drive-motivation"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/crossing-the-chasm",
      "name": "crossing-the-chasm",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/crossing-the-chasm",
        "installs": 5600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [143, 146, 131, 146, 262, 194, 125, 129],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans a tech product's jump from early adopters to the mainstream with Geoffrey Moore's Crossing the Chasm: one beachhead, a whole product, proof-led messaging.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Growth stalled after early adopters, or you're planning go-to-market for a technical product"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks one narrow beachhead segment, fills out the whole product with partners, and repositions from revolution to proven value"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A go-to-market plan and a score out of 10 from seven checks, with the fix for each failure"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike blue-ocean-strategy",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, creates a new market; this one takes a new technology from enthusiasts to mainstream buyers"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Geoffrey Moore's Crossing the Chasm. The technology adoption life cycle runs from innovators and early adopters to the early majority, late majority and laggards. The chasm sits between early adopters, visionaries who tolerate bugs to get ahead, and the early majority, pragmatists who want proven solutions and references from peers like them. Since pragmatists won't buy without references that don't exist yet, it follows a D-Day plan: pick one narrow beachhead segment with an urgent, expensive problem, scored from 20 or more candidates; build the whole product pragmatists expect, including integrations, onboarding, support and partners; position with a fill-in formula against the real alternative, often spreadsheets or manual work; and sell through channels pragmatists trust, with case studies and \"proven solution\" messaging. After the beachhead it expands segment by segment, each one's references winning the next (the bowling-pin strategy), until demand takes off (the tornado). Seven diagnostic questions set the score. For freemium and product-led B2B SaaS it reads a reference file first that remaps each step. Other reference files hold scoring worksheets and case studies such as Salesforce and Zoom.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/crossing-the-chasm"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wondelai/skills/inspired-product",
      "name": "inspired-product",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wondelai/skills/inspired-product",
        "installs": 5400,
        "weeklyInstalls": [137, 134, 119, 126, 251, 185, 115, 121],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Moves product teams from feature factory to empowered with Marty Cagan's Inspired: problems to solve, discovery that tests four risks, outcome-based roadmaps.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Your roadmap is a list of features, teams just ship what they're handed, or you're deciding what to build next"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Gives teams problems instead of features, and tests ideas for value, usability, feasibility and viability before building"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Team and process advice and a score out of 7 from seven checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike continuous-discovery",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, is a weekly interview routine; this one also covers team structure, vision and strategy"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Based on Marty Cagan's Inspired, with Empowered by Cagan and Chris Jones as further reading. Its premise is that most products fail because nobody wants them, and that empowered teams, a product manager, a designer and engineers given a problem to solve and judged on outcomes, beat feature teams handed a roadmap. Product work runs on two tracks: discovery decides what to build, delivery builds it. Discovery tests four risks, value, usability, feasibility and viability, with prototypes, tests with five real users, interviews about behavior and short engineering spikes, and expects 10-20 iterations per idea that reaches delivery. Before design work, an opportunity assessment asks which business objective an idea serves, for whom, what problem it solves and how success will be measured. A vision two to five years out and a strategy that sequences markets and problems give teams context, turned into OKRs and outcome-based roadmaps. Delivery ships small, instrumented increments behind feature flags. It also covers stakeholders who hand over solutions. Seven diagnostic questions set the score.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wondelai/skills/tree/d8d5d75f83da9f16d8aad5bab5f5b341a4770a30/inspired-product"
        },
        "tags": [
