      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans academic literature searches with Boolean queries, databases to try and key terms, and structures literature reviews from the papers you share.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're starting a literature review or thesis chapter and need a search plan, or want papers you've collected summarized and organized"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A search strategy with a Boolean query, variants, ranked databases, synonyms and an order to search in, or a literature review by theme with methods, findings and gaps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Doesn't",
              "text": "Run searches or open papers: by its own account it can't query databases, reach paywalled text or know papers past its training cutoff, so you share abstracts or papers"
            },
            {
              "label": "Includes",
              "text": "General and field databases and preprint servers, the PICO framework, citation chaining, a three-pass reading method, and APA, IEEE and Chicago citation formats"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Claude Office Skills. You give your research question, field, scope and purpose, and the agent drafts a search plan: an optimized Boolean query with broader, narrower and related variants, a ranked list of databases with reasons, key terms and synonyms per concept, and the order to search them in, with citation chaining on key papers and quality signs to check, such as a peer-reviewed venue and reproducible results. Its reference tables cover general databases like Google Scholar, Semantic Scholar, Scopus and Web of Science, field databases from PubMed to IEEE Xplore and Westlaw, and preprint servers such as arXiv, bioRxiv and SSRN. For health questions it frames queries with PICO (population, intervention, comparison, outcome). When you share papers or abstracts, it summarizes their findings and methods and writes a literature review grouped by theme, with a comparison of methods, key findings, research gaps and directions for future work. It also gives a three-pass reading method and critical questions about a paper's research question, method, results and limitations. It formats references in APA 7, IEEE or Chicago.",
