              "text": "Write the executive summary last and lead with client benefits, offer pricing options with the higher one first, and make the next step easy to say yes to"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Claude Office Skills. You describe the opportunity, your solution and pricing, and the type and length of proposal you need, and the agent fills one of three templates. The full sales or service proposal covers an executive summary that can stand alone, the client's situation, goals and requirements, the proposed solution with deliverables and phases, priced options with one marked as recommended, what is and isn't included, a timeline, case studies and team, next steps and terms. A quick quote has the scope, a line-item price table with tax, dates, terms and a signature line; a partnership proposal sets out the value for each side, roles, revenue sharing and a pilot with KPIs. Its writing advice: show you understand the client in their own terms, focus on outcomes rather than activities, anchor pricing with the higher option first, and remove friction from saying yes. You can ask for brief, standard or comprehensive length, a conservative, modern or technical style, and a document, slides or email-friendly format, all written as Markdown: the Word and PowerPoint tools its frontmatter names are never called. It notes that legal terms should be reviewed by counsel.",
