          "text": "From Claude Office Skills. You describe your data, the story it should tell, your audience and where the chart will appear. The agent picks a chart from tables grouped by purpose (comparison, trend, distribution, part-to-whole, and specialized charts such as funnel, gauge, heatmap, radar and Sankey) and a decision tree: bars to compare items, lines over time, pie or donut only for five items or fewer, a treemap for hierarchies, a histogram or box plot for distributions, scatter or bubble charts for relationships, a funnel or Sankey for flows. Its write-up gives the recommended chart with ECharts and Chart.js configurations, a color palette and type sizes, the best practices it applied and alternatives for other conditions; it also offers Excel chart setup instructions and CSS styling tips. The rules it applies: little ink that isn't data, clear titles and labeled axes with units, bar charts starting at zero, consistent scales, seven colors at most, attention to colorblind viewers, and tooltips, zoom or drill-down where data is dense.",
