      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a discounted cash flow valuation: free cash flow projections, WACC, terminal value, value per share and a WACC-by-growth sensitivity table.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an estimate of what a company's shares are worth from its future cash flows, compared with the market price"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Projects unlevered free cash flow, computes WACC with CAPM, values the terminal year by Gordon growth or an exit multiple, and discounts it all back"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A Markdown model: value per share against the current price with an undervalued, fair or overvalued call, assumptions, FCF table, valuation bridge, sensitivity tables and risks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Models",
              "text": "A standard five-year DCF, two-stage or three-stage"
            },
            {
              "label": "Defaults",
              "text": "Assumptions you leave out get industry defaults; its framework puts terminal growth at 2-3% and sector exit multiples between 5x and 15x EBITDA"
            },
            {
              "label": "Limits",
              "text": "Results hinge on assumptions, terminal value is often 60-80% of the total, and it doesn't suit early-stage unprofitable companies; not investment advice"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Claude Office Skills. You provide three to five years of revenue, EBITDA, capex and D&A, shares outstanding, optionally the share price, and the sector, then set growth, margin, capex, working-capital, terminal-growth and discount-rate assumptions or leave them to industry defaults. The agent follows four steps: unlevered free cash flow as EBIT after tax plus D&A minus capex and the change in working capital; WACC from the cost of equity by CAPM and the after-tax cost of debt; terminal value by the Gordon growth model or an EBITDA exit multiple; and enterprise value discounted at WACC, turned into equity value and divided by shares outstanding. Its bridge to equity value subtracts net debt and then adds cash, which counts cash twice if net debt already nets it out, so check that line. The report fills one template, with a grid of value per share across WACC from 8% to 10% and terminal growth from 1.5% to 3%, and the effect of moving each key driver. Its tips suggest cross-checking against P/E and EV/EBITDA multiples. Its frontmatter lists spreadsheet and chart tools of the repo's office-mcp server, but its instructions never call them.",
