      "id": "trailofbits/skills/plugins/variant-analysis/skills/variant-analysis",
      "name": "variant-analysis",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/trailofbits/skills/variant-analysis",
        "installs": 7200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [152, 160, 166, 183, 227, 265, 282, 280],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Hunts for other instances of a bug you've already found, widening a search pattern one step at a time and triaging each match.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've found one bug and want to know where else its root cause shows up, including \"are there others like this?\""
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "States the root cause, writes a pattern matching only the known bug, then generalizes one element at a time until over half the matches are noise"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report of confirmed variants by severity, every pattern tried with its match and false-positive counts, and a CI rule against regressions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "ripgrep for recon, Semgrep for iteration and CodeQL for precision, with starter queries for Python, JavaScript, Java, Go and C++"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Finding a first bug with nothing in hand; it points to audit-context-building for that"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. One root cause usually shows up in several places, and rarely in the module where the first instance was found. The skill works in five steps. It first pins down why the code is wrong, not what it does, and lists where variants could hide: related identifiers, other forms of the same mistake, data-type edge cases. It then writes an exact pattern that must hit the known bug, since a pattern that misses it means the bug was misunderstood. Generalizing changes one element at a time, from variable names to surrounding structure to the security property itself, rereading every match after each change. Triage argues against each candidate, looking for guards, sanitizers and type constraints, and checks whether it is reachable, attacker-controlled and unprotected; code nothing calls yet is still reported, at lower severity. Null-equality bypasses and code that contradicts its own docs get checked on purpose. The report fills a bundled template and keeps the patterns that failed. Its plugin also ships /variant-analysis:variants, which runs the steps across parallel subagents, one per direction of search, until a sweep finds nothing new.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/variant-analysis/skills/variant-analysis"
        },
