      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks code against the spec, whitepaper or design doc it implements, reporting which requirements hold, are contradicted or missing, and undocumented behavior.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have an authoritative document, such as a whitepaper, protocol spec or design note, and the code that should implement it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs its plugin's workflow, which splits the documents into checkable requirements, checks each in its own subagent and has other agents try to refute every divergence"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "spec-compliance/REPORT.md plus one file per requirement, each with one of six verdicts and the searches behind it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also finds",
              "text": "Behavior the code has that no document mentions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Its Claude Code plugin; the skill itself only routes to the plugin's command and agent"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. Every gap between a document and its code is either a bug or a documentation fix, and deciding which is the finding. The skill doesn't check requirements in the main conversation, because judging one means reading its enforcement, callers and callees, and thirty of those can't be checked honestly in one context. Instead it runs /spec-to-code-compliance:spec-compliance on a path, optionally naming the spec and how many requirements to check, or sends the spec-compliance-checker agent at a single requirement. The verdicts are implemented, partial, contradicted, stronger-than-spec, absent and undecidable. Partial, which holds on the tested paths and fails on one nobody tried, is usually the most serious; absent is only as good as its record of searches; undecidable is a finding against the document; stronger-than-spec is a constraint nothing documents. A divergence whose refutation agents both failed is marked unverified, and requirements below the cut are listed as not checked. Severity follows consequence, from a bundled rubric, and a domain guide covers contracts, C and C++, services, decompiled firmware, and RFCs or standards used as the spec.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/spec-to-code-compliance/skills/spec-to-code-compliance"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:security",
