        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a custom Semgrep rule for a bug pattern test-first, preferring taint mode, and iterates until every vulnerable and safe test case passes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a Semgrep rule that catches a specific vulnerability or bug pattern in your code, or enforces a coding standard"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads seven Semgrep and Testing Handbook docs pages, writes the test file first, dumps the AST, writes the rule and reruns semgrep --test until all pass"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A folder named after the rule id holding exactly one rule YAML and one annotated test file"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules",
              "text": "Taint mode first for data flow, no generic-language rules, no todo test annotations, and optimizing only after every test passes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Running existing rulesets or scans without custom rules"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. Its seven-step checklist goes: analyze the problem, write tests first, read the AST, write the rule, iterate until semgrep --test passes, optimize, then a final run. Tests must include safe cases marked ok, such as sanitized input or hardcoded values, alongside vulnerable ones marked ruleid, because a rule that only matches bugs can still break trust with false positives. It prefers taint mode, which follows untrusted input from a source to a dangerous sink, over plain pattern matching, which can't tell eval(user_input) from eval of a literal, but switches approach when taint doesn't work out. It holds itself to 100% passing tests, one rule per YAML file and specific rather than broad patterns. It rejects shortcuts such as \"the pattern looks complete\" without running the tests, \"one test is enough\", and skipping the AST dump because it looks complex.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/semgrep-rule-creator/skills/semgrep-rule-creator"
        },
