          "code-quality"
        ],
        "note": "Needs the Semgrep CLI.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "trailofbits/skills/plugins/entry-point-analyzer/skills/entry-point-analyzer",
      "name": "entry-point-analyzer",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/trailofbits/skills/entry-point-analyzer",
        "installs": 6300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [136, 117, 116, 146, 177, 189, 212, 222],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Lists every externally callable, state-changing function in a smart contract codebase, grouped by who may call it, to map an audit's attack surface.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're starting a smart contract audit and need to know which functions can change state and who can call them"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Detects the language, runs Slither's entry-points printer on Solidity if installed, then classifies every externally callable, state-changing function"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A markdown report counting and listing entry points as public, role-restricted, restricted (review required) or contract-only, each with file and line"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "Solidity, Vyper, Solana (Rust), Move on Sui and Aptos, TON (FunC and Tact) and CosmWasm"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves out",
              "text": "View, pure and other read-only functions; it maps the surface and doesn't hunt for vulnerabilities"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. The report is a map of the attack surface, not a list of bugs: public functions that change state come first, as the place to start looking. Access is judged from the actual checks, not from names. It verifies what a modifier does, counts a callback with no caller check as public, flags a check against a configured address whose identity isn't established for review, and records msg.sender != tx.origin as a call-path condition rather than trust. When a role is ambiguous it flags the function for review instead of guessing. It notes access control inherited from parent contracts and every access-related modifier, and watches for initializers, upgrades, pause and fee-setting functions. Move public functions without entry are included because transactions can still reach them. It loads a reference file for each detected language, and without Slither, or when Slither fails, it does the analysis by hand. Files it can't parse are listed as warnings, and you can limit it to one directory. Vulnerability hunting is left to skills such as audit-context-building.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/entry-point-analyzer/skills/entry-point-analyzer"
        },
