      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Gets your codebase ready for an outside security audit with Trail of Bits' checklist: review goals, static analysis cleanup, test coverage, scope and documentation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An external security review starts in a week or two and you want your codebase ready for the auditors"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks your goals and concerns, runs static analysis, measures coverage, finds dead code, checks scope and build steps, then drafts documentation"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An audit prep package: goals document, triaged static analysis, coverage report, scope list, tested build steps, a frozen commit, docs and a checklist"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike audit-context-building",
              "text": "That one is for auditors learning code they're about to review"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Your platform's analyzers, such as Slither, dylint or golangci-lint"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Trail of Bits' pre-audit checklist, from its building-secure-contracts plugin, in four steps. First it helps write review goals: the security level you want, the areas that worry you and the worst case, documented for the assessment team. Second it clears easy issues: it runs static analysis, triages the findings and helps fix the simple ones while documenting accepted risks, measures test coverage and suggests tests for the gaps, and finds unused code and libraries. Third it makes the code accessible: a file list marking what's in and out of scope, build instructions tested on a fresh environment, a frozen commit on its own branch with locked dependencies, and copied or forked code marked so the review focuses on yours. Fourth it drafts documentation: flowcharts and sequence diagrams, user stories, on-chain and off-chain assumptions, actors and privileges, function invariants and parameter ranges, NatSpec for Solidity, and a glossary. It rejects shortcuts such as trusting an old static analysis run, \"coverage looks decent\" without measuring, or freezing the version at the last minute. A suggested timeline spreads the work over the two weeks before the audit.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/building-secure-contracts/skills/audit-prep-assistant"
