            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits' building-secure-contracts plugin. The six patterns come from Solana's account model: a cross-program invocation (CPI) to a program ID the caller chose, program-derived addresses (PDAs) created without the canonical bump, accounts deserialized without checking their owner, authority actions without a signer check, spoofed sysvar accounts on versions before 1.8.1, and instruction introspection with absolute indexes. Three are rated critical, two high and one medium. Each step searches for the relevant calls with ripgrep and checks for Anchor's safer account types, such as Program, Account and Signer. The coverage table is mandatory: every pattern gets a verdict, n/a needs a reason, and the sysvar row cites the solana-program version rather than being dropped, because a report that covers two patterns and stays silent on four reads like a clean program. It also suggests unit and Anchor integration tests that should fail on a wrong program ID, a non-canonical bump or a missing signature, and points to Trail of Bits' Solana lints. Detailed patterns, code examples and mitigations live in a separate reference file.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/building-secure-contracts/skills/solana-vulnerability-scanner"
        },
