      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Measures which code a fuzzing campaign actually reaches with llvm-cov or gcov, and turns uncovered regions into harness, seed or dictionary work.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A fuzzer has plateaued, you've changed a harness, or you want to know why some code is never reached"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds a coverage-instrumented binary, replays the fuzzing corpus through it, and generates text or HTML reports with llvm-cov or gcovr"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A coverage report and what blocks the fuzzer, such as magic-value checks that need dictionary entries or seed inputs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "libFuzzer, AFL++, cargo-fuzz and honggfuzz, for C, C++ and Rust"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "clang and LLVM's coverage tools, or GCC with gcovr; Rust needs nightly with llvm-tools"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits' Testing Handbook skills. Coverage shows whether a harness reaches the code you care about and whether a change helped. Its key rule is to measure coverage from the corpus left after each campaign, not from the fuzzer's live statistics, so the numbers compare across fuzzers. For C and C++ it adds a small runtime that feeds every corpus file to the harness; when the corpus holds crashing inputs, a forking version keeps them from stopping the run. Reading the report, it looks for uncovered blocks, magic-value checks, unreachable code and changes from the baseline: rising coverage means keep fuzzing, falling means fix the harness, flat means add dictionary entries or seed inputs. It warns against comparing fuzzer-reported coverage, building with -O3, counting harness code, mixing LLVM and GCC instrumentation, and checking only once. It also covers incremental gcov runs, filtering large codebases by directory, comparing coverage between two campaigns, CMake targets and notes per fuzzer.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/testing-handbook-skills/skills/coverage-analysis"
