        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds timing side channels in cryptographic code by compiling it, scanning the assembly or bytecode for variable-time instructions, and tracing which ones touch secrets.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing or reviewing signing, encryption, key exchange or key derivation code and worry about timing attacks"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs a bundled analyzer per file that flags divisions, secret-dependent branches, early-exit comparisons, secret-indexed lookups, weak randomness and variable-time encoding, then traces whether a secret reaches each"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A verdict per flagged instruction with the data flow behind it, naming the compiler, architecture and optimization level used"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "C, C++, Go, Rust, Swift, Java, Kotlin, C#, PHP, JavaScript, TypeScript, Python and Ruby"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike constant-time-testing",
              "text": "That one measures timing on a running binary; this one only inspects compiler output and never runs your code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. The analyzer has no data-flow analysis, so a failed run is a worklist, not a list of vulnerabilities: for each flagged instruction the agent asks whether an operand comes from a key, plaintext, nonce or token, and says why when it doesn't. It always passes --warnings, since without it the analyzer reports only division, modulo and weak randomness, and stays silent on early-exit comparison of an authentication tag, the most common real timing bug. Native code is checked at more than one architecture and optimization level, because a cmov at -O2 can become a branch at -O0, and a fix is re-checked across compilers, targets and levels including Os and Oz, since relying on the compiler to strength-reduce a constant divisor works only where it chooses to. Confirmed comparison bugs get the language's constant-time primitive, such as hmac.compare_digest or crypto.timingSafeEqual. Per-language guides list dangerous instructions and their replacements, and a table states what each language's report can't see. It is static only: cache and other microarchitectural channels, and native code the JVM or .NET JIT produces, are invisible to it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/constant-time-analysis/skills/constant-time-analysis"
        },
