      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs and improves fuzzing harnesses for C, C++ and Rust: mapping raw bytes onto an API, structured inputs, determinism and what to fuzz together.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing a first fuzz target, a campaign finds nothing, crashes won't reproduce, or the API needs structured input"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks entry points such as parsers, writes a minimal harness, adds size checks, splits the bytes into typed values, then iterates on coverage, speed and reproducibility"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Harness code for LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput or Rust's fuzz_target!, with notes for libFuzzer, AFL++, cargo-fuzz and go-fuzz"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules",
              "text": "Handle every input size, never call exit(), join threads, stay fast and deterministic, reset global state and fuzz one format per harness"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits' Testing Handbook skills. A harness is the function that receives the fuzzer's random bytes and routes them into the code under test, so it decides which code gets exercised at all. The steps start from the best targets, such as parsers, deserializers, validators and security-critical code, then build up from a one-line call to input-size checks and typed values, by casting bytes to integers or with LLVM's FuzzedDataProvider. Patterns cover interleaved harnesses that pick an operation from the input, Rust structure-aware fuzzing with the arbitrary crate, and Protocol Buffers with libprotobuf-mutator. For crashes that won't reproduce, it replaces randomness with a generator seeded from the input, mocks time and PIDs, and resets globals each iteration. Anti-patterns include blocking I/O, heavy logging, leaks and mixing unrelated formats in one harness. When the code under test itself breaks these rules, it points to the fuzzing-obstacles skill for patching it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/testing-handbook-skills/skills/harness-writing"
        },
