      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Ports an existing Semgrep rule to other languages, running applicability analysis, tests first, AST-based translation and semgrep --test for each language.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have a working Semgrep rule and want the same detection in Go, Java or other languages"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs its plugin's workflow, which for each language judges applicability, writes tests first, translates the rule against the target's AST and fixes it until semgrep --test passes"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One folder per applicable language with the ported rule, renamed <id>-<language>, and its test file, plus which languages passed, failed, didn't apply or can't be parsed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike semgrep-rule-creator",
              "text": "That one writes a new rule from a bug description; this one starts from a finished rule"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. Each language goes through four phases on its own. Applicability asks whether the bug class, and an equivalent of each source, sink and sanitizer, exist there; a not-applicable verdict goes to an independent refuter before the language is dropped, and whether Semgrep can parse the language at all is checked separately, since Perl has no frontend and Elixir's parser is Pro-only. Tests come next: at least two vulnerable and two safe cases, including the language's own idiomatic safe form. Translation follows the target's AST rather than how the source looks, keeps the original's intent and mode, and records where it was ported from. Validation must end with all tests passing on the Semgrep version recorded at the start, and the workflow retries failed validation up to three times. It warns that \"all tests passed\" can mean zero tests were graded, and that a green port is a candidate for review, not proof the bug exists there. It ports only what you asked for, without fixing the original or adding languages. For a single language you can run the phases by hand.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/semgrep-rule-variant-creator/skills/semgrep-rule-variant-creator"
        },
