        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds fuzzing dictionaries of keywords, magic bytes and tokens from source, headers, binaries or specs, and wires them into libFuzzer, AFL++ or cargo-fuzz.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're fuzzing a parser, protocol or file format, or coverage stalls at checks against fixed strings"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes a dictionary file of quoted tokens, filled by asking an LLM or extracting strings from headers, man pages or binaries, and passes it to the fuzzer"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A .dict file, such as HTTP methods and headers or PNG and JPEG magic bytes, plus the flag that loads it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "libFuzzer (-dict), AFL++ (-x) and cargo-fuzz; go-fuzz has no dictionary support, so it turns the entries into seed corpus files instead"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits' Testing Handbook skills. A dictionary gives the fuzzer domain tokens to splice into its mutations, so it can get past early validation that random changes rarely satisfy. The format is one quoted string or key=\"value\" pair per line, with hex escapes for binary bytes and # for comments, and the same file works across libFuzzer, AFL++ and cargo-fuzz. It shows how to generate entries with a prompt to ChatGPT or Claude, a grep over header files, a man page for a CLI's options, or strings over a binary, and gives patterns for protocol keywords, file-format headers and configuration keywords. It keeps dictionaries to 50 to 200 focused, deduplicated tokens rather than thousands or full sentences, and suggests comparing coverage with and without one. It also covers AFL++'s auto-generated dictionaries and combining several files. It isn't worth it for pure algorithms with no input format, or when the corpus already reaches high coverage.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/testing-handbook-skills/skills/fuzzing-dictionary"
        },
