      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Gets an independent review of your uncommitted changes, branch diff or a commit from Codex or Antigravity, and compares them when you use both.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want an independent review from another model, through Codex or Antigravity, of your uncommitted changes, a branch diff or one commit"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Captures the diff once, builds a review prompt with your project instructions and focus, and runs codex exec, agy or gemini on it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Findings with provider, model, severity, file and line, impact and a suggested fix; with both providers, where they agree and disagree"
            },
            {
              "label": "Doesn't",
              "text": "Apply fixes or post the review anywhere"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Codex, Antigravity or Gemini CLI installed and signed in"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. It takes the provider, scope and focus you give and asks only for what's missing: Codex, Antigravity or both; uncommitted changes including untracked files, a branch against a base it resolves from the remote's default branch, or one commit; and a focus such as security or performance, otherwise general correctness and maintainability. Both providers get the same captured diff, so the comparison covers the same changes, and agreement between them isn't treated as proof. For an unnamed Google CLI it prefers Antigravity, and uses Gemini CLI only when you ask, explaining the move to Antigravity if the account no longer supports Gemini CLI. Codex returns structured JSON findings through a review schema; Antigravity returns prose. It won't silently truncate a diff that's too big, stops if there are no changes, never turns on automatic write approval, and treats a nonzero exit, missing output or invalid JSON as an incomplete review rather than a clean one. Its prompt and output files go in temporary files outside the checkout.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/second-opinion/skills/second-opinion"
