    {
      "id": "trailofbits/skills/plugins/testing-handbook-skills/skills/constant-time-testing",
      "name": "constant-time-testing",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/trailofbits/skills/constant-time-testing",
        "installs": 5700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [106, 104, 96, 118, 124, 154, 160, 180],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Tests whether cryptographic code runs in constant time, checking statistically with dudect and pinpointing leaking lines with Timecop under Valgrind.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need to know whether crypto code that handles keys, nonces or passwords leaks them through how long it takes to run"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes a dudect harness that times fixed against random inputs, and if the t-test flags a leak, marks the secrets for Timecop to trace under Valgrind"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A statistical verdict on timing leakage, the source line behind a leak, and constant-time fixes re-checked with longer runs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike constant-time-analysis",
              "text": "That one, a Trail of Bits plugin, inspects compiler output statically; this one measures the code as it actually runs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Valgrind for Timecop; dudect is a single C header"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits' Testing Handbook skills. A timing side channel exists when execution time depends on secret data, and the skill names the four patterns behind most of them: branches on secrets, table lookups indexed by secrets, and division or shifts by secret values on some CPUs. It lays out four families of tools, formal proofs such as ct-verif, symbolic execution such as Binsec, dynamic tracing and statistical measurement, and starts with the last: dudect times two classes of input, fixed and random, and Welch's t-test shows whether their timings differ. A high t-value signals a leak but not where it is, so Timecop marks the secret memory as undefined and Valgrind reports every branch or memory access that depends on it. Fixes swap branches for bitwise selection and use constant-time comparisons, then dudect runs again for 30 minutes or more across compilers, optimization levels and CPU architectures. It also suggests a 5-10 minute dudect run in CI that fails the build on leakage, pinning the test to an isolated core to cut noise, and checking the assembly with objdump for branches the compiler added. Full setup lives in its sibling dudect and timecop skills.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/testing-handbook-skills/skills/constant-time-testing"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:security",
