      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Analyzes DWARF debug info in compiled binaries: searches DIE trees with dwarfdump, verifies integrity, explains the standard and writes parsing code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're inspecting a binary's or dSYM's debug sections, verifying debug info a compiler produced, answering a DWARF standard question, or writing code that reads DWARF"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds DIEs, the entries describing each function, variable and type, by name, address or attribute, escalating from dwarfdump flags to grep to a pyelftools script"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "DIE listings, verification results, answers checked against the spec, or DWARF-parsing code, by default a Python script using pyelftools"
            },
            {
              "label": "Verifies",
              "text": "Debug info with llvm-dwarfdump --verify, and compares --statistics across compiler versions or optimization levels"
            },
            {
              "label": "Looks up",
              "text": "The standard on dwarfstd.org and in LLVM's or libdwarf's source rather than answering from memory"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "llvm-dwarfdump or libdwarf's dwarfdump, or readelf; pyelftools for scripts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. It first checks which dwarfdump is installed, since libdwarf's and LLVM's take different options. It knows the traps: on macOS the debug info stays in the .o files or a .dSYM bundle rather than the linked executable, pyelftools reads only ELF, and a --find lookup uses accelerator tables that can miss names, so it falls back to an exhaustive --name search. For parsing code it prefers an existing library, libdwarf for C/C++, pyelftools for Python, gimli for Rust, debug/dwarf for Go or LibObjectFile for .NET, defaulting to Python, and lists the pitfalls to handle and to look for in review: optional attributes, attributes inherited through abstract_origin or specification links, and types wrapped in chains of const and pointer entries. When a modern compiler emitted an old DWARF version, it looks for the -gdwarf-N flag in the build, which GCC records in the producer string. Runtime debugging with gdb or lldb, and reverse engineering beyond the DWARF sections, are out of scope.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/dwarf-expert/skills/dwarf-expert"
        },
