      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits Algorand TEAL and PyTeal contracts for 11 platform-specific vulnerabilities, such as rekeying and unchecked close-to fields, with a verdict for every pattern.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're auditing an Algorand stateful app or smart signature, or checking that fixes for reported Algorand bugs hold"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds TEAL and PyTeal files, runs Trail of Bits' Tealer analyzer if installed, then sweeps all 11 patterns by hand and checks each transaction type's fields"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A coverage table giving every pattern found, clear or n/a with evidence, then each finding with location, attack scenario and recommended fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "Rekeying, unchecked fees, CloseRemainderTo and AssetCloseTo, group size, replay, access control, asset IDs, opt-in DoS, inner transaction fees and clear state"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of the per-chain scanners in Trail of Bits' building-secure-contracts plugin, drawing on its Not So Smart Contracts database. Each pattern has a severity: critical for rekeying, account or asset closing and access-control bypasses; high for fees, asset IDs, group size and clear-state handling; medium for replay, opt-in denial of service and inner-transaction fees. The coverage table is the point: a report that covers four patterns and says nothing of the other seven reads like a clean contract, so every row needs a verdict, clear names the field or check searched for, and n/a needs a reason. It rejects shortcuts such as trusting a clean Tealer run, which covers only some of the patterns, saying PyTeal or Beaker handles a check without naming the version and mechanism, or believing a RekeyTo check sits in the other program without citing it. It also recommends unit tests with a proof-of-concept exploit for each pattern, and running Tealer in CI to fail builds on critical or high findings.",
