            {
              "label": "The patterns",
              "text": "FunC's true is -1, so 1 used as true breaks logic; Jetton notifications must come from the stored wallet; forwarded TON must leave gas"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits' building-secure-contracts plugin, one of its per-chain scanners. FunC has little automated tooling, so this scan is mostly manual review, and the coverage table is what makes it auditable: even with three patterns, every row needs a verdict, clear names what was searched for, and n/a needs a reason. The fake-Jetton pattern is rated critical: any contract can send a transfer_notification, so a handler that credits deposits without comparing the sender to the Jetton wallet address the contract stored lets an attacker credit tokens it never sent. Using positive integers as booleans, and forwarding user-chosen TON amounts without checking them against the incoming message value, are rated high. It rejects shortcuts such as \"the sender is obviously the Jetton wallet\" or \"the message is non-bounceable, so gas doesn't matter\", and suggests sandbox tests that check true is -1, that fake Jetton transfers are rejected and that underfunded forwards fail.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/building-secure-contracts/skills/ton-vulnerability-scanner"
        },
