      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits Substrate FRAME pallets for seven vulnerabilities, including unchecked arithmetic, panics that stop block production, wrong weights and weak origin checks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're auditing custom pallets or runtime code for a Polkadot parachain or a standalone Substrate chain"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Checks each dispatchable for arithmetic, panics, weights, origin and write order, greps pallets for unwrap and raw operators, and runs the benchmarks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A coverage table giving all seven patterns found, clear or n/a with evidence, followed by the findings"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "Arithmetic overflow, panics, weights and fees, verify first and write last, unsigned transactions, bad randomness and bad origin"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits' building-secure-contracts plugin, one of its per-chain scanners. Overflow, panics and bad origins are rated critical: plain +, - and * wrap in release builds, so balances need checked or saturating math; an unwrap, expect or unchecked index can stop a node from processing blocks; and ensure_signed only proves that someone signed, where a privileged call needs ensure_root or a custom origin. Weights must grow with an operation's real cost, or spam becomes cheap. Before Substrate v0.9.25, storage written before validation persists on error, so that row names the runtime version rather than being dropped. Unsigned transactions need replay protection, and randomness should come from BABE rather than the collective-flip pallet. Each row of the table needs a verdict, clear names what was searched for and n/a gives a reason. It also checks the tests: unit tests for every dispatchable, test-fuzz runs for panics, benchmarks for weights and try-runtime for migrations.",
