      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes and reviews YARA-X rules that detect one malware family without flooding you with false positives, checked by bundled lint and atom scripts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing a YARA rule from malware samples, reviewing someone else's, chasing false positives or porting legacy YARA rules to YARA-X"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Extracts candidate strings with yarGen or FLOSS, discards weak ones, writes the rule with cheap checks first, then lints it and tests it against clean files"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A named rule with full metadata, or for a review, the error and warning codes its linter and atom analyzer report"
            },
            {
              "label": "Atoms",
              "text": "The 4-byte pieces YARA matches first; short or repetitive strings make poor atoms and force slow checks on too many files"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The YARA-X CLI (yr) and uv for its scripts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. It targets YARA-X, the Rust successor to YARA that runs VirusTotal's production systems. Its rules come from detection practice: target one family, not a category; reject strings under 4 bytes, API names, common paths and anything found in Windows system files; expect to discard 80% of what yarGen suggests; use nocase or wide only with evidence; anchor and bound every regex; and order conditions from filesize to magic bytes to strings to modules. It warns that uint32() reads little-endian, so a magic-byte check written in file order compiles and never matches. Packed samples call for the unpacked payload or a packer rule, and when strings fail it pivots to entropy, import hashes or PE structure. It says when to give up on a rule, such as one matching six or more clean files, and covers the crx and dex modules for Chrome extensions and Android apps, rule names like MAL_Win_Emotet_Loader_Jan25, and migrating legacy rules. A review script runs yr check, yr fmt, the linter and the atom analyzer in one call and prints one JSON result.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/yara-authoring/skills/yara-rule-authoring"
        },
