      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds secrets that C, C++ or Rust code never wipes, or whose wipes the compiler optimizes away, and tests findings with proof-of-concept programs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're auditing C, C++ or Rust code that holds keys, passwords, tokens or other secrets and need to know they're wiped after use"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs phases of worker agents: source analysis, compiler IR and assembly comparison across optimization levels, PoC generation and validation, then a report"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "final-report.md and findings.json, each finding with severity, confidence, evidence and a suggested fix, plus the generated PoCs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Never",
              "text": "Modifies the audited code; it writes its analysis to a temporary working directory"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "clang and a compile_commands.json for C/C++, or a buildable crate with nightly cargo and uv for Rust; the Serena MCP server is optional"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. A key wiped with memset can be deleted by the compiler as a dead store, so reading the source can't prove secrets are cleared. Its 11 finding types range from source-level gaps, such as no wipe, a partial wipe, a wipe missing on some paths, an untracked copy of a secret or an insecure allocator, to compiler-level ones: a wipe present at -O0 but gone at -O1 or -O2, secrets left on the stack or spilled from registers, and, when enabled, incomplete unrolled wipe loops and error paths without cleanup. A finding needs two independent signals to be confirmed, and a compiler-removal finding is never reported without an IR diff. Supported findings get a proof-of-concept program that is compiled, run and checked against its claim, and you decide on any that fail that check. Recognized wipes include explicit_bzero, memset_s, SecureZeroMemory, OPENSSL_cleanse, sodium_memzero and Rust's zeroize crate. Arguments like \"the compiler won't optimize this away\" don't downgrade a finding, and an interrupted run resumes from its saved state.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/tree/e4dd0bb5b15535f7c5a0834187dffba6ac37e4ff/plugins/zeroize-audit/skills/zeroize-audit"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:security",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:official",
          "stack:c",
          "stack:cpp",
          "stack:rust",
          "security",
          "code-review"
        ],
        "note": "Its 11 worker prompts sit in the zeroize-audit plugin's agents folder, outside the skill, so install the plugin from the trailofbits/skills marketplace. C/C++ needs clang and a compile_commands.json, Rust needs nightly cargo and uv, and it compiles and runs the PoCs it writes.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
