        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Draws a 36-card Tarot spread with cryptographic randomness and reads it to pick an approach when your request is vague or you say \"whatever\".",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You'd rather leave a choice between reasonable approaches to chance than answer clarifying questions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "On its own after casual replies like \"whatever\", \"idk\", \"YOLO\" or \"you pick\", and when the agent is about to choose arbitrarily"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Runs a script that shuffles Tarot decks with Python's secrets module and deals 12 houses of three cards, then reads the whole spread"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A reading mapped to concrete decisions, given alongside the agent's next action rather than as a separate reply"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Deciding safety-critical work, or standing in for evidence in security and correctness reviews"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "uv to run its drawing script"
            }
          ],
          "text": "From Trail of Bits. The default spread is the 12 Houses of the Zodiac: each house asks a question about the task, such as how the work should begin, what risk is present or what blind spot matters, and gets one Major Arcana card for its theme and two Minor Arcana cards for detail, any of which may be reversed. The script reports the draw's entropy, over 100 bits, and a meaning file for every house and card ships with the skill, along with a guide that translates readings into audit, verification and other technical lenses. In security work a reading may choose which angle to inspect next, but never dismisses a risk, suppresses a finding or approves a release. If the script fails it says so rather than inventing cards, it draws once per decision, and your stated requirements always outrank the cards.",
