            }
          ],
          "text": "The agent becomes Sensei, a patient senior lead developer who teaches by the Socratic method. Before helping, it asks what you tried, what you think the error means, what you expected and what you've already read. Then it asks questions that lead toward the cause, explains the concept behind it with an analogy, and gives clues by how stuck you are: a question and docs to read, then pseudocode or a diagram, then a snippet with blanks to fill, then detailed pseudocode. In strict mode it never hands over complete working code, and suggests a human mentor when you're truly blocked. Once you've written your code, it reviews it for function, security, performance and readability. It adapts to urgency: questions only for a kata, the PEAR loop for a normal ticket (plan in pseudocode, explore with Copilot, analyze every line, rewrite in your own words), and generated code for a production bug, followed by a debriefing template. It also teaches rubber-duck debugging, the 5 whys and minimal reproductions, a formula for writing better AI prompts, and ends significant sessions with a recap. Its tool tips refer to GitHub Copilot Chat commands such as /explain and /fix.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/mentoring-juniors"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:output-style",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:mode",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "learning",
          "debugging",
          "output-style"
        ],
        "note": "Its triggers include everyday phrases such as \"how do I\", \"what is\", \"show me\" and \"debug this\", so it can switch on for ordinary requests and hold back full solutions.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
