      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Makes the agent explain every approval request, error and command result in plain English with color-coded risk levels, for non-developers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You aren't a developer, say a lawyer, PM or writer, and want to know what the agent is asking to do and why"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Before each approval, says what the action is, why, how risky it is and what yes or no means, and translates errors and output"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Plain-English replies with jargon defined on first use, a roadmap for multi-step tasks, and a summary of changed files with how to undo them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Risk levels",
              "text": "Low for reading and searching, moderate for creating, editing or installing, high for commands, deletes and websites, critical for pushes, secrets and system settings"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "For the rest of the conversation, until you say \"turn off noob mode\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Written for",
              "text": "GitHub Copilot CLI users, though its rules fit any agent that asks before acting"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Noob Mode is a translation layer for people who use a coding agent without a software background. Once on, it puts a fixed block before every action that needs your approval: what it wants to do in one plain sentence, why, a risk level, and what happens if you approve or decline. Risk follows a table, from low for reading files to critical for pushing to a shared server or touching credentials, and it says when a high-risk action is safe in context, such as a read-only search. It defines each technical term the first time it uses it, backed by a bundled glossary of over 100 terms, explains ideas like branches and APIs with everyday analogies, and translates errors and command output, git status codes included, into what went wrong and what you can do. Multi-step tasks start with a numbered plan, choices come with trade-offs and a recommendation, and each task ends with the files created, changed and deleted and how to undo it. It also prefers the least destructive option and asks before destructive actions even when the agent wouldn't have to.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/noob-mode"
        },
