      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a Figma frame or component into code built from your project's components and tokens, checked against a Figma screenshot for 1:1 parity.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have a link to a Figma frame or component and want it built in your codebase to match the design"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Fetches the node's design context, a screenshot and its assets through the Figma MCP server, then rewrites Figma's React and Tailwind output in your project's conventions"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Components that reuse or extend your existing ones and use your tokens, validated against the screenshot for layout, type, color, states, responsiveness, assets and accessibility"
            },
            {
              "label": "When they conflict",
              "text": "Your design-system tokens win over raw Figma values, with spacing or sizes adjusted minimally to keep the look, and deviations explained in code comments"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Editing the Figma file itself, building screens in Figma or writing Code Connect mappings: it sends those to the repo's other Figma skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A design-to-code workflow from OpenAI's catalog of Codex skills, built on the Figma MCP server. From a Figma URL the agent extracts the file key and node ID; with the figma-desktop server it uses the node selected in the open file instead. It fetches the node's structured design context (layout, typography, colors, tokens, variants, spacing), falling back to a metadata map and child-by-child fetches when the response is too large, then a screenshot that stays the source of truth throughout. Images and SVGs come from the server's localhost asset endpoint: no new icon packages, no placeholders. Figma's output, usually React with Tailwind, is treated as a description of design and behavior, not as code to keep: the agent reuses your existing components, extending one rather than duplicating it, maps Figma values to your color, type and spacing tokens, and follows your routing, state and data-fetching patterns. New components get typed props and JSDoc comments. Before finishing it validates against the screenshot, including hover, active and disabled states, responsive constraints and WCAG accessibility. The openai/skills repository is marked deprecated; its README points to openai/plugins for current Codex skills.",
