      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Keeps one Playwright session alive in Codex's js_repl to test a local web or Electron app, functionally and visually, after every code change.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "The agent is building a local web or Electron app in Codex and should check its own work in a real browser before calling it done"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Keeps Playwright's browser, page and Electron handles alive in a js_repl kernel, reloading for renderer changes and relaunching for main-process ones"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks against",
              "text": "A QA inventory written first: every requirement, control, state and claim it means to sign off on, plus at least two off-happy-path scenarios"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Signoff only after functional QA with real input, a separate visual pass backed by screenshots, viewport-fit checks and a short exploratory pass"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Codex with js_repl enabled, run with --sandbox danger-full-access for now, and Playwright installed with npm in the project"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike playwright",
              "text": "That one, OpenAI's CLI-based skill, drives any site command by command; this one is a QA loop for your own app with handles kept alive"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A QA workflow from OpenAI's catalog of Codex skills, built on Codex's js_repl tool, a persistent JavaScript kernel. Setup installs Playwright in the project; a bootstrap cell loads it once, and top-level handles for the browser, its pages and the Electron app are reused across iterations. Web apps run at an explicit viewport by default (1600×900, or 390×844 with touch for mobile), with a separate native-window pass when window size or DPI matters; Electron always runs as a native window. Before testing, the agent writes a QA inventory from your requirements, what it actually built and the claims it plans to make, and maps every item to a check. Functional QA uses real clicks, keys and taps, takes each toggle through its full cycle and ends with 30 to 90 seconds of exploration. Visual QA is a separate pass over the initial viewport, the densest and post-interaction states and a smaller window, looking for clipping, contrast, spacing and weak aesthetics, with screenshots as evidence that metrics can't overrule. Cleanup is manual: leaving Codex doesn't close the browser or Electron app. The openai/skills repository is marked deprecated; its README points to openai/plugins for current Codex skills.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/openai/skills/tree/49f948faa9258a0c61caceaf225e179651397431/skills/.curated/playwright-interactive"
        },
