      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs, builds and audits eval tasks for your own agent with Harbor, from a spec you approve to a fully read run.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want evals for an agent you're building: tasks that run it in a controlled setup and score what it did"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads your agent's code, any traces and existing evals, proposes one task grounded in them, writes its spec with you, then builds, checks and runs it through Harbor"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A Harbor task folder per eval, with instruction, Docker environment, verifier tests and a hidden Task.md spec, plus run results and evidence"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "A World Knowledge Skill: a small skill in your repo of reusable facts about the project, reused and corrected with each later task"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks you",
              "text": "To approve each spec and World Skill change, unless you ask for an end-to-end build, and to confirm model, trials, timeout and maximum cost before scored runs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Harbor, the framework that packages and runs these eval tasks, with Docker locally or another environment Harbor supports"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A loop for building agent evaluations one task at a time, from LangChain. The agent first reads your agent's harness (prompts, model loop, tools, memory), any traces you supply and existing evals, then proposes one task grounded in that evidence. It writes a Task Spec, a Task.md holding the exact request, starting state, scoring and fairness rules, which is never shown to the agent under test, and drafts the World Knowledge Skill, the repo-local skill of reusable project facts, alongside it. You revise both until you approve. It then builds the Harbor task, tests its verifier against a valid result, a valid alternative, a wrong one, a shortcut, a forbidden side change and missing evidence, runs your real agent and reads the whole trajectory, not just the score. Failed runs are sorted into agent failures and defects in the harness, environment or verifier, and only the non-agent ones get fixed. It defaults to local, frozen or simulated dependencies, never writes to production and never asks you to paste secrets. Once several tasks have proved the shared knowledge, it can propose several specs at once and build the approved ones in parallel.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain-skills/tree/126c384d8552dcb476b226c244172186cc3f4328/config/skills/eval-engineering"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "stack:harbor",
          "stack:docker",
          "agent-development",
          "testing",
          "verification",
          "skill-authoring"
        ],
        "note": "Needs Harbor, the agent-eval framework, run locally with Docker or in a supported cloud environment; scored runs of your agent cost model usage.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "dotnet/skills/plugins/dotnet-test/skills/test-anti-patterns",
      "name": "test-anti-patterns",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/dotnet/skills/test-anti-patterns",
        "installs": 5500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [269, 312, 292, 315, 371, 367, 439, 366],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96c3d68dad2855e3ab5178aa1a262789d86a1466",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits a test suite for tests that verify nothing, flaky or order-dependent tests and other anti-patterns, with a severity-ranked report and fixes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know whether your tests actually prove anything, or why some of them are flaky"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Inventories every test and fixture in scope, checks each against an anti-pattern catalog in four severity tiers, and reads the production code behind suspicious ones"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A report with counts by severity, each Critical or High finding with its location and a replacement assertion holding the exact expected value, plus what the tests do well"
            },
            {
              "label": "Doesn't",
              "text": "Edit or run your tests; fixes come in the report for you to apply"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Test code in .NET, Python, JavaScript and TypeScript, Java, Go, Ruby, Rust, Kotlin, Swift, PowerShell and C++, in each framework's own terms"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike test-smell-detection",
              "text": "That one is the same plugin's deeper audit against the testsmells.org catalog; this one is a quick pragmatic review, and exhaustive behavioral gaps go to test-gap-analysis"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A pragmatic test review from the .NET team that works across languages. Critical anti-patterns give false confidence: no assertions, un-awaited async assertions, \"coverage touching\" (calling every public member just to raise coverage), assertions that compare a value with itself, swallowed exceptions and always-true checks. High covers flakiness (sleeps, wall-clock reads, unseeded randomness), order dependency through shared state, over-mocking, testing private internals, broad exception assertions, and transformation tests whose input is already in the expected form. Medium and Low cover vague names, magic values, near-identical tests that should be parametrized, giant tests, leftover prints and undisposed resources. Before reporting, the agent keeps a private ledger so no test is skipped, and checks the real production contract before calling an assertion tautological. It is told not to inflate severity: a clean suite gets a clean verdict, and idioms such as Go table-driven tests or bare pytest asserts aren't flagged. Untested branches next to a finding go in a separate adjacent-gaps section, not the anti-pattern count.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/dotnet/skills/tree/96c3d68dad2855e3ab5178aa1a262789d86a1466/plugins/dotnet-test/skills/test-anti-patterns"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "testing",
          "code-review",
          "code-quality"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
