      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds the likely cause of slowness on a ClickHouse-managed Postgres instance from its metrics and slow-query stats, and recommends a fix without applying it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A Postgres instance hosted on ClickHouse Cloud is slow, CPU-bound, low on throughput or thrashing its cache"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scrapes the instance's Prometheus metrics once, pulls per-query-pattern runtime, IO and call counts, and matches the signal to a known problem shape"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A write-up of symptom, evidence, hypothesis with any alternative cause it can't rule out, a short-term fix and long-term follow-ups"
            },
            {
              "label": "Doesn't",
              "text": "Run DDL or cancel or terminate backends; you apply the fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Can't see",
              "text": "Query plans, per-table scan counters or vacuum and ANALYZE times, so it reasons from IO and timing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "A ClickHouse Cloud API key and secret, plus the organization and service IDs"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ClickHouse's own root-cause workflow for Postgres on ClickHouse Cloud, in six fixed steps. Because the two APIs it uses are in beta, it first fetches the OpenAPI spec and maps the field names it will cite instead of trusting memory. It scrapes the Prometheus endpoint once for current values: a cache hit ratio well below about 95%, or connections climbing toward the pool limit, is a signal on its own, and a second scrape for deadlock and rollback rates happens only when writes look congested. In parallel it pulls the Slow Query Patterns API, which totals calls, runtime, blocks and rows per normalized query. The combined signal is matched to one of three shapes, each with its own heuristic: a read-path full scan, an N+1 hot loop from the app, or write congestion. If nothing matches, it shows the top patterns and asks which workload you recognize rather than inventing a cause; if no pattern carries real duration, it stops and says what it looked at.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/clickhouse/agent-skills/tree/d3b2b5ba43a8b80c2d08858b68d53edc701b5ace/skills/clickhouse-managed-postgres-rca"
        },
        "tags": [
