    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/blast-radius",
      "name": "blast-radius",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/blast-radius",
        "installs": 7400,
        "weeklyInstalls": [373, 436, 528, 614, 548, 565, 601, 567],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-17"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds what a change could break beyond its diff, and proves the one fact it's safe because of by running real code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A small-looking change makes you uneasy, or you ask what it could break somewhere else"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Finds the one fact the change's safety rests on, looks where a symbol search can't, and proves that fact with a script or test it runs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "What changed, the safety fact proven or marked unproven, confirmed risks with file:line, odds and cost, what was cleared, and the cheapest test to run before merging"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Listing the callers isn't the job, since grep does that in a second; the job is the breakage grep won't show. It reads the source of the library the code calls, with its pinned version and any local patch, works out when things run (microtasks, unmount and teardown, Solid versus React), and follows what a symbol search misses: the JSON an API returns, a database column, a wire format, another language reading the same bytes, a feature flag, code three hops downstream. Each fact gets a level of proof, from \"you said so\", worth nothing alone, through pointing at the line and walking the failure step by step, to a script that calls the real code and fails loudly, to a repro in the running app. A big or wide change goes to pstack's arena skill, so several models answer and their answers are merged. It pulls the PR and commits with step 2 of pstack's why skill, writes the report through unslop, and strips anything private before it goes public. It completes a set with how, which says what code does, and why, which says why it's shaped that way.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/blast-radius"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "verification",
          "testing"
        ],
        "note": "Borrows a step from pstack's why skill, sends wide changes to its arena skill and writes its report through unslop: install them alongside.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/how",
      "name": "how",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/how",
        "installs": 6900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [411, 492, 585, 563, 611, 508, 392, 342],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Explains how a part of your codebase works, at the depth a senior engineer needs to onboard, using read-only subagents.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You ask how something works, want a walkthrough before changing code, or ask where code should live and which package owns it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Sends one read-only subagent for a narrow question; for a subsystem, two to four explorers on separate angles, then one explainer"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An explanation in sections: overview, key concepts, how it works, where things live, gotchas"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike why",
              "text": "That one, its companion in pstack, digs up the motivation behind the code rather than what it does"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of pstack, Lauren Tan's agent workflows in Cursor's plugin repo. It aims for a working mental model of a subsystem, not something that reads like annotated source. A simple question, such as one module, a small utility or one function, goes to a single explainer subagent that explores and explains in one pass. A complex one, such as a subsystem across files or services, a cross-cutting feature or a full architectural overview, is split into two to four distinct angles, each explored by its own subagent at the same time, and one explainer writes the answer from their findings. When in doubt, it takes the simple path, and when the scope is ambiguous it states its reading and goes ahead so you can redirect. The agent presents the explainer's output with only light edits. Every subagent is read-only. Models come from pstack's model rule when it's set, otherwise Grok for the explorers and Claude Opus for the explainer.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/how"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "learning"
        ],
        "note": "Written for Cursor's subagents and model names; the pstack plugin for Claude Code installs an adapted copy.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/why",
      "name": "why",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/why",
        "installs": 6600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [400, 459, 548, 533, 604, 492, 383, 335],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Digs up why code is built the way it is, from git history, PRs and connected tools, and returns a cited answer.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You ask why code works this way, why a choice was made or what caused a regression, or want its history before changing it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Collects the code's git blame, history and PR threads into one brief, then sends one subagent per evidence source it finds, all at once"
            },
            {
              "label": "Sources",
              "text": "git and gh always; issue trackers, docs, team chat, observability, error tracking and analytics warehouses when an MCP server for them is connected"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A cited report that keeps what was found apart from what's inferred, with competing hypotheses, unknowns and every source consulted or skipped"
            },
            {
              "label": "Before a change",
              "text": "It turns the findings into what to preserve, change, avoid and watch as a risk"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "gh and subagents; runs only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The companion of pstack's how skill: how explains what code does, why explains the forces that shaped it. It first pins down the target and the question, guessing from the conversation when you're vague and saying how it read you. That brief, which it calls the code anchor (file paths and line ranges, key symbols, recent commits, PR numbers with their bodies and discussion, linked tickets), goes to every investigator. It maps each connected MCP server to one of seven evidence categories and spawns one investigator per category; when the target looks defensive, such as retries, timeouts, null checks or feature flags, investigators also get an incident-postmortem playbook. A category is skipped only with a written reason, and a missing source is reported as a gap, not a choice. A synthesizer subagent then writes the answer under a confidence framework, and the agent presents it without changing its confidence language. Investigators run in agent mode, because read-only mode strips MCP access, but are told not to write anything. Models come from pstack's model rule, otherwise Grok for the investigators and Claude Opus for the synthesizer.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/why"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "stack:git",
          "stack:github",
          "architecture",
          "research",
          "git"
        ],
        "note": "Written for Cursor: it finds MCP servers the way Cursor exposes them and names Cursor's models; the pstack plugin for Claude Code installs an adapted copy.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/technical-writing",
      "name": "technical-writing",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/technical-writing",
        "installs": 6200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [401, 466, 633, 546, 568, 555, 440, 387],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-02"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes and reviews docs, RFCs, READMEs, PR descriptions and commit messages to a layered standard: Diátaxis, Google developer style, STE and Global English.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing or reviewing docs, RFCs, READMEs, PR descriptions or commit messages"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks one mode per document (tutorial, how-to, reference or explanation), then applies sentence rules from Google's developer style guide, Simplified Technical English and Global English"
            },
            {
              "label": "Above all",
              "text": "Cut words that do no work, use the short everyday word, and fix a sentence another way when a rule makes it worse"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also",
              "text": "Mixes sentence lengths and asks for a view and specifics, so text that obeys every rule still doesn't read machine-written"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it, with /technical-writing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its first layer, Diátaxis, sorts a document by two questions: does the reader want to act or to understand, and are they learning or working. Tutorials open with what the learner will build and show the expected output at every step; how-tos solve a reader's problem with no background; reference only describes, dry and complete; explanation alone may hold opinion. Google's developer style covers the reader as \"you\", active voice, conditions before instructions, headings that carry the point, and never \"simply\" or \"click here\". STE (Simplified Technical English) keeps one instruction per sentence, about 20 words at most, and one meaning per word. Global English removes double readings: \"only\" next to what it limits, no slashes or semicolons, no idioms or Latin abbreviations, one name per thing. The codebase is its word list: real symbol, file, flag and command names, and no invented jargon; a new offender gets proposed as an addition to unslop rather than edited in. PR bodies should read in under a minute and link logs instead of pasting them. Every doc it touches also goes through pstack's unslop skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/technical-writing"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "docs-writing",
          "writing",
          "anti-slop"
        ],
        "note": "Applies pstack's unslop skill to every doc it touches: install it alongside.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/principle-fix-root-causes",
      "name": "principle-fix-root-causes",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/principle-fix-root-causes",
        "installs": 5800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [373, 413, 511, 410, 513, 500, 378, 320],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Makes the agent fix a bug at the first place the system went wrong, not where the symptom surfaced.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Debugging, and you want the fault fixed at its source rather than guarded where the error showed"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reproduces first, asks why at each step until it reaches a wrong decision in the code, and instruments instead of guessing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Key test",
              "text": "Is the state that triggered the symptom legitimate? If it should never exist, a check where it broke only hides the bug"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of pstack's principle skills, in Cursor's plugin repo. A symptom fix hides the evidence and leaves the fault free to surface somewhere else, and each one leaves a workaround for a bug nobody named. Guards, retries, swallowed errors, clamped values and discarded state are named as the usual ways to hide a bug. When something fails only after a restart, it suspects persisted state first: config files, caches, lock files, serialized state. It finds every place with the same fault, not only the one at hand, and fixes them all. A workaround that needs a paragraph of comment to justify it means the code is wrong. When the deciding fact took a deep dig, it proposes the cheap, always-on signal that would have shown it at first look.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/principle-fix-root-causes"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/arena",
      "name": "arena",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/arena",
        "installs": 5900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [285, 348, 474, 493, 511, 491, 446, 326],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Has several subagents attempt the same task, then picks a base and grafts in the best parts of the rest.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "One attempt at a non-trivial artifact, such as a design or a piece of code, could lock in the wrong shape"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes a rubric, runs candidates in parallel, has a judge from another model family score them, picks a base and grafts the best parts of the others into it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One artifact, with a note on the base and why, the judge's verdict, each graft and its source, what was rejected, and the verification result"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it, with /arena or \"throw it in the arena\""
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of pstack, in Cursor's plugin repo. Every candidate gets the same prompt, which acts as the contract, while the rubric of three to six gradeable criteria stays with the picker and the judge. Runners come from pstack's model rule, by default one Claude Opus and one Grok, with fallbacks when a model is refused; more runners cover more design directions. Each writes to its own git worktree, or a temp folder, with a short rationale naming the alternatives it considered and rejected. The judge is never from the parent agent's model family and works while the agent reads every candidate end to end and scores each criterion itself; agreement confirms the base, disagreement means rereading both rationales. The base is the one a future maintainer can extend most easily, with ties going to the cleaner boundary or smaller API. Grafts are usually one or two things per losing candidate, folded in so the result keeps one mental model. Candidates that converge need no graft; candidates that diverge wildly mean the task was underspecified, so it reframes and reruns instead of averaging. The result is verified against the real artifact.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/arena"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "agent-workflows"
        ],
        "note": "Written for Cursor's subagents and model names; the pstack plugin for Claude Code installs an adapted copy.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
