      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sketches a change's usage, types, signatures and module boundaries with several models before code, then implements against the chosen sketch.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're about to start non-trivial work and jumping straight to code would lock in the wrong shape"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Grounds itself with how, and with why when ownership or layering changes, has arena runners sketch two or more structurally different designs with stub bodies, then writes the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The caller's usage, a file of types and signatures or a module map, a rationale with the synthesis decision, then the implementation"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checkpoint",
              "text": "Only if you ask for one; otherwise it goes straight from the chosen sketch to code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it, with /architect or \"architect this\""
            }
          ],
          "text": "It first builds a real mental model of every system the new code touches, running pstack's how skill, and why when the design changes ownership or layering; it skips this only for greenfield work. Arena runners then sketch types, signatures and module boundaries with not-implemented bodies and pseudocode. Each sketch is screened against a list of design red flags, assuming the next contributor is an agent that sees only the files it opened and copies the nearest example, and among viable designs it prefers the one that hides more behind a smaller public surface. The sketch is the contract: a parameter it didn't anticipate is a question to raise, not friction to absorb. When implementation keeps fighting the sketch, such as the same workaround across unrelated code, special-case branches for unrelated edge cases, types that need any or casts, or callers who must know internal rules, it scraps it: reruns how over what's built, redesigns as if the new constraints were there from day one, subtracts before adding, and sketches again. For adversarial pressure on the design before implementing, it points to pstack's interrogate skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/architect"
        },
        "tags": [
