        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Has the agent design types so the compiler rejects impossible states: branded primitives, parsing at boundaries and types derived from schemas.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Designing types, reviewing a signature, or writing code in any statically typed language"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Brands primitives like UserId and OrderId so they can't be swapped, parses external data into typed models at boundaries, and derives types from the schema that owns the shape"
            },
            {
              "label": "Aims for",
              "text": "A compiler that rejects impossible states, swapped primitives and unhandled variants, and errors and special cases defined out of existence rather than handled"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of pstack's principle skills, in Cursor's plugin repo, for any typed language: a branded intersection in TypeScript, a newtype elsewhere. A branded value is validated once, when it's created, and trusted downstream. RPC payloads, JSON, IPC messages, CLI arguments, config files, environment variables and database rows count as untyped until a parse function turns them into the typed model. When a protocol buffer, OpenAPI spec, GraphQL schema, database migration or design-token file owns a shape, the type comes from it instead of a hand-written copy. For where validation goes it points to pstack's boundary-discipline principle, and for TypeScript syntax to its typescript-best-practices skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/principle-type-system-discipline"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-quality",
          "domain-modeling"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/principle-boundary-discipline",
      "name": "principle-boundary-discipline",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/principle-boundary-discipline",
        "installs": 5300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [324, 360, 423, 334, 360, 470, 360, 320],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Puts validation and error handling at system boundaries, trusts internal types, and keeps business logic in pure functions free of the framework.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Wiring validation, error handling or framework adapters"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Validates and handles errors defensively at CLI arguments, config files, external APIs and network protocols, then trusts typed data inside with no re-checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Two tests",
              "text": "Is this data crossing a boundary right now? If not, validation is redundant. Can this be a pure function the shell calls? Then extract it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of pstack's principle skills, in Cursor's plugin repo. Scattered validation is noisy and redundant and gives a false sense of safety, and logic kept out of framework wiring can be tested without the framework. Config is validated when it's parsed, raw data becomes domain types at the boundary, and nil checks deep in call chains go when the boundary already checked. Across a boundary it exposes domain concepts, not the boundary's private representation: no transport, storage, framework or wire types re-exported through the public surface, general mechanism inside and special-purpose policy at the edge. Parsing, prompt construction and scoring are each pure transforms: raw bytes to typed state, structured state to a string, state to results.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/8ecfb19f844b67a4bc7af7f66c366d57bc7b6d2b/pstack/skills/principle-boundary-discipline"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-quality",
          "architecture"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/engineering/skills/architecture",
      "name": "architecture",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/architecture",
        "installs": 7000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [139, 134, 100, 110, 120, 123, 119, 119],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-13"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "ab4cb1cd383b4eb87383be8cf4e70cb5b2a2348d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a technology choice, design review or new component into an architecture decision record that weighs each option's complexity, cost, scalability and team fit.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're choosing between technologies, such as Kafka or SQS, reviewing a design proposal, or designing a new component"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An ADR in Markdown: status, context, decision, a table per option on complexity, cost, scalability and team familiarity, trade-offs, consequences and action items"
            },
            {
              "label": "With connectors",
              "text": "It looks for earlier ADRs and design docs in your knowledge base, links related epics and tickets, and creates implementation tasks in your project tracker"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike system-design",
              "text": "That one walks a design through requirements, deep dive and scaling; this one records one decision and its options in a fixed template"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "As /architecture with the decision or system, or when you ask about one"
            },
            {
              "label": "Part of",
              "text": "The engineering plugin of Anthropic's knowledge-work plugins"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An ADR, or architecture decision record, is a short document that captures one design decision, the options weighed and why one won. This skill writes one in a fixed template: status (proposed, accepted, deprecated or superseded), date and who signs off, then the context and the proposed decision. Each option considered gets a table rating complexity, cost, scalability and team familiarity, with pros and cons, followed by a trade-off analysis, the consequences (what gets easier, what gets harder, what to revisit) and a checklist of action items. It takes three kinds of request: a choice such as Kafka or SQS for an event bus, a review of an existing proposal, or the design of a new system, and points to the system-design skill for deeper frameworks. Stating constraints such as a deadline or a load target, naming the options and including latency, cost and maintenance needs sharpen its analysis. With a knowledge base connected it searches for earlier ADRs and design docs; with a project tracker it links related epics and tickets and creates implementation tasks.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/tree/ab4cb1cd383b4eb87383be8cf4e70cb5b2a2348d/engineering/skills/architecture"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:command",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "architecture",
          "docs-writing"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/engineering/skills/testing-strategy",
      "name": "testing-strategy",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/testing-strategy",
        "installs": 6700,
