      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Profiles a table or data file, its grain, keys, column types, nulls, distributions and quality problems, then suggests what to analyze next.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You meet a new table or file and want to know its shape and quality before analyzing it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Classifies each column, profiles nulls, distinct values, ranges and patterns, and flags quality problems such as duplicates, placeholder values and skew"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A data profile: an overview, a column summary table, quality issues by severity, and 3 to 5 suggested follow-up analyses"
            },
            {
              "label": "Data from",
              "text": "A connected data warehouse, queried live, or an uploaded CSV, Excel, Parquet or JSON file"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "As /explore-data with a table or file, or when you ask about a dataset"
            },
            {
              "label": "Part of",
              "text": "The data plugin of Anthropic's knowledge-work plugins"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A profiling command for a dataset. With a data warehouse connected, it resolves the table name, reads column metadata and runs profiling queries on the live data; with a file, it loads it and infers the types; with neither, it asks for one, or suggests profiling queries for a schema you describe. It works out the row count, the grain (what one row stands for), the primary key and the date range, and classifies each column as an identifier, dimension, metric, date, text, boolean or nested structure. Every column gets null rates, distinct counts and its most and least common values; numbers get percentiles, strings get length and format checks, dates get gaps and future dates. It flags nulls above 5% or 20%, surprising cardinality, placeholder values such as 999999 or TBD, duplicate keys, skew and inconsistent formats, then looks for join keys, hierarchies, correlations and redundant columns. It ends by naming the best columns to slice and measure by and suggesting 3 to 5 next analyses. A template for documenting a table for your team is included.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/tree/ab4cb1cd383b4eb87383be8cf4e70cb5b2a2348d/data/skills/explore-data"
