        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a question into parallel searches across your connected chat, email, storage, wiki and tracker, in each tool's own syntax, then ranks the results.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You ask something whose answer is spread across your company's chat, email, documents, wiki and project tracker"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Classifies the question, such as a decision, status, document or person query, and writes keyword and semantic searches for each connected source"
            },
            {
              "label": "Ranks by",
              "text": "Keyword match, freshness, authority and completeness, weighted by query type, with wiki pages trusted over chat for policy questions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Falls back",
              "text": "Broadens terms and drops date filters when searches come back empty, and skips unavailable or rate-limited sources, noting the gap"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks",
              "text": "One clarifying question, only when a query has genuinely different readings"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "On its own when needed; user-invocable: false hides it from the slash menu"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The query planning behind enterprise search, which turns one plain question into searches run in parallel across every connected source. It first classifies the question: a decision, a status check, a document, a person, a fact or policy, a date, or an open exploration, each favoring different sources. It extracts keywords, people and projects, time ranges, source hints and exclusions, writes several variants when a topic goes by different names, such as k8s for Kubernetes, and picks semantic search for conceptual questions and keyword search for exact terms and filters. It translates filters such as from:, in:, after: and type: into each tool's syntax, with examples for chat, wiki and project-tracker searches. Results are scored on keyword match, freshness, authority and completeness, with weights that change by query type, and merged into one answer. When a source is missing or returns nothing, it notes the gap and broadens the query step by step, dropping date filters first, and suggests changes if everything comes back empty.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/tree/ab4cb1cd383b4eb87383be8cf4e70cb5b2a2348d/enterprise-search/skills/search-strategy"
        },
