        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Searches all your connected chat, email, cloud storage, project tracker, CRM and wiki at once and answers with the sources it found.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're looking for a decision, document or discussion and don't know whether it's in chat, email, a doc or a tracker"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Splits your question into a search per connected source in its own syntax, runs them in parallel, then merges and ranks the results"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A direct answer or summary with a source list of tool, channel or document, and date, or what to try when nothing matches"
            },
            {
              "label": "Filters",
              "text": "from:, in:, after:, before: and type:, translated for each source"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "At least one work tool connected through MCP; without one it tells you how to connect them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "As /enterprise-search:search with your question, or when you ask where something is"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The search command of the enterprise-search plugin. It checks which MCP sources are connected, then reads your question for what you want (a decision, a document, a person, a status update or a conversation), the people and projects named, time limits, hints about where to look, and filters such as from: or after:. For each connected source it writes a targeted query in that tool's own syntax, and runs them all at once, so one failing source doesn't hold up the others. It merges the same information found in several places, then ranks by relevance, freshness, authority and completeness, trusting official docs for facts and conversations for what was discussed. The answer fits the question: a direct answer with sources for a decision, a summary with counts per source for an exploration, or the item itself plus related finds for a lookup. An ambiguous query gets one clarifying question first; no results get suggestions for broader terms, another time range or another source; sources that failed are named.",
