      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Structures blog posts, social posts, newsletters, landing pages, press releases and case studies with per-channel templates, SEO rules, headline formulas and CTAs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're drafting a blog post, social post, newsletter, landing page, press release or case study"
            },
            {
              "label": "It gives",
              "text": "A section-by-section template for each, writing rules per channel, an on-page SEO checklist, headline and hook formulas, and call-to-action guidance"
            },
            {
              "label": "For example",
              "text": "Blog headlines of 60 characters or less, email subject lines under 50, LinkedIn posts around 1,300 characters before \"see more\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "When the agent picks it up for marketing writing; user-invocable: false hides it from the slash menu"
            },
            {
              "label": "Part of",
              "text": "The marketing plugin of Anthropic's knowledge-work plugins"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference the agent applies while writing marketing content. Each of six content types gets a structure. A blog post has a keyword-bearing headline, a 100 to 150 word introduction that opens with a hook, three to five sections under H2s, a conclusion with a call to action and a meta description under 160 characters. A landing page has a benefit headline under ten words, value propositions, social proof, objection handling and a repeated CTA. A press release has a dateline, a lead answering who, what, when, where and why, and boilerplate. A case study runs challenge, solution, quantified results and a customer quote. Social posts and newsletters get their own outlines. Channel rules cover blogs (an 8th-grade reading level, short paragraphs, subheadings every 200 to 300 words), LinkedIn, X, Instagram, Facebook, email and web pages. The SEO section sets one primary and two or three secondary keywords and where they go, with a checklist for titles, meta descriptions, URLs, headings, alt text and links. Seven headline formulas, six hook types and CTA rules on wording, one primary action and placement finish it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins/tree/ab4cb1cd383b4eb87383be8cf4e70cb5b2a2348d/marketing/skills/content-creation"
