        "updated": "2026-11-07",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans a product's or site's structure before visual design: site map, navigation, content priority, user flows, naming and URL rules.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've written a design brief, or know what you're building, and need navigation and page structure settled before visual design"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the brief if there is one, scans your routes, layouts and navigation components, then asks structural questions, each with its recommended answer"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "INFORMATION_ARCHITECTURE.md next to the brief: a site map with URLs, navigation, content priority per page, user flows, a naming glossary and URL rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "Extends",
              "text": "The structure your codebase already has, rather than proposing one that ignores it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The information-architecture step of Julian Oczkowski's designer workflow, between the design brief and the build. It looks for .design/*/DESIGN_BRIEF.md, taking the most recent or asking which feature, and without a brief asks what you're building and for whom. It then reads what already exists: Next.js, React Router, Vue Router or SvelteKit routes, header, sidebar and footer components, layouts, URL patterns and any CMS or content directories. Its questions cover at least what users need to find or do, ranked by frequency, how deep navigation may go, which content grows over time, whether different user types need different entry points, and the one view where users spend 80% of their time. The document maps every page with its URL pattern, sets out primary, secondary, utility and mobile navigation, ranks the content on each major page with reasons, and walks the critical user flows with their decision points. It also fixes one UI label per concept, lists the layout components pages share, and plans how growing sections handle pagination, filtering or search.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/julianoczkowski/designer-skills/tree/c259656c76d9758d7ead46b0d2f125cbe84f8665/information-architecture"
        },
