      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds frontend interfaces in one of eight named aesthetic philosophies, mobile-first with dark mode, reusing the components and tokens your codebase already has.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building components, pages or an app and want a distinct look instead of generic AI output"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scans your codebase for components, tokens, themes and fonts to reuse, commits to a named aesthetic and says why, then writes the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Working components or pages in the chosen style, mobile-first, with dark mode and a reduced-motion query"
            },
            {
              "label": "Philosophies",
              "text": "Dieter Rams, Swiss, Japanese minimalism, Brutalist, Scandinavian, Art Deco, Neo-Memphis and Editorial, each with type, color, layout, spacing, motion and detail rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "Mobile-first",
              "text": "Starts at 375px with min-width breakpoints, 44px touch targets, 16px body text and a mobile navigation pattern"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike Anthropic's frontend-design",
              "text": "It works from a menu of eight named styles with set parameters, and always adds dark mode"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The build step of Julian Oczkowski's designer workflow, also usable alone. Before writing code it lists the components already in your project with their props, finds CSS variables, Tailwind config, UI framework themes, Storybook, fonts and layout patterns, and extends what matches instead of duplicating it. It works out what problem the interface solves and for whom, its tone and its hard constraints, then commits to an aesthetic: one you name, the closest match to a vibe you describe, or one it picks and explains. Each philosophy sets fonts, palette approach, grid, spacing, motion and details, such as hairline borders and slow fades for Japanese minimalism or hard-edged bright shadows for Neo-Memphis. General rules: distinctive fonts instead of Inter, Roboto or Arial, CSS variables for color, motion focused on page loads and state changes, and no purple gradients on white or predictable card grids. Dark mode uses design-tokens' palette when that file exists, follows both the system setting and a manual toggle, and avoids plain inversion; a reduced-motion query is always included.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/julianoczkowski/designer-skills/tree/c259656c76d9758d7ead46b0d2f125cbe84f8665/frontend-design"
