          "text": "The brief step of Julian Oczkowski's designer workflow. It starts by asking for a detailed description of what you want to build, who it's for and your constraints. It then scans your project for CSS variables, Tailwind config, Material UI, Chakra or shadcn themes, component folders, Storybook stories, token files, UI dependencies, font loading and existing layouts, and treats the components it finds as the vocabulary the brief must extend. The interview walks each branch of the design, settling decisions one at a time, and covers at least the primary user and the job they're trying to get done, what success looks like, emotional tone, references and anti-references, hard constraints, and which content is real or placeholder. The brief's aesthetic direction names a philosophy or a vibe, with frontend-design's list as the reference, and each experience principle should resolve a tension, such as confidence over speed. The brief goes into a folder named after the feature, so briefs for different features don't overwrite each other.",
