        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes opening hooks and titles for long-form articles in English or French: 3 or 4 hook types, two candidates each, for you to pick.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You need an opening line, lede or title for a blog post, newsletter, essay or long LinkedIn article, or your intro falls flat"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks 3 or 4 different hook types from an 18-hook catalog and writes two candidates for each, specific to your article"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Labelled options you pick by letter, such as \"1B\", then a note on what your pick commits the rest of the article to"
            },
            {
              "label": "Languages",
              "text": "English and French, with advice on what doesn't translate between them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Social feed posts, taglines, email subjects, ad copy, landing-page headlines or end-of-article CTAs"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A hook's one job, it says, is making the reader want the second sentence, through five levers: open a gap, break a prediction, drop into a scene, promise a payoff or borrow weight. The agent confirms the topic, audience, language, length and venue, then proposes 3 or 4 hooks on different levers, with at least one intellectual, one sensory and one reader-direct option, and waits for your pick; \"more\" brings different hooks, not rewordings. A table matches hook types to article types, so a tutorial gets a direct problem or a promise rather than a scene. Each candidate is checked against clichéd openers and current AI tells, such as \"In today's fast-paced world\" or \"It's not just X, it's Y\". Titles work the same way: 3 to 5 across distinct formulas, within each platform's length limit. An opening you already wrote stays in as option 0. When technical-article-writer calls it, it returns the chosen hook to that skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/samber/cc-skills/tree/50a33836b20f690c668af557bd9ef9d07a8c7ef2/skills/copywriting-hooks"
        },
