            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "The meaning and the register: formal French stays formal, since formulaic, not formal, is the problem"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike blader's humanizer",
              "text": "That one is what this one suggests for English prose; this one targets French tells such as anglicisms, guillemets and spacing before punctuation"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its instructions are in English; its patterns, examples and replacements are French. Content patterns include inflated significance, name-dropped media coverage, tacked-on -ant participles, promotional language, vague attributions and the challenges-then-optimism section. Language patterns include a table of overused words (crucial, également, défi, holistique…) with what to use instead, \"constitue\" or \"dispose de\" where plain être or avoir would do, and anglicisms from English-first models, such as \"faire du sens\". Formulaic openers and closers go, along with chatbot leftovers, servile tone, filler and hedging. Structural patterns go deeper: intros that announce and conclusions that recap, sections that could swap places, every claim balanced, no dated or named detail, nothing left open. Removing patterns isn't enough, it says, so it also adds voice: opinions, \"je\", irony, admitted ignorance, a rare word, and, where the register allows, slang and regionalisms from France, Belgium, Québec or Switzerland, dropped \"ne\" and everyday abbreviations. It applies its rules about 80% of the time on purpose, since text that dodges every tell is uniform in its own way, and treats three tells together in one passage as the signal. Run it once: repeated passes flatten the voice again.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/samber/cc-skills/tree/50a33836b20f690c668af557bd9ef9d07a8c7ef2/skills/humaniseur-fr"
        },
