      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes Substack newsletter issues and web posts in your voice or a client's, with titles and hooks to pick and growth prompts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing a newsletter issue or essay, ghostwriting one in someone's voice, or turning a blog post, talk or thread into one"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Matches the voice, interviews you, offers 5 titles and 3 hooks to pick from, writes the piece, adds growth elements, then humanizes it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "The piece in Markdown with a subject line and preview text, or SEO title, description and slug, plus image ideas and a Notes teaser"
            },
            {
              "label": "Two choices",
              "text": "Your voice or ghostwriting, and an email-first issue or a web-first post, each with its own formatting rules"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike technical-article-writer",
              "text": "That one, from the same repo, writes blog posts without a newsletter; this one plans for Substack's feed, Notes and subscriptions"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It treats Substack as a newsletter crossed with a social network whose algorithm rewards subscriptions, not engagement. In your own voice, it reads recent posts from your Substack if you share the URL and sums up your voice in 5 to 7 points for you to confirm; ghostwriting first builds a voice guide of 10 to 15 markers from the person's transcripts and writing. The interview asks topic, format, audience, objective, series context and length, and waits for answers. Issues get paragraphs of 2 or 3 sentences, code under 10 lines and a TL;DR past 1,500 words; web posts allow longer paragraphs and code, more images and a table of contents past 2,000 words. Growth elements are a genuine reply question, a specific share prompt, recommendation cross-references and a standalone version for Substack Notes. With a paid tier, it advises keeping the best work free, and everything free under 1,000 subscribers. A humanizer pass leaves the title and hook intact. LinkedIn and X promotion posts and Midjourney prompts come only if you accept them.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/samber/cc-skills/tree/50a33836b20f690c668af557bd9ef9d07a8c7ef2/skills/substack-ghostwriting"
