        "updated": "2026-11-07",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Drives a booted iOS Simulator from inside Orca, with taps, gestures, typing, buttons and the accessibility tree, shown live in Orca's emulator pane.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You work in Orca and an iOS change needs checking on a simulator, or the agent must use an app there"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Attaches a simulator to your worktree, then taps, types, swipes, presses buttons and rotates through Orca's emulator commands, finding targets in the accessibility tree"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A device the agent drives while you watch it live in Orca's emulator pane"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Orca on a Mac with the Xcode Command Line Tools, and the app already built and installed with xcodebuild or simctl"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike orca-emulator-android",
              "text": "That one drives Android devices over adb and can also install apps, grant permissions and read logcat"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Orca is Stably's desktop app for running many coding agents in parallel. The SKILL.md is a short stub: it picks the right Orca executable, warning that on Linux a bare orca command outside Orca's terminals usually starts the GNOME screen reader, then loads the guide that matches the installed Orca version. That guide maps each goal to an orca emulator command: list devices and sessions, attach a simulator and make it active for the worktree, tap, gesture, type, press home or the side button, rotate, read the accessibility tree, pass any other command straight to serve-sim, the simulator tool Orca wraps, and stop the helper or shut the simulator down. Coordinates run from 0 to 1 from the top left, so the agent taps an element at its frame's center, and typing is ASCII only. It tells the agent to stop the helper when done, since it holds the device until Orca quits, and how to get input working again when Xcode's Device Hub blocks it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/stablyai/orca/tree/555bd481bcf6ac3b541cce37a4da50bbdb253008/skills/orca-emulator"
        },
