      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds native-feeling Expo screens by studying real apps first, following strict navigation, anti-slop and motion rules, and verifying recorded flows in a simulator.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building or polishing mobile screens in Expo or React Native, such as onboarding, paywalls, tab bars, sheets or settings, and want them to feel native"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Has the agent study real apps' screens of the same type first, then build to fixed laws for native fidelity, navigation, anti-slop styling and motion"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Screens checked against a definition of done: both themes, safe areas, Dynamic Type, every state, and a whole-flow screen recording watched frame by frame"
            },
            {
              "label": "Navigation",
              "text": "Decides push or replace, modal, sheet or overlay, and makes sign-in, onboarding and purchases one-way doors that back can't reopen"
            },
            {
              "label": "Anti-slop count",
              "text": "One accent hue, radii from one stated scale, no emoji in UI chrome, no gradients without a brand reason, one label per intent, counted before the simulator pass"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "An iOS Simulator or Android emulator; the Appllama MCP, a paid library of real app screens, is optional"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Appllama's build bar for mobile screens, which works with or without its MCP. Its first rule is to study before drawing: with the Appllama MCP connected, the agent looks at 20 to 30 real screens of the type through the appllama-usage skill and extracts the pattern, not the pixels. It builds on Expo Router, Reanimated, FlashList and expo-image, following twelve native fidelity laws, such as semantic colors in both themes, native controls, SF Symbols, continuous corners and the platform type ramp, plus six navigation laws and nine anti-slop laws. Motion passes a frequency gate first: what users meet a hundred times a day stays the platform default, anything a finger drives is a spring that keeps the release velocity, and the rest runs under 300 ms with a strong ease-out. It also covers state architecture, perceived performance and generated illustrations in one style. A screen isn't done until the agent has run it in the simulator, recorded the whole flow, back paths and keyboard included, and found no dropped frames, flashes or layout jumps, with 60 fps measured on a release build.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/appllama/appllama-skills/tree/dd5caaec3d5d50ad7fc0324da238119c6b7c3707/skills/appllama-app-design-skill"
