      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits GitHub Actions workflows for attacks an outside contributor could pull off, reporting only findings with a traced exploit path and a fix.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a repo's .github/workflows, its composite actions and the scripts they load checked for exploitable flaws"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Classifies each workflow's triggers, loads the matching attack references, runs eight checks from pwn requests and expression injection to supply chain and runners, and traces each attack"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Findings rated Critical to Medium, each with entry point, payload, how it runs, impact, a proof-of-concept sketch and a fix, plus Needs verification and Reviewed and cleared lists"
            },
            {
              "label": "Threat model",
              "text": "Only attackers without write access, who can open fork PRs, issues and comments; workflow_dispatch inputs and protected-branch pushes are out of scope"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't flag",
              "text": "Safe patterns such as expressions in if: or with:, SHA-pinned actions, or first-party actions on version tags"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike security-review",
              "text": "That one, Sentry's general security review, hands GitHub Actions workflows to this one when it's installed"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Sentry's review of CI workflows, built from attack patterns in real GitHub Actions exploits, including StepSecurity's analysis of the HackerBot Claw campaign. It looks for pull_request_target workflows that check out fork code, ${{ }} expressions expanded inside run: blocks, comment-triggered commands with no author check, long-lived tokens reachable by untrusted code, workflows that load CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md or a Makefile from a PR, unpinned third-party actions in jobs with secrets or write access, over-broad permissions, and self-hosted runners, caches and artifacts. A finding needs a concrete exploitation scenario: with all five elements it is reported, with a broken link it goes under Needs verification, and theoretical issues are dropped. Before reporting, the agent reads each full workflow, traces the trigger and its if: conditions, confirms an outside attacker controls the value, and checks for mitigations. When nothing qualifies, it says no exploitable vulnerabilities were identified.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/getsentry/skills/tree/e94f22214528d69899d09c627b3315ddc3544816/skills/gha-security-review"
