            {
              "label": "Unlike Anthropic's pptx",
              "text": "That one uses the same two libraries; this one splits a deck into slide modules with a fixed theme contract and can hand slides to subagents"
            }
          ],
          "text": "MiniMax's PowerPoint skill covers three jobs. Reading runs markitdown on a deck. Editing, or building from a template, follows an XML workflow in its references. Creating from scratch has seven steps: research the topic, audience and tone; pick a color palette and font pairing from its design system and a style, Sharp, Soft, Rounded or Pill; plan an outline where every slide is a cover, table of contents, section divider, content or summary page, with layouts varied across slides; write each slide as its own runnable JavaScript file exporting createSlide, which can also render a preview, up to five at a time with subagents when available; compile them into a 16:9 deck; and run QA. Every slide reads the same five theme colors by fixed key names, and every slide but the cover carries a small page-number badge in the bottom-right corner. Fonts default to Arial for English and Microsoft YaHei for Chinese. QA is required: assume there are problems, extract the text, search it for placeholder text, and complete at least one fix-and-verify cycle. Its pitfalls list bans accent lines under titles, # in hex colors and async slide functions.",
