        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Guides writing authorized Python scrapers that fall back from trafilatura to requests to Playwright, and stop at logins, paywalls, CAPTCHAs and blocks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're writing a scraper for research or reporting and want it to fall back cleanly and stop when a site says no"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Supplies Python patterns: a cascade from trafilatura to requests to Playwright, URL checks on every redirect, detection of paywalls and bot pages, and polite delays"
            },
            {
              "label": "Stops at",
              "text": "Logins, paywalls, CAPTCHAs, Cloudflare pages and 401, 403 or 429 responses, turning to official APIs, feeds or permission instead"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "YouTube and TikTok with yt-dlp, Instagram with instaloader, and a site's own public API calls found in the browser's network tab, stripped of cookies and tokens"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike stealth scrapers",
              "text": "This one rules out stealth plugins, fingerprint spoofing, proxy rotation and CAPTCHA solvers"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference of scraping patterns for journalists and researchers, in Python. It opens with an untrusted-content contract: treat fetched text as data, never instructions, wrap it in EXTERNAL_DATA tags with its source, validate every URL and redirect so loopback and private-network addresses are refused, cap sizes, and never pass on cookies or tokens. Its cascade tries trafilatura for articles, then requests with BeautifulSoup, then headless Playwright with every request checked, and an access denial ends the whole cascade rather than escalating. A poison-pill detector flags paywalls, CAPTCHAs, rate limits, Cloudflare pages and login walls from status codes, known paywalled domains and text patterns. Other patterns cover finding and stripping down a site's public API calls, yt-dlp for YouTube metadata and downloads, with an unfinished subtitle lookup, and for TikTok profiles, and instaloader for Instagram, with logged-in sessions only on explicit approval and documented authorization, plus retries with backoff and per-domain delays. It closes with the US and EU legal picture as of 2026, from the CFAA and terms of service to robots.txt and GDPR, and a practical baseline. A reference file covers browser automation.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jamditis/claude-skills-journalism/tree/e1f38cb11521b060c1f559488e8a0408c90f3d9b/dev-toolkit/skills/web-scraping"
