        "description": "Checks and polishes an existing English LaTeX paper: compile errors, venue format, citations, grammar, logic, related work, tables, pseudocode and AI-sounding prose.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have an English .tex paper for a journal or conference and want it compiled, checked or polished, not written from scratch"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Picks the smallest matching module for your request, runs its bundled script, and returns findings as LaTeX comments with line, severity and priority"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modules",
              "text": "Compile, format, bibliography, grammar, sentences, logic, literature, section writing, expression, translation, title, figures, pseudocode, de-AI, claim-forward, experiments, tables, captions, abstract and venue adaptation"
            },
            {
              "label": "Protects",
              "text": "Citations, labels, references, macros, math and numbers, which it leaves untouched by default, and it never invents citations, metrics or results"
            },
            {
              "label": "Claim-forward",
              "text": "Puts your claims before caveats and removes self-weakening wording and stacked hedges, without deleting a limitation or raising a claim past its evidence"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike its siblings",
              "text": "The repo's paper-audit reviews and scores a paper, and latex-thesis-zh is for Chinese theses"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A router for targeted work on an English LaTeX paper project. From your request it infers the smallest matching module, reads only that module's reference file and runs its script with uv, such as compile.py, verify_bib.py, check_format.py or deai_check.py; a few modules, such as section writing, captions and venue adaptation, are driven by the model alone. Compatible checks run in a fixed order: compile and bibliography, format and figures, grammar and de-AI, logic and experiments, section writing, then polish, which goes coarse to fine. Findings come back as LaTeX comments, and every rewrite block lists what changed, the tokens it protected, whether the meaning still needs checking, and risks such as overstatement. Edits stay minimal by default, set by two separate dials: a goal (grammar, clarity, concision or coherence) and a strength (minimal, moderate or restructure). It treats your .tex and .bib files as untrusted data, loads includes only inside the project root, compiles with shell escape off unless you confirm, checks bibliographies online only if you opt in to sending citation metadata, and points to venues' AI-disclosure rules rather than helping evade detectors. Venue templates cover IEEE, ACM, NeurIPS, ICML and Springer LNCS.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/bahayonghang/academic-writing-skills/tree/8c7d0748309e49c592e7f6cba184c66845517064/academic-writing-skills/latex-paper-en"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "category:science",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "stack:latex",
          "stack:python",
          "writing",
          "research",
          "anti-slop"
        ],
        "note": "Needs uv to run its bundled Python scripts, and a LaTeX installation to compile.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": "xhigh"
      }
    },
