      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns Google Stitch screens into a React, Vite and TypeScript dashboard, with DESIGN.md tokens, TanStack Query data hooks and optional read-only Web3 calls.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You've designed a data-dense dashboard in Google Stitch, Google's AI UI design tool, and want it as React code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Fetches a screen through the Stitch MCP server, saves its HTML and screenshot, maps DESIGN.md tokens to CSS variables, then splits the screen into components, pages and hooks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A React 18, Vite and TypeScript src/ tree with TanStack Query for data and, if you need it, read-only contract calls with viem or ethers"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "WCAG 2.2 AA contrast with the design.md linter, focus order, 375px and 1280px widths, no secrets in the repo and no any on contract ABIs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Stitch MCP server, a project DESIGN.md, which the repo's design-md skill writes, and a Vite, React and TypeScript project you've already scaffolded"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of Google's Stitch skills, written as a frontend engineer's checklist for data-dense dashboards. The agent finds the Stitch MCP tool prefix, fetches a screen by project and screen ID, and keeps its HTML and screenshot under .stitch/designs/. It bridges DESIGN.md colors, typography and spacing into CSS variables in src/index.css, then rebuilds the screen from a mapping table: semantic tables rather than div grids, buttons with visible focus rings, labelled form fields with errors linked by aria-describedby, and skeletons marked aria-busy while data loads. Data fetching goes through TanStack Query, with presentational components kept pure. For Web3 dashboards it makes read-only contract calls with viem, wagmi or ethers, formats token amounts, shows the network and chain ID, explains transaction errors in plain words with a block explorer link, and never puts private keys in code; RPC URLs come from environment variables. A final checklist covers contrast, keyboard order, responsive widths, secrets and strict TypeScript.",
