      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Drives a browser through the browse CLI, locally or in Browserbase's cloud, where CAPTCHA solving, residential proxies and verified browsers get past bot checks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "The agent needs to browse, read, fill forms or click through a site, including JavaScript-heavy or bot-protected ones"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Opens a page, reads its accessibility tree with element refs, acts on a ref, then snapshots again to confirm, until the task is done"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "In a clean local Chrome, in your own running Chrome with its logins through --auto-connect, or in a Browserbase cloud session"
            },
            {
              "label": "Switches",
              "text": "To Browserbase on a CAPTCHA, a bot check, a 403 or 429, or an empty page, but not for docs, wikis or localhost"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The browse CLI, which it installs with npm if it's missing; Chrome for local mode, a Browserbase API key for remote"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Browserbase's browser skill, built on its browse CLI. The agent sets where the browser runs on the first command: --local for a clean, isolated Chrome, --auto-connect to attach to a debuggable Chrome you already run, with its cookies, --cdp for a specific DevTools target, or --remote for a Browserbase session, also the default when an API key is set. Remote sessions add Browserbase Identity and verified browsers, automatic reCAPTCHA and hCaptcha solving, residential proxies in 201 countries and sessions that persist. Commands cover navigation, page state as an accessibility snapshot, text, HTML or Markdown, clicks, typing, form fills, uploads, drags and scrolls, waits, and tabs. It prefers snapshots to screenshots, which are slower and cost vision tokens. A separate cdp command streams DevTools events, such as network and console traffic, as NDJSON. When done, browse stop closes the session. Troubleshooting covers stuck daemons, a missing Chrome and failed Browserbase sessions.",
