      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds distinctive frontend interfaces by asking about purpose and tone, committing to one bold aesthetic, and avoiding fonts, colors and layouts that look AI-made.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building a web page, component or app and want it to look designed, not generated"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Asks about purpose, tone, constraints and what should make it memorable, offers two or three directions with trade-offs, then commits to one and builds it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Avoids",
              "text": "Inter, Roboto, Arial and Space Grotesk, generic SaaS blue, purple gradients on white, glassmorphism and cookie-cutter layouts"
            },
            {
              "label": "Breaks habits with",
              "text": "Dozens of named tones from brutalist to Y2K, designer and era prompts, and dice rolls for palette, layout, type, motion and density"
            },
            {
              "label": "Builds with",
              "text": "shadcn components it expects in @/components/ui, Tailwind, Phosphor icons and sonner toasts, testing with the Playwright MCP server when it's available"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike frontend-design",
              "text": "This one is based on Anthropic's skill and adds the questions up front, randomized choices and detailed rules for color, type, motion and accessibility"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Bencium's expansion of Anthropic's frontend design skill, at close to a thousand lines; unlike the repo's innovative-ux-designer, which starts from ten type-only drafts, it builds the requested interface directly. Before coding, the agent asks what the interface is for, who uses it, which aesthetic extreme fits and what one thing people should remember, then presents two or three directions and commits fully to one. A long tone list runs from brutally minimal and editorial to glitch, vaporwave and wabi-sabi. When the agent falls into safe patterns, it asks what a designer such as Sagmeister or Dieter Rams would do, reframes the page as a magazine spread or a protest poster, or rolls dice for warm or cool color, layout direction, type personality, motion and density. Standards cover color and contrast, typography, layout, checklists for motion and visual effects, and UX patterns. Implementation prefers shadcn components, Tailwind classes, Phosphor icons and sonner toasts, with loading skeletons and mobile-first layouts. It requires WCAG 2.1 AA, 44-pixel touch targets and visible focus, and tests visuals and accessibility with the Playwright MCP server when available. It ends with patterns to avoid and when to break its own rules.",
