            }
          ],
          "text": "It draws Salesforce architecture as text-based diagrams, in its reply, ready to live in docs, READMEs or issues. The agent asks for or infers the diagram type, the systems or objects involved, whether you want Mermaid, ASCII or both, and how styled it should be. It then picks a structure: sequence diagrams for time-ordered exchanges such as OAuth or API callouts, flowcharts for ERDs, system landscapes, role hierarchies and Agentforce agent-to-topic-to-action maps. Before an OAuth diagram it loads the template for that grant type, such as authorization code with PKCE, JWT bearer or device flow, and before an ERD the matching data-model template. Its rules keep diagrams readable: numbered steps where order matters, few fields per object unless you ask, color only when it helps, a color-blind-friendly palette, and splitting a crowded ERD into sub-diagrams by domain. For a real org's ERD it can use the repo's object and field skills, or its own script, which flags objects over 2 million records and shows sharing defaults. For OAuth, Agentforce and Flow details it defers to the repo's matching skills. Each answer ends with a short explanation of the main relationships, flow direction and assumptions.",
