            }
          ],
          "text": "The entry point of Understand Anything. In seven reported phases, a scanner subagent lists every file, code and non-code alike, such as configs, docs and infrastructure, with languages, frameworks and imports; a script groups the files into batches; file-analyzer subagents, up to five at once, extract nodes and edges; a reviewer checks the assembled graph; an architecture subagent assigns files to layers such as API, service and data; and a tour builder orders a walkthrough starting from the README or entry point. The graph has 13 node types, from files and functions to endpoints, tables and pipelines, and 26 edge types. Before a full scan it writes a starter .understandignore from your .gitignore and waits for you to review it, and past 100 files it suggests scoping to a subdirectory. Options force a full rebuild, run a deeper LLM review, exclude paths, analyze another directory, write summaries in another language, or update the graph on every commit. Run in a git worktree, it writes to the main checkout so the graph survives.",
