      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Edits footage into a finished video by conversation: transcribes, cuts on word boundaries, grades color, adds subtitles and animations, and checks the render.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have raw takes, such as a talking head, tutorial, interview or travel footage, and want an edited video without a timeline editor"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Transcribes every take word by word, proposes a plan in plain English, and edits only after you approve it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "final.mp4, with subtitles if you want them, plus an edit decision list and a project.md memory, all in an edit/ folder beside your footage"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "Its render at every cut for flashes, audio pops and hidden subtitles, and measures loudness, up to three passes before you see it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "ffmpeg, the cloned repo's Python helpers, an ElevenLabs key, and subagents for animations"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Video Use treats the transcript as the main view of your footage. It transcribes each source with ElevenLabs Scribe at word level, caches the result, and packs it into a phrase-level file the agent reads to choose cuts, looking at frames only at decision points. It describes the material, asks questions shaped by it, and proposes a strategy of four to eight sentences covering shape, takes, cuts, animations, grade and subtitles. Once you confirm, it writes an edit decision list, with an editor subagent picking the best take of each beat when there are many; cuts snap to word boundaries with padding and 30ms audio fades, color grades apply per segment, and animations are built in parallel subagents with HyperFrames, Remotion, Manim or PIL. Subtitles, when you want them, always go on last so overlays can't hide them. It previews at 720p, inspects every cut boundary of the render and measures loudness, and for anything you'll publish a critic subagent roasts the cut. Feedback leads to re-renders without re-transcribing, and each session is logged in project.md for next time.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/browser-use/video-use/tree/43cfc566833548093455c9d4a46bd69913b7e552"
