              "label": "Costs",
              "text": "It states an estimate and waits for your go before rendering: about $27 for six 1080p scenes through Monid; a native mobile version roughly doubles it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The Higgsfield CLI for stills, the Monid CLI or Higgsfield credits for Seedance video, and ffmpeg"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Scroll only drives time: the page scrubs pre-rendered video, so the camera really moves. The interview asks what the world is about in your own words, then the brand kit (imported from your site, given or proposed), the art direction (clay diorama by default), the camera style (fly-through, forward walkthrough or locked isometric glide), five to seven sections with their copy, whether to add a native 9:16 mobile version, and the render tier. One style preamble goes into every scene prompt so the stills read as one world. Seams are the hard part: where clips join, both sides must be the same rendered frame, never the still, or the page pops. A walkthrough chains forward legs, each starting from the last one's final frame; a fly-through renders a dive into each scene plus connectors that pull up and fly to the next. Frames sent to Monid go through its file storage, since it takes only public links. Clips are encoded at native resolution with short keyframe gaps and loaded as blobs, so seeking works on any host. It then drives the page in a headless browser, comparing screenshots either side of each seam, and checks phones and reduced motion.",
