      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4ae2317ddb22b046e26455e4f9c9b3aaaf62212d",
        "updated": "2026-11-09",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Gives the agent a shared vocabulary and product surveys for designing a software factory: agents that turn tickets into merged code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing a software factory: a system of agents that turns tickets, PRs and schedules into merged code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Fixes one vocabulary for the design, such as task, run, runner and orchestrator, and names the canonical term when you use another word"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A design, in the conversation: a product picked for each service, and each task's answers to five questions, from what triggers it to what stops duplicate work"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike domain-modeling",
              "text": "That one builds your project's own glossary as you go; this one brings a fixed vocabulary for one kind of system"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you invoke it; its author marks it in progress, so expect changes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A software factory is a system of agents that turns tickets, PRs and schedules into merged code, on infrastructure you choose. This skill gives you and the agent one set of terms for designing one. A task is a kind of work defined once, such as review or implement; a run is one execution of it, started by a trigger such as a ticket change or a clock, on a runner, a disposable machine. Around them sit the services: the ticket tracker, which is also how humans talk to the factory, the git host, the orchestrator that starts and tracks runs, and an optional observability platform. It advises starting slow: one throttled trigger at a time, braked by automated checks, automated review and human review. A design is done when you've picked a product for every service and every task says what triggers it, what it does, what it triggers next, where a human steps back in, and what stops two runs doing the same work. To help you choose, it comes with example tasks, grouped by the problem they solve, such as untriaged tickets, and a survey of the products for each service.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/4ae2317ddb22b046e26455e4f9c9b3aaaf62212d/skills/in-progress/software-factory"
        },
