        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Keeps a LOGIC.md next to every source file and directory, describing its business logic for readers who never read the code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An agent writes your code and you want to follow its business logic without reading the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Writes and updates a LOGIC.md beside each source file and directory that holds business logic: what the code does, why, and which user stories it serves"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A some-file.LOGIC.md next to each file and a LOGIC.md in each directory, from the whole product's story down to each part"
            },
            {
              "label": "Reviewing",
              "text": "You read a change's LOGIC.md diff instead of its code"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "By itself whenever the agent writes or updates a LOGIC.md, as after changing code; ask it to install LDD to generate them all"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Logic-driven development, LDD for short, keeps a plain-language description of the business logic, what the code does for the product, next to the code. Every source file gets a some-file.LOGIC.md and every directory a LOGIC.md. Each is written for a technical product manager who knows the project and programming but never reads the code: a short description, then each piece of business logic with its context, such as the user stories it serves and why it exists. A glossary explains any coined term, and each concept keeps one name across all the files. Code that isn't business logic, such as examples, gets none, and tests only get a list of what they cover. The root LOGIC.md tells the whole product's story, and deeper ones tell each subsystem's. After a change, the agent updates every LOGIC.md it affects, not only those beside the changed code. Asking it to install LDD generates them for the entire codebase at once; asking it to reset LDD deletes them all and writes them again from scratch.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/brillout/skills/tree/5f3a48113e3039adb4e5d289d0c4de4680b6cc45/skills/logic-driven-development"
        },
