        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scans a legacy codebase for its unwritten conventions, settles conflicts with you one question at a time, and writes them to .ai-style-rules.md for the agent to follow.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're bringing an AI agent onto an older, hand-written codebase and don't want its code to drift from the project's existing style"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Reads the code, sampled by project size, for four things: file layout, naming of state and flags, where shared utilities live, and error handling"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks you",
              "text": "Only about real splits, one question at a time, with a file from each side and four choices; lopsided splits go to the majority on their own"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": ".ai-style-rules.md at the project root: exemplar \"golden files\", naming rules and a list of DONTs, stamped with the commit it was built from"
            },
            {
              "label": "Enforcement",
              "text": "Your choice of a reference in CLAUDE.md, that plus a hook before every edit, or neither; with it, each coding task opens by naming the exemplar it follows"
            },
            {
              "label": "On later runs",
              "text": "It checks only recent git changes against the rules, and appends a dated evolution log instead of rewriting them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike codebase-onboarding",
              "text": "That one writes a CLAUDE.md about the stack, architecture and where to look; this one codifies only the coding conventions new code must match"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The skill aligns how code is organized, not which tech it uses or how good it is. It first counts source files to decide how much to read: every file in a small project, infrastructure plus samples in a medium one, git summaries and targeted reads in a large one. A tiny minority pattern, under 5% and fewer than 10 cases, is resolved for the majority and goes into the DONTs; a near-even split, or one as narrow as 3 to 2 in a small project, is put to you with each side's file and four options: follow one, follow the other, treat it as evolution and update the rules, or give a new rule. Golden files are copied for their structure, not their bugs. A CLAUDE.md reference is the recommended enforcement; the settings.json hook is for when you want it enforced mechanically, and it never picks that without asking. It works with any language, and git helps but isn't required.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/inherit-legacy-style"
        },
