      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a new feature through ECC's gated pipeline: research, a plan you approve, test-first implementation, review, and a commit you confirm.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want ECC's agents to build a capability that doesn't exist yet, not fix a bug or change existing behavior"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Researches existing libraries and code, has a planner agent write a task list, then builds each task test-first and has it reviewed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Stops twice",
              "text": "After the plan, until you approve it, and before the commit, until you confirm the diff and messages; work too small for a plan gets only the second stop"
            },
            {
              "label": "Right-sizes",
              "text": "Trivial or small features skip research and planning and go straight to tests, review and commit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike /feature-dev",
              "text": "That ECC command runs the same flow on its own; this one shares orch-pipeline's size check and two stops with the other orch skills"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "ECC installed, and orch-pipeline, which defines the phases and hands each to an ECC agent such as planner, tdd-guide or code-reviewer"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A thin wrapper over orch-pipeline, the shared engine of ECC's orch skills. It sets the engine for net-new work: a default size of standard, which runs research and planning, no scaffold step, which only orch-build-mvp uses, and implementation that starts by writing failing tests for the new behavior, then making them pass. The engine sizes the request first, so a trivial feature goes straight to tests, review and commit. Between the two stops the run continues without asking. ECC's security-reviewer agent joins when the feature touches a sensitive area such as authentication. For a bug or a change to existing behavior, ECC has orch-fix-defect and orch-change-feature, which SkillHub doesn't list.",
