    {
      "id": "affaan-m/ecc/skills/benchmark-methodology",
      "name": "benchmark-methodology",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/affaan-m/ecc/benchmark-methodology",
        "installs": 5000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [262, 166, 150, 241, 414, 408, 472, 443],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-15"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scores a set of competitors on nine weighted dimensions with evidence-anchored 1 to 5 rubrics, and plots them on the two-sided tension your positioning aims for.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You have a scoped list of competitors and need comparable, evidence-backed scores rather than gut-feel rankings"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scores each rival 1 to 5 on eight dimensions, from positioning clarity to pricing transparency, and separately on both poles of the client's strategic tension"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One Markdown profile card per competitor, with scores, one-line justifications and sources, its quadrant on the tension 2×2, and what the client can learn or exploit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Strategic tension",
              "text": "Two qualities the client wants to hold at once, such as memorable and credible; who else already sits high on both is the key finding"
            },
            {
              "label": "Written for",
              "text": "Design and brand studios: it judges site craft, case studies, packaged offers and enterprise-readiness, from sources such as Clutch.co and LinkedIn"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike competitive-landscape",
              "text": "That one maps a startup's market with Porter's five forces and Blue Ocean Strategy; this one scores each rival on the same rubric, with a source for every score"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "The client's positioning brief, and a competitor set from competitive-platform-analysis, which SkillHub doesn't list; it names no tool for browsing or screenshots"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The middle step of ECC's competitive analysis: competitive-platform-analysis produces a tiered set of competitors, this skill scores them, and competitive-report-structure assembles the cards into a client report. The positioning brief supplies the tension, the client's differentiator and the brand balance its recommendations must keep. The weights (positioning 18%, voice 15%, visual craft 15%, offer packaging 12%, evidence 12%, enterprise-readiness 10%, thought leadership 8%, pricing 5%, tension 5%) steer emphasis only: it never blends them into one score, since an average hides the asymmetries that matter, and never averages the two tension poles, since the gap between them is the finding. Evidence is gathered cheapest first: the competitor's site, with screenshots of the homepage and one case study, then case studies, review directories, LinkedIn, portfolio platforms and content channels. No score goes in without a justification and a source. Its bias controls downgrade claims nobody corroborates, score craft apart from credibility so a plain site that wins big clients isn't marked down, note strong but quiet studios found in directories, and reread every score side by side so a 4 means the same for every competitor.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc/tree/d2ef2680594f9a17526a6e1a2e18b3d242c5d17b/skills/benchmark-methodology"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:business",
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "research",
          "marketing"
        ],
        "note": "It's the middle step of ECC's competitive analysis, between competitive-platform-analysis and competitive-report-structure, which SkillHub doesn't list. Its dimensions are written for design and brand studios.",
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/create-verification-skill",
      "name": "create-verification-skill",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/create-verification-skill",
        "installs": 5300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [216, 228, 346, 353, 496, 569, 471, 387],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-12"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a project-local skill that launches your app, drives it the way a user does and captures evidence, and proves the skill by running it once end to end.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Your project has no scripted way for an agent to launch the app and prove a UI, CLI or service change works"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works out from the repo how the app starts, how an agent can drive it and what evidence it can capture, then writes a skill for exactly that"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A .cursor/skills/verify-<app>/ skill with launch, health check, drive, evidence and cleanup steps, and a feature map seeded with the top 3–5 user-facing features"
            },
            {
              "label": "Proves it",
              "text": "Runs the new skill once end to end on one feature, and checks the evidence survives cleanup, before handing it over"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike verification-loop",
              "text": "That one runs the build, type checks, lint and tests on finished work; this one writes a reusable skill that drives the running app the way a user does"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it; maintain-verification-skill, which comes with pstack but isn't listed here, keeps the map current as the app changes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It answers from the repo, asking you only what it can't observe: what a user touches (web UI, CLI, desktop or mobile app, API, library), the documented dev command with its ports, env vars, seed data and auth, any existing harness, and whether two instances can run side by side. If the app doesn't build or start, it fixes that or reports it first. The generated skill is written for an agent reading it cold: launch from an empty data folder until the app is actually usable, a read-only \"doctor\" check of whether the instance is worth driving, such as the right build on a port it owns, a drive recipe with the repo's real selectors and commands, proof standards (the real user path, the action and its result, side effects such as files or rows), and a cleanup that kills only what it started and keeps the evidence. Each feature file says how a user reaches it, how to drive it and what end state proves it; a journey the seed data can't satisfy is reported as failing, never weakened. If it proved the skill in a stand-in, such as a container, it lists the steps still unverified.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3/pstack/skills/create-verification-skill"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:setup",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "verification",
          "testing",
          "skill-authoring"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/show-me-your-work",
      "name": "show-me-your-work",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/show-me-your-work",
        "installs": 5100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [223, 260, 319, 325, 391, 479, 362, 299],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Keeps an append-only TSV log of each decision in long or unattended work, with its reason and evidence, then audits it against the transcript and has another model review it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "An agent works long, unattended or in several phases, and you want to see what it decided and why when you come back"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Logs one row per decision: time, phase, what was done, why, a pointer to evidence such as a commit or file:line, and the result"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A decisions.tsv kept out of git by default, and replies ending in an Attention section of flags from a reviewer on another model"
            },
            {
              "label": "Before handing back",
              "text": "Checks its rows against the run's transcript, adds rows for missed forks and wrong claims, then has a subagent from another model family review the trail"
            },
            {
              "label": "Append-only",
              "text": "A wrong row gets a new row that supersedes it, never an edit or a deletion, even during the audit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike handoff",
              "text": "That one compacts a conversation into a document for the next agent; this one logs decisions as they happen, with evidence, for a human reviewer"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it; it reads transcripts from Cursor's agent-transcripts folder and needs subagents on a different model"
            }
          ],
          "text": "pstack's audit trail: its other skills send their record here instead of inventing one. Rows stay on one line and read the way you'd tell a teammate what you did, with pstack's unslop rules applying to log text; evidence is a pointer, never a paragraph. It logs decision points, not every action: a fork chosen, a unit finished with its check, a pivot or revert and its trigger, a blocker. The helper scripts/log.sh stamps the time, writes the header on first use and quotes cells a spreadsheet would read as formulas. When several runs share a log, each marks where it began with a start row, so the audit checks only its own rows. The log lives at decisions.tsv in the work folder, or .audit/<task-slug>.tsv when several efforts run at once, and is committed only when a reviewer needs the trail to trust the result. The reviewer from another model family flags weak or missing evidence, verification claimed without proof, choices that look risky in hindsight and gaps you'd miss on a skim; \"No flags\" is a valid answer. It picks the reviewer's settings per the poteto-mode skill, which comes with pstack but isn't listed here.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3/pstack/skills/show-me-your-work"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "verification",
          "handoff",
          "agent-workflows"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/principle-build-the-lever",
      "name": "principle-build-the-lever",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/principle-build-the-lever",
        "installs": 5100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [309, 342, 399, 333, 392, 472, 353, 295],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-26"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Makes the agent build a codemod, script or generator for any non-trivial change instead of doing it by hand, so the work can be rerun and checked.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Doing any non-trivial edit, migration, analysis or check, not just bulk work"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds the tool that does or proves the work, such as a codemod, script, generator or SQLite query, instead of working by hand"
            },
            {
              "label": "Proof of use",
              "text": "A file in the diff: with no codemod, script, generator or subagent skill there, the principle wasn't applied"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of pstack's principle skills, in Cursor's plugin repo. The lever is the tool: it does the work the same way every time, reruns for free, and gives a reviewer one artifact to read and rerun, turning \"trust me\" into \"run this\". The bar is triviality, not repetition: only a couple of obvious edits skip it, and a one-off still earns a lever when the lever is what makes the work checkable. The agent does the first unit by hand to learn the recipe, builds the tool, and proves it by rerunning it on that unit and diffing against the hand-done version; the tool must be safe to rerun. A script that handles every unit in one pass beats fanning out to subagents. When work is fanned out, the lever is a skill every subagent reads, holding the recipe, the check and what not to touch, kept where they can't edit it. Per the laziness-protocol principle, it builds the smallest script that does the job, never a framework, and commits it when the work outlives the session. Unlike encode-lessons-in-structure, which turns a repeated instruction into a lasting guardrail, it's about the work in front of you.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3/pstack/skills/principle-build-the-lever"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "verification"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
