    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/principle-make-operations-idempotent",
      "name": "principle-make-operations-idempotent",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/principle-make-operations-idempotent",
        "installs": 5000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [287, 332, 387, 305, 391, 484, 365, 304],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Has the agent design commands, lifecycle steps and processing loops so rerunning them, even after a crash halfway, converges on the same end state.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Designing commands, lifecycle steps or processing loops that must survive crashes, restarts and retries"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Derives each step from the state that exists: reads it, compares it to the goal and applies only the difference, so a rerun finds nothing left to do"
            },
            {
              "label": "The test",
              "text": "What if it runs twice? What if the last run crashed at any point? Does rerunning reach the same end state?"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of pstack's principle skills, in Cursor's plugin repo. An idempotent operation reaches the same correct state however many times it runs and wherever the previous run stopped; otherwise every restart becomes a debugging session. Whatever a run leaves behind, such as a lock, a temporary file, a half-written record or a claimed job, must be recognizable as stale by the next run, judged by evidence that holds wherever the crash happened, not only in the common case. Its examples: write to a temporary path and rename it into place, and compare artifacts by content rather than by creation order. If any answer to the test is \"it depends on what state was left behind\", the operation needs a reconciliation step.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3/pstack/skills/principle-make-operations-idempotent"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "backend"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/principle-minimize-reader-load",
      "name": "principle-minimize-reader-load",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/principle-minimize-reader-load",
        "installs": 5100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [290, 322, 390, 326, 398, 474, 355, 308],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Has the agent judge code by how many layers a reader must trace and how much state they must hold, and collapse wrappers that don't pay for themselves.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Reviewing or reshaping code that's hard to trace"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Judges code by the layers a reader must trace and the state they must hold, then inlines one-caller wrappers, collapses pass-through layers and shrinks mutable state"
            },
            {
              "label": "The test",
              "text": "Can a new reader answer \"where does X come from?\" and \"what can change X?\" in under 30 seconds? If not, cut layers or state"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of pstack's principle skills, in Cursor's plugin repo. Reader load is the work a reader does to understand code; lines of code, cyclomatic complexity and \"clean architecture\" are only proxies for it. Its two axes are independent: a flat file with 50 globals can be as hard to follow as a six-layer adapter stack. Adapters with no second implementation and speculative indirection go too. Each layer should change the abstraction, and an interface should hide meaningful decisions rather than make readers learn both its surface and its implementation. State stays as narrow as possible: pure functions over mutation, locals over fields, fields over module state, module state over globals, and derived values over synced copies. Invariants are named once at the boundary, not in every consumer. Before adding a layer or a piece of state, the agent asks whether it saves at least as much reader load elsewhere. It calls itself the human counterpart of the guard-the-context-window principle: a reader's working memory is finite too.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3/pstack/skills/principle-minimize-reader-load"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-quality",
          "refactoring",
          "code-review"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cursor/plugins/pstack/skills/principle-redesign-from-first-principles",
      "name": "principle-redesign-from-first-principles",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/cursor/plugins/principle-redesign-from-first-principles",
        "installs": 5100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [311, 323, 398, 328, 413, 477, 359, 312],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Has the agent integrate a new requirement by redesigning as if it had been there from the start, and carry the change through types, docs and examples.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "A new requirement has to fit into an existing design"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Redesigns as if the requirement had been a founding assumption instead of bolting it on, then carries the change through types, docs, examples and rationale"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "Only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of pstack's principle skills, in Cursor's plugin repo. The agent reads every affected file to understand the current design, asks what it would build from scratch with the new requirement, thinks through the whole redesign, then delivers it in increments. It calls this the way to keep options open when changing an existing design.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/a5539b15a3d56e04992565aafee2b37051329ce3/pstack/skills/principle-redesign-from-first-principles"
        },
