        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Draws technical illustrations of objects and concepts, such as exploded assembly views, from photos, CAD models or descriptions, as a viewer page with SVG, PNG and PDF downloads.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want a drawing that explains how an object is built, such as an exploded assembly view, or a concept like screen subpixels drawn as stacked layers"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Builds one 3D model in JavaScript from your photos, CAD model or description, then draws every view from it in fine linework on plain paper"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A standalone HTML viewer that switches between assembled and exploded views, colors and labels parts, and downloads SVG, PNG and PDF"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "evidence.md, recording for each part of the object whether it was supplied, estimated from a photo, left out, or invented and labelled a concept"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "Making parts: nothing is toleranced, invented dimensions stay invented, and it points you to your CAD tool's drawing workflow instead"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Node.js 22 or later, three npm packages, and Chrome or an SVG rasterizer such as rsvg-convert for its proof images"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike anatomy",
              "text": "That one invents a working machine to stand for an abstract idea; this one draws the real object, or a concept as layered plates, as a technical illustration"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Teardown aims for the look of an assembly print: dark outlines on plain paper, open interiors, and parts spread apart along the paths they fit together by. It first separates the object to draw from any reference that only shows the style you want, and won't invent internals it can't see: hidden construction is left out, asked for, or marked illustrative. It then copies its scripts into tools/teardown/ in your project, writes drawing.json and a model script, and builds the geometry with its own helpers, or projects an existing CAD model. Because every view comes from one model, rib and hole counts agree between views. It renders proof images, crops close-ups and fixes the worst visible fault first until its checks pass; if it can't render, it says the drawing is unverified. The page is one file that works offline, with sliders for spacing and any editable dimensions, one part shown alone, fills, and an Annotations switch that names each part with a leader line. Numbered balloons and dimensioned sheets come only when you ask. It leaves type, color, accessible embedding and the surrounding interface to the repo's better-* skills.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills/tree/8b254eb75dadf3615a6abce87aed28e1b6e4d77c/skills/teardown"
        },
