        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Points you to the lessons of the AI Engineering from Scratch course that cover a topic, question or bug, and to the command to run next.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You want to know which lessons of AI Engineering from Scratch cover a topic or the bug you're fighting, or what to do next in the course"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Scans the course README's lesson tables for one to three lessons; for a bug, it reads each candidate lesson to confirm it covers the concept behind it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "A reply of about 12 lines: each lesson with why and a link to the course site, prerequisites if needed, and the skill to run next"
            },
            {
              "label": "Routes elsewhere",
              "text": "MCP, Agent Skills, reverse engineering, Claude certification and interview practice go to their own tutors, which SkillHub doesn't list"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike learn",
              "text": "That one teaches a lesson and records your progress; this one only points you to lessons and writes no files"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The course has 542 lessons in 20 phases, and course-guide is its map. Ask about a topic (\"how do diffusion models work\"), a struggle (\"loss goes to NaN\") or your progress (\"am I ready for phase 7\"). A struggle goes to the concept behind it: a NaN loss points to the loss-function and numerical-stability lessons, not a framework FAQ. Progress questions are answered from your LEARNING.md when there is one; otherwise it suggests start-learning. If nothing matches, it says so and names the closest phase rather than invent a lesson. Its next step is learn to be taught the lesson now, check-understanding to test yourself on a phase, or start-learning for a plan, written the way your agent calls skills: /learn in Claude Code, learn in Codex, plain words elsewhere. It reads the README and the glossary from a local clone, or fetches them from GitHub.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/a509402c0b5455d11ddc2b5fa83cc8c0d2e0692b/skills/course-guide"
