      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8aa14dd16a1340a6049e6d7cd58e2ed52333a550",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Has the agent log one wide, structured event per request per service, with business and deployment context, instead of scattered log lines.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're adding or reviewing logging in a backend service, or deciding how a new service should log"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Has the agent build one wide event per request per service, filling it in as the request runs and logging it once at the end, errors included"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "One JSON record per request with timing, outcome, user, business and deployment details such as commit hash and region, instead of scattered console.log lines"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules",
              "text": "One logger set up at startup, middleware that times and emits the event, only info and error levels, the same field names across services, no plain-text log lines"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike observability-and-instrumentation",
              "text": "That one instruments a feature with logs, metrics, traces and alerts; this one covers logs only, as one wide event per request"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A wide event, also called a canonical log line, is one structured record holding everything about a request. Its point is answering questions you didn't plan for, such as \"which free-trial users hit this bug\", by querying any field. So the skill wants many fields per event, including fields with millions of distinct values, like user and request IDs. Every event carries business context (subscription tier, cart value, feature flags, account age) and environment context (commit hash, service version, region, instance ID), and a request ID passed between services ties their events together. Middleware starts the event, times it and emits it in a finally block, so it's logged even on failure; handlers only add business context. Four rule files give incorrect and correct TypeScript examples, with the pino logger, for wide events, context, structure and pitfalls. It cites the author's post \"Observability Wide Events 101\", loggingsucks.com and Stripe's post on canonical log lines.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/boristane/agent-skills/tree/8aa14dd16a1340a6049e6d7cd58e2ed52333a550/skills/logging-best-practices"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:cloud",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "observability",
          "backend"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
