        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Scans a codebase into a project-doc.md of its stack, architecture and conventions plus an AGENTS.md, then rescans only what changed and asks before updating AGENTS.md.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're setting up ShipMate, a pipeline of coding agents, in a repo, or want its project notes refreshed after code changes"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "First run: maps the stack, dependencies, architecture, folders, conventions and test coverage into project-doc.md, then rewrites it as AGENTS.md rules for agents"
            },
            {
              "label": "Later runs",
              "text": "Rescan the files changed in the last commit and patch project-doc.md; on an architectural change, it asks before patching AGENTS.md"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "project-doc.md in .claude/pipeline/, an AGENTS.md at the repo root with do and don't rules and notes for each pipeline agent, and a short summary"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "The understand-anything and context-mode plugins, when installed, for deeper analysis and keeping large outputs out of context; otherwise find, grep and git"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike create-agentsmd",
              "text": "That one writes an AGENTS.md of setup, test, build and PR instructions; this one adds a fuller project-doc.md and notes for each ShipMate agent, and keeps both current"
            },
            {
              "label": "Part of",
              "text": "ShipMate, whose orchestrator, architect, developer, reviewer and QA agents read the AGENTS.md it writes before any work"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The full scan writes .claude/pipeline/project-doc.md: tech stack, key dependencies with versions, architecture pattern, a folder map, code style inferred from the actual code, data architecture, auth and error handling, how services talk, test coverage and entry points. It then writes AGENTS.md as instructions rather than a copy: do and don't style rules, architecture guardrails, testing requirements, where code goes, security rules, and notes for each agent, such as which paths the reviewer treats as critical. MERN projects (MongoDB, Express, React, Node) get extra notes, such as keeping login tokens in httpOnly cookies rather than localStorage. Later runs are delta scans: they take the files changed in the last commit, patch only the affected sections, and look for a new framework, directory pattern, dependency swap or auth pattern. AGENTS.md changes only when you answer y, and only in the affected sections. Each run ends with a summary, including test coverage, and marks the scan done in the pipeline's state.json.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/9c61ab243def98f72da12abd41cfcf5bcd91af9d/plugins/ship-mate/skills/scan"
