    {
      "id": "jakubkrehel/oklch-skill/skills/oklch-skill",
      "name": "oklch-skill",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jakubkrehel/oklch-skill/oklch-skill",
        "installs": 5100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [290, 259, 235, 214, 224, 143, 131, 178],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-01"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "0e81f040e16d48fcc61d3e1b7ea548542371321f",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Converts colors to OKLCH and builds palettes, contrast checks, gamut fallbacks and Tailwind v4 themes with it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're moving a web project's colors to OKLCH, building a palette, fixing failing contrast or setting up a Tailwind v4 theme"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Converts hex, rgb and hsl to oklch() and leaves the rest of the CSS alone, builds 50 to 950 scales at one hue, and fixes contrast by changing lightness only"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Every changed color in a Before and After table, plus CSS variables, sRGB fallbacks or a Tailwind v4 @theme"
            },
            {
              "label": "OKLCH means",
              "text": "A CSS color format of lightness from 0 to 1, chroma (colorfulness) and hue in degrees, where equal lightness steps look equally bright across hues"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike better-colors",
              "text": "That one, by the same author, builds ramps with a color library and names semantic tokens; this one is a reference on OKLCH itself, with no scripts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "OKLCH matches how people see color better than HSL: a step in lightness looks the same for every hue, and the hue stays put as a color gets lighter or darker, where HSL blues drift toward purple. The skill holds the rules and four reference files. Conversion changes only the color values, leaving alone keywords like currentColor, gradient functions and third-party configs that expect hex. Palettes spread lightness evenly and give each step the same share of the most chroma its hue can show, so blues, greens and reds look equally vivid and stay displayable; dark mode reverses the light palette rather than picking new colors. Contrast is checked with APCA, a newer contrast measure (|Lc| of 60 for body text), or WCAG 2 (4.5:1), and fixed by moving lightness alone. Colors outside sRGB, such as Display P3, get a @media (color-gamut: p3) wrapper with an sRGB fallback. Values are written to three decimals. It points to oklch.fyi to explore colors interactively.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jakubkrehel/oklch-skill/tree/0e81f040e16d48fcc61d3e1b7ea548542371321f/skills/oklch-skill"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:css",
          "stack:tailwind",
          "design-system",
          "accessibility",
          "design"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "jeffallan/claude-skills/skills/rag-architect",
      "name": "rag-architect",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jeffallan/claude-skills/rag-architect",
        "installs": 5000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [81, 89, 95, 99, 136, 112, 135, 119],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-21"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs and builds retrieval-augmented generation systems: chunking, embeddings, the vector store, hybrid search, reranking and retrieval evaluation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're building search or question answering over your own documents and want retrieval you can measure, not just one that runs"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works through requirements, vector database, chunking, retrieval and evaluation, with a check after each step, such as every chunk keeping its source"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An architecture diagram, a vector database pick with trade-offs, a chunking strategy, the query-to-results flow and an evaluation plan with pass and fail thresholds"
            },
            {
              "label": "Code",
              "text": "Python with LangChain's text splitter, OpenAI embeddings, Qdrant, BM25 keyword scoring, Cohere reranking and Ragas for evaluation"
            },
            {
              "label": "Bar to pass",
              "text": "Context precision of 0.7 and recall of 0.6 on a labeled question set before a language model is connected"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike rag-implementation",
              "text": "That one is a reference of options with LangChain and LangGraph code; this one is a staged build with a check at each step and set thresholds"
            }
          ],
          "text": "RAG, retrieval-augmented generation, means finding the passages of your documents that answer a question and handing them to the model. This skill treats retrieval as the part to get right before the model is involved. Its rules: compare embedding models on your own data, don't take a default chunk size of 512 untested, tag every chunk with its source, timestamp and section, combine vector and keyword search, rerank the top results, and filter by metadata such as tenant. Ingestion must be safe to rerun, with IDs derived from each chunk's content so duplicates collapse. Embeddings are versioned and kept apart from app code, so the model can change later. Precision@k, recall@k, MRR and NDCG are tracked, along with latency over time. Five reference files load as needed: a comparison of Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant, Chroma and pgvector, embedding models from OpenAI, Cohere, Voyage and open source, chunking strategies, retrieval techniques such as rank fusion and cross-encoder reranking, and evaluation with Ragas.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jeffallan/claude-skills/tree/078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3/skills/rag-architect"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:python",
          "agent-development",
          "databases"
        ],
        "model": "Claude Opus 5.5",
        "effort": null
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "jeffallan/claude-skills/skills/cloud-architect",
      "name": "cloud-architect",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jeffallan/claude-skills/cloud-architect",
        "installs": 5000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [91, 96, 97, 103, 128, 117, 141, 103],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3",
        "updated": "2026-11-12",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs cloud architectures on AWS, Azure or GCP, with migration plans, cost optimization and disaster recovery, checked against the Well-Architected Framework.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "You're designing a system on AWS, Azure or Google Cloud, planning a move to the cloud, cutting cloud costs or planning disaster recovery"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "Works through discovery, design, security, cost, migration and operations, checking that nothing is a single point of failure and network links are live before cutover"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "An architecture diagram with data flow, the reason for each service, IAM, network and encryption design, a cost estimate, and a deployment and rollback plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rules",
              "text": "At least 99.9% availability, least-privilege access, infrastructure as code, cost allocation tags, multi-region for critical workloads, and disaster recovery that's tested"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Your cloud's CLI and account for its checks and cost queries, which it spells out for AWS and Azure; its examples are mostly AWS with Terraform"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike multi-cloud-architecture",
              "text": "That one maps equivalent services across four clouds for designs that span them; this one takes one design from discovery through migration to operations"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It acts as a cloud architect working in six stages. Migration uses the 6Rs: for each app, rehost it as is, replatform with small changes, repurchase as SaaS, refactor, retire or retain it, moving in waves. Each stage has a check: after design, every component needs a redundancy plan; before cutover, VPC or VNet peering must show as active; after migration, load balancer targets must be healthy; after a disaster recovery test, actual recovery times are recorded against the targets for downtime (RTO) and data loss (RPO). Its examples are a least-privilege IAM role limited to one S3 bucket, a VPC with public and private subnets across two availability zones, an auto-scaling group that targets 60% CPU, and CLI queries for the biggest costs of the last 30 days on AWS and Azure. Five reference files load as needed: AWS with its Well-Architected Framework, Azure, Google Cloud, multi-cloud and vendor lock-in, and cost (FinOps).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jeffallan/claude-skills/tree/078a25cc5bfd8011459be7b91d004e900c1d02d3/skills/cloud-architect"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:cloud",
